\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\b0\par\par\uc2 3,851 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 September 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, IRAN, RUSSIA, AMINI, ZELENSKYY, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 SULLIVAN, RUSSIA, IRAN, BIDEN, PUTIN, NUCLEAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GLOBAL UNREST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE-ON-ONE WITH WH NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER JAKE SULLIVAN\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO, IRAN, NUCLEAR, BIDEN, UKRAINE, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 44 DAYS TO MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE-ON-ONE WITH SEN JOHN BARRASSO\par \par \uc2 JAMES, TRUMP, LAWSUITS, DOCS, JAN 6\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: WEDNESDAY\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, INVESTIGATION, FRAUD, JAN 6, DOCS, ELECTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP'S LEGAL WOES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP CLAIMS WITHOUT EVIDENCE HE DECLASSIFIED MAR-A-LAGO DOCS\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM, POLL, BIDEN, TRUMP, JAN 6, 2024, MCCARTHY\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: FRIDAY\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY (REP-CA) (HOUSE MINORITY LEADER)\par \par \uc2 Democrats have no plan for the problem they created. We can secure our border. We can become energy independent, where your price of gas is lower. We can build an education system that has a parents' bill of rights.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Now, House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy went to Pennsylvania and unveiled on what, what he calls a Commitment to America. We didn't hear him mention the right to choose. We didn't hear him mention Medicare. We didn't hear him mention Social Security.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Kevin McCarthy, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  out there this week, as we head into the midterms. We also have are our brand-new ABC News poll. We're going to talk about all that here on our Roundtable, joined by \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , Donna Brazile, Julie Pace, the executive editor of the AP, and Rachel Scott, our congressional correspondent. Welcome to all of you. Chris, let's start with the poll. We see an even divide among Democrats and Republicans in the generic. But that's not saying much for Democrats, as we head into the midterms.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC/WAPO POLL: BIDEN APPROVAL AT 39%, SPELLING TROUBLE FOR DEMOCRATS\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, it's not. Look, the key numbers that I looked, George, was the President at 36 on his approval rating, 21 points underwater, that 74 percent of the people in the poll said the economy is bad, they characterized it, and that the - it's the top issue to 84 percent of the people. That's all bad news for the Democrats in that poll. And I will tell you, I was talking to a family farmer this week. Just to put a - what we have to look forward to, he said since Biden got into office, his diesel fuel costs were up 222 percent on his farm, and his nitrogen costs are up 262 percent.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's not only bad for them and their ability to make money, but, worse, it's gonna hit us in supermarkets all over this country. This is hardly over, and that's what these numbers are telling. People are feeling that, and they're gonna vote that way, and that's not good for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Is there any argument the Democrats can make that will make inflation not the top issue on voters' minds as they go to the polls?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, look, I think Democrats understand that the stakes are high. This is a consequential midterm, and we know we have to defy history. That being said, look at the alternative. What are the Republicans planning to do to harm you, to harm the economy? \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has really done a, a fabulous job, I think, in making sure that every day Americans can feel the, the, the things that he's doing, whether it's lowering gas prices, making sure that our supply chain is now moving forward. He just stopped a rail strike which really would have crippled our economy. So, look, we know we have a big job to do in motivating people to get out to vote. But, most importantly, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is playing the long game. That's why I'm still with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Julie Pace, you just saw...\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm ridin' with Biden, baby. I'm ridin' with Biden.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 ...we, we just saw Donna frame, set the frame for the election, really. Republicans want it to be a referendum on the economy and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Democrats want it to be a choice between two agendas.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC/WAPO POLL: VOTERS BACK GOP TO HANDLE ECONOMY, INFLATION\par \par \uc2 JULIE PACE (ASSOCIATED PRESS EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR)\par \par \uc2 And that's why we are in a little bit of uncertain territory when it comes to, to these midterms. And look, history tells us, obviously, that the President's party does poorly in the midterms, especially when the economy is in a state like it's in right now. There is so much uncertainty about the economy. Even people like Fed Chair Jerome Powell say we don't really know what's happening. The economy is just, frankly, acting weirdly. The one thing that is keeping Democrats motivated right now is, to Donna's point, they feel like they can present a credible alternative choice.\par \par \uc2 JULIE PACE (ASSOCIATED PRESS EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR)\par \par \uc2 They feel like abortion has changed the game, here. They feel like it's motivating voters. But I think we are, we're really in for a turbulent and uncertain stretch between now and the midterms.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Abortion works for the Democrats. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , to some extent, works for the Democrats, as well. And - that it's up against and it shows in our poll as well, immigration, inflation and crime for the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC/WAPO POLL: DEMS FAVORED ON ABORTION, 64% OPPOSE ROE REVERSAL\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. And I think you have these two different scenarios sort of playing out, here. Yes, is abortion energizing Democratic voters? We've seen that. Obviously, the special election right here in New York, Pat Ryan won his special election in a critical swing district when he was down in the polls by campaigning and making abortion a central issue. Kansas, a conservative state, votes to protect abortion rights. But when you ask registered voters what issue is at the top of that list, and you see inflation and the economy and you see 74 percent of Americans say that the economy is in a bad state right now, and not only that, you have independents thinking that Republicans would do a better job of handling the economy, that all points to an extraordinary set of challenges for Democrats trying to keep the House and Senate.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, George, let's, let's talk about the abortion issue, because 84 percent of the voters say the economy is their top issue. Only 62 percent say abortion. And when you go deeper into the poll and look at some of the cross tabs on this, what our poll is showing is that the pro-life people are more motivated by the abortion issue to vote in these midterms than the pro-choice people are.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That hasn't been the case this summer, so far.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, it hasn't, but, but this is the snapshot we're taking right now in September. I think the further you get away from the Dobbs decision and closer to the midterms, the less of a motivating factor abortion is. And obviously, from these numbers...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...the more of a motivating factor the economy is. And, and I understand history is this way as Julie said, and we better pay heed to history. It's, it's gonna happen again. When you have a bad economy, that is what people vote on. They don't vote on anything else, because that's what affects them and their families, every day.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Julie, the counter...\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Over, over nine million jobs being created, so let's not doom the economy when people are going back to work, when people are trying to find the right job, the right fit. The economy is not in the tank. We know the market. We know all of this.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I, I want you to argue against 84 percent of people in the country. Great (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I'm not, I'm arguing against 84 percent.\par \par \uc2 JULIE PACE (ASSOCIATED PRESS EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR)\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible) .\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm just saying the reality is, is that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has presided over an economy that has come back from the brink. Look, we had a pandemic that took over a million lives in this country, and we had people who stopped working. So, things are improving. Are they where we want them to be? No, but we're not where we started.\par \par \uc2 JULIE PACE (ASSOCIATED PRESS EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR)\par \par \uc2 This is what frustrates Democrats, though. It's very similar to what \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  faced in his first midterm, as well.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I remember.\par \par \uc2 JULIE PACE (ASSOCIATED PRESS EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR)\par \par \uc2 The trend lines were moving in one direction, and yet people didn't feel it. Democrats sometimes chalk that up to a communications problem. But I think Democrats have to ask themselves, why are the numbers showing that and people do not feel that way? What is, what is, what is the thing they can do to try connect the dots there for them.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) 'Cause the, the American people see another shoe is gonna drop. It's unsettling. I mean, I know it, because I talk to my own family.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that's, well, and that's...\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I talk to my friends. I, I know what happens when you go to the grocery store. Because when I go to the grocery store, I better bring a coupon, because I know the prices are a little high.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I went food shopping the other day for...\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You did?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...for our family and I picked up a bag of Tostitos. A bag of Tostitos...\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...was $6.59. Like, I'm sorry, that's nuts. And, and the...\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Man, that was a big bag.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, it wasn't that big. Let me tell you, man...\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A big bag.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...it wasn't that big, and I'll, George knows this. He was involved in this. This always happens to incumbent presidents, that they don't get credit for the economy it lags.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yep.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It happened to Bush 41 in 1992. The economy was coming back, but people didn't believe it.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  in '94.\par \par \uc2 ROUNDTABLE GUEST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Yep.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They didn't believe it. And so, the problem is in making the argument that Donna's making is, you, you are swimming upstream on that argument, and you better come up with something else.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Because 84 percent of the people in this poll don't buy it, and that's not a winning strategy.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But, but not only that, but also the approval ratings. And when we look at history, when we have presidents that have approval ratings as low as this president, not only less than 50 percent, less than 40%, they lose seats. Thirty-seven seats, on average, their party loses in a midterm election year.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The counter-argument that Nancy Pelosi made to that when I spoke with her this week, Donna, is that there's not a lot of low-hanging fruit out there for Republicans...\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...that you don't have the traditional Democratic bump in the House back in the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right. Look, she, and by the way, I trust her, I trust her gut on this. And the other thing is that you gotta think about what Democrats are faced with. We're faced with election-deniers. We're faced with people who are acting like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , that they are somehow or another going to change what happened in the last election. So, I think we also need to look at it state by state, race by race. It is, it's hard pickings, but I do think that the Democrats have done a - fabulous job when selecting good candidates, and they know exactly what to talk about...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2022 MIDTERM ELECTIONS JUST OVER SIX WEEKS AWAY\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Chris, it's been fascinating to watch out in the Senate candidate. We saw it with Dan (PH) Bolduc in, in New Hampshire, one of the candidates who is hard on election denialism during the primaries, now trying to pivot away from it. Can you do that, is the question?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Probably not, effectively, especially in a state like New Hampshire, where people pay attention and they're plugged into their elections. They're not detached folks. I think they're going to look at Dan (PH) Bolduc and be skeptical that somebody like him could be saying, "I will not change horses in midstream, the election was stolen," and then, the day after the primary, to go on a national news show and say, "You know, I've been researching this, and I don't see any evidence for it."\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, the simple fact is that we've been saying all along, some of us, and I've been saying it on this show, that this is moving away from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And it is a slow move, but it is a move. And we also talked a few weeks ago, right here, about how DOJ would do in this special master litigation. I told you I thought they'd win, and they did. And now the classified documents are being reviewed again. This stuff is happening, 'cause the law matters and common sense does matter, ultimately.\par \par \uc2 JULIE PACE (ASSOCIATED PRESS EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR)\par \par \uc2 I do think that one thing that we have to watch, though, in this election is, I do think you're right that this is happening at some of those top-tier races, secretary of state's races, state legislative races. This is where, heading into 2024, depending on the outcome of those races, yes, the messaging might change at the top level of some of the, some parts of the Republican Party, but what's happening in the races that could actually make a difference in terms of integrity of elections, the ability to protect democracy. This is gonna be hugely consequential at those down-ballot races.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, Donna, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  says this is slowly moving away from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and I think I actually agree with him. But the polling, right now, is not really showing that.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's showing in - among Republican voters he's holding firm. Our poll shows him defeating \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in 2024.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is, his house of cards are - really crumbling down. He's under investigation just about everywhere. Too bad he's not in Louisiana. We'll put him under investigation just for showing up. But, look, the truth is, is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is, at this point, the major player in the Republican Party. But, at the same time, these candidates are gonna have to own their own messages. He was in North Carolina. Budd is gonna have to own his message. Mr. Oz is gonna have to own his own message and, and his, and Mr. Vance.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Donald trump has gotten these, these candidates into the dance. Now they're gonna have figure out if they know the groove. And I think that's where Democrats will have an advantage, especially because we have better quality candidates across the country.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He will be centerstage in, in the House, as well. This week, the January 6th Committee is coming back.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JAN 6 COMMITTEE EXPECTED TO HOLD FINAL HEARING WEDNESDAY\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, the January 6th Committee is coming back, the last hearing before the midterms. They're trying to wrap this up, put a fine point on this, but it's still very much a work in progress, because they're getting new information almost every single week, here. But the hold that we're talking about that Trump has on this, especially when you look at the legislation that came forward in the House, reform to the Electoral Count Act, the very thing that Trump tried to exploit on January 6th. When you have a scenario where you only have nine Republicans in the House voting for that, 203 voting against reform for that, and all of those Republicans that voted for it aren't even returning to Congress next year.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Chris, you brought up the Court of Appeals and the special master this week. When you look at what is happening now in that documents case, do you agree with what we saw, what we heard from Dan Abrams, the chances that he could be indicted are probably increasing, there?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP CLAIMS WITHOUT EVIDENCE HE DECLASSIFIED MAR-A-LAGO DOCS\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, I think these are, these are all self-inflicted wounds by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And, and, you know, one of the things that I would always say to clients are, you know, don't out-loud fight with the Department of Justice. Like, you know, we'll fight in court and do the things we need to do. He's doing the exact opposite. His lawyers aren't fighting any of this in court. They're really not. They're not putting forward any of these arguments. He's putting forward all these arguments on television. There comes a point where prosecutors are human, too, and if you want to keep daring them, they may just actually do it.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I still don't think he's gonna be charged, but I will say this much about what Dan said, the more you absolutely antagonize with nonsense arguments on television that your lawyers won't make in court because they're afraid they'll be sanctioned if they do because they have no evidence, you're pushing yourself closer to a self-inflicted indictment. And I don't want to see that happen, just because I don't think it's good for our country. But he's pushing himself in that direction.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we don't see him making any moves to get off the campaign trail, despite what a lot of Republican leaders might want.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP CONTINUES TO TEASE 2024 BID DESPITE LEGAL WOES\par \par \uc2 JULIE PACE (ASSOCIATED PRESS EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR)\par \par \uc2 We absolutely do not. In fact, he's using his campaign trail appearances to make the exact arguments that we're talking about, here. I mean, I think one of the things that will be really fascinating, as these legal cases move forward, is do we start to hear from other Republicans, particularly potential 2024 contenders who say, we just don't need this as a country. We don't need somebody running for office, even a former president, even in investigations we may not agree with, who's under so much legal scrutiny. This is not good for the country. We haven't heard that quite yet at a large scale, but I would be watching for that from DeSantis and Pence and others.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, I think Republican leaders are co-dependent with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So, and the, to, to the extent that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is out there every weekend in all of these major battleground states, he's trying to motivate the Republican base. But you know what? He's, he's energizing Democrats, and he's basically helping us with independents. So, as far as I'm concerned, if I can contribute a dollar and some coins to his gas budget, because he's basically running - around the country begging for money, then let him go. Let him continue to campaign across the country, because it's helping us prove the case that this is not a referendum on Biden.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's a choice between having someone who incited a mob, incited an insurrection, and someone who's trying to save this country and keep our, our people happy.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel, one of the things you saw this weekend, most, most top Republicans will defend \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if asked about it, but they won't bring him up on their own, Kevin McCarthy this week.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MCCARTHY UNVEILS AGENDA IF REPUBLICANS RECLAIM HOUSE\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Not a single mention of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in Kevin McCarthy's speech unveiling the public agenda this past week. Obviously, they know that America First policies, right, a popular with Republican voters, but a lot of Republican voters don't want the drama that comes around with all of these investigations and having the former president in the spotlight. I thought that was very notable there that he did not mention his name once, and also didn't talk about these false claims about the 2020 election, either, right, that we see a lot of candidates across the country actually campaigning on.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) FiveThirtyEight has analysis that shows 68 percent of Americans will have an election denier on the ballot this November. The stakes are incredibly high. But, yes, you see Republicans trying to split the difference here and try to create some distance between themselves and the former president. And unless you press on it like you did with Mr. Barrasso, you can't get a firm answer.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That is all we have time for today. Thank you all very much.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Coming up, a first look at our new political show streaming on \uc2 Hulu\uc2 . It's called "Power Trip."\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220928ei9p00007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ARIZONA NEAR TOTAL ABORTION BAN JUDGE SAYS CENTURY OLD LAW CAN TAKE EFFECT\b0\par\par\uc2 692 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 September 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Sunday\par \uc2 WNSU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 IAN, FLORIDA, EMERGENCY, DUDELY, FIONA, DAMAGE, OUTAGES\par \par \uc2 ANTI-WAR, PUTIN, PROTEST, DRAFT, REFERENDUM, ZELENSKYY, SULLIVAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VIOLENT CRACKDOWN IN RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, BAN, ARIZONA, DEMS, REPS, POLL, CHENEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ARIZONA NEAR TOTAL ABORTION BAN\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tom Soufi Burridge, our thanks to you, tonight. Back here at home, the midterm elections are just 44 days away, and abortion will be a major issue for voters. Arizona, tonight, with a near-total ban, as a new poll shows a majority of Americans disapprove of the Supreme Court's decision overturning Roe v. Wade. And what that poll also reveals about 2024, whether Democrats and Republicans want President Biden and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to run again. Here's ABC's White House correspondent, MaryAlice Parks.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ARIZONA NEAR TOTAL ABORTION BAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUDGE SAYS CENTURY OLD LAW CAN TAKE EFFECT\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, early voting beginning in several states, a key issue in the midterm elections, abortion, back front and center in Arizona. Now, in a controversial decision, a state judge saying a century-old near-total abortion ban can take effect in the state, the same weekend Republicans' 15-week ban went into law. The old statute, with language dating back to 1864, long before Arizona was even a state, has Democrats up in arms.\par \par \uc2 STATE OFFICIAL (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Women and girls will die because of it.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ARIZONA NEAR TOTAL ABORTION BAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLL: AMERICANS OPPOSE SCOTUS ABORTION RULING\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Democrats hoping strong feelings on abortion move voters to the polls. According to our new ABC News/"Washington Post" poll, Americans broadly oppose the Supreme Court's ruling overturning Roe v. Wade, with Americans trusting Democrats by a 20-point margin on the issue.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And some Republicans are taking notice. In Wisconsin, the Republican candidate for Governor, Tim Michaels, flip-flopping, now saying he would support exceptions to the state's abortion ban for rape and incest. Both parties facing a crossroads, our poll finds, with a majority of Democrats wanting the party to replace President Biden at the top of the ticket 2024. Republicans facing a divide, split almost evenly on whether former President Trump should represent them.\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (REPUBLICAN CANDIDATE FOR GOVERNOR FOR ARIZONA)\par \par \uc2 The new Republican Party is the party of we, the people.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Trump endorsing the Republican candidate for Governor of Arizona, Kari Lake, who does not accept the results of the 2020 election. Fellow Republican \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  speaking at the Texas Tribute Festival in Austin this weekend, saying, bluntly, she will campaign for Democrats to make sure election deniers like Lake are defeated.\par \par \uc2 REP LIZ CHENEY (REP) (WYOMING)\par \par \uc2 If we elect people who've said that they're not going to certify results or who are going to try to steal elections, then we really are putting the republic at risk.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) MaryAlice Parks, joining us, now. And, MaryAlice, we heard \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , there. She and the other members of the January 6th committee will be back before the Americans people this week with another hearing, and there are some new details starting to emerge about that day.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. One of the committee members acknowledging today that the committee is aware of this detail that's been making headlines, that there could have been a call between the White House switchboard and a January 6th rioter.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, the committee has been really careful not to get ahead of this hearing. It could be their last one. But they are promising that they will fill in some of those details and show a coordinated plot from the former President to overturn the election results, and they are promising that this hearing will be sweeping and have new information. Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) MaryAlice, thank you. And stay with ABC News. David and the entire team will have live coverage of Wednesday's January 6th hearings, starting at 1:00 PM Eastern.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSU000020220927ei9p00004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 EARLY MID-TERM VOTING UNDERWAY IN SEVERAL STATES REPUBLICAN LEADER LAYS OUT "COMMITMENT TO AMERICA"\b0\par\par\uc2 660 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 September 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIONA'S FURY: MASSIVE HURRICANE\par \par \uc2 IAN, FLORIDA, EMERGENCY, DESANTIS, STORM\par \par \uc2 FIONA, CANADA, LANDFALL, STORM\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, RUSSIA, UKRAINE, WAR, SANCTIONS, JEAN-PIERRE, DRUEKE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, PUTIN, NUCLEAR, UKRAINE, RUSSIA, COSTS\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, IRAN, AMINI\par \par \uc2 DOW, LOW\par \par \uc2 BIRD FLU, TURKEY, EGGS\par \par \uc2 FENTANYL, PHOENIX\par \par \uc2 MIDTERMS, VOTING, MCCARTHY, RYLE\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now turning to "Your Voice, Your Vote." With just 45 days until the midterm elections, early voting is already under way in some states. This morning, we're on the road in Arizona where ABC's Ike Ejiochi has the latest and has been talking to voters. Good morning, Ike.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Erielle. You're right. Football is upon us here in Arizona, but so are the issues ahead of the midterms, issues Arizona voters say should be a main concern for any elected leader.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, a 15-week abortion ban passed by the Arizona State Legislature earlier this year will go into effect. An Arizona judge ruling that the state can enforce the ban, which means abortion clinics will eventually close and women seeking abortions will have to go out of state.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Is there a topic or an issue on the top of your mind that will get you to the polls?\par \par \uc2 VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 The abortion issue. I'd like for it to stay legal hopefully everywhere.\par \par \uc2 VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I definitely am going to vote to keep abortion access alive and well in Arizona if I can through the midterm election.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In less than 50 days out to the midterms, House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy outlining the Republican party's midterm agenda to secure the border, fight inflation, and hire more police.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MCCARTHY (HOUSE MINORITY LEADER)\par \par \uc2 What the commitment is is a plan, a plan for a new direction.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Here in Arizona, immigration is also a big concern in the state. Arizona Governor \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2  joining several other Republican governors across the country in busing migrants to major cities, including Washington, DC, sending nearly 2,000 migrants on dozens of buses to the nation's capital so far.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH RYLE (\uc2 ASU\uc2  STUDENT)\par \par \uc2 The fact of the matter is that they're coming and whether we stop the immigration or whatnot, it'll still happen just like any other occurrence.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In this election cycle, Arizona will feature several Republican candidates who still dispute the outcome of the 2020 election, including gubernatorial candidate Kari Lake and Mark Fincham running for secretary of state.\par \par \uc2 VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 It's a federal election, and I think it's a substantial claim to, like, not believe that.\par \par \uc2 VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I don't have an issue with them running on that. It's not going to make or break how I vote either.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Here in Phoenix, politics and college football is top of mind as the Arizona State Sun Devils take on the Utah Utes. And one thing both sides can agree on is the need to hydrate. Over 20 years of drought in Arizona fueled by climate change is getting voters thinking about what their elected officials can do.\par \par \uc2 VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Start by implementing a few things, like we could work on trying to cut back water usage.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, we know water is an issue here in Arizona, which is why hydrating will be key for tonight's game between \uc2 ASU\uc2  and the Utah Utes. Temperatures by kickoff is going to be about 100 degrees. And the majority of the voters I spoke to say whoever wins these races, the water crisis needs to be on the top of their minds. Janai.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Ike, thank you for that update.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221017ei9o00032\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEW STUDY ON MATERNAL DEATH & PREGNANCY CDC: 4 OF 5 PREGNANCY-RELATED DEATHS PREVENTABLE\b0\par\par\uc2 733 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 September 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 HURRICANE FIONA, POWER OUTAGE, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, HURRICANE FIONA, TRACK\par \par \uc2 IMMIGRATION, SALAZAR, INVESTIGATION, DESANTIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRIMINAL INVESTIGATION INTO MIGRANT FLIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS SHERIFF OPENS PROBE INTO MOVE BY FLORIDA GOV. DESANTIS\par \par \uc2 PRISONER SWAP, FRERICHS, TALIBAN, BLINKEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICAN FREED BY TALIBAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: U.S. NAVY VET RELEASED AFTER NEARLY THREE YEARS IN PRISONER SWAP\par \par \uc2 QUEEN ELIZABETH II, \uc2 KING CHARLES III\uc2 , FUNERAL\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, UKRAINE, ZELENSKYY, NUCLEAR THREAT\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, INFLATION, INTEREST RATES, RECESSION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALL EYES ON THE FED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILL INTEREST RATES GO UP AGAIN?\par \par \uc2 OLIVERSON, RECOVERY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 PAPINI, SENTENCING, REYES, KIDNAPPING, HOAX\par \par \uc2 "SERIAL", SYED, MOSBY, SUTER, CONVICTION, LEE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STUNNING REVERSAL IN "SERIAL" CASE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ADNAN SYED FREED FROM PRISON AFTER JUDGE VACATES MURDER CONVICTION\par \par \uc2 "DANCING WITH THE STARS", PREMIERE, BLAIR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 VICTOR OQUENDO\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE, FIONA, PUERTO RICO, CATEGORY 3\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HURRICANE FIONA GAINING STRENGTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR CATEGORY 3 STORM HEADING NORTH\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE\par \par \uc2 QUEEN ELIZABETH II, MEMORIAL, FUNERAL, MONARCH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FINAL FAREWELL TO THE QUEEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ROYAL FAMILY & THOUSANDS GATHER\par \par \uc2 PREGNANCY, MORTALITY, PREVENTIONN, SUTTON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW STUDY ON MATERNAL DEATH & PREGNANCY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC: 4 OF 5 PREGNANCY-RELATED DEATHS PREVENTABLE\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to our "GMA" cover story and a new CDC report on maternal death and pregnancy. The CDC finding more than 80 percent of pregnancy-related deaths in the US were preventable. ABC News medical contributor Dr. Darien Sutton joins us with more. Dr. Sutton, it's always great to have you. So, help us break down these numbers. These are pretty staggering and terrifying, to be quite honest. So, what is going on here?\par \par \uc2 DR. DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, good morning, Mary. Researchers looked at over 1,000 pregnancy-related deaths, and they found some pretty concerning and disturbing findings that I think are important and I wanted to share here, the first of which is that mental health conditions, such as substance uses disorder, as well as self-harm, were the leading cause of maternal mortality by 23 percent. That was followed closely by heart-related conditions, by 22 percent, and additional causes included bleeding, infection, blood clots and high blood pressure. But the most concerning number that you pointed out was that more than 84 percent were deemed as preventable, and they defined it as during that period of pregnancy, there was at least one thing that could be changed that would avert death and save a life.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DIFFERENCES AMONG WOMEN OF DIFFERENT RACES\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's really shocking, and, you know, we still are seeing racial disparity, Black women, we know Black mothers are dying at a much higher rate than white mothers. Why is, what is behind this disparity?\par \par \uc2 DR. DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, I think that there are multiple factors behind this, one of which is that it's probably connected to the general disparities that we see in the general population. In the study, we saw that Black mothers were more likely to die of heart and cardiac conditions, Hispanic mothers and white mothers more likely to die of mental conditions and Asian mothers more likely to die from bleeding. I think it's multifactorial and nuanced, but it's definitely something that needs to be addressed.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, looking ahead, going forward, what can we do when it comes to prevention? What can people, families, mothers be doing?\par \par \uc2 DR. DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I always think about how to address the systemic issues by multiple factors, one of which, as providers, we can update protocols and guidelines to address complaints quicker, systems, making them better for perinatal care and postpartum care and then for patients, reminding them to take care of their chronic conditions and keep up to date with any new issues and reminding patients about what postpartum depression looks like, including things such as hopelessness, inability to participate in things that you normally would like or ideas of self-harm. If you have any of these concerns, stressing to mothers, that you should reach out for help and remembering it's always okay to ask for help.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Always okay to ask for help. Dr. Sutton, thank you so much. George.\par \par \uc2 DR. DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Of course.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221012ei9k0003c\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CAMPAIGNS IN HIGH GEAR WITH 52 DAYS LEFT UNTIL MIDTERM POLITICIANS VISIT KEY STATES\b0\par\par\uc2 669 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 September 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEATHER ALERT: FIONA'S FURY\par \par \uc2 FIONA, PUERTO RICO, FLOODING, MUDSLIDES\par \par \uc2 MIGRANTS, SELF, D'AGOSTINO, DEL CAMPO\par \par \uc2 BORDER, WHITE HOUSE, RESOURCES\par \par \uc2 ROYALS, GUARD, KING CHARLES\par \par \uc2 QUEEN ELIZABETH, MOURNERS, BECKHAM\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, WAR, GRAVES\par \par \uc2 GRINER, WHELAN, BIDEN, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 CAMPAIGNS, MIDTERM, ELECTIONS, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the countdown to the midterm elections. With just 52 days until election day, ABC's White House correspondent MaryAlice Parks is in Ann Arbor finding out what Michigan voters are saying about the big issues that could determine how they cast their ballots. MaryAlice, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, it's game day here in Ann Arbor. Good morning, Whit. The University of Michigan Wolverines set to take on the Uconn Huskies. Of course, here on campus at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 , they cheer go blue, but we are just 52 days until the election and so, of course, the big question is whether this state will go blue.\par \par \uc2 MICHIGAN RESIDENT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Go blue.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT VOTERS IN MICHIGAN ARE SAYING ABOUT THE BIGGEST ISSUES FOR THEM\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The countdown to election day less than two months away, high stakes campaigns now in high gear.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Are you planning to vote in November?\par \par \uc2 MICHIGAN RESIDENT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHIGAN RESIDENT (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I am.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) On the ballot this November in the battleground State of Michigan, abortion, a proposal to change the state constitution to guarantee abortion access, front and center on voters' minds.\par \par \uc2 MICHIGAN RESIDENT (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I think both sides are really energized.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What issues matter most to you?\par \par \uc2 MICHIGAN RESIDENT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I think for me the biggest thing is probably abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 MICHIGAN RESIDENT (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I think restricting people from, like, things like abortion, I mean, that's just pretty dangerous, in my opinion.\par \par \uc2 MICHIGAN RESIDENT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I think I'm going to stick mostly Democrat because of the overturning of like Roe V Wade, so I'm definitely focused on trying to get women's rights.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This Republican woman telling us she plans to vote for the proposal to keep abortion access, but also for the staunchly anti-abortion rights Republican candidate in the governor's race.\par \par \uc2 MICHIGAN RESIDENT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 It's my body, I can do what I want with it. That's truly how I feel.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's two women on the ballot for governor here. Tudor Dixon, a businesswoman challenging Michigan's Democratic Governor, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , who's campaigning for a second term. Whitmer hand in hand with President Biden this week as he visited the Detroit Auto Show, announcing major funding for electric vehicle charging stations.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOE BIDEN (UNITED STATES OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 Great American road trip is going to be fully electrified.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Motor City, car companies betting big on electric vehicles. We asked voters outside the auto show about it and that support from the White House.\par \par \uc2 MICHIGAN RESIDENT (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Yes, it's a lot of excitement. Yep.\par \par \uc2 MICHIGAN RESIDENT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 We have a lot of autoworkers \u8211\'20\'13 retired autoworkers in my family, some are for, some are against.\par \par \uc2 MICHIGAN RESIDENT (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I just don't think the infrastructure is there yet.\par \par \uc2 MICHIGAN RESIDENT (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I think it's good that they're taking action to have a plan for the environment.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, there are also several key House Congressional races in this state that we'll be watching very closely. And other issues that voters told me were top of mind, climate change, protecting the environment, and, of course, the economy, with inflation and food prices so high. Janai.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So interesting to see what politics they care about while they're out there caring about sports. MaryAlice, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221012ei9h00009\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ABORTION BAN BATTLE PROPOSED NATIONAL BAN DIVIDES REPUBLICANS AHEAD OF MIDTERMS\b0\par\par\uc2 329 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 September 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BIDEN, RAILWAY STRIKE, DEAL\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, ZELENSKYY, GUTERRES, WAR\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, CHINA, XI, PUTIN\par \par \uc2 QUEEN ELIZABETH II, TRIBUTES\par \par \uc2 QUEEN ELIZABETH II, TRIBUTES\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, BAN, PENCE, GRAHAM\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're going to get the latest now on the battle over abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  has voiced support for a national abortion ban proposed by Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , which is creating new controversy as candidates compete in the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION BAN BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROPOSED NATIONAL BAN DIVIDES REPUBLICANS AHEAD OF MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Congressional correspondent Rachel Scott is tracking it all. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) George, good morning. And I can tell you that abortion is the last thing that many Republicans want to be talking about heading into these very critical midterm elections. But Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  put this issue front and center, introducing a bill that would impose a nationwide ban on abortion at 15 weeks. One Republican in his corner, former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . He says he supports the ban, calling the issue profoundly more important than any short-term politics. Other Republicans, though, say that decision needs to be left up to the states. And with just under two months to go until the midterms, they say the focus needs to be on the economy, inflation, securing the border.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Republicans also saw what happened in the conservative state of Kansas, overwhelmingly voting to protect access to abortion. Since then, we have seen many Republican candidates across the country backtrack on their stances on this issue. Some even going as far as scrubbing their campaign websites to remove their positions. Democrats clearly see an opening here and they are hoping it galvanizes their base, Cecilia.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, they certainly do. Okay, Rachel, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221011ei9f00007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GRAHAM ABORTION BAN BILL PROPOSED 15 WEEK BAN DIVIDES GOP AHEAD OF MIDTERMS\b0\par\par\uc2 626 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 QUEEN ELIZABETH II, TRIBUTE, PROCESSION\par \par \uc2 RAIL STRIKE, WATTERS, SWONK, PELOSI, TINKHAM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR RAIL STRIKE THREAT\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, ZELENSKYY, WAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ZELENSKYY VISITS LIBERATED REGION\par \par \uc2 INVESTIGATION, \uc2 NFL\uc2 , FAVRE, WELFARE MONEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FAVRE TEXT MESSAGES REVEALED\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM, ABORTION BAN, MASTERS, BOLDUC, ROMNEY, PELOSI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GRAHAM ABORTION BAN BILL\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) With less than two months until the midterms in this country, the reaction tonight to Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  proposing a nationwide ban on abortion at 15 weeks saying, if Republicans take control of Congress, it will get a vote. Tonight, many prominent Republicans now trying to distance themselves from this idea after Roe was overturned and with signals that this issue could be decisive come November. Rachel Scott, on the Hill, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROPOSED 15 WEEK BAN DIVIDES GOP AHEAD OF MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, Republican around the country forced to grapple with abortion, an issue they've been trying to avoid ever since the Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade. Now it's front and center, after a surprise move by one of their own, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  introducing a bill to ban abortion nationwide at 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (REP) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 If we take back the House and the Senate, I can assure you we'll have a vote on our bill.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's exactly what Republicans don't want to talk about. They see abortion as an issue that will help Democrats in November. In Arizona today, Senate candidate \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  trying to change the subject.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE MASTERS (SENATORIAL CANDIDATE ARIZONA)\par \par \uc2 I'm here to talk about illegal immigration, today.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 So, you don't want to talk about that?\par \par \uc2 BLAKE MASTERS (SENATORIAL CANDIDATE ARIZONA)\par \par \uc2 I mean, I said yesterday, yeah, I support it. I hope it passes. It's not going to pass.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In New Hampshire, just hours after becoming the Republican Senate nominee, Don Bolduc faced with questions about Graham's abortion bill.\par \par \uc2 DON BOLDUC (REPUBLICAN SENATE NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, here's the deal with that. I believe the federal government should stay out of it.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Republicans saw what recently happened in Kansas. The conservative state voting overwhelmingly to protect abortion rights. It came after a surge in women registering to vote after the Supreme Court overturned Roe, according to Target Smart Insights, a Democratic data firm.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What about messaging on this, Senator Graham? Do you think it's effective messaging?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (REP) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 You need to stand up for what you believe, right? That's a good thing.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But many of his colleagues say Republicans should be talking about something else.\par \par \uc2 POLITICIAN (MALE)\par \par \uc2 So, I think the focus should be on inflation.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITT ROMNEY (REP) (UTAH)\par \par \uc2 Inflation is the big issue.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Democrats clearly see an opportunity.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (DEM-CA) (SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE)\par \par \uc2 What you're seeing there is a conflict within the Republican Party. They are digging a hole, and they just keep digging it.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, Republicans clearly divided on this, but former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  says he supports that nationwide ban, insisting the issue is profoundly more important than any short-term politics, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Rachel Scott, tonight. Thank you, Rachel. When we come back, here, here in New York, the three children who police say were drowned on Coney Island. We have news on the mother, tonight. And breaking news involving R. Kelly, at this hour.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220915ei9e00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\b0\par\par\uc2 3,609 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 September 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 MIDTERMS, BIDEN, TRUMP, MAGA, MCCARTHY, MAR-A-LAGO\par \par \uc2 BOTTOMS, BIDEN, SPEECH, MAGA, QUINNIPIAC, HOGAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KICKOFF TO MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE-ON-ONE WITH WH SENIOR ADVISER KEISHA LANCE BOTTOMS\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL, BIDEN, SPEECH, TRUMP, MAR-A-LAGO, UK, IRAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KICKOFF TO MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE-ON-ONE WITH REP MICHAEL MCCAUL\par \par \uc2 SILVER, 538, PA, RACE, GOVERNOR, SENATE, CANDIDATE\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: TUESDAY\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, SPEECH, MIDTERMS, MAGA, PELTOLA, ROE, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, let's bring in the Roundtable, ABC News political director Rick Klein, "USA Today" Washington bureau chief Susan Page, "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " national political reporter, host of "The Run Up" podcast, Astead Herndon, and Politico national political correspondent, Meridith McGraw. Welcome to all of you. And Rick, what a week, what a week. That speech the, the President, who had themes of unity and compromise, why the big change?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN MOVES FROM CALLS FOR UNITY TO ATTACKS ON MAGA REPUBLICANS\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is a speech that the President felt strongly about delivering. It's not necessarily a speech that Democrats, frankly those running in close races right now, were keen of hearing him say. I have been struck by the split that this has, this has brought about. It's not just Republicans who are criticizing this. You've got a lot of Democrats, well, some are saying, "Look, about time. Why, why haven't you said this earlier, President Biden?" But you've got a lot that are running in some states like Pennsylvania that are basically silent on this, because they're running their own races.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the idea of, of coming against unity, in some sense, it feels to them almost like Trump, and that's, that's not the way you win, almost by definition. If you're Josh Shapiro, if you're \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , you're gonna need Trump voters to vote for you this fall, and they recognize that. They're running races that are a little bit different than that. And this is message that, look, this is a, it's a bold play and it's something that a lot of people in the base thought was overdue. But I, whether this is actually the message that prevails, I think, is very much an open question.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Susan, did he reach the soul of the nation with that speech, in your opinion?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN: TRUMP AND MAGA THREATEN 'FOUNDATIONS OF OUR REPUBLIC"\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE ("\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 " WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Well, I, I guess, I guess I don't know if it turns out to be a political benefit or not. But I do think that if the President thinks this is true, maybe he has to say it out loud, even if there is some blowback to it. I'd also note that he didn't do this on Labor Day and in the final weeks of the, of the sprint to the election. He did it at the end of the week leading up to Labor Day. And I don't, I'm, I guess I do not think this is a theme that the White House is gonna be striking from now to the next, the next seven weeks.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and Astead, it, it, it, it also seems clear that the White House believes, just from what \uc2 Keisha Lance Bottoms\uc2  was saying, is, well, they're kind of beyond trying to unify any of the MAGA Republicans into the fold.\par \par \uc2 ASTEAD HERNDON ("NEW YORK TIMES" NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, they're using "soul of the nation" as a throughline through their campaign, but it's different, the level of the, the diversion of unity they're pitching right now. The unity of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's campaign was, if you remove \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the country will come back together. The unity of the inauguration was, "Oh, this, this January 6th thing has happened, well, now people will definitely come to this side." I think what we're hearing, as of last week, is, is trying to unite people against that MAGA wing.\par \par \uc2 ASTEAD HERNDON ("NEW YORK TIMES" NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER)\par \par \uc2 So, even if the White House is continuing that theme of the soul of the nation, I think they should acknowledge that this version of unity and coming together is certainly a different tone and tenor than the one he started his campaign with.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and Meridith, Rick touched on this, obviously, but are both parties - content to let the midterms be just Trump v. Biden?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MIDTERMS SHAPING UP TO BE TRUMP V BIDEN REMATCH\par \par \uc2 MERIDITH MCGRAW (POLITICO NATIONAL POLITICAL ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 Well, we know the Biden White House wanted the President to make this speech for months, and he really felt a sense of urgency just with the past few weeks, with threats to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and with sort of this rallying around \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that we've seen. But the Biden administration really thinks that this is going to be a winning message for them, pitting the, the midterms against Biden and Trump. But, really, the question is, whether Biden's talk of a threat to a democracy being a priority for voters, is that going to outweigh voter concerns about inflation and about economy that really are top of mind, right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE ("\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 " WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Well, the Biden, Biden people want it to be about Trump.\par \par \uc2 MERIDITH MCGRAW (POLITICO NATIONAL POLITICAL ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 Right.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE ("\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 " WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 And the Trump people want it to be, I mean, the Republicans want it to be about Biden. Democrats want it to be about Trump.\par \par \uc2 MERIDITH MCGRAW (POLITICO NATIONAL POLITICAL ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 About Biden.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE ("\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 " WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 And so, which, which prevails? I mean, at the, at the minimum, the White House succeeds in making this less of a referendum on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , which is a tradition of these first-term midterms. Every time Trump's name is out there, look at how uncomfortable it made Congressman McCaul.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE ("\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 " WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 You know, he criticized Biden.\par \par \uc2 MERIDITH MCGRAW (POLITICO NATIONAL POLITICAL ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE ("\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 " WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 He did not defend Trump in the interview you just did with him.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And he is not an election denier.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE ("\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 " WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 And he's not an election denier, but he clearly did not want to talk about Trump. So, you see the two parties want this to be a referendum, but on the other guy's president.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and the Republicans really just can't talk about that as much, in any way. Astead, we talked about the Quinnipiac poll that shows two, and this is astonishing...\par \par \uc2 ASTEAD HERNDON ("NEW YORK TIMES" NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...two-thirds of the country believe democracy is in danger, but they have very different views of that. Of course, the Republicans think Biden, and the Democrats think Trump. So, and I asked her about that, \uc2 Keisha Lance Bottoms\uc2 , how do you lead?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: QUINNIPIAC POLL: TWO-THIRDS SAY DEMOCRACY UNDER THREAT\par \par \uc2 ASTEAD HERNDON ("NEW YORK TIMES" NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How do you lead a country? And, and what is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  done to try to bridge that divide?\par \par \uc2 ASTEAD HERNDON ("NEW YORK TIMES" NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER)\par \par \uc2 But it's interesting, and the answer, she talks about the bipartisan legislation, the gun legislation, they were able to pass infrastructure, reaching across the aisle, but I think there has to a be a recognition that those things were really secondary to the, to the promise that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was giving to voters. His pitch was about unity, about bringing folks, about bringing folks together. And so, even if there have been legislative accomplishments that the White House is trying to tout that will see midterm candidates tout down ballot, I do think you're seeing some voters kind of question the White House, because that foremost promise has not been fulfilled.\par \par \uc2 ASTEAD HERNDON ("NEW YORK TIMES" NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER)\par \par \uc2 This country is not quote/unquote, "unified." But I do think that Biden has had a tough task, here, because it goes beyond the bully pulpit. It's a really political system itself that is, I think, struggling with that partisanship, with that polarization.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Meredith, you've, you've been out in the country. You, you talk to voters. I know you do. We all talk to voters. The MAGA Republicans aren't changing their minds about anything. So, this the right approach?\par \par \uc2 MERIDITH MCGRAW (POLITICO NATIONAL POLITICAL ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 They've, the MAGA Republicans have certainly dug in their heels, and I think even more so in the past few weeks, but I don't think President Biden's address was focused at them. He made great pains to say he wasn't addressing all Republicans. But look, I think he's really trying to win over independents, Republicans who feel alienated by their own party and Democrats he thinks he can really drive out to vote in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Rick, "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reporting this weekend that many Democrats are now quietly getting used to the idea that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  might be the nominee. What does that mean? What about those who were hoping they might have a chance?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUST OVER TWO MONTHS TO 2022 MIDTERM ELECTION\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I, a couple things are gonna happen in the next few months that I think will shape 2024 on both sides of, of the, of the aisle. First, you've got to have the midterm elections. And whether this rebound that we've seen with Democrats, recently, if that actually means that they can hold on to the Senate, maybe add a seat or two, and maybe even hold the House, although I think that's a, a stretch, that changes, I think, how you look at \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as a political actor. Another big factor in this is Trump. The biggest argument that any Democrats might have about why Biden should run again, he's the one guy that beat Trump last time.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMS MAKE GAINS AS LABOR DAY MARKS START OF MIDTERM SPRINT\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) If Trump looks like more a threat, less of a threat, that changes it. I don't think we were gonna see 2024 positioning on the Democratic side until after the midterms, anyway. I think this recent upswing in polls kind of delays it a little bit more. It gives Biden maybe a little bit more place to, to keep his own role in the party, this speech this past week, and whatever he does on the campaign trail over the next couple months. If that's successful, I think that changes where the future of party might, might head.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMS PUSH ELECTION-DENYING CANDIDATES IN GOP PRIMARIES\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and Susan, I got to go back to, to another question that I asked \uc2 Keisha Lance Bottoms\uc2 , and that is, the funding of extreme candidates by the Democrats. How much does that hurt them, and should Biden speak out and say, wait a minute? I mean - again, the, the Democrats are hoping if they fund these extreme candidates, and they'll run against those extreme candidates and that they will win, but the look is not good.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE ("\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 " WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 She, the, what about the look she gave you? She did not answer...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She did not want to answer that question.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE ("\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 " WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 ...the question. She does not defend this practice of trying to boost the most extreme candidate on the other side in the hopes of an easier general election, but the risk is, sometimes the other candidate might get actually elected, and you've helped elect, put in an office an election denier. It also cedes the high ground. The Democrats, where, in the, the cases where the Democrats have done that, they have ceded the political high ground. And I think that we, I think, I would like to hear President Biden answer a direct question about this.\par \par \uc2 ASTEAD HERNDON ("NEW YORK TIMES" NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER)\par \par \uc2 But, but I also think that we, if there's anything we've learned from the last couple of years is that some of these democracy questions go beyond just the ballot box, too. In promoting some of these candidates and their messages, that also helps to take root, even if it's just among that MAGA Republican base. Certainly, January 6th was an example of people refusing to accept even what we saw from those lawful and legal elections. So, there's another risk, too, that the threats to democracy aren't just going to be at the ballot box, but that, in the Democrats promoting some of these candidates, they actually further entrench something that strikes at democracy's core.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRAT PELTOLA DEFEATS PALIN IN ALASKA SPECIAL ELECTION\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Meridith, on the midterms, Democrat Mary Peltola won a special House election in Alaska this week, defeating Trump-backed \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2 . What does this say about the Democrats chances? And, and this was a little bit of an unusual race, as well.\par \par \uc2 MERIDITH MCGRAW (POLITICO NATIONAL POLITICAL ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, it was an unusual race. They used ranked-choice ballots, here. But the conventional wisdom really has been that we're going to see this red wave, come November, but really, what we're seeing is, is it could be more of a ripple, now. And a big part of that really is the Roe decision and the motivation that Democrats have had to, to come out and vote. And in the case of Alaska, I think \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2 's own popularity in the state, which isn't so great, these days, played a big role. But Democrats are really seeing some wind at their backs in the wake of Roe and also I think with some of these legislatives wins that they've been able to get with Biden.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Peltola didn't really didn't go after Palin. They, they became friends when they were both pregnant.\par \par \uc2 MERIDITH MCGRAW (POLITICO NATIONAL POLITICAL ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Palin didn't go after her, specifically.\par \par \uc2 MERIDITH MCGRAW (POLITICO NATIONAL POLITICAL ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 No. And, you know, this was a special election. Democrats have fared particularly well in some of these special elections, especially after Roe. But we are gonna see \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2  go up against her again in the general election. This was just to, to take that seat until January.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She does have another shot, but the ceiling that it might represent for MAGA candidates is real. The fact that Palin was the first choice of so many, but not the second or third choice, that's what allowed the Democrats to, to sneak into this seat.\par \par \uc2 MERIDITH MCGRAW (POLITICO NATIONAL POLITICAL ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 Exactly.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and Susan, it does seem clear the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe has really dramatically altered the political dynamics, here. How much of an issue will that be in the midterms, do you think?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMS HOPEFUL ROE REVERSAL WILL GALVANIZE VOTERS\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE ("\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 " WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 I think it is the number-one reason we have seen these midterms turn on a dime in the space of a month or two. This is not the midterm election that either Democrats or Republicans thought we were gonna see, and there are a couple of reasons for that, but the number one reason is the mobilization of people who support access to abortion, and especially women, to register to vote, to turn out to vote in these special elections, and to vote for Democrats and against Republicans who have had, basically had a free ride on, free ride on abortion for so long, where they could stand up against access to abortion, but the Roe v. Wade decision provided a kind of a safety net. Abortion rights supporters did not see that right as threatened. Now they've seen that overturned, and that has changed, entirely.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and Rick, a bit about the Senate race, more about the Senate race, you now have the GOP campaign chief \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  lashing out at \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , seemingly already pointing fingers over who may or may not win.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILL REPUBLICANS WIN BACK CONGRESS IN NOVEMBER?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, I, I talked to a Republican strategist that's close to these races this past week. He said, look, no one's gonna remember this fight if the Republicans win the Senate. If they don't, they will, because this question of candidate quality, which was what got this started. McConnell has been critical of quote/unquote "candidate quality." And you look at people like Dr. Oz, you look at \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , you, you look at, you, you look at JD Vance in Ohio, and you start to wonder, wait a second, are these the best people out there? And \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  took kind of a hands-off approach, driven in part by Trump, to let these primaries play out.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And now Republicans are stuck with some candidates that they're gonna have to even, either try to salvage or maybe lose opportunities around. So, I think it, it matters greatly for the future of Republican leadership in the Senate, but I think this question about Trump's influence is still being felt. He was very successful in the primaries. That's not the same as getting someone that can actually win a general election.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Which leads me right back to the investigation of Mar-a-Lago. Astead, have the revelations this week, especially those photos, changed not just politics, but legally. I know you're not a lawyer, but...\par \par \uc2 ASTEAD HERNDON ("NEW YORK TIMES" NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...is it more likely?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP DOCUMENTS INVESTIGATION KEEPS GOP ON DEFENSE\par \par \uc2 ASTEAD HERNDON ("NEW YORK TIMES" NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER)\par \par \uc2 I'll, I'll speak to the politics. I mean, I definitely can, I think, I think we definitely have seen that since Trump and his lawyers have pushed for more information to come out, that it's actually reversed onto them. We are getting more and more details that make clear the level of obstruction and, and not working with the Department of Justice that we have seen from the Trump campaign. And they have also not answered the core question of why, right? The question that you ask to the congressman about why were there such classified documents, highly classified documents at Mar-a-Lago?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FORMER AG BARR: CLASSIFIED DOCS AT MAR-A-LAGO 'UNPRECEDENTED'\par \par \uc2 ASTEAD HERNDON ("NEW YORK TIMES" NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER)\par \par \uc2 That is something now that Bill Barr, Trump's own attorney general, to your point, is speaking out against on Fox News. The facts aren't in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's direction. What they are going to try to do is put the politics in their direction, to say that this is a culmination of long-term Democratic efforts to not see him as legitimate, wrap it up in Ukraine, wrap it up in the Steele dossier, wrap it up in saying that it was the Democrats who committed the first sin in Trump's administration.\par \par \uc2 ASTEAD HERNDON ("NEW YORK TIMES" NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER)\par \par \uc2 And that, the problem for Democrats, though, is Republican House can now - a Republican House can, can come to a majority driven by that MAGA wing and still not be a majority of the electorate, at large. Democrats have a problem where they have to, they have to overcome a high structural barrier to really maintain the House, otherwise that MAGA wing will be coming for them in the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Meredith, we have about 20 seconds. How do you view how this investigation will affect things?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP ATTACKS 'VICIOUS' \uc2 FBI\uc2  IN FIRST RALLY SINCE MAR-A-LAGO SEARCH\par \par \uc2 MERIDITH MCGRAW (POLITICO NATIONAL POLITICAL ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 You know the, the former president has really tried to use this all to his political advantage, but I would look to how so many Republicans have answered questions about this. It really does seem like there's a mood shift here and a real sense that the former president could be in much more trouble than he's letting on.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, thanks. Great to see all of you. Have a wonderful Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Up next, tens of thousands of Mississippi's capital city are still without access to safe drinking water. We'll talk with Jackson's mayor about the dire situation and urgent efforts to restore the water supply. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220907ei9400006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 OHIO VOTERS WEIGH IN ON ISSUES THAT MATTER ABORTION, ECONOMY, POLICE BRUTALITY ON MINDS\b0\par\par\uc2 615 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 September 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEATHER ALERT: WILDFIRE EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRE, CALIFORNIA, EVACUATION, LEV, SONG\par \par \uc2 HEAT WAVE, TEMPERATURES, WEST\par \par \uc2 HOLIDAY, TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 FBI\uc2 , MAR-A-LAGO, CLASSIFIED, GRACK, BARR\par \par \uc2 LAWYERS, CIPOLLONE\par \par \uc2 KIDNAPPED, JOGGER, GARRETT, ROBINSON, HARTMAN\par \par \uc2 JOBS, EMPLOYERS, HIRING, REPORT\par \par \uc2 VOTERS, OHIO, MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the economy certainly on the minds of many Americans. This, as the midterms are fast approaching. And Ohio voters are weighing their choices in one of the most closely watched Senate races in the country. So, what issues matter most to them? ABC's Mona Kosar Abdi is in Columbus to find out.\par \par \uc2 MONA KOSAR ABDI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) On and off the field, Ohio State football has an unmitigated grip on the Buckeye State. Every fall here in Columbus, fans paint the town scarlet and gray in a grand display of commitment to the team. And this weekend is no different as the Buckeyes set their sights on defeating Notre Dame. But this solidarity displayed in the state's capital a far cry, Ohioans I spoke to say, from the divisiveness shown when it comes to politics.\par \par \uc2 OHIO RESIDENT (MALE)\par \par \uc2 100 percent it's divided.\par \par \uc2 MONA KOSAR ABDI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A key issue top of mind for many here, the economy.\par \par \uc2 OHIO RESIDENT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 When I came here originally this was an affordable to live in and a lot of the neighborhoods here in Columbus that were livable and affordable are no longer affordable.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MIDTERM ELECTIONS\par \par \uc2 MONA KOSAR ABDI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Ahead of the midterm elections, another polarizing issue voters say is driving them to the polls is focused around women's reproductive rights, an issue also taking center stage in the state's high-stakes \uc2 US Senate\uc2  race between Democratic Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and Trump-backed author JD Vance.\par \par \uc2 OHIO RESIDENT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 A lot of women don't even know they're pregnant until after six weeks. I think that is beyond cruel.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OHIO ABORTION LAW\par \par \uc2 MONA KOSAR ABDI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Following the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe V Wade, a restrictive abortion law went into effect in Ohio, essentially banning abortions at around six weeks with no exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOBBS RULING\par \par \uc2 MONA KOSAR ABDI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Since the Dobbs ruling, Ohio has seen a female voter registration increase of more than six percent according to "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ." And on the heels of a 20-year-old unarmed black man shot by a Columbus police officer while in bed, his family calling on protests this weekend to remain peaceful. One mom we spoke with says that officer-involved shooting incident makes her concerned for her own son's safety.\par \par \uc2 OHIO RESIDENT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I'm afraid every time my son walks out the door. At any age for our black children, we have to teach them there is a different set of rules governing them versus their counterparts.\par \par \uc2 MONA KOSAR ABDI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And a number of students I spoke with also mentioned President Biden's student loan forgiveness plan. One OSU senior I spoke with said it doesn't nearly go far enough while one osu parent I spoke with said that it's not fair to those who have paid back their student loans. Back out here live you can tell the party has already started. Despite the political divisions being clear, today it is all about Ohio State football, guys.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And no matter how you feel about the Buckeyes, you know they like to have fun on football Saturdays. Mona, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221003ei9300009\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\b0\par\par\uc2 4,398 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , MAR-A-LAGO, SEARCH, DOJ, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , SCHIFF\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , MAR-A-LAGO, REDACTED, DOCS, OBSTRUCTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP CRIMINAL INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOJ UNSEALS REDACTED MAR-A-LAGO AFFIDAVIT\par \par \uc2 BLUNT, TRUMP, MAR-A-LAGO, BIDEN, LOAN, INFLATION\par \par \uc2 SANDERS, BIDEN, LOAN, PLAN, MIDTERMS, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROGRESSIVE PUSH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE-ON-ONE WITH SEN BERNIE SANDERS\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM, MONITOR, ABORTION, PELOSI, BIDEN, SENATE\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: WEDNESDAY\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM, BIDEN, DEMS, LOAN, FORGIVENESS, TRUMP, DOCS\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: THURSDAY\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 MAGA Republicans don't just threaten our personal rights and economic security. They're a threat to our very democracy. They refuse to accept the will of the people. They embrace, embrace political violence. They don't believe in democracy. We must be stronger, more determined, and more committed to saving America than the MAGA Republicans are destroying America.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Biden kicking off his midterm campaign and went on to call the Republican Party semi-fascist. Let's talk about this on our Roundtable, joined by \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , our chief White House correspondent, Cecilia Vega, our chief Washington correspondent \uc2 Jon Karl\uc2 , and former Democratic senator from North Dakota, Heidi Heitkamp. And, Chris, let me begin with you. We saw the President's not shying away from the midterms, even though a lot of Democrats don't wanna be campaigning with him this fall, and he's determined to make this election a choice, not a referendum.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LESS THAN 80 DAYS UNTIL 2022 MIDTERM ELECTIONS\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sure. If, if I were him, I wouldn't want it to be a referendum, either, you know, with inflation where it is, with people feeling the way they are about crime in streets across the country, with parents really upset about what's going on across the board educationally in the country and some of the positions Democrats have taken. If I were \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , I would wanna run against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  again. What he knows is he beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  two years ago, and if he turns this into an election about him and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , he's got a much better chance of doing less poorly.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But there is no scenario under which \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  does well in this election. He's gonna lose the House of Representatives. He's gonna lose some governorships, and the Senate is up for grabs. And so, if you're for him, this is the best argument you could make, given all the circumstances, I don't blame him for making it. And the key is, will Republicans stay focused, or will they get distracted? If they stay focused, they'll do well. If they get distracted, less well.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Clearly, Cecilia, the White House want to capitalize on recent Democratic victories.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN RETURNS TO CAMPAIGN TRAIL IN MARYLAND\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and actually, you're really seeing that. You saw it there with the President, this week. Look, there, it really wasn't that long ago, and I know you know this, George, from your, Jon, from your reporting, too. You were talking to Democrats a few weeks ago, and they would throw their hands up openly and say that they'd lost everything and walk away from the midterms, basically. But there has been a real shift in the conversations that you're having with folks. Even inside the White House, there is this sense of optimism that, perhaps they're not as confident as you, but perhaps that they will actually keep some seats in the House.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMS OVERPERFORM EXPECTATIONS IN EARLY HOUSE RACES\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They know the victory is still a really hard one. They've got some very competitive states at play. But it is this, this legislative wins that they've had on health care and climate, and it's also Roe. It's abortion. They are saying that they see the numbers in terms of the polling. They are seeing it in terms of turnout in states like Kansas, where you're seeing more women registered in the wake of the Supreme Court ruling, and they're really believing in their messaging that this is gonna be an issue that sticks come the fall.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Heidi, is abortion enough to make the difference?\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think it's a huge factor, but also what we're not talking about is the trend lines in the economy. You saw an uptick in the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 's consumer sentiment. You're seeing gas prices with a three instead of a five in front of them. You're seeing, still, a very, very robust employment. Even if you're laid off, you can find a new job. And so, I think there's a sense that, that, that the economy will not be as big of a, of a voting issue as what we would've predicted, probably, three months ago. So, what steps in?\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What steps in is all the social issues, like - like the abortion issue. I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the gift that keeps on giving, right? He reminds people that that time was chaotic. We need stability, if we're gonna manage the economy. We need stability if government's gonna function, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not helping the Republican cause in any way, shape or form.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Which explains the President's strategy.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, absolutely, and, look, there, there is economic anxiety out there. Gas prices are down. Inflation seems to be heading down. But, look, if, if you're going out and trying to buy a house, you're, you're paying almost double what, what you are in terms of your mortgage rates. There is economic anxiety. True, the unemployment rate is down. But there is no question, as Cecilia says, they feel much better going into the midterms. They believe they will hang on to the Senate. They could even pick up a Senate seat or two.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They're going to lose some governorships, as well, in a lot of these races, because of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It's because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has gone in and supported, successfully supported, in Republican primaries, candidates who are not strong general election candidates.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Cecilia, I wonder if, if these recent successes are one of the factors that pushed President Biden over the line on this student loan debt forgiveness program. It does appear that it was something he'd been struggling with for months.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN ANNOUNCES UP TO $20K STUDENT LOAN FORGIVENESS\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No. I mean, look, he really did not want to go there, and behind the scenes, there was a two-year lobbying effort on the part of Schumer, on the part of Warren. Look, just even right before this announcement officially came out, the \uc2 NAACP\uc2  like, really came at them and publicly shamed them. So, he was really pushed to do this. He, he had the very same concerns that Republicans are raising about fairness and perception. And he said, made it clear behind the scenes he did not want these, this - debt forgiveness to go to people like his own family\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But you're right, he was pushed there because the White House Democrats very much believe that this could, could be a galvanizing issue when it comes to young voters come the fall. They really believe that this is a winning one.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and it does appear, Chris, at least in the first week, that it probably is looking a little bit more like a win than a loss for the President. Actually, giving away money in an election year can't hurt.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 43 MILLION AMERICANS WILL QUALIFY FOR LOAN FORGIVENESS\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, it can hurt, and it's a lose/lose, George, and this is why. First off, it's a lose, because ultimately, this is gonna be found to have been illegal. He needs to get this money appropriated by Congress. He didn't. And then one of the reasons he was hesitating, and Cecilia knows this, is because the legal advice he was getting was you can't do it. Nancy Pelosi, a year ago, said, you can't do it. We have to do it. So, he knows he's done something that's illegal and over the top, but the worst part of this is the American people are not stupid. You're right, giving away money is always a pretty good thing in politics, but here's what they understand, it does nothing to control college costs.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The reason people have higher loans is because college is more expensive, and this does not make college less expensive. It makes it more expensive, when you're giving away things. Here's what he should do, he wants to get on the side of working people in this country, he should say to all these colleges and universities, if your tuition goes up more than the rate of inflation, then you don't participate in any federal program, no student loans, no Pell grants and no research grants. Keep costs at the rate of inflation, or you're out of the federal pie.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That, that is an argument, Heidi, that a lot of Democrats have been making.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMS DIVIDED OVER BIDEN'S STUDENT LOAN FORGIVENESS PLAN\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I, I mean, I, this is not a program that I would've supported. I think there's a way to give student loan relief. The one, one of the challenges you have is people have been out there 10 years, and they haven't paid any principal, because the interest is too high. He could've done something on interest. Let's just remind everybody, there isn't going to be a check. There's going to be a write-down of student loan interest, and so the question is, is that an appropriation? Isn't it an appropriation? Is this right? I think that, that we can argue the politics, or we can argue the policy, but the politics of this are much more complicated.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, what's a Republican, what's Ron Johnson going to say when he's in Madison campaigning to students or people with student debt, I'm going to take that away from you now, something the President promised? And the President promised this during the campaign. I think that this is something that he believes he needed to do to fulfill a campaign promise. We've seen the numbers that you ran this morning. Overwhelmingly, this supports minority students and, again, this becomes that kind of dividing point in America.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's actually beyond, it's actually beyond what he promised in the campaign.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He said $10,000 in, in forgiveness, now we're at $20,000 in forgiveness for Pell grant recipients. Look, one factor here is costs. The costs are massive, here, and the White House has been squirrelly about how much it would cost. For the first several days after the rollout, they wouldn't say how much. They said, we can't really project how much it will cost, but they spoke with specificity about who would benefit and how much they would benefit by.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, that's one thing I guess I just...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And now we're seeing, George, they're, they're saying $240 billion over ten years. Estimates, estimates are now...\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Which is much lower.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...you know, by, by, by several groups, over $500 billion over 10 years.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And but, George, a minority sees - "The Wall Street Journal" printed this week, 18 percent of Hispanic students who will qualify for this, 18 percent of Hispanic students. This is not gonna be some boon to them. And in the end, what, what people are gonna see here is what Barack Obama's chief economist said this week, which is, it is wholly irresponsible to throw this in there while we're trying to also battle inflation.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I, I, I guess, I, I guess, though, that's where I guess I disagree with, with Heidi. The politics on this actually seem quite easy to me, and maybe I'm completely, completely wrong, but the policy is complicated. It is going to cost a lot. It likely could get overturned in the court.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It very well could, but the White House keeps sticking to this one figure, 20 million Americans will see zero debt by the time they are done with this program. This is overwhelmingly, they say, going to end up helping the, the majority of people who have student loan debt. That is their, their, their cause, going into the midterms with this. They believe it's a winning issue. And you're right, they really struggled with these numbers. They also really can't say whether this is going to help cut costs down long, long term with college.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It won't.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and will they, will colleges then, therefore, hike tuition prices? There's no guarantee that won't happen.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And I think it's also important to understand the income caps, because people are saying, "Oh, it's gonna be these doctors and lawyers and pharmacists who get this." No. Any doctor or lawyer who doesn't exceed the cap right now is really not working. And so, I think it was measured to try and address lower-income folks who felt like they couldn't get ahead. And I think, you know, like I said, I wouldn't have done it this way, but dang, it's a good - it's fulfilling a campaign promise, and it's speaking to young people who may not be motivated. If the Republicans get out there and say, you've been promised this, we're gonna take it away, are they gonna be motivated to show up in the midterms?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) George?\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And I think that's the question.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) People in this business, in the business I've been in, have been trying to figure out how to motivate voters from 18 to 34 forever, and they've...\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, maybe this'll do it, Chris.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, maybe, maybe this'll do it. I can hear that. Every consultant who's trying to sell you an idea tries to tell you this in your politics, and guess what? They don't vote.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'll, well, I'll tell you what does do it though and I'll bring this to Jon, and it'll help us switch subjects, as well. In 2016, in 2018, in 2020 \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did motivate 18 to 34-year-olds on the other side. We - we saw the affidavit, this week. I know you've, you've been in touch with the Trump world. They are worried about this.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOJ UNSEALS REDACTED MAR-A-LAGO AFFIDAVIT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW MUCH LEGAL JEOPARDY DOES TRUMP FACE?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, look, publicly, what they're saying is this is rallying Republicans to Trump's defense. This makes it more likely that he will run for president, more likely that he will win the Republican nomination, campaigning against this political action by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the DOJ. Privately, they are really concerned, and one of the big concerns here is that Trump has nobody defending him. If you look at his legal team, it is comically inept and inexperienced. All of the big names who defended him through the first two impeachments, through the, the Mueller investigation, they are gone. There is real concern that he needs to bring in a heavy-hitting criminal defense attorney.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Can he get one?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, he's been asking. I mean, I know of several that have been approached who have said no. I even know of one prominent criminal defense attorney who was approached who didn't even return the phone call. So, he, and, and this raises another question, George, is the idea of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  running for president again and being the front-runner for the Republican nominations, will, will Republicans be comfortable supporting a candidate who cannot even hire a, a criminal defense attorney? I mean...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, that is, that is a big question. But, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , you saw me talk to Roy Blunt, earlier. He couldn't even say or he struggled to say that taking all these - government documents to Mar-a-Lago is wrong.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AFFIDAVIT REVEALS 184 CLASSIFIED DOCUMENTS RETURNED IN JANUARY\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, it, it, it shouldn't be a hard thing to say. Yeah, so I'll say it, George, so you can see that a Republican can. That's wrong, for you to take Top Secret, classified documents back to your house...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Even if you put a padlock, a padlock...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It, no matter what kind, when I was US Attorney it was wrong for me to do, when I was governor it was wrong for me to do, and when you're president it's wrong to do. Here's the thing about the lawyer stuff, look, lawyers take client...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I take it you haven't gotten the call.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I have not. Lawyers take clients for two reasons, either because they think it's a good case, or they think they're gonna get paid. With \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , it's neither, okay?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The case is not good, and he does, he has a reputation for not paying his lawyers. He's developed that well-earned reputation over the course of time. And so, he, the reason that no one's rallying is 'cause they, the lawyers don't want to put themselves in that spot with a client who is impulsive...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...and who is obviously in trouble, but here's the big...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Chris, I was gonna say, put on your prosecutor's hat for a second.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You heard me talk to Mary McCord earlier in the program. Had I taken these documents home I would've been prosecuted. Had she taken them home she would've been prosecuted. But it is different for a former president.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, it's always different. And, first of all, George, I would've prosecuted you if you brought those documents home. Let there be no doubt. But it is different for a former president, and, and it's different, and I used to say in our office when we would have, not a president, when we - we investigated a sitting governor when I was US Attorney, and what I would say to my prosecutors was, this has to be clear, so that even anyone in the state of New Jersey, no matter your level of education, no matter where you work, no matter your party, can hear these facts and say, you know what, that's wrong, and whoever did that needs to be prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, the, the bar for DOJ here is, you have to articulate a case as to why I think this threatened national security. Should they get the documents back? Absolutely. And whatever they needed to do to get those documents back, they should've done. But here's the other problem you're talking to Dan about this, about there being a long time that the documents, well, you're damned if you do and damned if you don't in that situation as the, as the DOJ. If you executed a search warrant on week three, afterwards they would've said, "Ah, you didn't even give the President a chance to voluntarily return them."\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) If you go through the process that it appears they did go through where they asked nicely, negotiated with lawyers, subpoenaed, second subpoena and still don't get it all back, and then you execute a search warrant, people say, "Hey..."\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) "...you, why, if it was so important, why'd you wait that long?"\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But, you know, but Heidi, I, I think that's a fair point and I also think Chris' point about national security, but we don't know what we don't know here...\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right, right.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...based on the redactions. And there does appear to be some evidence from some reporting that there was, there were people going in and out of the storage area...\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...and moving boxes, putting containers...\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...putting files into different containers.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...and there, there is reporting that this would disclose maybe our assets, that this already has hurt our, but we're over our skis on this. We've got to give Merrick Garland and the Department of Justice time to make these decisions and latitude, and the second-guessing is, is natural in a political environment. But to me this is serious enough, it has to be done well. If you are going to prosecute the former president, you better win. Right?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well...\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You better, you better have, have a very, very strong case. And so, to me, there, there is all this speculation about what's in these documents, whether he, he agreed to give them back and didn't give them back. Let's just give this some time, because it's too important to not give it some time.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and we don't know why they did this. I mean, that is - there are many unknowns, but one of the unknowns is, was the primary reason for seeking and getting this search warrant to get the documents back and to secure them, or was it to prepare to indict the former president? We don't know. Is there a decision here to prosecute \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They may not know, yet.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Or, I mean, they, yeah, or, or is it, we need to secure these documents?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They better know.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They got a grand jury.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But, by the way...\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...they better know, George...\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, I agree.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Because if you're Merrick Garland and you authorize this search warrant, you'd better know why you did it.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and I used to say all the time in high-profile cases when someone would come to me for a search warrant, you better have enough to indict, if that's your goal, before you even ask me for the search warrant, because when you execute a search on a high-profile individual, everyone believes they're guilty.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, then does that answer well, then does that answer your question?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The, the, the...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, because here, it's different, because these documents themselves also have great value. You're not, usually when you're searching for something, you're searching for evidence of a crime. Here, you know that this stuff is top secret, classified stuff. You've got to get that back, no matter what.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, that's why it's a bit of a different situation than...\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and you know there was a crime committed. And...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, sure.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...clearly, there has been a crime committed. You can indict this tomorrow. The question is, whether the President is gonna get preferential treatment, and to your argument, Chris, you think he should, and I think that...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, I, I don't think he should. Let's be fair. I think he...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's different.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...it is a practical matter, that's the way it works.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, and that is gonna have to be the last word right now. As you've both said, we have a long way to go on this.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Coming up, one year after the US withdrawal from Afghanistan, Martha Raddatz reports on the story of one lucky family who escaped.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220830ei8s00007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MIDTERM MONITOR SPECIAL ELECTIONS AFTER SCOTUS RULING\b0\par\par\uc2 1,032 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , MAR-A-LAGO, SEARCH, DOJ, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , SCHIFF\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , MAR-A-LAGO, REDACTED, DOCS, OBSTRUCTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP CRIMINAL INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOJ UNSEALS REDACTED MAR-A-LAGO AFFIDAVIT\par \par \uc2 BLUNT, TRUMP, MAR-A-LAGO, BIDEN, LOAN, INFLATION\par \par \uc2 SANDERS, BIDEN, LOAN, PLAN, MIDTERMS, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROGRESSIVE PUSH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE-ON-ONE WITH SEN BERNIE SANDERS\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM, MONITOR, ABORTION, PELOSI, BIDEN, SENATE\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: WEDNESDAY\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (DEM-CA) (SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE)\par \par \uc2 Republicans should be very, very, very scared this morning about their prospects. I have never believed that we would not hold the House. That's my mission, and that's what I hope to accomplish, and I have a plan to do it.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC THIS WEEK: 2022 VOTE: MIDTERM MONITOR\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  celebrating after Democrat Pat Ryan won a special election for a House seat in New York State on Tuesday, offering new hope to Democrats in the midterms. Political director Rick Klein is at the Midterm Monitor. And, Rick, midterms always tough for the party in control, but Democrats are feeling a lot better than they did at the beginning of the summer.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SPECIAL ELECTIONS AFTER SCOTUS RULING\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, George. Democrats are starting to get voters out. We saw it in the surprise result in Kansas, where that abortion initiative went down, abortion rights preserved in a deep red state. And we're seeing it in just about all of the special elections that have happened since the Dobbs ruling overturn, overturning Roe v. Wade. There's been four of them, Democrats and Republicans squaring off, just like they will this fall. And in each one of those four races since Dobbs, the Democrat has done better than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did two years ago. Starting in Nebraska, a deep red district, but the Democrat did six points better than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did just two years ago.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NY 19TH CONGRESSIONAL DISTRICT\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Minnesota, same situation, three points better than Biden. And last week, in western New York, you had a Trump-plus 11 district. The Democrat did three points better than Biden did there, as well. Now, look, the Republicans won all of those seats, but where they didn't win, this was a closely watched race last week, because this is a swing district. This is the, the lower Catskills, the, the Hudson Valley, and, and it's a district that voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  just two years ago. Democrats wanted to hold on to it, and they did. They, they did it by outperforming Biden's number by three points. The Democrat, Pat Ryan, won that race. And the way he did it, George, he told voters there, explicitly, abortion rights are on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What are the other Democratic targets, going forward?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION ON THE BALLOT\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, look, abortion rights are, at least hypothetically, on the, on the, on the playing field everywhere, but there's are a couple of states that are gonna vote explicitly on whether to keep abortion legal or to outlaw it. You've got initiatives in places, including California, Montana, Kentucky, also Vermont and, and Michigan is likely to, to join that list, as well. And the places to watch, George, to your question, the battleground states where you now have a Democratic governor and Republicans controlling at least part of the legislature, take a look at places like Minnesota, Wisconsin, Michigan, Pennsylvania, some of the biggest, most important battlegrounds, and who controls the governor's office there could determine the future of abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Add to that places like Texas, Georgia, Florida, Democrats are hoping to make the choice this fall explicitly about choice.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the question is, can they, can they do that? You heard Speaker Pelosi right at the beginning of this segment saying she always believed the Democrats are gonna hold the House. She has kind of a lonely figure, in that view. If you look at history, the party in control almost always loses seats, especially when you consider President Biden's approval rating.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CURRENT HOUSE CONTROL\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and the math here is just so stark, George. Even with that victory in the special election, Republicans still need to flip only five Democratic seats this fall. Most of the battleground continues to be Democratic-controlled seats, but what has changed, George, even if most Democrats still don't believe that they'll control the House, some are starting to think that they might be able to minimize the losses, to take some marginal seats off the table, given some of the other dynamics at play right now, and their hope at this point is that even if the Republicans do end up controlling the House, it might be a very narrow majority.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the Senate is a different story. The Democrats have a reasonable chance there of maintaining control.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2022 SENATE BATTLEGROUNDS\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, look, the battleground has always favored the, the, the party that is out, but if you look at the states in play, you see so many states where Republican candidates continue to trail their Democratic opponents. Many of those Republicans were selected in the primaries by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Many of them denied the results of the last election. Many of them, frankly, have been flawed candidates. Right now, our partners at FiveThirtyEight still think Republicans are, are not favored to control the Senate next year.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rick Klein, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Roundtable's up next. And one year after the fallout of Afghanistan to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , Martha Raddatz reports on how one Afghan interpreter and his family managed to escape. They were some of the lucky ones.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Roundtable is here and ready to go. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220830ei8s00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 LOUISIANA MOTHER DENIED AN ABORTION DOCTORS TOLD DAVIS HER PREGNANCY WAS NON-VIABLE\b0\par\par\uc2 465 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEAVILY REDACTED DOCUMENT: AFFIDAVIT RELEASED\par \par \uc2 AFFIDAVIT, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , MAR-A-LAGO, TRUMP, CLASSIFIED, COHEN\par \par \uc2 LLOYD, REDACTED, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , MAR-A-LAGO, TRUMP, CONCERN\par \par \uc2 POWELL, FEDERAL RESERVE, PAIN, JOB\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, NUCLEAR, EMERGENCY, RADIATION, KOTIN\par \par \uc2 POPE, CARDINALS, PONTIFF\par \par \uc2 STUDENTS, LOAN, DEBT, BIDEN, SMITHERS\par \par \uc2 MODERNA, \uc2 PFIZER\uc2 , MRNA, COVID, SUING, TECHNOLOGY\par \par \uc2 LOUISIANA, MOTHER, ABORTION, DAVIS\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) To Louisiana now where this morning a mother of three is sharing her personal struggle. Nancy Davis was told by doctors that her pregnancy was non-viable. They recommended an abortion, but she says she was told that because of Louisiana's strict law, she couldn't have the procedure in her home state. Here's ABC's Mireya Villarreal.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Nancy Davis is a mother of three pregnant with her fourth and now at the center of a complicated battle over abortion that's playing out around the country.\par \par \uc2 NANCY DAVIS (SAYS SHE WAS DENIED AN ABORTION)\par \par \uc2 Ten weeks into my pregnancy, I found out that my baby had a rare birth defect called (inaudible). They told me that I should terminate the pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Doctors told her the baby's skull wouldn't develop and could not survive. Davis agreed to have an abortion, but said she was denied the service.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOCTORS WOULD NOT PERFORM ABORTION IN LOUISIANA. PREGNANT WOMAN DEMANDS CLARIFICATION OF STATE'S RESTRICTIONS\par \par \uc2 NANCY DAVIS (SAYS SHE WAS DENIED AN ABORTION)\par \par \uc2 Basically they said I had to carry my baby to bury my baby. They seemed confused about the law and afraid of what would happen to them if they performed a criminal abortion according to the law.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Adding to the confusion, conservative Louisiana legislators putting out a statement after hearing her story, saying Davis is within the exception that allows for abortion if the mother is carrying a child that cannot survive outside the womb. So far, 15 states have ceased nearly all abortion services since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v Wade in June. But the Biden administration with a small victory this week, a federal judge granting a preliminary injunction, blocking parts of Idaho's near total ban on abortions. Five other states have bans that have been temporarily blocked in court, but could be implemented in the near future.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As for Nancy Davis, she is 14 weeks pregnant and planning to go out of state to get this abortion done. Her attorney Ben Crump is telling us that she may be the first in Louisiana to experience this, but she certainly won't be the last. Guys.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mireya, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220927ei8r0005t\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR GOES VIRAL ON TIKTOK WHY HER REQUEST FOR A MAMMOGRAM WAS DENIED\b0\par\par\uc2 872 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOCUMENT THAT JUSTIFIED MAR-A-LAGO SEARCH\par \par \uc2 MAR-A-LAGO, DOCUMENTS, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , CRENSHAW, SCHIFF\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, NUCLEAR, KOTIN, ZELENSKYY, PUTIN, TROOPS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, STUDENT DEBT\par \par \uc2 FED, POWELL, INTEREST, RATES, ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 RAINFALL, FLASH FLOODING, RIVERS, RESIDENTS\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM, BAN, TAILPIPE, ELECTRIC, EMISSIONS\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS, US OPEN, GRAND SLAMS, RETIRE, DJOKOVIC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE THREAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 NASH, ABDUCTION, SUSPECT, ESCAPE, SCREAM, WALSH\par \par \uc2 BOATERS, RESCUE, COOLER, POLICE, VIDEO\par \par \uc2 ELTON, BRITNEY, "HOLD ME CLOSER", MIER\par \par \uc2 CATCH, FOOTBALL\par \par \uc2 STORY2\par \par \uc2 MAR-A-LAGO, SEARCH, DOCUMENT, RELEASE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAR-A-LAGO SEARCH FALLOUT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUDGE ORDERS RELEASE OF KEY DOCUMENT FOR SEARCH\par \par \uc2 STUDENT LOANS, BIDEN, CANCELLATION, BACKLASH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN ADDRESSES RALLY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT TOUTS DEBT RELIEF BUT FACES BACKLASH\par \par \uc2 NORDSTROM, \uc2 MACY'S\uc2 , OVERSTOCK, SALES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIG RETAIL SALES ON THE WAY?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NORDSTROM/\uc2 MACY'S\uc2  PLANNING OVERSTOCK DISCOUNTS\par \par \uc2 TATA, "90210", ACTOR, ALZHEIMER'S\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CELEBRATING JOE E. TATA OF "90210" FAME\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BELOVED ACTOR PASSES AWAY AFTER ALZHEIMER'S\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER, MAMMOGRAM, TIKTOK, HEALTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR GOES VIRAL ON TIKTOK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHY HER REQUEST FOR A MAMMOGRAM WAS DENIED\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to a Stage 4 breast cancer thriver sharing her story on TikTok about being diagnosed at 29 years old after initially being denied a mammogram. Elwyn Lopez is back with more on that, and Elwyn, and this young woman now encouraging others to advocate for their own health.\par \par \uc2 ELWYN LOPEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Amy, good morning. When Philecia La'Bounty says she felt a lump in her breast, she says she immediately knew something was wrong. Eight months later, she got a diagnosis that turned her life upside-down.\par \par \uc2 PHILECIA LA'BOUNTY (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 It's really important to show the really hard parts of cancer.\par \par \uc2 ELWYN LOPEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It started out as an ordinary day for Philecia La'Bounty. The 29-year-old says she was watching a movie with her boyfriend when her life took an unprecedented turn.\par \par \uc2 PHILECIA LA'BOUNTY (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 I had a sports bra on, and it became a little uncomfortable. I adjusted myself. On my left breast, I felt a small, probably like the size of a marble, just something that wasn't really normal for me. They did just a basic ultrasound and a physical examination. The results came back that it was just a benign cyst, nothing out of the ordinary, no concerns. My blood work turned out great.\par \par \uc2 ELWYN LOPEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) La'Bounty says she requested a mammogram twice but was denied due to her age and no family history of breast cancer. Mammograms work best in older adults because of the tissue density of younger women. They are not as accurate for those under 30 and may produce inaccurate results, doctors telling ABC News if an ultrasound shows a concerning finding, aspiration or biopsy should be performed and a follow-up visit scheduled.\par \par \uc2 DR. JESSICA SHEPHERD (VERYWELL HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Now, in cases such as these, where we may have women who may not fit that guideline based on age for a screening test, is really being an advocate for yourself and really using your voice when it comes to you knowing that there's something that needs to be paid attention to, or there needs to be investigation.\par \par \uc2 ELWYN LOPEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) La'Bounty says doctors identified a small lump in her breast and said she should return if it causes her discomfort. When she returned eight months later, that lump had grown to eight centimeters.\par \par \uc2 PHILECIA LA'BOUNTY (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 I went back in, and when we went back in for that second appointment, it was like I had just, like, unleashed everything. It just kept spiraling until a point where they told me I, in fact, had breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 ELWYN LOPEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A biopsy that confirmed she had Stage 4 breast cancer. Women ages 25 to 29 comprises 0.1 percent of new breast cancer diagnosis in the US, and the now-35-year-old says her Stage 4 cancer diagnosis led her to the lifelong mission of helping other women.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HER MISSION TO HELP OTHER WOMEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR GOES VIRAL ON TIKTOK\par \par \uc2 PHILECIA LA'BOUNTY (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 If you take one thing from my story, it's to push for yourself. Don't settle unless you absolutely know, 100 percent, that you, what you're going through is normal, or it's not going to risk your health, that you know that you did everything in your power and then some to get what you deserve. I feel like it gives me another sense of hope.\par \par \uc2 ELWYN LOPEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In Philecia's case, it's unclear whether a mammogram would have been beneficial to her. She is currently in remission, but Amy, she says she will be fighting this for the rest of her life.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She certainly will, but she is a fighter and a thriver and such an advocate for women to take back their own health. So, Elwyn, thank you for that story. We appreciate it. Whit.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220927ei8q0003c\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 LOUISIANA WOMAN'S ABORTION STRUGGLE STATE LAW LEADS TO CONFUSION AND HEARTBREAK\b0\par\par\uc2 557 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRUMP, AFFIDAVIT, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , SEARCH, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, AFFIDAVIT, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , SEARCH, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE STORMS STRIKE\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, POWELL, INFLATION, STOCKS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 'PAIN' AHEAD IN INFLATION FIGHT\par \par \uc2 LAWSUIT, \uc2 PFIZER\uc2 , MODERNA, MILLER, POLIO, VACCINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MODERNA SUES PFIZER\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, DAVIS, COLE, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LOUISIANA WOMAN'S ABORTION STRUGGLE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Next, a Louisiana mother facing a gut-wrenching decision. At 10 weeks pregnant, she says doctors told her that her baby would die shortly after birth, and they recommended she terminate the presidency. But because of Louisiana's strict ban on abortion, she says no doctors would agree to perform the procedure. Now, she's seeking a way forward. Here's ABC's Mireya Villarreal.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATE LAW LEADS TO CONFUSION AND HEARTBREAK\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, a Louisiana mother of three sharing her struggle to end a nonviable pregnancy, complicated by the lingering battle over abortion in this country. Doctors told her, her baby would not develop a skull and could not survive.\par \par \uc2 NANCY DAVIS (SEEKING ABORTION)\par \par \uc2 Basically, they said I had to carry my baby to bury my baby. They seemed confused about the law.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Nancy Davis says she was denied an abortion because providers worried they would violate Louisiana's trigger ban on abortion and face legal consequences. Adding to the confusion, conservative Louisiana legislators putting out a statement after hearing her story, saying Davis is within the exception that allows for abortion if the mother is carrying a child that cannot survive outside the womb.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC COLE (PARTNER OF NANCY DAVIS)\par \par \uc2 Until you're actually in this situation and going through it, you don't understand how complex it is.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CEASED NEARLY ALL ABORTION SERVICES\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) So far, 15 states have ceased nearly all abortion services since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in June. But the Biden administration with a small victory this week, a federal judge granting a preliminary injunction blocking parts of Idaho's near-total ban on abortions. Today, on Women's Equality Day, President Biden calling on women to make their voices heard on Election Day.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BATTLE OVER ABORTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN: "YOU'RE GOING TO HEAR WOMEN ROAR ON THIS ISSUE"\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 You're going to hear women roar on this issue, and it's going to be consequential.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Whit, as for Nancy Davis, she is 14 weeks pregnant and planning to travel out of state to get this abortion done. Her attorney Ben Crump telling us she may be the first in Louisiana to experience this, but she certainly won't be the last. Whit?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mireya Villarreal, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE YEAR LATER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN HONORS HEROES' KILLED\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And somber anniversary, today. One year ago, 13 US service members were killed in the Afghanistan\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It happened during the chaotic US withdrawal from the country after 20 years of war. A suicide bomber struck at the airport gate. More than 100 civilians were also killed. President Biden, today, saying he is praying for the families and loved ones who lost a piece of their soul.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220829ei8q00007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ABORTION BATTLE IMPACTS PRIMARIES DEMOCRAT WINS NY SWING DISTRICT CAMPAIGNING ON ABORTION RIGHTS\b0\par\par\uc2 511 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 STORM, FLOODING, RESCUE, EVACUATIONS, BILBRO, CROSS\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORM, TRACK, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, STUDENT LOANS, MASON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STUDENT DEBT RELIEF FOR MILLIONS\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, INVASION, ZELENSKYY, US AID\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY ATTACK ON UKRAINE TRAIN STATION\par \par \uc2 PRIMARY, RYAN, MOLINARO, PELOSI, ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION BATTLE IMPACTS PRIMARIES\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back here at home, tonight, and to the ABC News countdown to the midterms and the eye-opening results from the primaries, overnight. Tonight, here, what a key congressional race in New York state might reveal about the role abortion rights could play in the midterms. The Democrat Pat Ryan winning in a major upset, and he focused on a woman's right to decide for herself. Rachel Scott on the race, tonight, and what it could signal.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRAT WINS NY SWING DISTRICT CAMPAIGNING ON ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 SUPPORTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Everybody for Pat Ryan.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, a surprise Democratic upset in a key congressional race, a new sign abortion could be a driving force in the midterm elections. Democrat Pat Ryan of upstate New York winning Tuesday, after making abortion rights his central focus.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE-ELECT PAT RYAN (DEM) (NEW YORK)\par \par \uc2 When the Supreme Court ripped away reproductive freedoms, access to abortion rights, we said, "This is not what America stands for."\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) His Republican rival, Marc Molinaro, reading from the GOP campaign playbook, urging voters to focus on inflation and crime, instead.\par \par \uc2 MARC MOLINARO (DUTCHESS COUNTY EXECUTIVE)\par \par \uc2 It does a disservice to the people in this district to be focused on anything other than they are concerned about the cost of living. They're concerned about safety in their communities.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) That message falling short. Ryan securing a victory in the swing district President Biden won by fewer than two points.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (DEM-CA) (SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE)\par \par \uc2 Republicans should be very, very, very scared, this morning.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It comes after voters in Kansas, a conservative state, overwhelming rejected a move to strip abortion rights from the state's Constitution. In the weeks after the Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade, 70% of newly registered voters in Kansas were women, according to Target Smart Insights, a Democratic data firm.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WISCONSIN, MICHIGAN AND PENNSYLVANIA\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The group says there are similar surges in key battleground states, Wisconsin, Michigan, and Pennsylvania, where the percentage of women registering to vote for the first time outpaced men by about 12 points.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And back here in New York, the Democratic winner, Pat Ryan, a West Point graduate, did campaign on fighting inflation, but he made it clear that choice was on the ballot this primary election, and Democratic turnout exceeded expectations, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Everyone studying each and every one of these races to see if it signals something coming with the midterms. Rachel Scott, tonight. Our thanks to you, as always.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220825ei8o00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ALARMING NEW STUDY ON LATE-STAGE CERVICAL CANCER RESEARCHERS FIND STEEP RISE IN CASES\b0\par\par\uc2 721 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRUMP, SWALWELL, CLASSIFIED DOCUMENTS\par \par \uc2 FLASH FLOODING, STORM, RESCUES\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 PRIMARY, ELECTION, CRIST, FRIED, DESANTIS\par \par \uc2 PRIMARY, ELECTION, NADLER, MALONEY, PATEL\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, UKRAINE, INVASION, DUGINA, DUGIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINE ON HIGH ALERT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FEARS RUSSIA COULD ESCALATE ATTACKS AS NATION MARKS INDEPENDENCE DAY\par \par \uc2 ITALY, INVESTIGATION, ZANIER, CRASH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: U.S. SERVICE MEMBER ARRESTED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUSPECTED OF DUI AFTER FATALLY STRIKING BOY IN ITALY\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, \uc2 PFIZER\uc2 , FAUCI, BOOSTER\par \par \uc2 BRADY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRADY'S BACK!\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BUCS QB RETURNS TOP TRAINING CAMP AFTER NEARLY TWO WEEKS OFF\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 PLANE, CRASH, KAMAL, NJEIM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLANE CRASH SURVIVORS SPEAK OUT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COUPLE ON MAKING IT THROUGH FIERY AIRCRAFT ACCIDENT\par \par \uc2 ARREST, HUTCHINSON, WORCESTER, DAMANTE\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, WEATHER, CANCELLATIONS, DELAY, STAFFING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AIR TRAVEL CHAOS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BAD WEATHER & STAFFING ISSUES LEAVE THOUSANDS STRANDED\par \par \uc2 MARIJUANA, RAHMANDAR, YOUNG ADULTS, TEENAGERS\par \par \uc2 HALL, DISC GOLF\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 STORY2\par \par \uc2 DOCUMENTS, TRUMP, MAR-A-LAGO, SEARCH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW FALLOUT FROM MAR-A-LAGO SEARCH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP HAD MORE THAN 300 CLASSIFIED DOCUMENTS\par \par \uc2 GIFT CARDS, UNUSED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CASHING IN ON UNUSED GIFT CARDS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SURVEY: AMERICANS HOLDING ON TO $21 BILLION\par \par \uc2 STYLES, SEXUALITY, SINGER, ROMANCE, PRIVACY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HARRY STYLES GETS CANDID\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OPENS UP ABOUT SEXUALITY, ROMANCE & PRIVACY\par \par \uc2 CERVICAL CANCER, HEALTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALARMING NEW STUDY ON LATE-STAGE CERVICAL CANCER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RESEARCHERS FIND STEEP RISE IN CASES\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We have an alarming new study about women's health. Researchers finding a steep rise in late-stage cervical cancers. Our chief medical correspondent, Jennifer Ashton, is here with this. And I want to get this right. The study looked at cervical cancer rates based on stage and ethnic background. What did it find?\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Robin, it's about the age of the woman, the stage of the cancer, race, ethnicity, even where they live, with women in the South found to be at highest risk. So let's, let's go through the findings of this study. Good news and bad news here. On the good news side, the rate of early-stage cervical cancer decreasing overall. That's largely due to the HPV vaccine that's been out since 2006. The rates of late-stage cervical cancer stable or going up. The largest rate of increase seen in white women, younger women and women in the South, late-stage cervical cancer more common and more deadly in black women. So, this was an important study to tease out the differences in this one type of cancer.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's so important to lay it out like that. So, what are the screening recommendations? Because sometimes it seems like it changes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It does, and I will tell you that this is clear. It's not mysterious. It's not controversial. It is different based on a woman's age, whether they've had an abnormal pap smear in the past. It's not one size fits all. But basically by age 21 to 29 years old, a pap smear alone, every three years, a woman should not get a pap smear before the age of 21. When you're 30 to 65 years of age, it could be a pap smear alone every three years or something called co-testing, HPV and pap smear every five years. This does not mean that you don't need to see your gynecologist or health care provider, however, for other reasons.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is encouraging that we're able to say a particular cancer can be prevented.\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right and it's all about vaccination, Robin. For girls and boys, it's recommended they get the HPV vaccine starting at age 11. It is approved up to, for men and women up to age 46. And in the world of cancer, the HPV vaccine is called the single greatest advance in oncology and preventing cancer in the last century.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Jen, I'm sure we'll see you on "GMA3."\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, you will.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, thanks so much. Whit.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220921ei8n0000n\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NATION'S LARGEST EMPLOYER EXPANDS ABORTION COVERAGE \uc2 WALMART\uc2  TO PROVIDE "TRAVEL SUPPORT"\b0\par\par\uc2 120 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS MORNING: FLOOD ALERT\par \par \uc2 TROPICAL STORM, TEXAS, ARIZONA, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 STANDOFF, NUCLEAR, ZELENSKYY, RUSSIAN, GUTERRES\par \par \uc2 AID, UKRAINE, WAR, AMMUNITION, DRONES, HOWITZERS\par \par \uc2 ISIS, SENTENCE, ELSHEIKH\par \par \uc2 ASYLUM, ARIZONA, TEXAS, MIGRANTS, BANKS\par \par \uc2 WALMART\uc2 , ABORTION\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Retail giant \uc2 Walmart\uc2  is expanding its abortion coverage for employees. The company's plan will now cover abortion for employees when, quote, there is a health risk to the mother, rape or incest, ectopic pregnancy, miscarriage, or lack of fetal viability. The new policy also offers travel support. This comes as a judge in Michigan has blocked prosecutors from enforcing a 1931 law that bans abortion in the state.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220920ei8k0005r\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FLORIDA ABORTION BATTLE COURT: TEEN NOT "MATURE" ENOUGH TO END PREGNANCY\b0\par\par\uc2 706 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 CDC, REVIEW, COVID, WALENSKY, OVERHAUL, JHA, BOOSTERS\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI, ELECTION TAMPERING, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GIULIANI TESTIFIES BEFORE GRAND JURY\par \par \uc2 CHENEY, PRIMARY ELECTION, TRUMP, PENCE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHENEY'S LANDSLIDE LOSS\par \par \uc2 RUSHDIE, ATTACK, MATAR, IRAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUSPECT: "SURPRISED" RUSHDIE SURVIVED\par \par \uc2 LAKE MEAD, CLIMATE CHANGE, SKELETAL REMAINS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIFTH SET OF REMAINS DISCOVERED\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, WAIVER, WARREN, COHEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLORIDA ABORTION BATTLE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We turn next, tonight, to the newest battle over abortion rights in this country, after Roe versus Wade was overturned. The new firestorm tonight in Florida involving the case of a 16-year-old girl, arguing she should be allowed a waiver to get an abortion. The court denying that request. And ABC's Victor Oquendo, from Florida, now.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COURT: TEEN NOT "MATURE" ENOUGH TO END PREGNANCY\par \par \uc2 VICTOR OQUENDO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, a 16-year-old girl is now at the center of the debate over abortion in this country. In the wake of the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade, she's pleading in a Florida court to get an abortion, but an appellate court upholding a lower court's ruling that the girl is not sufficiently mature enough to terminate her pregnancy. The teen, referred to as Jane Doe 22-B in court documents, was ten weeks pregnant. She is described as "parentless," living with a relative and has a state-appointed guardian. She's asked the judge to waive the state's parental notification and consent requirements for minors, arguing "she is sufficiently mature" and "is not ready to have a baby," adding, her guardian supported her decision. Retired Florida Judge Jeri B. Cohen was not involved in these legal proceedings, but presided over countless cases like this over her career.\par \par \uc2 JERI B COHEN (RETIRED FLORIDA JUDGE)\par \par \uc2 If she's forced to carry a child to term that she does not feel she has the emotional, physical or financial capability to do, that could be extremely traumatizing.\par \par \uc2 VICTOR OQUENDO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The girl, who doesn't have a job and is studying for her GED, can make her case again, but time is of the essence. Florida Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  signed a 15-week abortion ban into law this year. And now, one state attorney, suspended from his job for vowing not to prosecute those who violate that law, Democrat Andrew Warren, filing a lawsuit in federal court after DeSantis removed him from office earlier this month for what the governor called incompetence and neglect of duty.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WARREN (FORMER FLORIDA PROSECUTOR)\par \par \uc2 This is about the governor's illegal suspension of the duly elected state attorney. This is about the governor's ability to violate the law.\par \par \uc2 VICTOR OQUENDO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And as for that 16-year-old girl, a dissenting judge saying that if she is able to come back to court and adequately articulate her request, she may still be able to get a waiver for an abortion. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Victor Oquendo following this case in Florida, tonight. Victor, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OPIOID EPIDEMIC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PHARMACY CHAINS TO PAY $650M+\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Next, tonight, the fight to help victims of the opioid crisis in this country. A federal judge in Cleveland, tonight, awarding $650 million in damages rewarded to two Ohio counties in a landmark lawsuit they won against CVS, \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  and \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , claiming the way those companies distributed opioids to customers caused severe harm to their communities.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The money will be used to help fight the continuing opioid crisis and the families destroyed by this in Lake and Trumble Counties. That's outside Cleveland. All three companies say they will appeal this.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When we come back here, tonight, the new video coming in after that coach was shot and killed on the youth football field. Witnesses now accusing a former \uc2 NFL\uc2  star of starting the fight. It was that \uc2 NFL\uc2  star's brother, another coach, accused of firing the gun. And then, the horrific fall from a drawbridge, a tourist visiting Milwaukee, and authorities, tonight, asking, "How did this happen?"\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220818ei8h00007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CELEBRATING OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN BELOVED ACTRESS & SINGER IN HER OWN WORDS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,007 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 RAID, TRUMP, MAR-A-LAGO, GARLAND\par \par \uc2 PRIMARIES, ELECTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH-STAKES PRIMARIES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTERS GO TO THE POLLS IN FOUR STATES\par \par \uc2 HEAT, SAFETY, HOOLEY\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, HEAT WAVE\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, MISSILES, TSYMBALIUK, RUSSIA, WAR, GUTERRES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NUCLEAR CONCERNS TODAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OFFICIALS' NEW WARNING AS \uc2 UN\uc2  CONDEMNS ATTACKS AROUND UKRAINIAN PLANT\par \par \uc2 SEARCH, MUSALLAM, RODNI, NIEMAN, SMITH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: URGENT SEARCH FOR MISSING TEENAGER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLICE SAY 16-YEAR-OLD WHO VANISHED MAY HAVE BEEN ABDUCTED\par \par \uc2 GAS PRICES, INFLATION, ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 NEWTON-JOHN, TRIBUTE, PEARL, FOSTER, TRAVOLTA\par \par \uc2 CONN, NEWTON-JOHN, TRIBUTE\par \par \uc2 INFLATION, SCHOOL SUPPLIES, SHOPPING, TIPS\par \par \uc2 EMA, SKATEBOARDING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AHEAD ON GMA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JENNETTE MCCURDY TALKS NEW MEMOIR\par \par \uc2 STORY2\par \par \uc2 FBI\uc2 , MAR-A-LAGO, TRUMP, CLASSIFIED, DOCUMENTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FBI SEARCHES TRUMP'S MAR-A-LAGO HOME\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOURCES: SEARCH RELATED TO CLASSIFIED DOCUMENTS\par \par \uc2 LYME DISEASE, VACCINE, TRIAL, CDC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 PFIZER\uc2  LAUNCHES LATE-STAGE TRIAL FOR LYME DISEASE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ILLNESS AFFECTS HUNDREDS OF THOUSANDS PER YEAR\par \par \uc2 HEAT, ALERT, WEATHER, NORTHEAST, EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEAT EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ILLNESS AFFECTS HUNDREDS OF THOUSANDS PER YEAR\par \par \uc2 MCCURDY, ACTOR, MEMOIR, "I'M GLAD MY MOM DIED"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JENNETTE MCCURDY OPENS UP IN NEW MEMOIR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FORMER CHILD STAR ON HER LIFE BEHIND THE SCENES\par \par \uc2 NEWTON-JOHN, ACTOR, SINGER, BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CELEBRATING OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BELOVED ACTRESS & SINGER IN HER OWN WORDS\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, George, we're going to turn now to some of our favorite memories of Olivia Newton-John. The actress, singer, passed away, as we know, yesterday in California at the age of 73 after bravely facing breast cancer for decades. And we're going to open the "GMA" vault here.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, we definitely are, Robin, so many memories. We all have them. Fans and stars paying tribute to Dame Olivia Newton-John, this morning, the beloved actress and Grammy winner leaving behind so many memorable movie scenes and songs and, yes, even some inspiring moments right here on "GMA."\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Her crystal clear voice, optimistic altitude and unforgettable smile lit up the silver screen and our "GMA" studio for decades. Olivia Newton-John, a global sensation and our dear friend, joining us back in 1978, the year "Grease" was released, to discuss her now iconic role of Sandy, a character she says was not exactly like her.\par \par \uc2 INTERVIEWER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Are you trying to change your image? You're Miss Apple Pie, just sweet. Do you want to change your image at all?\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN (ACTRESS AND SINGER)\par \par \uc2 I just thought, when I was offered the part to do, it would be a lot of fun because I get a chance to play this other lady at the end, and I'd never actually dressed up in that manner, and it gave me such a kick to do it. I found I had a whole other side that I didn't know about.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In '81 telling us she never thought she'd achieve so much fame and success.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN (ACTRESS AND SINGER)\par \par \uc2 When you're starting out in the very beginning, you're much more keen because it's all new to you, and now I feel that I've kind of achieved everything that I ever set out to and more, really because I didn't think I'd get this far. So, now everything is kind of icing on the cake.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Taking our viewers on a jet ride in 1983, piloted by her "Grease" co-star, \uc2 John Travolta\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOHN TRAVOLTA\uc2  (ACTOR)\par \par \uc2 That means it can do it on its own.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN (ACTRESS AND SINGER)\par \par \uc2 And no hands?\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Their friendship unlike any other.\par \par \uc2 JOHN TRAVOLTA\uc2  (ACTOR)\par \par \uc2 I had done a recording of an album called "Let Her In," which was a hit, like, back in '76, and I actually imitated your pose that you had done on one of your earlier albums, where you're in a blue shirt. This is it.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN (ACTRESS AND SINGER)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, that, that does look very familiar.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The pair joining us again in 2012.\par \par \uc2 INTERVIEWER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 How does it go?\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Her light shining brightly, even while battling breast cancer, telling our Diane Sawyer in 2005 how she remained positive.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN (ACTRESS AND SINGER)\par \par \uc2 I used to visualize it. So, it's, everything is what you think about it, and instead of visualizing it as poison I used to visualize it as golden light that was going through my body to kill the bad things.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is a beautiful song she says has helped her through, well, the roughest times in her life, Olivia Newton-John.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Singing her way through it with this song she performed right here in Times Square.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, we just adore her. She brought her throngs of fans together with "Grease," with her music, with her devotion to raising money for cancer research and for a cure, and she will continue to do so in years to come. We are so hopelessly devoted to Olivia Newton-John.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Lovely.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It was interesting because earlier, when we were talking about her, you looked at me, and she said, she was really that kind? I was like, yeah, she really, because you just, you think, oh no, no one could be that. But I'm telling you, it didn't matter what walk of life you were, she treated you like you were the only person...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Certainly shined through.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It did. It did. It did. Thank you, thank you, Lara.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, my pleasure. Let's get to Ginger.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220902ei890003d\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CELEBRATING OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN BELOVED SINGER & "GREASE" STAR PASSES AWAY AT 73\b0\par\par\uc2 1,092 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 RAID, TRUMP, MAR-A-LAGO, GARLAND\par \par \uc2 PRIMARIES, ELECTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH-STAKES PRIMARIES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTERS GO TO THE POLLS IN FOUR STATES\par \par \uc2 HEAT, SAFETY, HOOLEY\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, HEAT WAVE\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, MISSILES, TSYMBALIUK, RUSSIA, WAR, GUTERRES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NUCLEAR CONCERNS TODAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OFFICIALS' NEW WARNING AS \uc2 UN\uc2  CONDEMNS ATTACKS AROUND UKRAINIAN PLANT\par \par \uc2 SEARCH, MUSALLAM, RODNI, NIEMAN, SMITH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: URGENT SEARCH FOR MISSING TEENAGER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLICE SAY 16-YEAR-OLD WHO VANISHED MAY HAVE BEEN ABDUCTED\par \par \uc2 GAS PRICES, INFLATION, ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 NEWTON-JOHN, TRIBUTE, PEARL, FOSTER, TRAVOLTA\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Also, this morning, we are celebrating the life of Grammy Award winning singer and beloved actress Olivia Newton-John.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CELEBRATING OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BELOVED SINGER & "GREASE" STAR PASSES AWAY AT 73\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The "Grease" and "Xanadu" star died Monday morning at her home in Southern California after a valiant fight with breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Amy is here with more on her life and legacy from pop star, movie star and we both know how passionate she was about cancer research and treatment.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She was truly a magical person. And I know that Olivia Newton-John once said, music is a healer. Well, her voice brought so much light and love into the hearts of her fans all around the world. The tributes pouring in for this amazingly warm woman who her "Grease" co-star Stockard Channing said was the essence of summer.\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MOVIE, "GREASE"\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN (ACTRESS AND SINGER)\par \par \uc2 Tell me about it, stud.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Charming her way into the hearts of millions, Olivia Newton-John becoming a superstar on the big screen for decades, famously winning over \uc2 John Travolta\uc2 ...\par \par \uc2 JOHN TRAVOLTA\uc2  (ACTOR)\par \par \uc2 Sandy.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN (ACTRESS AND SINGER)\par \par \uc2 Danny?\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...and viewers as the lovable Sandy in the 1978 hit "Grease," passing away Monday morning after her battle with breast cancer. Born in England in 1948 and raised in Melbourne, Australia, from the age of five, Newton-John's first passion was music, appearing on talent shows. Her singing career skyrocketed, selling over 100 million albums. Her smash hit "Physical" named Billboard's biggest hit of the '80s.\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MUSIC VIDEO, "PHYSICAL"\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Newton-John taking home four Grammy Awards for pop hits throughout the '70s and early '80s.\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MUSICAL, "XANADU"\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Her starring role in the musical "Xanadu" becoming a fan favorite. But it was that 1978 leading role as transfer student turned Pink Lady that catapulted her to the top, starring as then rising star \uc2 John Travolta\uc2 's love interest. Her health took a turn, diagnosed with breast cancer for the first time in 1992, undergoing surgery and chemotherapy, living in remission for 25 years. She became a fierce advocate for detection, opening foundations devoted to cancer research and treatment. In 2017, she revealed her cancer had returned and spread to her bones. Her husband telling the world she died peacefully at her Southern California ranch surrounded by her family and friends. Overnight, tributes pouring in for the woman who always shined with love and light.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRIBUTES POUR IN FOR SINGER & "GREASE" STAR\par \par \uc2 BARRY PEARL (DOODY IN GREASE "LOVE LIFE")\par \par \uc2 She just embodied that glow. She was a person who did not meander through life. She penetrated existence. And her artistry is a manifestation of all of that love and light that she is.\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MUSIC VIDEO, "THE BEST OF ME"\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Earlier this morning, "GMA" speaking with her friend and song collaborator David Foster.\par \par \uc2 DAVID FOSTER (RECORD PRODUCER/SONG WRITER)\par \par \uc2 She's the only superstar that didn't act like a superstar. She would always just float into the studio with the best attitude. She really was America's sweetheart but she was Australian.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Actor \uc2 Hugh Jackman\uc2  writing, "One of the great privileges of my life was getting to know her. It's no secret Olivia was my first crush." Her "Grease" co-star \uc2 John Travolta\uc2  writing, "Your impact was incredible. I love you so much. We will see you down the road. Yours from the first moment I saw you, and forever, your Danny, your John." The pair reuniting in recent years for charity, speaking with me in 2012 about their friendship and how they help one another.\par \par \uc2 JOHN TRAVOLTA\uc2  (ACTOR)\par \par \uc2 We really trust each other and we're good friends and we never doubt that we'll be there for each other. And that's just a fact. And I think we keep our friendship at a high criteria and we deliver for each other, you know. It's just that simple.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN (ACTRESS AND SINGER)\par \par \uc2 The same experience making that movie was incredible, and we both are so grateful people still love that film and still discovering it.\par \par \uc2 JOHN TRAVOLTA\uc2  (ACTOR)\par \par \uc2 They ask us.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Olivia Newton-John is survived by her husband John, her daughter Chloe, a brother, a sister, several nieces and nephews. And her daughter posting this sweet throwback photo yesterday as well as this photo on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  just a couple of days ago. She wrote, "I worship this woman, my mother, my best friend." Her family is asking that any donations made in her memory go to the Olivia Newton-John Foundation Fund. And it was so wild yesterday when I came into work, I was listening to my favorite Olivia Newton-John song, "Magic."\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I was singing it as I came into the studio yesterday, no idea obviously, what was going to happen. But she was magic, wasn't she? She just embodied love and light and all of the things people have said about her. She's just, I mean, it was weird not talking to you guys.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, no, no.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know you had such a personal connection with her, too, and I think we all feel the loss but she leaves so much behind.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, she was so genuine. So genuine.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) "Magic" is one of my favorite ones, too.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So good.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Amy, thank you. Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220902ei8900033\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ELI LILLY\uc2  GROWING BUSINESS OUTSIDE RESPONDING TO INDIANA'S NEW ANTI-ABORTION LAW\b0\par\par\uc2 153 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, INVESTIGATION, CINCINNATI\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER, HARRIS, SANDERS, BILL, BIDEN, CLIMATE CHANGE\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, LAKE, ELECTION, SABATO, CPAC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FORMER PRESIDENT TRUMP TAKES CENTER STAGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP RECEIVES STRONG SUPPORT FROM FELLOW REPUBLICANS AT CPAC\par \par \uc2 ELI LILLY\uc2 , ABORTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 ELI LILLY\uc2  GROWING BUSINESS OUTSIDE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DRUG COMPANY RESPONDING TO INDIANA'S NEW ANTI-ABORTION LAW\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) One of the largest employers in the state of Indiana is now speaking out over the state's new law restricting access to most abortions.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Pharmaceutical company \uc2 Eli Lilly\uc2  says the new restrictions could make it more difficult for managers to recruit talent from around the world. The drugmaker also added that that means it now plans on growing the company's business outside of Indiana, which could be a very costly move for that state.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220823ei870002x\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 2022 VOTE: MIDTERM MONITOR\b0\par\par\uc2 1,032 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK WITH GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS\par \par \uc2 COONS, INFLATION REDUCTION ACT, BIDEN, 2024\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE PREPARES FINAL VOTE ON INFLATION REDUCTION ACT\par \par \uc2 ROUNDS, INFLATION REDUCTION ACT, ABORTION, 2024\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE PREPARES FINAL VOTE ON INFLATION REDUCTION ACT\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, CHINA, DRILLS, AL-QAEDA, RUSSIA, GRINER\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: WEDNESDAY\par \par \uc2 RICHARDSON, FM, GRINER, RU, PRISON, SWAP, FOGEL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRISONER SWAP NEGOTIATIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRITTNEY GRINER SENTENCED TO NINE YEARS IN RUSSIAN PRISON\par \par \uc2 2022, MIDTERM, MONITOR, VOTERS, CANDIDATES\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 TELEVISION CLIP: CAMPAIGN AD\par \par \uc2 DICK CHENEY\uc2  (FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 There has never been an individual who was a greater threat to our republic than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He tried to steal the last election using lies and violence to keep himself in power after the voters had rejected him. He's a coward. Liz is fearless. She never backs down from a fight. There's nothing more important she will ever do to lead the effort to make sure \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is never again near the Oval Office. And she will succeed.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2022 VOTE: MIDTERM MONITOR\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  blasting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in a campaign ad for his daughter ahead of her primary next week. Cheney is Trump's biggest target now. He's coming out with some key wins in this week's primaries. Political director Rick Klein back with his Midterm Monitor. And Rick, let's start with our new poll with \uc2 Ipsos\uc2 . Strong job numbers this week, but President Biden and the Democrats still facing voters who are quite sour on the economy.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR VOICE: YOUR VOTE 2022\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PERCEPTION OF ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GETTING BETTER, 12%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GETTING WORSE, 69%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STAYING THE SAME, 18%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ENTHUSIASM ABOUT VOTING IN NOV.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, George, no meaningful approval reading increase for, for, for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  across the board, or on a range of issues. He is still stuck in some low numbers. And get this, 69 percent of Americans still think the economy is getting worse despite the drop in gas prices, despite those positive economic news, and we're seeing that spill over into voter enthusiasm. Republicans more likely to say that they are ready and eager to go vote than Democrats, and those numbers have not changed, George, for months, now.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPPORT CANDIDATES WHO FAVOR\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But one sign that might, something that might change going forward, in the wake of that big vote in Kansas on Tuesday to preserve abortion rights, we asked people are you more likely to support a candidate who wants to keep abortion legal or who supports policies that would limit abortion access, and that is a striking number, 49 percent. That's almost half of Americans who say that they would consider the issue and they're more likely to vote for a candidate who would keep abortion legal in their state. That suggests that, look, well, the economy is a drag on Democrats. There are other issues that come into view, and if abortion is one of them, Democrats could see a big motivating effort around that.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Kansas aside, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had a big night on Tuesday. How does his record look across the primaries?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2022 AUGUST PRIMARIES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE BATTLEGROUND STATES\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This was another week with a MAGA sweep. We saw it in Arizona. We saw it in Michigan. He's got big tests coming up in Wisconsin, as well as with \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 's race in Wyoming. And, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's success really matters the most when it comes to the Senate map. This is what we're looking at, there are 10 seats that are likely to determine control of the Senate. Of those 10, eight of them are open the Republican side, where you have a competitive Republican primary. And when you look at those eight states, you see that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has won his candidate in almost every one of them.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2022 REPUBLICAN OPPORTUNITIES\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  in Arizona, the latest to join a list that had already included Nevada, as well as North Carolina. We have JD Vance in Ohio. Dr. Mehmet Oz in Pennsylvania, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in Georgia. Most of those candidates like Trump deny the legitimacy of the last election. Many of those candidates have never run for office, before. So, the concern among some Republicans right now, George, is while Trump knows what a Republican primary voter wants, that's not the same as a general election.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Pennsylvania, Georgia, Arizona, they're behind, right now, his candidates.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right and that's exactly the concern, is that when you see that - polling gap begin to open up, how do you overcome that when you've got Trump's people on the ballot?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTION ADMINISTRATORS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Meantime, you also saw those who support the idea that Donald - that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did not win the 2020 election, basically support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's big lie, did well in some of these down ballot races as, as well, including those who are going to administer elections.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, six states and counting where the Republican nominee for, for a statewide office that oversees an election, denies the legitimacy of the last election. That includes people like a secretary of state candidate in Arizona and in Michigan, also candidates for governor in Texas and Pennsylvania. Those are two states where the governor appoints the next secretary of state. And, and look, this is some, this is a big deal, because this could easily be the biggest takeaway from the midterm elections, George. You've got people who falsely claim that the last election was stolen who could be in a position to oversee the next election.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that is ominous. Okay, Rick Klein, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Roundtable's up next. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220810ei8700006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\b0\par\par\uc2 3,867 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK WITH GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS\par \par \uc2 COONS, INFLATION REDUCTION ACT, BIDEN, 2024\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE PREPARES FINAL VOTE ON INFLATION REDUCTION ACT\par \par \uc2 ROUNDS, INFLATION REDUCTION ACT, ABORTION, 2024\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE PREPARES FINAL VOTE ON INFLATION REDUCTION ACT\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, CHINA, DRILLS, AL-QAEDA, RUSSIA, GRINER\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: WEDNESDAY\par \par \uc2 RICHARDSON, FM, GRINER, RU, PRISON, SWAP, FOGEL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRISONER SWAP NEGOTIATIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRITTNEY GRINER SENTENCED TO NINE YEARS IN RUSSIAN PRISON\par \par \uc2 2022, MIDTERM, MONITOR, VOTERS, CANDIDATES\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 TELEVISION CLIP: CAMPAIGN AD\par \par \uc2 KA, VOTE, ABORTION, ABC/IPSOS, BIDEN, 2022, 2024\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: WEDNESDAY\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 With a record turnout, voters of Kansas defeated a ballot initiative to remove the right to choose an abortion from the Kansas constitution. Voters made it clear that politicians should not interfere with the fundamental rights of women.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL (REP) (KANSAS)\par \par \uc2 I'm deeply, emotionally sorrowful about the loss yesterday. My whole life's been about protecting the lives of moms and babies and, and I, I think that this was a step away from that.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Stunning result out of Kansas week, this week, rejecting that attempt to amend the Constitution which does protects abortion rights. One of the things we're going to talk about on our Roundtable joined by \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , \uc2 Democracy for America\uc2  CEO Yvette Simpson, our chief Washington correspondent \uc2 Jon Karl\uc2 , and executor editor of the AP, Julie Pace. And Chris, let me begin with you. What was your takeaway from Kansas?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KS VOTERS UPHOLD ABORTION RIGHTS IN STATE CONSTITUTION\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, look, I think this is what's going to happen across the country, George. People like me have been saying for decades that the states should make these calls, and the states are now going to make these calls. And I think that's why everybody who says that, well, this means that abortion's going to banned all over the country when the, the Dobbs decision came down, you know, it's going to change and vary from state to state to state, and sometimes you're going to get results that don't seem consistent with the state's color, where, in this case, Kansas, a very red state, went in that direction.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, you know, that's why I think a lot of the manufactured hysteria that some aspects of the Democratic Party went into is undercut by this result in Kansas. People are going to make their own decisions. Legislature are going to decide. Indiana did something very, very different. This is the way it's going to work across the country.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Manufactured hysteria?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMS HOPEFUL ROE REVERSAL WILL GALVANIZE VOTERS\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, no, I don't think so. I mean, I think Kansas is the exact opposite, right? It shows that this was an issue that people agree with, and when we get on the ground and organize voters in a red state across the state, in cities and rural areas and suburbs people show up. And so, unfortunately for Republicans and fortunately for us, if we continue for this same momentum, we make abortion access the issue, we might actually have enough energy to pull it off. I think that's something...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think one of the other things we saw, Julie Pace, is that when you talk about amending a Constitution, voters tend to reject radical solutions.\par \par \uc2 JULIE PACE (ASSOCIATED PRESS EXECUTIVE EDITOR)\par \par \uc2 They do. I think that feels so much more permanent for voters. And, and then I think what was notable in Kansas was not just the outcome, but it was the turnout numbers, seeing general election turnout numbers in a primary. And I think that's what has Democrats most emboldened right now is that it's, it's that enthusiasm and that energy to come out for these constitutional amendment votes. I think the challenge over the next couple of months for Democrats will be try to keep up the focus on something like abortion access at the same time that Americans are very much dealing with inflation, very much dealing with a lot of economic realities.\par \par \uc2 JULIE PACE (ASSOCIATED PRESS EXECUTIVE EDITOR)\par \par \uc2 And as you saw some of the numbers that Rick was showing us earlier, that still remains a really powerful important issue.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, that is going to be one of the big issues. Traditionally, abortion has not been a massive voting issue, especially in midterms. I guess the big question, is this year different?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IS KANSAS ABORTION VOTE BELLWETHER FOR NOV OUTCOMES?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And - look, this was an extraordinary event, I mean - this winning in Kansas with a 20-point margin voters rejecting the idea of, of a complete abortion ban. The question, though, George, is, does that enthusiasm extend to, to the candidate races for the midterms? Because in this case, there was one issue they were voting on, should abortion be outlawed? That one issue, huge turnout. The pro-abortion rights group wins, dominates. But, when you're voting for a candidate, you're also voting on, on other issues, and the issue in poll after poll after poll that we see that is still the number-one issue is inflation, gas prices, the economy.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Yvette, we just saw in our poll this morning, 69 percent sour on the economy, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  stuck in the 30s, right now.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC/IPSOS POLL: 69% SAY US ECONOMY GETTING WORSE\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. I think the reality is, we continue the debate about whether there's inflation, whether we're in a recession, and if you just ask people, they're struggling. And I know if the IRA is going to get there. I mean, we know that child...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The, the bill they're working on.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The bill that, the bill that just passed the Senate yesterday. People are still suffering. Childcare was not in there. Paid leave was not in there. We still have issues with wages. People are still feeling the pain. And if the issue at the ballot box is, do you feel like we're in a recession? We're going to lose on that. Do you feel like there's inflation? We're going to lose on that. We need to make sure we're showing the American people that we're doing everything we can to make them secure. And a small win on gas prices and a bill on climate right now is not going to get us all the way there.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Talk...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, George, you know, when you're arguing with people in a, in a 9 percent inflation world that they shouldn't be feeling pain, that the economy is actually good, that's just a losing argument, and they know it.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I agree with that.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They - that the American people feel it, they know it. And in the end, what we...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But it, it is a tale of two economies, though. You're right, the, the inflation rate is incredibly high, but look at those jobs numbers on Friday.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, but people, but people can't keep up with the costs of living their lives. So, they say okay, great, I've got a job, that's a good thing. But to get to that job I got to fill my car with gas, and it costs that much more. Then, when I'm on my way home from work, I go to the supermarket, and I get absolutely tanked there from a, from a, a dollar perspective. And then I look at what's going on with my kids, if I'm, am I sending them to school, and they're going to college, all those expenses are going way, way up. And people say, yeah, I have a job, but my life is worse now than two years ago.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we know from \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , that that's an incredibly powerful question, am I better off now than I was two years ago? And the American people don't feel it and that's why \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's in the 30s.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All that despite the fact, Julie Pace, that as Chris Coons sort of outlined, the President has had a pretty remarkable both legislative and executive action winning streak over the last several weeks.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE POISED TO PASS INFLATION REDUCTION ACT\par \par \uc2 JULIE PACE (ASSOCIATED PRESS EXECUTIVE EDITOR)\par \par \uc2 It's been a total shift in momentum for this White House, which has had a pretty rough year, on a lot fronts. And now, suddenly, you see this breakthrough on Capitol Hill in the Senate with Joe Manchin and Sinema jumping in here. You see the White house taking out, or the, the US taking out a terror leader in Afghanistan, and suddenly, it feels like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's got a bit of momentum. But I do think, to Chris' point, you know, it is really hard to convince people to feel something different than what they are experiencing.\par \par \uc2 JULIE PACE (ASSOCIATED PRESS EXECUTIVE EDITOR)\par \par \uc2 It is really hard to put out jobs statistics. It is really hard to point to even a, a big win on terrorism and make people feel differently than what they experience when they do go to their grocery store, and the White House knows that. They're going to try to do everything they can to harness this momentum, but they know they are fighting against some pretty strong headwinds, still.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) If you look at our, at our, our new poll, I mean, he's getting credit for almost nothing. He's - on a roll. I mean, there's no question that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is on a roll, legislatively, on the national security front, but he's getting no credit for it. And, look, inflation is, you know, still a factor. Gas prices are actually coming down and have been coming down for almost two months. The unemployment numbers are remarkable. Not only the unemployment numbers, but wage growth continues to rise. It's just not keeping pace with inflation.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it's an inside Washington role, George.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, when I think that...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's an inside Washington role. Like - oh, oh, they, they won on the Inflation Reduction Act, which is a ridiculous name, as you pointed out in the interview, but they won on that. They got Democrats to actually vote for a Democrat bill. What are we going to do, like, drop the confetti at the White House? I mean, this is ridiculous. It's an inside Washington role. The people in, in the rest of the country are saying, wait a second, I'm still paying much more for gas than I should. I'm still paying much more to cool my house. I can't get groceries on the table, and I'm worried about the future.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Any...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When you look ahead to the midterms, Yvette, one of the things the Democrats are counting on, if you just look to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's approval ratings and views on the economy, looking like a blood bath for Democrats. But right now, Dr. Oz is behind in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2022 MIDTERM ELECTIONS 3 MONTHS AWAY\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mark Kelly, a Democrat, is ahead in Arizona. JD Vance is fighting in Ohio. It does look like the Democrats are still in the game, at least in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 538: AT LEAST 160 GOP CANDIDATES DENY 2020 ELECTION RESULTS\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The Republicans are giving us gifts in the form of election deniers and people with rap sheets a mile long. I mean, you know, you got Dr. Oz, you got \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , you got JD Vance, these folks who do not relate to the people in their states, cannot connect with anybody, and people don't have faith in them. And I, I think we've been very fortunate. On the other side we've got some strong candidates who are going to take them on in Raphael Warnock and John Fetterman and, you know, others.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, I think we have to make sure that's that is the story. Not necessarily about what people are feeling, but the alternative is a Republican Party that doesn't believe in elections, that stormed the Capitol on January 6th, these folks who are abusers, and this issue with \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  praying with a guy in a cell. I mean, the Republican Party is a wacko party. The alternative is that you got to go with the Democrats in November.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And \uc2 Jon Karl\uc2 , we just heard \uc2 Mike Rounds\uc2  say he sure doesn't want \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on the ballot in 2022.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, look, almost nobody in Republican leadership wants \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to announce he's running before the midterms. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is on a roll, speaking of rolls. He's dominating these parties. You have candidates winning in state after state who would've had no chance without a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  endorsement, and they're winning the Republican primaries. But here's the thing, the more Trump wins, the more Republicans are poised to lose, because he is helping nominate candidates who are likely to lose coming into these races as underdogs, likely to lose races that Republicans should win.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, if Republicans don't win the Senate, there's going to be one reason and one reason only, and it's \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He has chased candidates out of races that would've won, or had a very good chance of winning in the fall, and he has nominated, helped nominate candidates who are likely to lose.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Julie Pace, little sign that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  cares about that.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP CONTINUES TO TEASE 2024 PRESIDENTIAL BID\par \par \uc2 JULIE PACE (ASSOCIATED PRESS EXECUTIVE EDITOR)\par \par \uc2 Well, I was just going to, to say, after Jon's point there, I, I think that is all likely true. I just don't think Trump cares, right? And I don't think he's going to look at these midterms as an indication of whether he should move forward or not with running for the presidency, or whether is, whether he could win or not. I think having him on the ballot still remains. I think this sort of very unique situation, I think there a lot of points of evidence that he would go into this presidential race in 2024 weaker than certainly he did, I think, in his other races. But again, I just don't think he is bringing that.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , as you know, it's perilous to predict what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will do. Can, can...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You think?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Can, can anyone convince him not to announce before 2022, and are you really convinced he's going to run?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, let's say a few things first. I've been sitting here for four and a half years now saying that the most important thing in an election is a candidate. More than atmosphere, more than money, candidates matter, and we're going to find that out in the fall. I think Yvette laid that out very clearly. You're going to find out how these candidates are going to perform. Right now, they're losing in these states. It's up to them. They've got wind at their back, as Republicans. They should win, so they have to perform.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOME IN GOP URGE TRUMP TO WAIT TO ANNOUNCE 2024 BID\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Am I convinced \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's going to run for president? I am not convinced. I believe it's 50/50 shot, right now, whether he runs or he doesn't. And I think Julie, when you say he doesn't care about, about whether or not that they can win in November, he doesn't care about that right now, because right now all he cares about is winning the primaries to show that he's still strong inside the party. But, let me tell you something, if they're showing, if Dr. Oz is losing by whatever it is now, 11 points I think it said, if Dr. Oz is losing by 11 in October, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's going to act like he never met him, let alone endorse him. Right?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How are (inaudible) going to react if they fail to win the Senate in a year where it was all set up...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, Jon, you know how...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...a huge Republican, how will Republicans...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know how they're going to react...\par \par \uc2 JULIE PACE (ASSOCIATED PRESS EXECUTIVE EDITOR)\par \par \uc2 But I think whether Trump takes that as a, as a referendum on him...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Of course he won't...\par \par \uc2 JULIE PACE (ASSOCIATED PRESS EXECUTIVE EDITOR)\par \par \uc2 ...and whether he acts off that.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, no, no. Julie, of course he won't, but he, he won't outwardly take it as a referendum on him, but he will know internally that what people are going to be able to say about him, and Republicans are going to be able to say about him is, loser. You led us down this path, and we lost. And you can count on the fact that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 's going to be saying that and a whole bunch of other people are going to be saying it. Here's the difference, real quickly, between right now and 2015. In 2015, no one took \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  seriously.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In 2015, they thought it was kind of entertaining, and they thought, well, we don't know if he's really serious about running or not. So, he had a certain atmosphere around him, both from other Republican officeholders and the media. None of that will exist eight years later in 2023 for him, and he's going to factor that into whatever he decides to do, 'cause he's not dumb.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Meantime, Yvette, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has to factor in the grumblings of a lot of Democrats, right now. Bill Richardson aside...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...a lot of Democrats are wondering...\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thanks, Bill.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...whether he should be running again, a lot of Democrats dodging the question, right now. What should he do?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERAL DEMS SKEPTICAL OF BIDEN 2024 RUN\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think that he should not run. I mean, I, you know, his numbers are really low. Overwhelmingly, the poll came out that Democrats don't want him to run. I mean, when, when folks rallied around him to be the President, there was the expectation that he would not run again, that he would actually empower someone to run. Who that person is going to be, I don't know, but his energy, the fact that he has not been able to keep his, he has not been able to keep...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You don't say \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is the heir apparent?\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She should be. I don't know that she's been positioned to do that. She should've been positioned powerfully and, and, and able to actually be a person that could lead, and she's been in the back. And I think that she should be empowered to be the leader. She's, she's powerful. She's young. She's able to get the energy, but she, we've not seen her strong, and I don't know why the Biden administration didn't position her that way. It has to be her because there would be an outrage to skip over the Vice President, especially the first African-American and Asian female vice president.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But they've got to do a lot of work to get her in the seat so that we can see her powerful, and that should be the work that we should be doing, right now. Not holding together with patches Biden who is definitely battered, has not kept the promises that he made in bipartisanship, in getting the kind of work done that he said he was going to do the last time.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I don't know if you can say he hasn't kept a promise, 'cause he's had a 50/50 Senate, he's had - a long winning streak. But there's no question, \uc2 Jon Karl\uc2 , that this talk, this entire discussion drives the White House crazy.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, absolutely. And, and the message from the White House is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is running for re-election, period, barring some, you know, health scare, he is running. But you talk to Democrats privately, key Democrats on Capitol Hill, leaders in the party, and there is just trepidation about this. There is a sense that, among many of them, that he shouldn't run, and also, a sense that if he doesn't run, that the primary is wide open.\par \par \uc2 SPEAKER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it's not just \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' to take, and it could be a very messy Democratic primary...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, when, when she ran, George...\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Which we don't need.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...when she, when she ran for president, it was a roundly unimpressive campaign, at least with the voters, and so candidates matter. I go back to theme, candidates matter. She's got to earn it.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we will be watching. Thank you all very much. Terrific discussion.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Coming up, as \uc2 NASA\uc2  prepares for two major space launches, Gio Benitez takes a closer look at the future of international cooperation in space.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220810ei8700007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SENATOR MIKE ROUNDS GOP SENS. BLAST BILL OVER TAXES, ENERGY PROVISIONS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,965 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK WITH GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS\par \par \uc2 COONS, INFLATION REDUCTION ACT, BIDEN, 2024\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE PREPARES FINAL VOTE ON INFLATION REDUCTION ACT\par \par \uc2 ROUNDS, INFLATION REDUCTION ACT, ABORTION, 2024\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE PREPARES FINAL VOTE ON INFLATION REDUCTION ACT\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, a Republican view, Senator \uc2 Mike Rounds\uc2  of South Dakota. Senator, thank you for joining us this morning. We know, you've had a long night, as well. We just heard Senator Coons say that this bill is going to be a boon to consumers, a boon for the economy. Your response?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RECONCILIATION BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOP SENATORS BLAST BILL OVER TAXES, ENERGY PROVISIONS\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MIKE ROUNDS (REP) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 I think he was partially right. He should've said boondoggle, in this particular case. It's not going to do much to help inflation. We're still going to have a problem, there. And, you know, at the same time they're going to be collecting about, real close to $740 billion in new tax revenue over the next supposedly five to 10 years, but most certainly it's not going to help get us through a tight - a tight time in which we're worried about coming out of a recession. We've two quarters in a row with declining GDP.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MIKE ROUNDS (REP) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 This is not the time to raise taxes, and most certainly it's not the time in which we start adding additional government employees. We're talking 87,000 more \uc2 IRS\uc2  employees. I don't think very many American citizens are going to want to see that.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As you know, the Joint Tax Committee found that the bill will raise taxes or increase spending over the long term, and there's no tax increase for families under $400,000.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FMR TREASURY SECS: FAMILIES MAKING BELOW $400K WON'T SEE TAX HIKE\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MIKE ROUNDS (REP) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, actually would disagree with that, because it's all passed down. And what you're going to see are normal American families actually see the impact of what happens whenever you put new taxes on our economy. Now, they talk about it being on big corporations, but if big corporations raise prices, they do pass it all down. So, from our perspective, we will see those tax increases coming down the line, and Americans are going to feel it. The, the bottom line on this is, is that what they're trying to do is to take dollars and then redistribute it back out to the places that they think it should be done. In this case, they're looking at things that they want to do primarily on climate change. This is not the time to be experimenting in that area.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, there are going to be tax breaks for energy, as well. And as you know, that this, there will be a lowering of prescription drug prices for people on Medicare. Those on Medicare tend to vote.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MEDICARE COULD NEGOTIATE DRUGS PRICES UNDER NEW BILL\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MIKE ROUNDS (REP) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, in this particular case, well, they've done two things. First, they took, they're taking dollars out of Medicare that would otherwise go to normal prescription drug prices. They're putting it into subsidize Obamacare, and we understand that that's a product they want to continue to subsidize. They're going to take money from Medicare, put it into Obamacare, but at the same time, they're saying that they're going to tell the - the drug companies that you have to sell it for less to the federal government.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP: ABC THIS WEEK: EXCLUSIVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BILL RICHARDSON DISCUSSES US RUSSIAN PRISONER SWAP NEGOTIATIONS TO FREE BRITTNEY GRINER AND PAUL WHELAN\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MIKE ROUNDS (REP) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 But first of all, it doesn't start for four years, and second of all - once it does start, what do you think the drug companies are going to be doing when they start being dictated to? And, and by the way, this is not a negotiation. This is where federal government literally walks on in and says, if you want to provide any prescriptions here, we will tell you how much we will pay, period, end of story. And if you don't like it, there's a huge, huge tax burden that we will inflict upon you. This is not healthy. It's not good, and it will cause problems in the marketplace.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: US ADDS 528,000 JOBS IN JULY, EXCEEDING EXPECTATIONS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You talked about the possibility of a recession. We just saw that jobs report on Friday, 528,000 new jobs in July, 3.5 percent unemployment, that's a 50-year low.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MIKE ROUNDS (REP) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 There are, there are two parts that we want to remember. First of all, we know that our GDP has gone down the last two quarters, so let's all recognize that that's accepted by everybody. That is down. The second part is, is that while we're very happy to see the job growth, positive thing, remember that back in the 1970s and moving into the 1980s, we actually saw job growth because unless your wages are keeping up with inflation, payrolls actually go down in value. And so what you're actually seeing, we believe, might be a precursor to what's to come. You're going to find out larger companies such as \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , are already talking about starting to reduce the number of people that they're going to be employing.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MIKE ROUNDS (REP) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 But, in the meantime, if your wages, if you can save on wages, because as inflation goes up, the value of the wages is not as great as it was unless it's also going up at the same or greater rate, well, then you can afford to have people stay on the payroll and your payroll actually goes down compared to what the, the products that you're producing or that you're buying and moving through the market. So, this is, while it's good and we want to see job growth, I don't think we can necessarily say that that is not a precursor yet to probably some more serious economic issues coming very shortly.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MIKE ROUNDS (REP) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 We hope that we're building our way out of a recession, but you don't do that by raising taxes. You do it by promoting and expanding businesses, getting the economy rolling again.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KS VOTERS UPHOLD ABORTION RIGHTS IN STATE CONSTITUTION\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's talk about the issue of abortion rights. We saw that vote in Kansas this week where voters rejected an attempt to amend the Constitution in a way that would've undermined abortion rights. I know there's now a move to put a constitutional amendment on the ballot in South Dakota to protect abortion rights. When you look at what happened in Kansas, do you think a ballot measure like that could pass in South Dakota?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MIKE ROUNDS (REP) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 I think this is one that, it's going back to where it should go, which is back to the people and back to the states. I like the idea of having people actually debate and discuss the issue. In South Dakota, we had a trigger law, which we actually signed when I was governor, and it said that, that basically abortion was going to be illegal unless it was to save the life of the mother. Now, I think the legislature can come back in. It's now been 17 years since that trigger law was put in effect, and they'll be able to discuss it, debate it, take public testimony and come back what they think is the best if they want a different alternative.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IS KANSAS ABORTION VOTE BELLWETHER FOR NOV OUTCOMES?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MIKE ROUNDS (REP) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 At the same time, in South Dakota, like other states, if the legislature does not do something that finds consensus, and there's always the possibility of a referendum, or a, a referred measure, or an initiated measure. So, it is back where it should be, and, and that is, is it's back to the states, and then we'll let the people and the legislative bodies decide exactly what they think is, you know, the long-term approach. Me personally, I am pro-life. I, I believe that abortion is wrong, but this is an issue that the legislative body should take on. They should debate it, and they should decide what will be the test of time in each state.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP CONTINUES TO TEASE 2024 PRESIDENTIAL BID\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Trump signaled again last night at the CPAC conference that he's looking at a run for president in 2024. Your colleague, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina, seems to be encouraging him to run. Are you going to join that effort?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MIKE ROUNDS (REP) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, on this particular case, I'm going to focus on the 2022 election. We've got to win that back, but I'll hold my - I'll keep my powder dry for the 2024 run. Let's see who else is coming up.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You want someone else?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MIKE ROUNDS (REP) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 I most certainly think it's going to be a wide-open field. You know, the one thing in the - in, in the Republican Party, we're going to be talking about the ideals, the principles moving forward. Democrats right now would love to see President Trump announce before the 2022, and I think the reason why is because they'd like to have that - draw attention away from the 2022 election and the candidates that they've got. And the other thing here is, is that right now, with President Biden as far underwater as what he is, their principles, their issues, the fact that inflation is at over nine percent, GDP is down, I think this is a good time for them to try to be looking at other things to talk about. And they're not going to want to talk about the economy, right now.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Am I, am I right to conclude from that that whatever President Trump decides, you want him to wait until after the midterms to announce?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MIKE ROUNDS (REP) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 Most certainly, I think that would be good because I think the Democrats would like to have him draw attention away from the 2022. We have to have a good, strong showing in the 2022. I feel very strongly about that, because we've got to be able to take back the House and would love to take back the Senate as well, and then we'll have divided government, but at least we'll be able to slow down some of these rather radical ideas that they're putting out, right now.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Senator Rounds, thanks for your time this morning.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MIKE ROUNDS (REP) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Coming up, Russia's signaling it's ready to discuss a prisoner swap after sentencing Brittney Griner to nine years in a penal colony. Negotiator Bill Richardson joins us, live.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220810ei8700003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEW SHOW "THIS FOOL" MICHAEL IMPERIOLI LIVE!\b0\par\par\uc2 1,213 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING OVERNIGHT: STRANDED IN DEATH VALLEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STRANDED IN DEATH VALLEY NATIONAL PARK\par \par \uc2 DEATH VALLEY, RAINFALL, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 FLIGHTS, CANCELED, DELAYED, WEATHER\par \par \uc2 SENATE, VOTE, BILL, CLIMATE CHANGE, HEALTH, TAXES\par \par \uc2 HIRING, RECESSION, JOBS\par \par \uc2 MONKEYPOX, OUTBREAK, DAYCARE, SEATON, SUTTON\par \par \uc2 ISRAELI, GAZA, PALESTINIAN, MILITANTS, STRIKES\par \par \uc2 NUCLEAR, POWER PLANT, UKRAINE, KOTIN\par \par \uc2 US, CHINA, TENSIONS, POLICY, TAIWAN\par \par \uc2 MINERS, TRAPPED, SCUBA DIVERS, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 HOUSE, FIRE, BAKER\par \par \uc2 CRASH, CAR, LINTON\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP: "THE DEVIL WEARS PRADA"\par \par \uc2 HECHE, CAR, CRASH, HOME\par \par \uc2 JONES, HOAX, PUNITIVE, LEWIS\par \par \uc2 E-BIKE, LAWSUIT, PARENTS, DAUGHTER, STEINSAPIR, RAD\par \par \uc2 KARDASHIAN, DAVIDSON\par \par \uc2 CORGIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 MONKEYPOX, VACCINES, OUTBREAK\par \par \uc2 TREATMENT, BREAST CANCER, FUNK, ENHERTU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 FDA\uc2  APPROVES BREAST CANCER DRUG\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRST TARGETED THERAPY FOR HER-2-LOW BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 "BATGIRL"\par \par \uc2 ABI-HASSAN, DOGS, RESCUE, BEAGLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOG DAYS OF SUMMER\par \par \uc2 PEACHSKINSHEETS, JILL & ALLY, BUTTER LONDON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALS & STEALS WITH TORY JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 "THE DEVIL WEARS PRADA", DUTRA, MUSTARD DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POP NEWS\par \par \uc2 IMPERIOLI, "THIS FOOL"\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM TV SHOW, "THE SOPRANOS"\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL IMPERIOLI ("THIS FOOL")\par \par \uc2 I'm not going to pay.\par \par \uc2 ACTOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Oh, yeah?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL IMPERIOLI ("THIS FOOL")\par \par \uc2 I'm serious.\par \par \uc2 ACTOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 No, serious is what happens if you don't pay by my hand.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL IMPERIOLI ("THIS FOOL")\par \par \uc2 In my thoughts, I use a technique of positive visualization. How come I always feel undermined? By what I did on the Triboro situation alone, I should have been made right then, boom.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are back now on "GMA" and that scene from arguably one of the greatest TV shows of all time. That, of course, "The Sopranos."\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And one of its stars, Michael Imperioli, hasn't slowed down since that show came to end in that diner.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MICHAEL IMPERIOLI LIVE\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He's checking in this fall for season two of "The White Lotus," and now is playing a minister in South Central on his new Hulu show, "This Fool." Michael, welcome to "GMA."\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL IMPERIOLI ("THIS FOOL")\par \par \uc2 Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's crazy, it's been 15 years since "The Sopranos'" finale. The series found this whole new audience during the pandemic. Why do you think it resonates with people so much?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL IMPERIOLI ("THIS FOOL")\par \par \uc2 I think, number one, it's really a well-done show, really well-written and well-acted and great filmmaking. And I think there's something about the loyalty of these characters and the kind of familial feeling that people find appealing somehow like this group that's unto themselves and kind of looks out for each other until they don't. And then, but I guess there's a lot of humor and somehow we've made these, you know, really dark people relatable, you know.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL IMPERIOLI ("THIS FOOL")\par \par \uc2 These people who live in this dark world and made it somehow relatable.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I want to know what was it like for you just that little clip?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL IMPERIOLI ("THIS FOOL")\par \par \uc2 Oh, it's hard, you know. You know, Jim was one of my best friends, you know, and he's not with us and it's very bittersweet seeing it. But I'm very proud of what we did together.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL IMPERIOLI ("THIS FOOL")\par \par \uc2 You know, and I miss him a lot and those are some of the best days, you know, in my career. So, I'm proud to be, have been a part of it.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Do you think we've seen the last of Christopher?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL IMPERIOLI ("THIS FOOL")\par \par \uc2 I would imagine.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL IMPERIOLI ("THIS FOOL")\par \par \uc2 I think so. I think so.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, let's fast forward. Talk about your new show here, "This Fool." It's a comedy about a minister working in South Central Los Angeles, a working-class neighborhood. Let's take a look at a clip and we'll talk about it on the backside.\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM TV SERIES, "THIS FOOL"\par \par \uc2 ACTRESS (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 You know what, I'm partially to blame. This is what I get for messing with someone shorter than me, short guys are (censored by network) evil.\par \par \uc2 FRANKIE QUINONES ("THIS FOOL")\par \par \uc2 5'6" is an average height.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL IMPERIOLI ("THIS FOOL")\par \par \uc2 You're 5'5" at best, now go back to your bush.\par \par \uc2 FRANKIE QUINONES ("THIS FOOL")\par \par \uc2 Good bye, whatever.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, you call yourself a Robin Hood of sorts. Tell us about the show and your character.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL IMPERIOLI ("THIS FOOL")\par \par \uc2 Well, it's about Frankie Quinones who's in the scene with me. He plays Luis, and he's an ex-con, ex-gang member who comes out of prison and his cousin played by Chris Estrada who created the show. He works with me at Hugs Not Thugs, which is a, it's an organization that rehabilitates ex-gang members, gives them work and we make cupcakes, really good ones. And so, I'm trying to, you know, I really believe in this cause. Minister Payne is kind of like a social justice warrior. He is all in and he will lie, he will steal, he will beg, he will cheat to keep this program going. It's not for himself. He'll live, you know, in a van if he has to, but he believes in this cause and he will keep it going by any means necessary, often very dishonest means.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Fascinating. Well, we can't let you leave without talking about "The White Lotus." The Emmy-nominated fan favorite in the second season. You're taking a trip to Sicily. We're in for a trip?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL IMPERIOLI ("THIS FOOL")\par \par \uc2 We were in Sicily for four months, just finished a couple weeks ago. And I play, I play a man who's traveling with his elderly father and his son and we're Italian Americans and we're going back to our homeland, our ancestral homeland. And it comes out in October.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And after four months in Sicily, you ate real good, didn't you?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL IMPERIOLI ("THIS FOOL")\par \par \uc2 Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, man. I bet that was incredible.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL IMPERIOLI ("THIS FOOL")\par \par \uc2 No complaints.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, season one, I loved season one of "The White Lotus." So, I'm really curious to see what you guys do in season two.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL IMPERIOLI ("THIS FOOL")\par \par \uc2 Yeah, if you liked season one, I think you're in for a treat.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Excellent.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, you heard it here first and "This Fool" premieres August 12th on Hulu. Still coming up here, thank you so much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL IMPERIOLI ("THIS FOOL")\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Treat yourself to more "Deals & Steals," and we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220823ei860000t\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 POP NEWS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,181 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING OVERNIGHT: STRANDED IN DEATH VALLEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STRANDED IN DEATH VALLEY NATIONAL PARK\par \par \uc2 DEATH VALLEY, RAINFALL, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 FLIGHTS, CANCELED, DELAYED, WEATHER\par \par \uc2 SENATE, VOTE, BILL, CLIMATE CHANGE, HEALTH, TAXES\par \par \uc2 HIRING, RECESSION, JOBS\par \par \uc2 MONKEYPOX, OUTBREAK, DAYCARE, SEATON, SUTTON\par \par \uc2 ISRAELI, GAZA, PALESTINIAN, MILITANTS, STRIKES\par \par \uc2 NUCLEAR, POWER PLANT, UKRAINE, KOTIN\par \par \uc2 US, CHINA, TENSIONS, POLICY, TAIWAN\par \par \uc2 MINERS, TRAPPED, SCUBA DIVERS, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 HOUSE, FIRE, BAKER\par \par \uc2 CRASH, CAR, LINTON\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP: "THE DEVIL WEARS PRADA"\par \par \uc2 HECHE, CAR, CRASH, HOME\par \par \uc2 JONES, HOAX, PUNITIVE, LEWIS\par \par \uc2 E-BIKE, LAWSUIT, PARENTS, DAUGHTER, STEINSAPIR, RAD\par \par \uc2 KARDASHIAN, DAVIDSON\par \par \uc2 CORGIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 MONKEYPOX, VACCINES, OUTBREAK\par \par \uc2 TREATMENT, BREAST CANCER, FUNK, ENHERTU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FDA APPROVES BREAST CANCER DRUG\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRST TARGETED THERAPY FOR HER-2-LOW BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 "BATGIRL"\par \par \uc2 ABI-HASSAN, DOGS, RESCUE, BEAGLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOG DAYS OF SUMMER\par \par \uc2 PEACHSKINSHEETS, JILL & ALLY, BUTTER LONDON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALS & STEALS WITH TORY JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 "THE DEVIL WEARS PRADA", DUTRA, MUSTARD DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POP NEWS\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're trying to be positive and grateful this morning. Mustard donuts are causing problems. Let's check in with Will Ganss again, back, back, back for the third time with "Pop News."\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back, back, back.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, I'm going to start with a Miranda Priestley quote, okay.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) "Florals for spring, groundbreaking." Will Ganss for "Pop News," groundbreaking. All right, everyone, gird your loins, "The Devil Wears Prada" is coming to Broadway.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "THE DEVIL WEARS PRADA: THE MUSICAL" HITS STAGE\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The musical version of the 2006 film holding its world premiere in Chicago and we're getting a first look at the all-new stage version. And can you feel the love tonight because the musical score is written by Sir Elton John?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Wow.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The music legend himself receiving a standing ovation while attending the first preview there in Chicago. The show's official opening night is tomorrow. And after Miranda Priestley, Andy Sacks and Emily wrap-up in Chicago, "The Devil Wears Prada: The Musical" will make its way to Broadway. And as Miranda herself would say, that's all.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's all.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think, we need to get a group outing to go see that.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm in.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Say less. I am excited for that. I'll get to your people and put it in the calendar. Okay, next to a "Pop News" update.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHICAGO WEATHERMAN GOES VIRAL FOR SWEET REACTION\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's the viral video on the internet that I could not get enough of this week. ABC 7 Chicago meteorologist and our friend Greg Dutra truly learning on the job. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 GREG DUTRA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, guys. Our winds will change. Right now kind of hung up just a little bit. Oh, I moved the map. I don't know, I can do that? No way.\par \par \uc2 NEWS ANCHOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Did you just discover that?\par \par \uc2 NEWS ANCHOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I've got to try it. Hold on, hold on, hold on.\par \par \uc2 GREG DUTRA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Can you believe it? Just touch it. You can go anywhere you want.\par \par \uc2 NEWS ANCHOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 That is so cool.\par \par \uc2 GREG DUTRA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I didn't know it. Our winds will change. Right now, kind of hung up just a little bit, I moved the map. I can do that?\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Greg sending us this message about his international internet infamy. Take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 GREG DUTRA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, guys, it's your weekend weather relief pitcher. I just want to say, honestly, I'm glad the video brought people joy. Honestly, the reaction from around the globe is overwhelming. And even if it's at my expense, it's nice that everybody can share a laugh about something and all agree that I probably should have known that it was a touch screen.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know what I love? His wife posted on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  that even though he's an international viral sensation, he was still flying in coach.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's how he rolls.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (Inaudible) laughing.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) My favorite comment that I saw on that was that he has golden retriever energy. So sweet. Love it.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He is the best.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Greg always on the go filling in for Ginger this Monday. Let's see what he discovers while he's in Times Square. And finally, I know you've been waiting for this moment. It's National Mustard Day.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONAL MUSTARD DAY DOUGHNUTS!\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And what pairs well with mustard? You guessed it. Donuts?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Seriously?\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) French's mustard teaming up with a New York bakery that would be Dough Donuts with this condiment creation comes with a yellow mustard icing that matches Janai's power suit. So, let's get into it.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We could smell this, too.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wait, you don't even like mustard.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, I am not a mustard person. But if I brought it, I better try it. Right?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes. Okay, how do you?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Whit, you didn't say come here, you.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean...\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) See, see, see?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Will doesn't like it.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I've got to be honest, it actually tastes better than it smells.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Really?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. So, it hits you hot going in, but the taste not bad.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Dough Donuts is actually really good.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Will is trying so hard. See, we're not...\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You're going to be all right, Will.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I don't know if I am.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I feel bad for you.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's really important they did this together.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We should say, Dough Donuts is giving away free mustard donuts at their New York City location. Their donuts are good. Maybe don't get the mustard ones.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You took another bite?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Coming up, Michael Imperioli is live on "GMA." We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He's still eating it.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STORY SYNOPSES\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220823ei860000s\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 "BATGIRL" FALLOUT DOES SHELVED MOVIE SIGNAL TROUBLE FOR STREAMING SERVICES?\b0\par\par\uc2 682 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING OVERNIGHT: STRANDED IN DEATH VALLEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STRANDED IN DEATH VALLEY NATIONAL PARK\par \par \uc2 DEATH VALLEY, RAINFALL, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 FLIGHTS, CANCELED, DELAYED, WEATHER\par \par \uc2 SENATE, VOTE, BILL, CLIMATE CHANGE, HEALTH, TAXES\par \par \uc2 HIRING, RECESSION, JOBS\par \par \uc2 MONKEYPOX, OUTBREAK, DAYCARE, SEATON, SUTTON\par \par \uc2 ISRAELI, GAZA, PALESTINIAN, MILITANTS, STRIKES\par \par \uc2 NUCLEAR, POWER PLANT, UKRAINE, KOTIN\par \par \uc2 US, CHINA, TENSIONS, POLICY, TAIWAN\par \par \uc2 MINERS, TRAPPED, SCUBA DIVERS, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 HOUSE, FIRE, BAKER\par \par \uc2 CRASH, CAR, LINTON\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP: "THE DEVIL WEARS PRADA"\par \par \uc2 HECHE, CAR, CRASH, HOME\par \par \uc2 JONES, HOAX, PUNITIVE, LEWIS\par \par \uc2 E-BIKE, LAWSUIT, PARENTS, DAUGHTER, STEINSAPIR, RAD\par \par \uc2 KARDASHIAN, DAVIDSON\par \par \uc2 CORGIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 MONKEYPOX, VACCINES, OUTBREAK\par \par \uc2 TREATMENT, BREAST CANCER, FUNK, ENHERTU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 FDA\uc2  APPROVES BREAST CANCER DRUG\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRST TARGETED THERAPY FOR HER-2-LOW BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 "BATGIRL"\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, now to our "GMA Cover Story" and the fallout over an upcoming "Batgirl" movie being scrapped. Here with more is Will Ganss once again.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "BATGIRL" FALLOUT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOES SHELVED MOVIE SIGNAL TROUBLE FOR STREAMING SERVICES?\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back in the club, out of the club, no. Comic book fans in a frenzy this morning following news that HBO Max has decided "Batgirl" was a $90 million mistake. But even if you're not a Barbara Gordon superfan, this decision might have lasting repercussions for your favorite streaming services, too.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, "Batgirl" defeated.\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MOVIE, "THE DARK KNIGHT"\par \par \uc2 HEATH LEDGER (ACTOR)\par \par \uc2 Why so serious?\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The bombshell decision to cancel the film's release putting a spotlight on a coming merger between streaming giants HBO Max and Discovery Plus into a single streaming platform. This as streaming services experience massive subscriber nosedives. \uc2 Netflix\uc2  says it lost nearly a million subscribers in its last earnings report. Those losses raising questions about whether films like "Batgirl" can turn a profit on the big screen or on streaming.\par \par \uc2 KELLEY CARTER (SENIOR ENTERTAINMENT REPORTER \uc2 ESPN\uc2 'S ANDSCAPE)\par \par \uc2 I don't think that any company is healthy enough to want to flush that much money, you know, down the drain. $90 million is significant. That project was significant.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) "Batgirl" had a $90 million budget and would have seen Michael Keaton back as the caped crusader on HBO Max. Execs there saying the installation into the Batman universe simply did not work. Fans freaking out.\par \par \uc2 FAN (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I feel like throwing away a movie you've already pretty much made was the dumbest decision possible.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) "Batgirl" the latest in a series of big-budget cancellations at HBO Max, including the $40 million animated "Scoob: Holiday Haunt."\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MOVIE\par \par \uc2 CARTOON VOICEOVER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Zoinks.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) \uc2 Warner Brothers\uc2  saying in a statement, "The decision to not release "Batgirl" reflects our leadership's strategic shift as it relates to the DC universe and HBO Max." Meanwhile, six more \uc2 Warner Brothers\uc2  movies quietly removed, sparking fears that there's more downsizing to come.\par \par \uc2 KELLEY CARTER (SENIOR ENTERTAINMENT REPORTER \uc2 ESPN\uc2 'S ANDSCAPE)\par \par \uc2 I think it's just going to kind of come down to what consumers want, what's the market actually saying. And I think right now, the market is probably saying not everybody needs a streaming app.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The drastic measures leaving a bleak outlook for the streaming industry overall. More beat up than ever.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That massive merger between \uc2 Warner Brothers\uc2  and Discovery is expected to be effective summer of 2023. Meanwhile, "Batgirl" herself, Leslie Grace, weighing in on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  saying, "To every Batgirl fan, thank you for allowing me to take on the cape and become my own hero. Batgirl for life."\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's crazy.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's crazy. A $90 million mistake.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the fact that the movie was pretty much almost done, yeah.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Whit?\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220823ei860000o\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 IN THE CLUB HOW NFT'S ARE HEATING UP NIGHTLIFE\b0\par\par\uc2 997 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING OVERNIGHT: STRANDED IN DEATH VALLEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STRANDED IN DEATH VALLEY NATIONAL PARK\par \par \uc2 DEATH VALLEY, RAINFALL, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 FLIGHTS, CANCELED, DELAYED, WEATHER\par \par \uc2 SENATE, VOTE, BILL, CLIMATE CHANGE, HEALTH, TAXES\par \par \uc2 HIRING, RECESSION, JOBS\par \par \uc2 MONKEYPOX, OUTBREAK, DAYCARE, SEATON, SUTTON\par \par \uc2 ISRAELI, GAZA, PALESTINIAN, MILITANTS, STRIKES\par \par \uc2 NUCLEAR, POWER PLANT, UKRAINE, KOTIN\par \par \uc2 US, CHINA, TENSIONS, POLICY, TAIWAN\par \par \uc2 MINERS, TRAPPED, SCUBA DIVERS, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 HOUSE, FIRE, BAKER\par \par \uc2 CRASH, CAR, LINTON\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP: "THE DEVIL WEARS PRADA"\par \par \uc2 HECHE, CAR, CRASH, HOME\par \par \uc2 JONES, HOAX, PUNITIVE, LEWIS\par \par \uc2 E-BIKE, LAWSUIT, PARENTS, DAUGHTER, STEINSAPIR, RAD\par \par \uc2 KARDASHIAN, DAVIDSON\par \par \uc2 CORGIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 MONKEYPOX, VACCINES, OUTBREAK\par \par \uc2 TREATMENT, BREAST CANCER, FUNK, ENHERTU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 FDA\uc2  APPROVES BREAST CANCER DRUG\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRST TARGETED THERAPY FOR HER-2-LOW BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 "BATGIRL"\par \par \uc2 ABI-HASSAN, DOGS, RESCUE, BEAGLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOG DAYS OF SUMMER\par \par \uc2 PEACHSKINSHEETS, JILL & ALLY, BUTTER LONDON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALS & STEALS WITH TORY JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 "THE DEVIL WEARS PRADA", DUTRA, MUSTARD DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POP NEWS\par \par \uc2 IMPERIOLI, "THIS FOOL"\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM TV SHOW, "THE SOPRANOS"\par \par \uc2 GIR, THE MIGHTY SOUND, FAHLO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALS & STEALS WITH TORY JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 CRYPTOCURRENCY, NFT, GRYSZOWKA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IN THE CLUB\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW NFT'S ARE HEATING UP NIGHTLIFE\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Go shorty. I hope you all heard Whit mention "Bourbons and Lacs" earlier. So, we're going to stick with that theme.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) "Bourbons and Lacs." It's a Master P. song. We've got 50 Cent, we've got them all going this morning.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the metaverse and the club. Economic struggles hitting crypto's bottom line. There's a new push to turn NFT's into a VIP experience. Get ready to hit the club. Reena Roy has all the details. We are trying to wrap our minds around what exactly this means.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, honestly, I still, am too. You know, it's a lot to unpack. We tried to keep it simple this morning. So, let start with this. Right now, it is no secret that the NFT market is not doing so hot, but some A-list celebrities and trendy businesses are trying to change that in hopes of escaping this so-called NFT winter and drawing in more investors.\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MUSIC VIDEO, "SANDBOX"\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) From Snoop to Steph Curry.\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM ADVERTISEMENT\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL VOICEOVER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 The world's leading expert on cryptocurrency.\par \par \uc2 STEPH CURRY (\uc2 GOLDEN STATE WARRIORS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 I'm not.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A-listers are cashing in on the crypto craze selling NFTs or non-fungible tokens and hosting exclusive virtual parties and offering meet and greets for their NFT holders. NFTs are unique digital assets bought and sold in cryptocurrency.\par \par \uc2 EXPERT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Think of an NFT like a painting. It's unique. It's that painting.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But in recent months, crypto has seen a crash as the US economy hits rocky territory, bringing on what experts are calling an NFT winter.\par \par \uc2 EXPERT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 You saw them sort of peak in popularity and then come down. All of these high-risk plays that investors had their money in kind of tumbled first.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Still, some are banking on a comeback as trendy bars and nightclubs create experience-driven NFTs.\par \par \uc2 BUSINESS OWNER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Our NFT is really focused on providing experiences, not things, to people and we just wanted to evolve with technology.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And here at this New York City nightclub, NFT holders can get VIP access to exclusive events.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Just show proof of an NFT purchase from the nightclub's website and you're in.\par \par \uc2 BUSINESS OWNER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 So, this is area is exclusively reserved for our NFT holders. Y'all have grown up with reward membership points, right? The only difference here is they're tied to an NFT. And the largest difference is it's something you can trade.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Nathan Gryszowka doesn't know much about the world of crypto, but says the idea of a membership program with perks piqued his interest.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN GRYSZOWKA (NFT HOLDER)\par \par \uc2 I love coming here whenever I can. So, having that access is one thing, but then actually, you know, just learning a bit throughout the process of how NFTs work.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Though NFTs are risky business, experts say they could be here to stay.\par \par \uc2 EXPERT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Markets happen in cycles and we predict that the NFT will happen in a cycle as well.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And experts say, if you are interested in investing in NFTs, make sure to do your research first to understand the world of cryptocurrency overall and start small with modest sums of money, so that it's not a huge loss if things don't work out as planned. And, guys, that's kind of my advice to directly to all of us.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Apparently, we're still trying to figure it out.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So...\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And definitely. If you guys test it out, test it out small, something that you can afford to lose if things don't go well.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Reena, thank you so much. We appreciate that. Coming up here on "GMA," all eyes are on invisible eyeliner. How to pull off the...\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) You've been doing this forever.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Apparently, those of us, male anchors have been doing this for a long time. How you can pull off the latest trend that's taking over in TikTok. We'll be right back. Fashion forward.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220823ei860000w\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NOW YOU SEE IT HOW TO PULL OFF TIKTOK'S INVISIBLE EYELINER TREND\b0\par\par\uc2 1,324 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING OVERNIGHT: STRANDED IN DEATH VALLEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STRANDED IN DEATH VALLEY NATIONAL PARK\par \par \uc2 DEATH VALLEY, RAINFALL, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 FLIGHTS, CANCELED, DELAYED, WEATHER\par \par \uc2 SENATE, VOTE, BILL, CLIMATE CHANGE, HEALTH, TAXES\par \par \uc2 HIRING, RECESSION, JOBS\par \par \uc2 MONKEYPOX, OUTBREAK, DAYCARE, SEATON, SUTTON\par \par \uc2 ISRAELI, GAZA, PALESTINIAN, MILITANTS, STRIKES\par \par \uc2 NUCLEAR, POWER PLANT, UKRAINE, KOTIN\par \par \uc2 US, CHINA, TENSIONS, POLICY, TAIWAN\par \par \uc2 MINERS, TRAPPED, SCUBA DIVERS, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 HOUSE, FIRE, BAKER\par \par \uc2 CRASH, CAR, LINTON\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP: "THE DEVIL WEARS PRADA"\par \par \uc2 HECHE, CAR, CRASH, HOME\par \par \uc2 JONES, HOAX, PUNITIVE, LEWIS\par \par \uc2 E-BIKE, LAWSUIT, PARENTS, DAUGHTER, STEINSAPIR, RAD\par \par \uc2 KARDASHIAN, DAVIDSON\par \par \uc2 CORGIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 MONKEYPOX, VACCINES, OUTBREAK\par \par \uc2 TREATMENT, BREAST CANCER, FUNK, ENHERTU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FDA APPROVES BREAST CANCER DRUG\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRST TARGETED THERAPY FOR HER-2-LOW BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 "BATGIRL"\par \par \uc2 ABI-HASSAN, DOGS, RESCUE, BEAGLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOG DAYS OF SUMMER\par \par \uc2 PEACHSKINSHEETS, JILL & ALLY, BUTTER LONDON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALS & STEALS WITH TORY JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 "THE DEVIL WEARS PRADA", DUTRA, MUSTARD DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POP NEWS\par \par \uc2 IMPERIOLI, "THIS FOOL"\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM TV SHOW, "THE SOPRANOS"\par \par \uc2 GIR, THE MIGHTY SOUND, FAHLO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALS & STEALS WITH TORY JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 CRYPTOCURRENCY, NFT, GRYSZOWKA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IN THE CLUB\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW NFT'S ARE HEATING UP NIGHTLIFE\par \par \uc2 INVISIBLE EYELINER, SCIBELLI, TREND\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I was just laughing because it looked like a crayon box. Welcome back. And we're going glam with a minimalist makeup trend that's taking TikTok by storm.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NOW YOU SEE IT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW TO PULL OFF TIKTOK'S INVISIBLE EYELINER TREND\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Invisible eyeliner, an easy way to get that liner look without all the fuss. Celebrity makeup artist Neil Scibelli joins us to show you how it's done. And so, first question, what is invisible eyeliner?\par \par \uc2 NEIL SCIBELLI (CELEBRITY MAKEUP ARTIST)\par \par \uc2 So, this trend has been blowing up all over TikTok. It's a way to create an illusion that you're wearing an eyeliner without actually having to apply any at all. So, it actually uses concealer as a way to sort of stencil out your shape of what you would want your eyeliner to look like, but your natural eyelid color is actually what acts as the eyeliner.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WING OR NO WING STYLE IS UP TO YOU\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, so very natural kind of glam. This is Ariana, one of our incredible interns with us this summer. She has one of her eyes done. Show us how you do it.\par \par \uc2 NEIL SCIBELLI (CELEBRITY MAKEUP ARTIST)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, so you just need a few quick products. First, to start, you really want to use a flat angled eye brush.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: USE A FLAT-ANGLED EYELINER BRUSH\par \par \uc2 NEIL SCIBELLI (CELEBRITY MAKEUP ARTIST)\par \par \uc2 I love this one here by Real Techniques from their everyday eyewear set. It's really great for creating that lifted angled shape around the eye. And what you would use as a concealer, you want one a concealer that doesn't budge, that doesn't smudge, that's going to wear all day and you want to create this sort of cat eye shape around the eye. And I love this one here by Rosen because it literally stays on all day, it's super long wear and we've carved out sort of a stencil on where we would want her eyeliner to be. And it's just right in this area here. And you can go as thin or thick as you want it. It doesn't have to be a wing.\par \par \uc2 NEIL SCIBELLI (CELEBRITY MAKEUP ARTIST)\par \par \uc2 And then you can use another brush, like sort of like a fluffier shadow brush and blend right above it just to highlight that entire area. So, it really opens up the eye and gives this lifted look while your natural eye color sort of just peeks, your lid color just peeks right from, you know, the lower part of the lid.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: USE A SMUDGE PROOF LONG WEAR CONCEALER\par \par \uc2 NEIL SCIBELLI (CELEBRITY MAKEUP ARTIST)\par \par \uc2 And to make it really pop, I always love traveling with pointed Q-tips. So, if you use Garnier's micellar water right on it, it actually really cleans up this look and sort of brings out that natural liner as well.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it doesn't have to be perfect? Because it's kind of that shadow.\par \par \uc2 NEIL SCIBELLI (CELEBRITY MAKEUP ARTIST)\par \par \uc2 Exactly.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Next up, we have Courtney.\par \par \uc2 NEIL SCIBELLI (CELEBRITY MAKEUP ARTIST)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Courtney writes our trivia questions, in case you were wondering.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How are you going to level up her look?\par \par \uc2 NEIL SCIBELLI (CELEBRITY MAKEUP ARTIST)\par \par \uc2 So, for those who do like a little bit more of a pop of color, we actually went with an eye shadow below the concealer here and we used this one by Makeup by Mario. It's a great just neutral brown.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I love this palette.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LEVEL UP LOOK WITH NEUTRAL EYESHADOW\par \par \uc2 NEIL SCIBELLI (CELEBRITY MAKEUP ARTIST)\par \par \uc2 Right? You can use it right along the lid, and then you want to use your concealer technique right around it in the same way that we did the first. And this is a great way for someone to go from day to night or if you're looking for more definition around the eyes without it being too much.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Also, if you're not good at drawing an eyeliner.\par \par \uc2 NEIL SCIBELLI (CELEBRITY MAKEUP ARTIST)\par \par \uc2 Exactly. If you're not good at drawing a liner, doing the shadow first actually just makes it easier to sort of just place the liner around the eye. And then this concealer gives a really lifted look to the eyes, as well.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I like the whole thing that we're doing with concealer to give you that face-lift look.\par \par \uc2 NEIL SCIBELLI (CELEBRITY MAKEUP ARTIST)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, it's actually it's a technique makeup artists have been doing for a while, too. Once an eye look is done, you can always use a little concealer right on the edges of the eyes to give it that more sort of lifted finished look.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the best part about this is it takes very few things.\par \par \uc2 NEIL SCIBELLI (CELEBRITY MAKEUP ARTIST)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And easy to do which a lot of makeup can be difficult. So, lastly, we have got Kayla here, another incredible intern. If you want something a little different, you've got an idea for a look that's minimalist in a minute.\par \par \uc2 NEIL SCIBELLI (CELEBRITY MAKEUP ARTIST)\par \par \uc2 Yes, I always suggest eye shadow crayons when you're in a pinch or if you are not really an advanced makeup artist. I love these by Charlotte Tilbury, it's the color chameleon eye shadow pencils. And eye shadow pencils or crayons are really a great way to get a quick eye look because you can use them as a liner, you can use them as an eye shadow. They're also super blendable with your fingers. They're sort of no brush required. You can use them...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SMUDGE EYESHADOW CRAYON WITH FINGERS\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Love these easy looks.\par \par \uc2 NEIL SCIBELLI (CELEBRITY MAKEUP ARTIST)\par \par \uc2 Right?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) These are amazing.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're almost out of time. Thank you so much for all of this.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FINISH THE LOOK WITH MASCARA\par \par \uc2 NEIL SCIBELLI (CELEBRITY MAKEUP ARTIST)\par \par \uc2 You're so welcome. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you for watching. We'll see you back tomorrow with mustard donuts.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, bagels.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220823ei860000x\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 DEALS & STEALS TREAT YOURSELF\b0\par\par\uc2 1,083 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING OVERNIGHT: STRANDED IN DEATH VALLEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STRANDED IN DEATH VALLEY NATIONAL PARK\par \par \uc2 DEATH VALLEY, RAINFALL, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 FLIGHTS, CANCELED, DELAYED, WEATHER\par \par \uc2 SENATE, VOTE, BILL, CLIMATE CHANGE, HEALTH, TAXES\par \par \uc2 HIRING, RECESSION, JOBS\par \par \uc2 MONKEYPOX, OUTBREAK, DAYCARE, SEATON, SUTTON\par \par \uc2 ISRAELI, GAZA, PALESTINIAN, MILITANTS, STRIKES\par \par \uc2 NUCLEAR, POWER PLANT, UKRAINE, KOTIN\par \par \uc2 US, CHINA, TENSIONS, POLICY, TAIWAN\par \par \uc2 MINERS, TRAPPED, SCUBA DIVERS, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 HOUSE, FIRE, BAKER\par \par \uc2 CRASH, CAR, LINTON\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP: "THE DEVIL WEARS PRADA"\par \par \uc2 HECHE, CAR, CRASH, HOME\par \par \uc2 JONES, HOAX, PUNITIVE, LEWIS\par \par \uc2 E-BIKE, LAWSUIT, PARENTS, DAUGHTER, STEINSAPIR, R