S)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's go to Ginger in Dallas.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Please.\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, thank you all so much. This is Okoso, five other dogs that are about to get adopted. I cannot wait to bring you all of that.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DROUGHT COMPARISON\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But we also have to talk about Dallas because hey, we're here. We're on day 61 of no rain in a row. That is their second-longest stretch, so more than two months, really tough to do. And look how the drought has exploded just in a year across the state of Texas. That's the big picture. Let's get a check now a little closer to home.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONAL MAP\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220901ei830003h\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PICNIC PERFECT COOKING WITH ALEX GUARNASCHELLI\b0\par\par\uc2 1,859 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS HELD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH STAKES PRIMARY SHOWDOWNS\par \par \uc2 VOTERS, KANSAS, ABORTION, TURNOUT\par \par \uc2 PRIMARIES, RESULTS, GOP, TRUMP, CONTENTIOUS\par \par \uc2 CIPOLLONE, DOJ, SUBPOENAED, EASTMAN, PROBE\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, TAIWAN, CHINA, TENSIONS, DEMOCRACY\par \par \uc2 ALERT, STATE DEPT, AL-ZAWAHIRI, LUTE, DRONE\par \par \uc2 KIRBY, ALERT, TALIBAN, DOHA, PELOSI, CHINA\par \par \uc2 SCULLY, DODGERS, HALL OF FAME, LEGACY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOT SPOTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 PUTIN, SANCTIONS, SALAMA, KABAEVA, CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 NFL, DOLPHINS, PENALIZED, BRADY, ROSS, PAYTON\par \par \uc2 CPSC\uc2 , RECALLED, T.J. MAXX, FINED, INFANT\par \par \uc2 PHILIP, PARACHUTE, TANGLED, FREEFALLING, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 BASKETBALL, BLUE COLLAR U, BUFFALO\par \par \uc2 STORY2\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, REFERENDUM, ELECTION, KANSAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KANSAS VOTES TO PROTECT ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTERS BLOCK AMENDMENT IN POST-ROE REFERENDUM\par \par \uc2 MONKEYPOX, CHILDREN, MEDICINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GROWING MONKEYPOX CONCERNS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIFTH CHILD IN U.S. TESTS POSITIVE FOR VIRUS\par \par \uc2 FLOOD, KENTUCKY, WEATHER, DESTRUCTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW THREAT TO KENTUCKY FLOOD ZONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SURVIVORS FACE DEVASTATION & DANGEROUS HEAT\par \par \uc2 LOVATO, SINGER, GENDER, IDENTITY, PRONOUNS, CRUZ\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMII LOVATO SPEAKS OUT ABOUT GENDER IDENTITY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHY SINGER ADDED 'SHE/HER' PRONOUNS\par \par \uc2 DISORDERED EATING, CHILDREN, HEALTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHILDREN & DISORDERED EATING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STUDY: KIDS AS YOUNG AS 9 ENGAGING IN BEHAVIOR\par \par \uc2 TECHNOLOGY, REFURBISHED, SAVINGS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BACK TO SCHOOL SAVINGS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BUYING REFURBISHED TECH TO CUT COSTS\par \par \uc2 STYLES, "ROAD HOUSE", NOSTALGIA\par \par \uc2 CMA, MUSIC, FESTIVAL, COUNTRY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BEHIND THE SCENES OF CMA FEST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE WORLD'S BIGGEST COUNTRY MUSIC EVENT RETURNS\par \par \uc2 STORY2\par \par \uc2 SERVICE DOG, BEST MAN, VETERAN, MORGAN\par \par \uc2 COOKING, GUARNASCHELLI, PICNIC, RECIPES\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, we're going through a lot of hand sanitizer because of the dog segment that we were having, but we, we're ready to go. We're ready to go because we have some great recipes for a perfect summer picnic, and we have the perfect person here to show us how to whip them up, chef Alex Guarnaschelli, whose show, "Alex vs America" just kicked off Season Number Two. Ooh, I love a cutlet.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 Yeah, I mean, who doesn't love a cutlet, right?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know, but you have kicked it up, of course, as always, to that little "Iron Chef" level, fried prosciutto and sage.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 Yes, so I've, I fried sage leaves and prosciutto. You know, this is like an "Alex vs America" type of trick I would throw out during the show. So you crisp those, and have that oil you fried all those luscious things in. So we season those up.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, let's get started here. How much of a seasoning do I put on it?\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 Go ahead. Get bold.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Get bold with it?\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 I like it. Go, baby, go. And then some dried oregano and then an egg. And here are two types of breadcrumbs, the finer breadcrumbs.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What does that add to the chicken, the two types of breadcrumbs?\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 Well these, the old-school kind of fine, toasty breadcrumbs give the great taste, and then the Panko breadcrumbs give the crunch, so half and half. That's a cool little tip if you want to kind of, and then we mix them right in here, and you smell that oregano and salt.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Already, already.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 Look at that expertly, I think I could take you as, like, a teammate in "Alex vs America" here.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It could be, I'm with you.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 If you're free. Are you free? I mean I know you, you're always, you're always here, but maybe you'll...\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He's got, he's got a few, yeah.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) For you I would come on, and we would burn together. We would win.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 I love it. So, these get fried in here where we fried everything else. They get all crispy.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Can I ask you? I'm sorry, how do you make sure? It's, everybody is so particular about how to make sure that the chicken is cooked correctly.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 And not overcooked. People write me, and they're like, "I'm afraid the chicken is not cooked, and now it's overcooked." You know what I do? I take it out, and I, honestly, I know this sounds odd, but I take them out when I think they're done, and I give, and I just touch them. It should feel somewhat firm to the touch, right, when they're not, oh my God, look at you. We could open a store right now.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, I'm ready for my own restaurant.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 You are really crushing the game. So, we come here for a little sandwich with a little mayo, to which I added just red wine vinegar. I love to add. You're still on the chicken. All right, well...\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm still on the chicken. Keep on going. I'm going to drop the cutlets here, and now we're done.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 This is mayo with a little acidity added, a little red wine vinegar stirred in, a little lettuce, and then we come here, and we go cutlet, and then we get that fried prosciutto instead of bacon, right. And the, and those sage leaves, and we close that up, and look at all those textures. And oh, wait, wait, wait, a little Parmesan cheese for that Italian touch.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Got to have that. But before we go to the next dish, we want to talk about "Alex vs America." Now, you take on three chefs.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 Yeah, and they come ready.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, how is the competition this season?\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 It's really tough. I mean, I was like, "Can I get some people that are, you know, like some straight out of culinary school so I can win some of this?" And it's just not that. And they're like, "You know what, when you throw the meat grinder, or you get, you burn something, we're just going, we're going to expose that other side of these competitions, that realness, right," because it's really real. There's so much pressure.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, it's a lot of pressure.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 And it can't be all buttoned up, you know.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And especially a lot of pressure on you.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 Massive. I come into the room and they're like, "Hey," and I'm like, "Ooh, I feel the chill in the air."\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, you're like the GOAT, and they're trying to take the GOAT down.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 Yeah, they are.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's not going to happen. That's not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 It's like an athletic, it's like, it's like a football game, right, or, you know, set up the Jell-O pit, and let's go.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's a competition.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So the macaroni salad that we have going here, how do you mix the pasta with the dressing that it, you know, can still hold on?\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 You know, that's a great, that's such a smart question. So Worcestershire, mustard, tobacco in the mayo, and would you, yeah, and...\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, I'll whisk that.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 I love that, he's so good.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, he loves, he's a good, he's a good sous chef.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 Now, when we make this mixture, we look at the cooked macaroni, we look at the mayor, and you can add a little water to loosen it, or you can leave it a little thick, toss it, and you can even add a little water to loosen the salad if it's too tight or nothing if it's clingy. You can kind of adjust, and then we have a little bit of onion, pepper and scallion, basil and dill. We mix.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It looks so good.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 Look, oh my God, you are really good.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Don't hurt yourself, Michael. Don't hurt yourself.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm working it.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 You are really good. You don't have to go to the gym. I'm telling you. So then...\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Triceps are tight right now.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 I see that, work that. So then you have this salad, and you can wrap these sandwiches in, you know, in paper for a picnic.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And just pour that in.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 Right on there, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You keep working. I'm going to eat. This is really good.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 And you can just mix, and you can kind of adjust, like I said. Don't be afraid to loosen with water or even a drop of salad dressing for a little acidity or lemon juice if you wanted to brighten it. And I make this a day in advance and let it sit in the fridge and hang out.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's a great idea.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 My daughter goes in with a spoon, I'm like, "Ava, I see where you made that, the little, the little valley in my macaroni salad."\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know how you can tell it's delicious? Because any time we usually make something, you only need, like, one or two examples to show that, how, you know, how to do it, our crew made sure they made enough for their lunch.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUARNASCHELLI ("ALEX VS. AMERICA")\par \par \uc2 You're right. That's a lot.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You guys aren't slick. We know what you're doing.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it's so tasty.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is really good.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Alex, thank you. Congratulations on season two. By the way, you can scan the QR code on the screen to get these recipes at goodmorningamerica.com.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You want to do that, trust me.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And new episodes, episodes of "Alex vs America" air Sundays on Food Network.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220901ei830003l\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 8:00 HALF HOUR\b0\par\par\uc2 512 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS HELD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH STAKES PRIMARY SHOWDOWNS\par \par \uc2 VOTERS, KANSAS, ABORTION, TURNOUT\par \par \uc2 PRIMARIES, RESULTS, GOP, TRUMP, CONTENTIOUS\par \par \uc2 CIPOLLONE, DOJ, SUBPOENAED, EASTMAN, PROBE\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, TAIWAN, CHINA, TENSIONS, DEMOCRACY\par \par \uc2 ALERT, STATE DEPT, AL-ZAWAHIRI, LUTE, DRONE\par \par \uc2 KIRBY, ALERT, TALIBAN, DOHA, PELOSI, CHINA\par \par \uc2 SCULLY, DODGERS, HALL OF FAME, LEGACY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOT SPOTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 PUTIN, SANCTIONS, SALAMA, KABAEVA, CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 NFL, DOLPHINS, PENALIZED, BRADY, ROSS, PAYTON\par \par \uc2 CPSC\uc2 , RECALLED, T.J. MAXX, FINED, INFANT\par \par \uc2 PHILIP, PARACHUTE, TANGLED, FREEFALLING, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 BASKETBALL, BLUE COLLAR U, BUFFALO\par \par \uc2 STORY2\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America, it's 8:00 am.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH-STAKES PRIMARIES\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Overnight, abortion rights upheld in Kansas. Voters reject changing the state's constitution that would remove protections. The first voters are weighing in on the issue after the Supreme Court ruling overturning Roe v. Wade as primaries in five states set up key showdowns in November's midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MONKEYPOX OUTBREAK\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Monkeypox outbreak, a fifth child now testing positive, Los Angeles the latest city to declare a health emergency. The latest on the growing concern.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHILDREN AND DISORDERED EATING\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Also this morning, a "GMA" health alert, children and disordered eating, a new report finding boys and girls as young as nine are suffering. Dr. Ashton is here with the warning signs parents should look for and how you can help.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMI LOVATO'S GENDER IDENTITY\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Demi Lovato talks more about her gender identity and why she's adopted she/her pronouns again after coming out as non-binary. The singer on her journey and why it's all about respect.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE VALUE OF REFURBISHED TECH\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Back to school savings, how refurbished tech can help cut costs. How much you can save and which products you should avoid.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOG DAYS OF SUMMER\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And "GMA's" dog days of summer is taking over Dallas. How one service dog gave this Air Force veteran a new lease on life, becoming his best friend and his best man. It's all ahead as we say...\par \par \uc2 VIEWERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Live in Times Square, this is "GMA."\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 That's a happy group out there.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, good morning, America. Happy group outside, fired up in Times Square. You know, they're really happy on a Wednesday hump day.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And you haven't even given us the traditional...\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Hump day.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 There you go. Now they're, now they're truly happy, Michael. And we're also, we are celebrating all dogs across America and the incredible ways they not only change our lives but, in some case, some cases, they actually save our lives. So Ginger is down in big D, in Dallas, and will have that for us.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 That is a sweet story.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220901ei8300039\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEW THREAT TO KENTUCKY FLOOD ZONE SURVIVORS FACE DEVASTATION & DANGEROUS HEAT\b0\par\par\uc2 617 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS HELD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH STAKES PRIMARY SHOWDOWNS\par \par \uc2 VOTERS, KANSAS, ABORTION, TURNOUT\par \par \uc2 PRIMARIES, RESULTS, GOP, TRUMP, CONTENTIOUS\par \par \uc2 CIPOLLONE, DOJ, SUBPOENAED, EASTMAN, PROBE\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, TAIWAN, CHINA, TENSIONS, DEMOCRACY\par \par \uc2 ALERT, STATE DEPT, AL-ZAWAHIRI, LUTE, DRONE\par \par \uc2 KIRBY, ALERT, TALIBAN, DOHA, PELOSI, CHINA\par \par \uc2 SCULLY, DODGERS, HALL OF FAME, LEGACY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOT SPOTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 PUTIN, SANCTIONS, SALAMA, KABAEVA, CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 NFL, DOLPHINS, PENALIZED, BRADY, ROSS, PAYTON\par \par \uc2 CPSC\uc2 , RECALLED, T.J. MAXX, FINED, INFANT\par \par \uc2 PHILIP, PARACHUTE, TANGLED, FREEFALLING, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 BASKETBALL, BLUE COLLAR U, BUFFALO\par \par \uc2 STORY2\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, REFERENDUM, ELECTION, KANSAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KANSAS VOTES TO PROTECT ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTERS BLOCK AMENDMENT IN POST-ROE REFERENDUM\par \par \uc2 MONKEYPOX, CHILDREN, MEDICINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GROWING MONKEYPOX CONCERNS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIFTH CHILD IN U.S. TESTS POSITIVE FOR VIRUS\par \par \uc2 FLOOD, KENTUCKY, WEATHER, DESTRUCTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW THREAT TO KENTUCKY FLOOD ZONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SURVIVORS FACE DEVASTATION & DANGEROUS HEAT\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the aftermath of that historic flooding in Kentucky that left at least 37 people dead. Thousands are without power. Eva Pilgrim is in Hazard, Kentucky, for us this morning. Good morning, Eva.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Michael. The devastation, as you can see, is everywhere. The recovery here in Eastern Kentucky is slow, and now Mother Nature is adding another challenge, triple-digit feel-like temperatures starting today. If you take a look from our drone, you can see just what these people in this area are up against, homes just decimated, missing from their foundations, roads in some places still impassable, and this heat coming as thousands remain without power and no way to cool off.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Authorities are opening cooling centers in the area today and warning people to be careful in this weather as they work outside doing cleanup. We've seen groups out checking on people. They will be going back out today doing those wellness checks and delivering food and water. Guys.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Have to keep those folks in Kentucky in mind. Thank you so much, Eva.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And coming up in our "GMA Morning Menu," Demi Lovato speaking out more about gender identity, her journey and how it's all about respect.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Also this morning, the alarming new study finding that boys and girls as young as nine are suffering with disordered eating issues. Dr. Ashton is here with the warning signs parents should watch for.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Plus, how to save on back-to-school tech. Becky Worley will tell you about refurbished devices and which gadgets to avoid. And "GMA's" Dog Days of Summer heads to Dallas. Ginger is there, and the paw-ty is about to get started. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Back here on "GMA," we're talking about working out on our own versus with a trainer. (Inaudible) for me when I'm on my own. I don't make it the full...\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Who needs the last two?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 I know, really. Just thought we'd share what we talk about. We got a "GMA" special event with the cast of Tony Award-winning sensation "Hamilton." They're coming on Friday.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 So excited. It'll be the first ever live morning TV performance. Get all the details on our website.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 That's going to be great. How many people have wanted to see "Hamilton"? And now you're going to get a chance.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Oh yeah, it's going to be fantastic.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220901ei830003c\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 DEMII LOVATO SPEAKS OUT ABOUT GENDER IDENTITY WHY SINGER ADDED 'SHE/HER' PRONOUNS\b0\par\par\uc2 782 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS HELD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH STAKES PRIMARY SHOWDOWNS\par \par \uc2 VOTERS, KANSAS, ABORTION, TURNOUT\par \par \uc2 PRIMARIES, RESULTS, GOP, TRUMP, CONTENTIOUS\par \par \uc2 CIPOLLONE, DOJ, SUBPOENAED, EASTMAN, PROBE\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, TAIWAN, CHINA, TENSIONS, DEMOCRACY\par \par \uc2 ALERT, STATE DEPT, AL-ZAWAHIRI, LUTE, DRONE\par \par \uc2 KIRBY, ALERT, TALIBAN, DOHA, PELOSI, CHINA\par \par \uc2 SCULLY, DODGERS, HALL OF FAME, LEGACY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOT SPOTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 PUTIN, SANCTIONS, SALAMA, KABAEVA, CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 NFL, DOLPHINS, PENALIZED, BRADY, ROSS, PAYTON\par \par \uc2 CPSC\uc2 , RECALLED, T.J. MAXX, FINED, INFANT\par \par \uc2 PHILIP, PARACHUTE, TANGLED, FREEFALLING, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 BASKETBALL, BLUE COLLAR U, BUFFALO\par \par \uc2 STORY2\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, REFERENDUM, ELECTION, KANSAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KANSAS VOTES TO PROTECT ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTERS BLOCK AMENDMENT IN POST-ROE REFERENDUM\par \par \uc2 MONKEYPOX, CHILDREN, MEDICINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GROWING MONKEYPOX CONCERNS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIFTH CHILD IN U.S. TESTS POSITIVE FOR VIRUS\par \par \uc2 FLOOD, KENTUCKY, WEATHER, DESTRUCTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW THREAT TO KENTUCKY FLOOD ZONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SURVIVORS FACE DEVASTATION & DANGEROUS HEAT\par \par \uc2 LOVATO, SINGER, GENDER, IDENTITY, PRONOUNS, CRUZ\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMII LOVATO SPEAKS OUT ABOUT GENDER IDENTITY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHY SINGER ADDED 'SHE/HER' PRONOUNS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're going to move on now to our cover story. Demi Lovato is speaking about gender identity and why she's adopted she/her pronouns again after coming out as non-binary in 2021. Juju Chang here with the details. Good morning, Juju.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, George. Hi, guys. Well, you know, Demi Lovato has been remarkably transparent over the years from struggles with addiction and eating disorders to sexual orientation and gender identity. Lovato says this latest pronoun shift shows that she's ever-evolving and willing to be vulnerable enough to share it.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Singer Demi Lovato speaking out about returning to she/her pronouns along with they/them, updating fans on her gender fluidity.\par \par \uc2 DEMI LOVATO (SINGER)\par \par \uc2 I'm such a fluid person when it comes to my gender, my sexuality, my music, my creativity. It's like I don't know where things are going to take me. Recently I've been feeling more feminine, and so I've adopted she/her again.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The singer made news last year, tweeting, "Today is the day I'm so happy to share more of my life with you all. I'm proud to let you know that I identify as non-binary and will officially be changing my pronouns to they/them moving forward." Now, Lovato speaking publicly for the first time on the podcast "Spout" since changing acceptable pronouns on her \uc2 Instagram\uc2  page in April.\par \par \uc2 PODCAST CLIP FROM "SPOUT"\par \par \uc2 DEMI LOVATO (SINGER)\par \par \uc2 I've actually adopted the pronouns of she/her again. I felt like especially last year, my energy was balanced and my masculine and feminine energy. I didn't feel necessarily like a woman. I didn't feel like a man. I just felt like a human. And that's what they/them is, is about for me. It's just about, like, feeling human at your core.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) \uc2 GLAAD\uc2  supporting Lovato, saying in a statement, "Everyone deserves the freedom and the latitude to question and explore their own gender identity. That exploration may include trying on different pronouns to see which ones feel most in alignment with one's gender." Other music stars have been open about their gender identity, Sam Smith and Janelle Monae both coming out as non-binary, and pop star Halsey listing she/they as acceptable pronouns on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NAOMI CRUZ (GENDER SPECTRUM)\par \par \uc2 Gender is a journey. We're all growing as people and learning more about ourselves, and gender is fluid. So, what you feel at this point in time may be one thing, and a gender identity you're using now is very valid.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Lovato adding that for her, gender expression is an ongoing learning process.\par \par \uc2 DEMI LOVATO (SINGER)\par \par \uc2 Nobody is perfect. Everyone messes up pronouns at some point and especially when people are learning. It's just all about respect.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Lovato says all of this exploration is part of her healing process, and for those who might get confused and misgender her pronouns, she says, "As long as you keep trying to respect my truth, and as long as I remember my truth," the shift will come naturally, Robin.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I appreciate her saying that because sometimes your, your intentions are good and you will, you won't say it correctly so I'm glad she, she said it like that.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, all right, Juju, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220901ei830003d\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GOOD MORNING AMERICA\b0\par\par\uc2 637 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS HELD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH STAKES PRIMARY SHOWDOWNS\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America, on this Wednesday morning. Voters in Kansas making a statement about abortion rights. Overnight, abortion rights upheld in Kansas. Voters rejecting a change to the state's constitution that would remove protections. The first voters are weighing in on the issue after the Supreme Court ruling overturning Roe versus Wade. As primaries in five states set up key showdowns in November's midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EX-WHITE HOUSE COUNSEL SUBPOENAED\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight, a major development in the criminal investigation of President Trump. Former White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  subpoenaed by the federal grand jury investigating the January 6th insurrection. The highest ranking official with Trump that day.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TAIWAN TRIP TENSIONS\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight, House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  meeting with Taiwan's President. Her visit sparking outrage in China. How the government is responding, and what the White House is saying. Spokesman John Kirby joins us live.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: URGENT ALERT\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Urgent alert. After the drone strike to take out Al Qaeda leader Ayman al-Zawahiri, the State Department warns US citizens around the world of a higher potential for anti-American violence.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW THREAT\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 New threat for hard hit Kentucky, with thousands without power after the devastating floods. Now temperatures that feel like triple digits moving in.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CLOSING IN ON PUTIN'S INNER CIRCLE\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Closing in on Putin's inner circle. The US now sanctioning his reported longtime girlfriend believed to be the mother of some of his children.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASSIVE PENALTY\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Massive penalty for the \uc2 Miami Dolphins\uc2  for attempting to lure Tom Brady while he was with the Patriots and then the Buccaneers.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEATH DEFYING FALL\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Death-defying fall. A paraglider, lucky to be alive, telling his story after his parachute got tangled around his body thousands of feet in the air. How he saved himself at the very last second.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CELEBRATING VIN SCULLY\par \par \uc2 VIN SCULLY (HALL OF FAME DODGERS ANNOUNCER)\par \par \uc2 It's time for Dodger baseball.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Celebrating a hall of fame voice.\par \par \uc2 VIN SCULLY (HALL OF FAME DODGERS ANNOUNCER)\par \par \uc2 In a year that has been so improbable, the impossible has happened.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Dodgers announcer Vin Scully passed away at 94. We look back on his over six decades in the broadcasting business.\par \par \uc2 VIN SCULLY (HALL OF FAME DODGERS ANNOUNCER)\par \par \uc2 What a marvelous moment for the country and the world.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 His signature sound behind so much history on the diamond.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Live in Times Square, this is "Good Morning America."\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America. And you said it, George. Signature sound. No one called a game like Vin Scully.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 He really set a standard.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 He was such a gentleman, and he was the voice of summer for so many. He really was.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 He sure was. We're gonna remember him. But we have a lot of news to get to this morning, including that surprising vote overnight in Kansas. The first state to vote on abortion since the Supreme Court overturned Roe V. Wade. Voters there triggered a strong backlash, affirming the state's constitution which protects those rights. Remember, Kansas it is a deep red state where there are far more Republicans than Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220901ei830002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PARAGLIDER'S DEATH DEFYING PLUNGE TANGLED CHUTE SENDS MAN FREEFALLING\b0\par\par\uc2 664 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS HELD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH STAKES PRIMARY SHOWDOWNS\par \par \uc2 VOTERS, KANSAS, ABORTION, TURNOUT\par \par \uc2 PRIMARIES, RESULTS, GOP, TRUMP, CONTENTIOUS\par \par \uc2 CIPOLLONE, DOJ, SUBPOENAED, EASTMAN, PROBE\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, TAIWAN, CHINA, TENSIONS, DEMOCRACY\par \par \uc2 ALERT, STATE DEPT, AL-ZAWAHIRI, LUTE, DRONE\par \par \uc2 KIRBY, ALERT, TALIBAN, DOHA, PELOSI, CHINA\par \par \uc2 SCULLY, DODGERS, HALL OF FAME, LEGACY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOT SPOTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 PUTIN, SANCTIONS, SALAMA, KABAEVA, CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 NFL, DOLPHINS, PENALIZED, BRADY, ROSS, PAYTON\par \par \uc2 CPSC\uc2 , RECALLED, T.J. MAXX, FINED, INFANT\par \par \uc2 PHILIP, PARACHUTE, TANGLED, FREEFALLING, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A paraglider out of control and plummeting thousands of feet to Earth all caught on camera. Gio Benitez tells us about the life-threatening plunge and what happened next. Good morning, Gio.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PARAGLIDER'S DEATH DEFYING PLUNGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TANGLED CHUTE SENDS MAN FREEFALLING\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Michael, good morning. Yeah, look, I rarely say this, but stop what you're doing because you cannot miss a second of this video. In fact, the Swiss paraglider says he only had one second left to get that rescue parachute working.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, the death-defying fall captured on camera. Watch, that's professional aerobatic paraglider Kevin Phillips thousands of feet in the air. At first everything seems normal then there appears to be some strong turbulence, almost instantly his chute becomes tangled. It gets worse and worse.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Phillips is seen twisting in the air free-falling. Then as the ground gets closer and closer, right before impact, Phillips is finally able to deploy his rescue chute. Gliding safely down to the ground with one second to spare.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN PHILLIPS (AEROBATIC PARAGLIDER)\par \par \uc2 Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now this morning Phillips living to tell his story right here on "GMA."\par \par \uc2 KEVIN PHILLIPS (AEROBATIC PARAGLIDER)\par \par \uc2 It was just really unlucky that I got tangled and wrapped up like this, but then in the end I was lucky actually to find my second rescue, which I could deploy like with my bare hands.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Phillips posting the video on his social media accounts, more than a million people watching. In caption Phillips says, the "Very last chance was to manually open the rescue package. Estimated time left, about one second. This was not the day to die."\par \par \uc2 KEVIN PHILLIPS (AEROBATIC PARAGLIDER)\par \par \uc2 I was really surprised that I still was calm. I just wanted, or I just needed to function, so I had not enough time to really freak out or to scream or whatever.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But even with this very close call, the 34-year-old isn't hanging up his chute just yet.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN PHILLIPS (AEROBATIC PARAGLIDER)\par \par \uc2 I'm going back up like tomorrow actually. The sport really, really gave me a lot. I think you shouldn't just like be scared of things like this.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back out there tomorrow. Now, remember, he's an aerobatic paraglider, and that means he's used to extreme stunts in the air, but in this case he says this was no stunt, Michael.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Gio, he knows the dangers. He had an accident five years ago.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, he sure does, Michael. In fact, he was temporarily paralyzed for about six months. He had to be in a wheelchair. He's walking okay now. He's just fine, but it's incredibly dangerous.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SAVE ON BACK TO SCHOOL TECH\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Very dangerous, but obviously he loves it. He's willing to take the risk. All right, Gio, thank you for bringing us that. And coming up later Becky Worley is helping you save when it comes to back to school tech. Is buying refurbished tech the answer? And next, we have our "Play of the Day." Of the hump day.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220901ei8300036\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 KANSAS VOTES TO PROTECT ABORTION RIGHTS VOTERS BLOCK AMENDMENT\b0\par\par\uc2 543 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS HELD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH STAKES PRIMARY SHOWDOWNS\par \par \uc2 VOTERS, KANSAS, ABORTION, TURNOUT\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The vote rejecting a proposed amendment to the state constitution that would have invalidated abortion rights in Kansas. Rachel Scott has been tracking this issue, and was there overnight as the results came in. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KANSAS VOTES TO PROTECT ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTERS BLOCK AMENDMENT IN FIRST MAJOR POST ROE REFERENDUM\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Robin, good morning. This was a massive show support for abortion rights in a conservative traditionally red state less than six weeks after Roe versus Wade was overturned by the Supreme Court, voters in this state of Kansas sent a clear message that the right to an abortion should be protected.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This was the moment the results came in. Kansas voters overwhelmingly decided to protect access to abortion.\par \par \uc2 PRO-CHOICE RIGHTS ACTIVIST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 It's a huge victory for Kansas, for women, for reproductive rights in America.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The first political test in a post-Roe America, handing abortion rights groups a major victory.\par \par \uc2 PRO-CHOICE RIGHTS ACTIVIST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 This sends a message that the women are coming out of the woodworks, like we are not going anywhere, and we are going to protect ourselves.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Voters blocked an amendment that would have stripped protections from the state's constitution, clearing the way for lawmakers to enact tighter restrictions or an outright ban. Anti-abortion rights activists saying it's far from over.\par \par \uc2 ANTI-ABORTION RIGHTS ACTIVIST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 If the last 50 years haven't shown anything, we've been fighting. We're not going to stop fighting.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The turnout soared. Lines stretching around the block. Voters on both sides energized after the Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 KANSAS RESIDENT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 We don't want to be known as an abortion state. We want to have some limits and we want to have the people have the right to say what they want in their own state.\par \par \uc2 KANSAS RESIDENT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 As somebody that's had an abortion, I think it's incredibly stigmatized, and I think that we need to get rid of that stigma. And it begins with keeping access to safe, legal and affordable abortion.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At least 14 states have stopped nearly all abortion services, but Kansas will remain a safe haven for abortions at least for now.\par \par \uc2 PRO-CHOICE RIGHTS ACTIVIST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 The overwhelming no vote just proves that people in states can stand up and say, no, you're not going to control my body. I hope this is an example for other states.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This primary was closely watched around the nation. President Biden released a statement after these results came in saying, "This makes clear the majority of Americans believe the right to an abortion should be protected." And in just a few hours the President is expected to sign an executive order to help support out-of-state patients who have to travel to get an abortion, George.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220901ei830002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CHILDREN & DISORDERED EATING STUDY: KIDS AS YOUNG AS 9 ENGAGING IN BEHAVIOR\b0\par\par\uc2 656 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS HELD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH STAKES PRIMARY SHOWDOWNS\par \par \uc2 VOTERS, KANSAS, ABORTION, TURNOUT\par \par \uc2 PRIMARIES, RESULTS, GOP, TRUMP, CONTENTIOUS\par \par \uc2 CIPOLLONE, DOJ, SUBPOENAED, EASTMAN, PROBE\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, TAIWAN, CHINA, TENSIONS, DEMOCRACY\par \par \uc2 ALERT, STATE DEPT, AL-ZAWAHIRI, LUTE, DRONE\par \par \uc2 KIRBY, ALERT, TALIBAN, DOHA, PELOSI, CHINA\par \par \uc2 SCULLY, DODGERS, HALL OF FAME, LEGACY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOT SPOTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 PUTIN, SANCTIONS, SALAMA, KABAEVA, CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 NFL, DOLPHINS, PENALIZED, BRADY, ROSS, PAYTON\par \par \uc2 CPSC\uc2 , RECALLED, T.J. MAXX, FINED, INFANT\par \par \uc2 PHILIP, PARACHUTE, TANGLED, FREEFALLING, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 BASKETBALL, BLUE COLLAR U, BUFFALO\par \par \uc2 STORY2\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, REFERENDUM, ELECTION, KANSAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KANSAS VOTES TO PROTECT ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTERS BLOCK AMENDMENT IN POST-ROE REFERENDUM\par \par \uc2 MONKEYPOX, CHILDREN, MEDICINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GROWING MONKEYPOX CONCERNS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIFTH CHILD IN U.S. TESTS POSITIVE FOR VIRUS\par \par \uc2 FLOOD, KENTUCKY, WEATHER, DESTRUCTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW THREAT TO KENTUCKY FLOOD ZONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SURVIVORS FACE DEVASTATION & DANGEROUS HEAT\par \par \uc2 LOVATO, SINGER, GENDER, IDENTITY, PRONOUNS, CRUZ\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMII LOVATO SPEAKS OUT ABOUT GENDER IDENTITY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHY SINGER ADDED 'SHE/HER' PRONOUNS\par \par \uc2 DISORDERED EATING, CHILDREN, HEALTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHILDREN & DISORDERED EATING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STUDY: KIDS AS YOUNG AS 9 ENGAGING IN BEHAVIOR\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to a parenting alert, an alarming new report on disordered eating among children finding both boys and girls as young as nine are engaging in behaviors like dieting and binging. And Dr. Jen Ashton is going to join us more with it. So, tell us, first of all, about the study.\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, Robin, just published in "JAMA," I think the important thing here is that they're really trying to get a read on how often this is going on, who is affected and when it starts. And as you said, it starts younger than a lot of us may think. What the study found, five percent of children overall engaged in binge-eating behaviors. Overall, 28 million Americans are affected by some type of eating disorder. So this is incredibly common. And most importantly, with respect to this study and its importance, most eating disorders actually begin in adolescence. So when they found nine, 10-year-olds starting to engage in binge eating, you know, that really shifts our timeline to try to be more aware earlier and try to intervene.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And still emphasizing girls and boys because sometimes you only talk about the girls.\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely, for sure. This is, this is not just a problem for girls.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, what are the warning signs parents should look for?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW TO SPOT THE WARNING SIGNS & WHAT YOU SHOULD DO\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Listen, I think this is really important because at this age and in older adolescents, parents could be the first ones to pick up on this. So the signs and symptoms, things that parents can notice, a sudden change in their child's appetite, sudden avoiding meal times, especially when people used to have family meals together, frequent calorie counting or an over-exercising, overall poor body image, constantly talking about weight, weight gain, losing weight.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, if a parent feels that their child has an unhealthy relationship with food, what should they do?\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I say fill the boat. Get some professional help. Talk to your child's pediatrician. Also, at the same time, try to respect your child's privacy because this is a difficult time for them. And also anticipate some degree of rebellion. As parents, we expect that across the board but particularly if you're dealing with a child with an eating disorder.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All good advice, as always. We'll see you on "GMA3."\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, my dear.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Michael.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220901ei830003e\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GROWING MONKEYPOX CONCERNS FIFTH CHILD IN U.S. TESTS POSITIVE FOR VIRUS\b0\par\par\uc2 352 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS HELD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH STAKES PRIMARY SHOWDOWNS\par \par \uc2 VOTERS, KANSAS, ABORTION, TURNOUT\par \par \uc2 PRIMARIES, RESULTS, GOP, TRUMP, CONTENTIOUS\par \par \uc2 CIPOLLONE, DOJ, SUBPOENAED, EASTMAN, PROBE\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, TAIWAN, CHINA, TENSIONS, DEMOCRACY\par \par \uc2 ALERT, STATE DEPT, AL-ZAWAHIRI, LUTE, DRONE\par \par \uc2 KIRBY, ALERT, TALIBAN, DOHA, PELOSI, CHINA\par \par \uc2 SCULLY, DODGERS, HALL OF FAME, LEGACY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOT SPOTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 PUTIN, SANCTIONS, SALAMA, KABAEVA, CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 NFL, DOLPHINS, PENALIZED, BRADY, ROSS, PAYTON\par \par \uc2 CPSC\uc2 , RECALLED, T.J. MAXX, FINED, INFANT\par \par \uc2 PHILIP, PARACHUTE, TANGLED, FREEFALLING, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 BASKETBALL, BLUE COLLAR U, BUFFALO\par \par \uc2 STORY2\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, REFERENDUM, ELECTION, KANSAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KANSAS VOTES TO PROTECT ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTERS BLOCK AMENDMENT IN POST-ROE REFERENDUM\par \par \uc2 MONKEYPOX, CHILDREN, MEDICINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GROWING MONKEYPOX CONCERNS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIFTH CHILD IN U.S. TESTS POSITIVE FOR VIRUS\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And George, now to new monkeypox concerns as kids prepare to head back to school. Another child in the US has tested positive. Stephanie Ramos joins us now with more. Good morning, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Robin, good morning. A fifth child, as you just said, has tested positive for monkeypox. This is the second child in the state of California, and there are also two pediatric cases in Indiana and one in Washington, DC. So, here's the deal with the monkeypox vaccine for kids. It is not widely available for kids under the age of 18. However, the FDA does grant access on a case-by-case basis. So, if a doctor thinks a child has been exposed to monkeypox, they can submit a request for a vaccine to the FDA, the agency telling ABC News, quote, "Numerous children have been given special permission to get the vaccine." Overall the number of reported monkeypox cases in the US has jumped significantly, now at more than 6,000. Los Angeles, the latest city to declare a health emergency, three states, California, Illinois and New York, declaring states of emergency. Guys.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our thanks to Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, thank you, Steph.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220901ei830003b\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 POP NEWS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,043 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS HELD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH STAKES PRIMARY SHOWDOWNS\par \par \uc2 VOTERS, KANSAS, ABORTION, TURNOUT\par \par \uc2 PRIMARIES, RESULTS, GOP, TRUMP, CONTENTIOUS\par \par \uc2 CIPOLLONE, DOJ, SUBPOENAED, EASTMAN, PROBE\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, TAIWAN, CHINA, TENSIONS, DEMOCRACY\par \par \uc2 ALERT, STATE DEPT, AL-ZAWAHIRI, LUTE, DRONE\par \par \uc2 KIRBY, ALERT, TALIBAN, DOHA, PELOSI, CHINA\par \par \uc2 SCULLY, DODGERS, HALL OF FAME, LEGACY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOT SPOTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 PUTIN, SANCTIONS, SALAMA, KABAEVA, CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 NFL, DOLPHINS, PENALIZED, BRADY, ROSS, PAYTON\par \par \uc2 CPSC\uc2 , RECALLED, T.J. MAXX, FINED, INFANT\par \par \uc2 PHILIP, PARACHUTE, TANGLED, FREEFALLING, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 BASKETBALL, BLUE COLLAR U, BUFFALO\par \par \uc2 STORY2\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, REFERENDUM, ELECTION, KANSAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KANSAS VOTES TO PROTECT ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTERS BLOCK AMENDMENT IN POST-ROE REFERENDUM\par \par \uc2 MONKEYPOX, CHILDREN, MEDICINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GROWING MONKEYPOX CONCERNS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIFTH CHILD IN U.S. TESTS POSITIVE FOR VIRUS\par \par \uc2 FLOOD, KENTUCKY, WEATHER, DESTRUCTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW THREAT TO KENTUCKY FLOOD ZONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SURVIVORS FACE DEVASTATION & DANGEROUS HEAT\par \par \uc2 LOVATO, SINGER, GENDER, IDENTITY, PRONOUNS, CRUZ\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMII LOVATO SPEAKS OUT ABOUT GENDER IDENTITY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHY SINGER ADDED 'SHE/HER' PRONOUNS\par \par \uc2 DISORDERED EATING, CHILDREN, HEALTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHILDREN & DISORDERED EATING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STUDY: KIDS AS YOUNG AS 9 ENGAGING IN BEHAVIOR\par \par \uc2 TECHNOLOGY, REFURBISHED, SAVINGS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BACK TO SCHOOL SAVINGS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BUYING REFURBISHED TECH TO CUT COSTS\par \par \uc2 STYLES, "ROAD HOUSE", NOSTALGIA\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And now we're going to throw it upstairs to you guys for some "Pop News" with Lara. Hey, Lara.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HARRY STYLES' "SIGN OF THE TIMES" REACHES 1 BILLION YOUTUBE VIEWS\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Michael, thank you so very much. Wish you were here. But we're going to get right to it, everybody, because lot to talk about. First in music news, it is a sign of the times, I guess. Harry Styles, he's on a roll. His 2017 debut single of the same name, that's his first solo hit after his One Direction days, well, that is now part of the billion views club, a huge accomplishment, a billion views on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 . And Harry is busy on his world tour.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HARRY STYLES: MATCHMAKER?\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He just wrapped the European leg in Portugal, where he lent a microphone to a guy who said, "Hey, can I sing just two lines of a song to my girlfriend." Harry complies. Well, the next thing you know, the guy surprises even Harry with an Elvis Presley tune, like crushes it, and then an impromptu proposal. It is all caught on video, that video racking up 32 million views on TikTok, where the user wrote simply, "Congrats, Marianna and Cole." So we can same the same, congrats, you two cuties. By the way, the US leg of Harry's tour starts very soon, 15 nights in MSG. I'll be at all 15.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Fifteen nights?\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Fifteen nights at Madison Square Garden.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, definitely going to go to one. He's so talented.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "ROAD HOUSE" REMAKE TO STAR JAKE GYLLENHAAL\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Also in the news this morning, remember the movie "Road House," you guys? Have you seen it?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, a young Patrick Swayze starred in the original. It was back in 1989. It was only a modest success but got more popular after Swayze passed. And now, well, it's considered a cult classic. And one of the people who loves the film, Jake Gyllenhaal, who will take on the lead role in a remake. This time around, he's going to play a former UFC fighter who takes a job as a bouncer at a rough-and-tumble roadhouse in the Florida Keys. But, yes, Robin, he soon discovers, though, it's not everything it seems in this tropical paradise. Uh-oh. Director Doug Limon says he's thrilled to put his own spin on the beloved "Road House" legacy and can't wait to show audiences what he and Jake are going to do with this iconic role. "Road House" set to begin shooting in the Dominican Republic later this month.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They're not going to go down to the Keys and shoot it?\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I guess it must be not that, be that easy down there.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Must not be.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You can lend your house.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And elsewhere in news...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NOSTALGIA HITS ABC PROMOS\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, and finally, we, it's funny that we're chatting because we found this incredible video. Well, yesterday we showed you that ABC primetime promo video. That was 1980. Some of our favorite stars, including Tom Hanks. Robin mentioned that she had a similar experience at one of her first jobs.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mentioned in the commercial break. I didn't mention on the air.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Guess what? You shouldn't have done that. We have great producers who dug it up, WLOX, Biloxi, Mississippi. Let's take a look, everybody.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, yes.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look at those moves, everybody. Robin. And the big finale.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It was a big finale. That was a big deal. Oh, my goodness. I cannot believe, I mean, I mentioned that in a commercial.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You were young and hungry, huh?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I needed the money, George. I needed the money.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) 1984, Robin.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I can't believe it. Oh, but hey, WLOX, TV 13, the news for South Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you very much, and there you have it.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Very, very good to me.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Be care what you say in the commercial breaks. That's the moral of this story. That's all we got for "Pop News."\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's enough.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We'll dig up something on you, George.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220901ei830003g\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 KANSAS VOTES TO PROTECT ABORTION RIGHTS VOTERS BLOCK AMENDMENT IN POST-ROE REFERENDUM\b0\par\par\uc2 530 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS HELD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH STAKES PRIMARY SHOWDOWNS\par \par \uc2 VOTERS, KANSAS, ABORTION, TURNOUT\par \par \uc2 PRIMARIES, RESULTS, GOP, TRUMP, CONTENTIOUS\par \par \uc2 CIPOLLONE, DOJ, SUBPOENAED, EASTMAN, PROBE\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, TAIWAN, CHINA, TENSIONS, DEMOCRACY\par \par \uc2 ALERT, STATE DEPT, AL-ZAWAHIRI, LUTE, DRONE\par \par \uc2 KIRBY, ALERT, TALIBAN, DOHA, PELOSI, CHINA\par \par \uc2 SCULLY, DODGERS, HALL OF FAME, LEGACY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOT SPOTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 PUTIN, SANCTIONS, SALAMA, KABAEVA, CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 NFL, DOLPHINS, PENALIZED, BRADY, ROSS, PAYTON\par \par \uc2 CPSC\uc2 , RECALLED, T.J. MAXX, FINED, INFANT\par \par \uc2 PHILIP, PARACHUTE, TANGLED, FREEFALLING, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 BASKETBALL, BLUE COLLAR U, BUFFALO\par \par \uc2 STORY2\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, REFERENDUM, ELECTION, KANSAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KANSAS VOTES TO PROTECT ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTERS BLOCK AMENDMENT IN POST-ROE REFERENDUM\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A lot of news to get to, as well, including the fallout from Kansas overnight, the first state to vote on abortion since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. Voters are triggering a strong backlash, affirming the state's constitution, which protects abortion rights. And remember, Kansas, this is a deep red state where there are far more Republicans than Democrats. Rachel Scott, excuse me, is tracking the story. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) George, good morning. It was a surprise show of support in a very conservative state, voters here in Kansas sending a message to protect abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This was the moment the results came in. Kansas voters overwhelmingly decided to protect access to abortion.\par \par \uc2 KANSAS VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I feel really proud to be a Kansan tonight.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The first political test in a post-Roe America handing abortion rights groups a major victory.\par \par \uc2 KANSAS VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 This sends a message that the women are coming out of the woodwork. Like, we are not going anywhere, and we are going to protect ourselves.\par \par \uc2 KANSAS VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 We're taking our power back.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Voters blocked an amendment that would have stripped protections from the state's constitution, clearing the way for lawmakers to enact tighter restrictions or an outright ban. Anti-abortion rights activists say it's far from over.\par \par \uc2 KANSAS VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 If the last 50 years haven't shown anything, we've been fighting. We're not going to stop fighting.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The turnout soared, lines stretching around the block, voters on both sides energized after the Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 KANSAS VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 We don't want to be known as an abortion state. We want to have some limits, and we want to have the people have the right to say what they want in their own state.\par \par \uc2 KANSAS VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 As somebody that's had an abortion, I think it's incredibly stigmatized, and I think that we need to get rid of that stigma, and it begins with keeping access to safe legal and affordable abortion.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Across the nation, at least 14 states have stopped nearly all abortion services, but for now, Kansas will continue to remain a safe haven, George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel Scott, thanks. Robin.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220901ei830003a\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PLAY OF THE DAY\b0\par\par\uc2 379 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS HELD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH STAKES PRIMARY SHOWDOWNS\par \par \uc2 VOTERS, KANSAS, ABORTION, TURNOUT\par \par \uc2 PRIMARIES, RESULTS, GOP, TRUMP, CONTENTIOUS\par \par \uc2 CIPOLLONE, DOJ, SUBPOENAED, EASTMAN, PROBE\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, TAIWAN, CHINA, TENSIONS, DEMOCRACY\par \par \uc2 ALERT, STATE DEPT, AL-ZAWAHIRI, LUTE, DRONE\par \par \uc2 KIRBY, ALERT, TALIBAN, DOHA, PELOSI, CHINA\par \par \uc2 SCULLY, DODGERS, HALL OF FAME, LEGACY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOT SPOTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 PUTIN, SANCTIONS, SALAMA, KABAEVA, CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 NFL, DOLPHINS, PENALIZED, BRADY, ROSS, PAYTON\par \par \uc2 CPSC\uc2 , RECALLED, T.J. MAXX, FINED, INFANT\par \par \uc2 PHILIP, PARACHUTE, TANGLED, FREEFALLING, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 BASKETBALL, BLUE COLLAR U, BUFFALO\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're back with our "Play of the Day." We had some high-stake hoops last night with the final four, The Basketball Tournament, that's the name of it. The Basketball Tournament. Blue Collar U winning the title with this dunk that ended the game.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Whoa.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Whoo. The tournament is made up of 64 teams, players from all over, including former \uc2 NBA\uc2  players, college, from overseas. The team made up mostly of former University of Buffalo basketball players winning $1 million. It was a winner take all tournament. And Blue Collar U, don't you love that name?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Blue Collar U, they dedicated each win to the victims of the May shooting at a Buffalo supermarket, and some players wore a victim's name on the back of their jerseys, while three jerseys read Buffalo Strong. And they also pledged to donate a portion of their winnings to benefit foster children and at risk youth.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good for them.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good for them. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely. Thank you for doing that. Coming up, oh, sorry, that's your line.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Go ahead, I'm listening.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, it's hump day, no, you go.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Coming up, help me read every other word. Coming up, wait until you hear the story of this Air Force veteran and his service dog. Ginger is in Dallas with day three of our dog days of summer, and there they are. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220901ei8300037\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 7:30 HALF HOUR\b0\par\par\uc2 394 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS HELD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH STAKES PRIMARY SHOWDOWNS\par \par \uc2 VOTERS, KANSAS, ABORTION, TURNOUT\par \par \uc2 PRIMARIES, RESULTS, GOP, TRUMP, CONTENTIOUS\par \par \uc2 CIPOLLONE, DOJ, SUBPOENAED, EASTMAN, PROBE\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, TAIWAN, CHINA, TENSIONS, DEMOCRACY\par \par \uc2 ALERT, STATE DEPT, AL-ZAWAHIRI, LUTE, DRONE\par \par \uc2 KIRBY, ALERT, TALIBAN, DOHA, PELOSI, CHINA\par \par \uc2 SCULLY, DODGERS, HALL OF FAME, LEGACY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOT SPOTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Back here on "GMA," Harry Styles is on a roll. His video, "Sign of the Times," launching him into an exclusive club. Lara's gonna have more on that coming up in "Pop News," and how Harry helped pull, one guy pull off the surprise of a lifetime for his girlfriend. "Pop News" coming up.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KANSAS UPHOLDS ABORTION\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Looking forward to that. Following a lot of headlines this morning, including that surprising vote overnight in Kansas, the first state to vote on abortion since the Supreme Court overturned Roe V. Wade. The vote rejected a proposed amendment to the state's constitution that would have invalidated abortion rights there.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEATH TOLL RISES IN CALIFORNIA WILDFIRE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Also the McKinney wildfire in Northern California has claimed two more lives, bringing the death toll to four. The fire has now burned more than 56,000 acres and remains 0% contained. A spokesman for the \uc2 US Forest Service\uc2  calls the fire region, very combustible and difficult for firefighters to access.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MONKEYPOX CONCERNS GROW\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Plus, the latest on the monkeypox outbreak. A fifth child has tested positive in the US. The number of confirmed cases in the country has climbed to 6,000. More on that coming up in our next hour.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STUNNING NEW IMAGES\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And take a look at this stunning new image from the James Webb Telescope. It's 500 million light-years away and it formed after a collision between a large spiral galaxy and a smaller star system collided. Scientists call it the cartwheel galaxy. That is beautiful.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Beautiful. Wow.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And we've got a lot more ahead, including a paraglider's desperate attempt to free himself from a tangled chute. He tells us about his last-second save. All caught on camera. That is coming up, George.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220901ei8300032\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE DECISION AMERICA POST-ROE\b0\par\par\uc2 3,641 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE: THE DECISION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, CRISIS, AZIZ, ALEXANDER, PINSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CORPUS CHRISTI\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At just 18 years old, Brooke Alexander is embracing the beauty and the monotony of motherhood\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 Hello.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) \u8230\'20\'26times two.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 She's got a higher-pitched cry, Olivia. Kendall's, like, she'll just start screaming. It's not really a cry. It's, like, a scream.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 I love you so much. I do.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Bottles, naps, tummy time.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 Whoa, look at the fishies.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Repeat.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 On a good day, we wake up and if they're still asleep I'll eat some breakfast, and maybe take a shower. And then I'll pump and feed them. By that time, they usually pass out. They are my day.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Texas teen runs a tight schedule, but it doesn't really leave time for anything else, right now.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 When I try to get them to go to bed, just, like, walk back and forth. It's been a really big adjustment. I was used to working and being able to go out and just kind of be free.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today, millions of women feel their sense of freedom is in jeopardy, caught in a firestorm of uncertainty in a post-Roe America. From states beginning to put the issue on the ballot\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I feel really proud to be a Kansan, tonight.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) \u8230\'20\'26to the President taking executive action.\par \par \uc2 CLIP OF PRESIDENTIAL INTERVIEW\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOE BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I said before, this fight is not over.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Behind the headlines, beyond the debate, hear from women caught between the law and a choice.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 Me being able to access both my abortions did make me a better mother. I was able to choose motherhood on my terms.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) One mother who knows what it means to choose abortion. Another who found herself facing the hardest decision of her life, as time ran out.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 Abortion didn't really seem like an option, because of the law.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And what happens when you live in an abortion desert?\par \par \uc2 JANA PINSON (EXECUTIVE DIR. PREGNANCY CNTR OF THE COASTAL BEND)\par \par \uc2 Our mamas come in and take classes.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We go inside a crisis pregnancy center and find out what makes some people call these places controversial, and others lifesaving.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Brooke's unexpected journey into parenthood actually started last fall. At the same time, Texas was about to enforce what was then the most restrictive abortion ban in the country.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 I found out about the law on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . I mean, I think that I'm in the middle. I don't, I don't want to persuade people, I think, and everybody's opinion is different.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Texas Senate bill 8 banned abortions after the sixth week of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 It's not that I didn't want kids. I didn't want kids at 18. I wanted to wait until I was like at least 25, 27, something like that. I was actually on birth control. It was really heartbreaking. I really tried to not get pregnant. I felt like it, it, it was gonna ruin everything.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Brooke had only been seeing her boyfriend for a few months. Billy was still finishing high school, a standout student and skateboarder.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 Me and Billy met at the skate park. We ended up texting each other on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and just kind of went on from there. We went to the mall, and eventually, he asked me to be his girlfriend. He was really responsible, and most boys his age aren't that way. And that was another thing that really attracted me to him.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) With time ticking, the young couple had a lot to figure out.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 I remember Billy saying, "I'd prefer that you get an abortion, but it's ultimately your choice, and I want you to do what you want to do." I was just hoping that the pregnancy was under six weeks, so I could get an abortion, but I knew that it probably wasn't.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) If she wasn't, Brooke knew she was facing a dilemma. Keep her pregnancy or travel hundreds of miles out of state for an abortion.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DALLAS\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 I have that. This is our sign.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Like Brooke, Maleeha Aziz knows what it's like to face an unplanned pregnancy young.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 Let me know where.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She was in college when her birth control failed.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 Here? Right here? Okay. My first abortion was about eight to nine years ago and I was new to the country. I was an immigrant. I didn't know a whole lot. I ended up traveling to Colorado Springs. And thankfully, I had a family member who could help me pay for it. But if I didn't, I, it scares me to think about what my reality could have been.\par \par \uc2 VOLUNTEER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Would you all like one?\par \par \uc2 BYSTANDER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Sure.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 Not everybody can afford to hop on a plane and go to another state and access the procedure. We need health care in all parts of the country, in every state.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 You could put it here, if you like.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Malia's abortion experience led her to a career in advocacy.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 Okay. Lower it a bit.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She's the community organizer for the Texas Equal Access Fund.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 It's a pretty decent turnout.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And on this hot summer day, she's at Dallas City Hall to put on a rally just days after the Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 INTELLIGENCE OFFICER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Can I speak to you, right now?\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 INTELLIGENCE OFFICER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I work with the intelligence unit.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THERE'S A COUPLE OF GROUPS THAT ARE COMING OUT TO COUNTER-PROTEST.\par \par \uc2 INTELLIGENCE OFFICER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 There's a couple of groups that are coming out to counter-protest.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 Not surprised.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IF FOR SOME REASON THEY START COMING IN, OR CAUSING ISSUES, PLEASE FLAG DOWN AN OFFICER AND LET THEM KNOW.\par \par \uc2 INTELLIGENCE OFFICER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 If for some reason, they start coming in or causing issues, please flag down an officer and let them know.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 We have been living this nightmare longer than most other people, and we are going to feel the impact of it more than some other states, unfortunately. Where are they? Where are the antis?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JESUS ******* HATES YOU.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Jesus (censored by network) hates you.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 Texas is a very hostile state to abortion. And minorities, low-income people of color are really going to feel the burden of overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 Despite the barriers, I could still have my abortion. Doing so allowed me to escape an abusive relationship, graduate from college with honors, bear the family that I have now and be a part of the career that I feel passionate about.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We first met Malia last year. Her daughter, Maya, was just one whenshe found out she was unexpectedly pregnant again. She and her husband decided they weren't ready for another baby so fast.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 I have no shame. I have no regrets. My two abortions don't bother me.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONAL MAP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATES CEASED NEARLY ALL ABORTION SERVICES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 15 STATES\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Since the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe versus Wade, the laws in many states have been thrown into confusion and chaos. Texas is one of at least 15 states that have ceased nearly all abortion services.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 T Fund has had to pause all help line operations and other funding operations\par \par \uc2 ADVOCATE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 They know who abortion bans will affect.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 We're focusing on advocacy, right now. And then, moving forward, when we have more information, we hope to be able to legally resume a lot of our operations.\par \par \uc2 DEMONSTRATORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We will make the system fall.\par \par \uc2 DEMONSTRATOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Brick by brick.\par \par \uc2 DEMONSTRATORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Brick by brick.\par \par \uc2 DEMONSTRATOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Wall by wall.\par \par \uc2 DEMONSTRATORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Wall by wall.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) After finding out she was pregnant, Brooke needed to know how far along she was. There are no Planned Parenthoods or abortion providers in the Corpus Christi area. So, like many people, she turned to a crisis pregnancy center.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 It just seemed like a place where I could get an ultrasound and a pregnancy confirmation and kind of just give me some, some information.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Brooke went to the pregnancy center of the Coastal Bend.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 When I walked in, I wanted an abortion. That was my goal.\par \par \uc2 JANA PINSON (EXECUTIVE DIR. PREGNANCY CNTR OF THE COASTAL BEND)\par \par \uc2 I wasn't here that day, but heard about her immediately. So, this is one of our five pregnancy centers.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Executive director Jana Pinson gave us a tour of one location.\par \par \uc2 JANA PINSON (EXECUTIVE DIR. PREGNANCY CNTR OF THE COASTAL BEND)\par \par \uc2 This is one of our rooms where we take them through their options about adoption, about parenting, and about abortion. As soon as we get that positive pregnancy test, we take them to the ultrasound room. This is our lab, where we do pregnancy tests, we do STD testing. And this is our ultrasound room. We show them what's exactly in their womb.\par \par \uc2 JANA PINSON (EXECUTIVE DIR. PREGNANCY CNTR OF THE COASTAL BEND)\par \par \uc2 And that speaks for itself. So, if she'd come to us and seen these babies and chosen abortion, we would have loved her just the same. Nobody wants to have an abortion. You don't grow up going, man, I'd love to have an abortion someday. Right? And so it's a crisis, and it's a huge decision.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2,500+ CRISIS PREGNANCY CENTERS IN THE U.S.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) There are more than 2,500 locations known as crisis pregnancy centers across the United States. They are nonprofit organizations, often faith-based, and advertise free ultrasounds and pregnancy information. While they sometimes employ doctors and nurses, they are not usually certified medical clinics.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 3:1 NATIONALLY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 9:1 TEXAS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Nationally, they outnumber abortion providers by a three-to-one margin. In Texas, that ratio is at least nine-to-one. We asked Pinson to walk us through how they assess clients when they arrive.\par \par \uc2 JANA PINSON (EXECUTIVE DIR. PREGNANCY CNTR OF THE COASTAL BEND)\par \par \uc2 If a girl walks into any one of our centers and she's abortion-minded or she's thinking about abortion that day, we'll send a nurse, immediately. Abortion-minded, is that, that is the only thing they're thinking. Like, they don't see another thought, another way out. Abortion vulnerable is someone who doesn't know what they're gonna do. And then we have likely to carry, that we call them. And those are the people that come in, and they're just happy to be pregnant.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It was on the ultrasound here that Brooke discovered she wasn't just past the six-week mark, she was well past it. And\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 She goes, "Do twins run in your family?" And I said, "Nope." And she goes, "I think there's two." And I said, "Are you sure?" Like, terrified. "Are you sure?"\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Brooke says she left that day in shock.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 I think abortion was still on my mind. I think it took a little while for me to completely accept it.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Still ahead, answering criticism.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 They are not replacements for clinics. They should be shut down and defunded, entirely.\par \par \uc2 JANA PINSON (EXECUTIVE DIR. PREGNANCY CNTR OF THE COASTAL BEND)\par \par \uc2 We never force anyone to do anything.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Plus, Brooke makes plans for the future.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE: THE DECISION\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE: THE DECISION\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 "Nightline: The Decision," continues. Here again, Rachel Scott.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At just 18, Brooke Alexander was facing the unthinkable, an unplanned pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 I was just about to start school. I really wanted to kind of make a name for myself and make a good life for myself. And finding out I was pregnant really felt like it would halt that, and it would be, like, so much harder to achieve that.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) After finding out she was more than six weeks pregnant with twins, Brooke knew if she still wanted an abortion, she would have to leave her home state of Texas. That idea was daunting.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 I knew that I would have to go to, you know, New Mexico or these states that allow abortion. And I knew it would be kind of a process to go on a plane by myself, I mean, it had been years since I'd been on a plane, and have this procedure done. You know what I mean? Almost alone.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A family friend recommended a place called the Pregnancy Center of the Coastal Bend.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 It was a place that seemed like they could help. And I didn't know, like, the whole idea behind it was to steer girls away from abortion. I felt a little steering, yeah, but nothing crazy.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Texas, Crisis Pregnancy Centers, also known as CPCs, are not overseen or regulated by the state's Health And Human Services Department.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 If you're wanting an abortion and you go to a Crisis Pregnancy Center, that's not what you're gonna get. They're not going to help you get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Abortion rights advocate Maleeha Aziz is familiar with CPCs. She says they will say anything to stop someone from choosing abortion, including false advertising.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 They are not replacements for clinics. If it was up to me, if you're asking my opinion alone, they should be shut down and defunded, entirely.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Jana Pinson, who runs five centers in the Corpus Christi region, including the one Brooke visited, says her centers don't operate that way.\par \par \uc2 JANA PINSON (EXECUTIVE DIR. PREGNANCY CNTR OF THE COASTAL BEND)\par \par \uc2 We never say anything that is a strong like, you need to choose life. We never say those words. We feel it in our heart. So, the way people talk about it, like, you, you forced them to keep their baby. No. We've given them actually choice, a choice to choose life. To choose adoption. And if they choose abortion, we love them just the same.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Coastal Bend website does advertise that they are the number-one source of abortion information for the area, and Pinson says that is true.\par \par \uc2 JANA PINSON (EXECUTIVE DIR. PREGNANCY CNTR OF THE COASTAL BEND)\par \par \uc2 There is no abortion clinic anywhere in this area. We were named by the other side as the largest abortion desert. We used to have seven abortionists, and we haven't had one since 2014.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Pinson says, collectively, her staff includes nine registered nurses, a nurse practitioner, and two medical directors who are OB/GYNs. The website does include a disclaimer at the bottom of the page saying the centers do not offer or refer for pregnancy termination or birth control. It also says information is provided as an educational service. The organization does receive funding from the state, but Pinson claims the bulk of her budget comes from donations.\par \par \uc2 JANA PINSON (EXECUTIVE DIR. PREGNANCY CNTR OF THE COASTAL BEND)\par \par \uc2 The funding has always been through individuals and churches, foundations.\par \par \uc2 JANA PINSON (EXECUTIVE DIR. PREGNANCY CNTR OF THE COASTAL BEND)\par \par \uc2 We've got a beautiful baby boutique area, shoes, books, everything you would have in a full-scale thrift store.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The center also runs a secondhand retail store. The employees are moms from the pregnancy center who can bring their babies to work with them, like Adrianne (PH).\par \par \uc2 ADRIANNE (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 There was so many girls that wanted this job, so I didn't think I was going to get it. It saved my life. It really did.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The moms who work here can stay 18 months. During her time, Adriana has been able to finish school as a certified nursing assistant and has started a new job as a lactation specialist.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANNE (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 Working here has changed my life, working on training skills. I worked on a really great resume, building and training other girls. If I did it, you can do it, too.\par \par \uc2 JANA PINSON (EXECUTIVE DIR. PREGNANCY CNTR OF THE COASTAL BEND)\par \par \uc2 This begins our parenting program. Our mamas and dads can come and do education, parenting classes, and earn points while they do it. They'll come in, and they'll watch a video. And with those points, they'll get, like, 48 diapers, a package of wipes, and four outfits. Then if they needed, like, formula or food, we don't use points for those. Some of our clients have never had to buy diapers, at all. Isn't that the best?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Brooke tried out the parenting program once, but didn't feel like it was for her.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 We've always been on Medicaid, as far as insurance. So, for the girls, it kind of just rolled over.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She and Billy did realize they needed a plan for the future.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 "You have to support us," is what I told him. The next day, I think, he called me at work and was, like, "What if I join the Air Force?"\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Billy enlisted, and he and Brooke got married. He is now in basic training.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 I think that he's gonna stick by us. And maybe when the girls go to school, I can start working again, or maybe I can start college even before then. I think it'll be harder, but that doesn't mean that I can't handle it.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 Will you take this stuff out for me?\par \par \uc2 BILLY'S DAD (MALE)\par \par \uc2 You putting the baby in there?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) While Billy's gone, she's thankful for the help at home.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 I'm not a witch, okay? I know I can be, sometimes.\par \par \uc2 BILLY'S DAD (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Come here. What are you doing?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Billy's dad and his wife, Becca, help her juggle the challenges of two babies.\par \par \uc2 BILLY'S DAD (MALE)\par \par \uc2 She's doing great. We're pitching in a little bit more. She's hardworking at it.\par \par \uc2 BECCA (BILLY'S DAD'S WIFE)\par \par \uc2 She's impressive for her age, or just for being a mom in general.\par \par \uc2 BILLY'S DAD (MALE)\par \par \uc2 She's always calm and cool and does what needs to be done. I would say for an 18-year-old mother, she's a pretty dang good one.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Brooke and Billy's parents knew that when the teens found out about the pregnancy, they were considering abortion.\par \par \uc2 BILLY'S DAD (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I believe it's a woman's, you know, they have to discuss it, but nobody can tell her what to do. I think the mom is, is the boss when it comes to that.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Brooke realizes her story is happening at the same time so many other people are facing tough decisions. She wants the world to know she had a choice. She chose to be a mom, and she's happy with the outcome.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 I love being a mom. I, I love it so much. I love my babies. I mean, of course, at times, it can be a little overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But Brooke says she also understands every person's life is different, and she wants everyone to be able to make that choice for themselves.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ALEXANDER (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 I, I just want to be a good mom. And that's kind of my life mission, right now.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE: THE DECISION\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE: THE DECISION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MALEEHA IS WORKING ON NEW REPRODUCTIVE JUSTICE ADVOCACY PROGRAMS REPRESENTING MARGINALIZED GROUPS.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JANA PINSON SAYS HER ORGANIZATION WILL BE EXPANDING TO A NEW $10 MILLION CENTER IN CORPUS CHRISTI.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BROOKE JUST CELEBRATED HER 19TH BIRTHDAY. THE TWINS ARE NOW FIVE MONTHS OLD.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HER HUSBAND GRADUATES FROM BASIC TRAINING NEXT WEEK.\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020220804ei8300002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS BATTLE KANSAS VOTERS CHOOSE TO PROTECT RIGHT TO ABORTION\b0\par\par\uc2 770 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS BATTLE\par \par \uc2 KS, ABORTION, VOTE, BIDEN, O'BRIEN, MARSHALL, 2022\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We begin tonight with the surprise vote on abortion rights and what it could mean for the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Voters in the traditionally conservative, solid-red state of Kansas overwhelmingly protecting the right to abortion less than six weeks after the Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade. On the heels of the Kansas vote, today, President Biden saying voters of Kansas sent a powerful signal. Several of last night's key races are still undecided, including the Republican primary for Arizona's governor, testing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's hold on the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Democrats and Republicans are now looking for momentum, with less than 100 days until the midterms. ABC's Rachel Scott is in Kansas, and leads us off.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS BATTLE\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Washington, today, President Biden praising the surprise show of support for abortion rights in Kansas, the first time voters made their voices heard since the Supreme Court struck down Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 REGISTERED VOTER (KANSAS)\par \par \uc2 I am so proud of Kansas, right now.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KANSAS VOTERS CHOOSE TO PROTECT RIGHT TO ABORTION\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 The court practically dared women in this country to go to the ballot box and restore the right to choose that the court had just ripped away after 50 years.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KANSAS: 2020 ELECTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP 56.21%, BIDEN 41.56%\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) By a resounding margin of 61-39, Kansas voters rejected an amendment to strip away the right to an abortion from the state's Constitution. Kansas is a ruby-red state. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won here by 15 points, and there are 350,000 more registered Republicans than Democrats. But last night's vote wasn't even close. Turnout was overwhelming, 900,000 people showing up to vote on a Tuesday, in the middle of August. Voters we spoke to, like Breanna and Kyle O'Brien, told us the stakes could not be higher.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What do you think is at risk?\par \par \uc2 BREANNA O'BRIEN (REGISTERED VOTER KANSAS)\par \par \uc2 I would say, first and foremost, it's not just about Kansas women. This is gonna be an example that's set throughout the whole US.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For the O'Briens, it's personal. Breanna had an abortion in June after learning their child would be born with rare and severe genetic conditions.\par \par \uc2 BREANNA O'BRIEN (REGISTERED VOTER KANSAS)\par \par \uc2 Every family has, has, should have that right to decide what's best for their babies.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Democrats hope what happened in Kansas will shape the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 The voters of Kansas sent a powerful signal that, this fall, the American people will vote to preserve and protect the right and refuse to let them be ripped away by politicians.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But in Washington, the Republican senator from Kansas, Roger Marshall, says, not so fast. He admits he was stunned by the results in his state.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL (REP) (KANSAS)\par \par \uc2 I was absolutely surprised, and I have no explanation. And, and again, it was just a gut punch. That's all I can tell you.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But Marshall tells us voters will have other things on their minds when they go to elect candidates in November.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL (REP) (KANSAS)\par \par \uc2 I, I think voters come November will be very focused on the cost of gasoline and groceries and, and, and rent. That's all I hear about.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel Scott joins us, now, from Overland Park, Kansas. And Rachel, the results in Kansas are clearly the story of the night. But we also saw key primaries in five states and big victories from Republican candidates who had \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's seal of approval.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, Linsey. And the starkest example was in Arizona, where you have primary winners up and down the ballot who have embraced Trump's false claims about the 2020 election. The Republican winner for secretary of state in Arizona even attended Trump's rally on January 6th. If these candidates were to win in November, they would have a significant role in overseeing the next presidential election, Linsey.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel Scott, our thanks to you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220804ei830002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WORLD NEWS TONIGHT WITH DAVID MUIR\b0\par\par\uc2 507 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS BATTLE\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Tonight, America's battle over abortion rights now at the center of the midterm elections. Voters in Kansas soundly rejecting a measure that would've eliminated abortion as a right in the Constitution of this solidly red state, the first indication of where voters stand following the Supreme Court decision overturns Roe versus Wade. President Biden is calling the vote a decisive victory. How one state's vote could reshape the midterms. Rachel Scott, standing by.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 100 MILLION UNDER HEAT ALERTS\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Also, tonight, 100 million Americans under heat alerts tonight, extreme temperatures from Texas to Maine. In flood-ravaged Kentucky, where thousands are without water or power, the heat index hit triple digits, today. Rob Marciano, tracking it all.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALEX JONES: SANDY HOOK "100% REAL"\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The courtroom showdown between conspiracy theorist Alex Jones and the parents of a Sandy Hook victim. For years, he has called the massacre a hoax. What he finally conceded on the stand today, and the family's attorney dropping a surprise bombshell.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHOOTING SUSPECT PLEADS NOT GUILTY\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 News, tonight, about the alleged gunman charged in the deadly mass shooting during the July 4th parade in Highland Park, Illinois, answering to 117 felony counts in court today. And a new image of one of the youngest victims to survive the attack.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINE NUCLEAR PLANT "OUT OF CONTROL"\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The alarming report from the \uc2 UN\uc2 's nuclear chief. Europe's largest nuclear plant is, quote, "completely out of control." His urgent plea to Ukraine and Russia to help avoid a nuclear disaster.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SPEAKER PELOSI'S DEFIANT MESSAGE TO CHINA\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's defiant message to China, following her whirlwind trip to Taiwan and meeting with leaders and dissidents. China's now launching military drills around Taiwan, following her visit.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CONGRESSWOMAN KILLED IN CRASH\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Late word, tonight, about a US Congresswoman killed in a horrific car crash, members of her staff among the victims.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEATH-DEFYING FALL\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The death-defying fall, thousands of feet in the air. The paraglider tangled in his parachute, plunging toward the ground. How he saved himself at the last possible second.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VIN SCULLY: 1927-2022\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And the voice of America's pastime, the gold standard.\par \par \uc2 VIN SCULLY (SPORTS ANNOUNCER)\par \par \uc2 Swung it out and missed. A perfect game.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Tributes to Dodgers announcer Vin Scully. More than six decades in sports broadcasting, one of baseball's greatest and most beloved icons.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC WORLD NEWS TONIGHT WITH DAVID MUIR\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 From ABC News world headquarters in New York, this is "World News Tonight with David Muir."\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening, everyone. Thanks so much for joining us on this Wednesday. I'm Linsey Davis, in for David.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220804ei830002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS BATTLE ON THE BALLOT KANSAS VOTERS HIT POLLS FOR FIRST REFERENDUM\b0\par\par\uc2 753 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BIDEN, AL QAEDA, ZAWAHIRI, PELOSI, TAIWAN, CHINA\par \par \uc2 AL QAEDA, ZAWAHIRI, SULLIVAN, PELOSI, CHINA, TAIWAN\par \par \uc2 SEARCH, FLOODING, BESHEAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEATH TOLL RISES IN KENTUCKY FLOOD DISASTER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AT LEAST 37 KILLED, HUNDREDS STILL UNACCOUNTED FOR\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRE, EVACUATION, JONES, FREEMAN\par \par \uc2 PRIMARY, ELECTIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH-STAKES PRIMARIES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTERS HEAD TO THE POLLS IN FIVE STATES\par \par \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS, VOTE\par \par \uc2 GUINNESS, MINI GOLF\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MINI GOLF WORLD RECORD!\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FOUR MEN PLAY 2,097 HOLES IN 24 HOURS TO RAISE MONEY FOR KENTUCKY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 MONKEYPOX, OUTBREAK, COLLINS, BENTER, PITTS\par \par \uc2 MONKEYPOX, OUTBREAK, VACCINE\par \par \uc2 GRINER, TRIAL, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 DAWSON, BAKER, FIJI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIJI HONEYMOON MURDER CASE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FORMER COLLEGE GIRLFRIEND OF AMERICAN CHARGED WITH KILLING NEW WIFE SPEAK\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS, SOCCER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 STORY2\par \par \uc2 DRONE, ZAWAHIRI, AL QAEDA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: U.S. DRONE STRIKE KILLS TOP AL QAEDA LEADER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT BIDEN: "JUSTICE HAS BEEN DELIVERED"\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, REFERENDUM, BALLOT, KANSAS, O'BRIEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS BATTLE ON THE BALLOT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KANSAS VOTERS HIT POLLS FOR FIRST REFERENDUM\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And George, now to primary day in five states. In Kansas, a high-stakes vote on abortion rights, the issue on the ballot for the first time since the Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade. Let's go back to our congressional Rachel Scott. She's there in Overland Park, Kansas. Good morning again, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning to you, again, Robin. And polls just opened minutes ago. The battle over abortion rights has turned to the states and, for the first time since the fall of Roe, voters are weighing in directly.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, with abortion on the ballot in Kansas, activists on both sides say the stakes could not be higher.\par \par \uc2 PETER CARA (PRO-LIFE CANVASSER)\par \par \uc2 I'm excited to see what the people of Kansas decide, but the lives of all these unborn children are at stake.\par \par \uc2 JACKIE KLAPPER (PRO-LIFE CANVASSER)\par \par \uc2 I believe strongly that women need to have this choice for themselves.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It will be up to the voters to decide whether the state's constitution should remove protections for abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 GABBY LARA (PRO-LIFE CANVASSER)\par \par \uc2 We want to not only make abortion illegal but unthinkable.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) If the amendment passes, it will clear the way for Republican state lawmakers to restrict access to abortion or ban it outright. We sat down with Brianna and Kyle O'Brien. The couple says they made a decision to terminate their pregnancy in June, after learning their child was diagnosed with rare and severe genetic conditions, causing significant disabilities and a shortened lifespan.\par \par \uc2 BREANNA O'BRIEN (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 And the best decision for me would be to have my baby, like, to be able to hold him and cuddle him, but I knew he couldn't be an infant forever. Every family has, should have that right to decide what's best for their babies.\par \par \uc2 KYLE O'BRIEN (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I mean, just quality of life, is it worth, is it worth living? And I would argue not in those circumstances.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The couple now sharing their story ahead of today's primary.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What is at risk this election day?\par \par \uc2 BREANNA O'BRIEN (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Every woman's right to freedom.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, activists say what happens here in Kansas will send a message to the rest of the country, but also it will have broader implications because Kansas has been a safe haven for out-of-state abortion patients, guys.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, thank you, Rachel. Coming up in our "GMA Morning Menu," the backlash over a lyric on Beyonce's new album, "Renaissance." What the superstar is doing now.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Also this morning, a little boy's battle against a mystery illness caused by a tick bite. The symptoms that all parents need to be on the lookout for.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Plus, are dollar stores a grocery gold mine? Becky Worley checked out prices as more people, you know, looking for bargains, and we are live in Tampa for day two of our Dog Days of Summer series. Look at them all there. There's Lori Bergamotto with a pup that could be your new best friend. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220901ei820000j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS BATTLE ON THE BALLOT KANSAS VOTERS HIT POLLS\b0\par\par\uc2 349 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BIDEN, AL QAEDA, ZAWAHIRI, PELOSI, TAIWAN, CHINA\par \par \uc2 AL QAEDA, ZAWAHIRI, SULLIVAN, PELOSI, CHINA, TAIWAN\par \par \uc2 SEARCH, FLOODING, BESHEAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEATH TOLL RISES IN KENTUCKY FLOOD DISASTER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AT LEAST 37 KILLED, HUNDREDS STILL UNACCOUNTED FOR\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRE, EVACUATION, JONES, FREEMAN\par \par \uc2 PRIMARY, ELECTIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH-STAKES PRIMARIES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTERS HEAD TO THE POLLS IN FIVE STATES\par \par \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS, VOTE\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You're there in Arizona, but in Kansas, a high-stakes vote on abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS BATTLE ON THE BALLOT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KANSAS VOTERS HIT POLLS FOR FIRST REFERENDUM SINCE SUPREME COURT RULING\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The issue on the ballot for the first time since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. Our congressional correspondent Rachel Scott is in Overland Park, Kansas, for us. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Robin, good morning. And, yes, it is the issue, not the candidates that is taking center stage in today's primary. Kansas becoming the first state in the nation to put this question of abortion rights directly to voters after Roe v. Wade was overturned.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Polls are just about to open here and advocates on both sides say the stakes could not be higher. They were out canvassing, door-knocking, urging people to vote. On the ballot, whether the state's Constitution should be amended to remove protections for abortion rights. Many here say this could lead to an outright ban on abortion in the state of Kansas. But for now, it is still legal. This state has become a safe haven for women traveling out of state, where abortion has been severely restricted.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) So, this will have broader implications for the rest of the country. And it's the first time that voters are weighing in in a post-Roe America, Robin.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And a lot of people are going to be watching. All right, thank you, Rachel. Michael?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Robin.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220901ei8200007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PRIMARY BATTLE ARIZONA GOVERNOR'S RACE & KANSAS ABORTION LAWS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,941 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOPIC\par \par \uc2 CONTENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 LAKE, ROBSON, TRUMP, PENCE, KANSAS, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. Thank you for joining us. Tonight, results coming in from hotly contested primaries across the country, and in Kansas, a major victory for abortion rights advocates. With the midterms less than 100 days away, Republicans in Arizona hoping to define their party's future. Whit Johnson is on the ground, in Paradise Valley.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Byron, the ballots are still being counted, but the energy is high here at Kerry Lake headquarters. Her campaign staffers tell me that Lake is taking in the gravity of the moment. Remember, she was a local news anchor here for more than 20 years. She's used to covering elections. Now, she's at the center of one.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Meanwhile, her top rival, her opponent in the Republican primary, Karrin Taylor Robson, she's the GOP establishment pick, she believes that she has all the momentum, right now. And she's showing well in some of the early results that are coming in. And she thinks that she is peaking at just the right time. This is a primary election that has gained national interest, and it's a battle over the identity of the Republican Party, putting former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  up against his former Vice President, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) With polls closed in Arizona and ballots being counted, supporters of Kari Lake hope she will be the Republican nominee for Arizona governor. Kari Lake, a former TV news anchor, catapulted into the national spotlight after being endorsed by former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 No one understands better than Kari how to fight back against the fake news media and the radical left.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight's primaries testing former President Trump's power over the Republican Party and perhaps a signal of where the GOP is headed.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The Trump endorsement meant a lot in Arizona. And the idea of running on the MAGA and Trump banner, that is how Kari Lake tried to define herself, her loyalty to the false claims around the last election. She did not have the Republican establishment backing.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Lake has been a fervent supporter of Trump, continuing to push the false claim that the 2020 election was stolen.\par \par \uc2 CLIP OF JULY 22 SPEECH\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (REP) (ARIZONA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 We will no longer accept corruptness, and I know for a fact we will no longer accept rigged elections. Who's with me on that?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Arizona was not stolen. Biden won this state by more than 10,000 votes. Lake's opponent, real estate developer Karrin Taylor Robson, has the endorsement of Trump's vice president, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , but even she hedged when I asked her this.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Do you think the election was legitimate?\par \par \uc2 KARRIN TAYLOR ROBSON (REP) (ARIZONA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I believe the wrong guy is in the White House. I also believe that my opponent and the media is obsessed with 2020. I'm obsessed with 2022 and 2024.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Trump's endorsement, paired with Lake's fiery rhetoric\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (REP) (ARIZONA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 When mama goes MAGA, it is game over.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) \u8230\'20\'26appealed to many voters.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SANCHEZ (ARIZONA VOTER)\par \par \uc2 All of my morals, they're, they align with Kari. I think she's very brave. I think she's also very loud, and she's also a fighter. I don't feel it's a (inaudible), at all. And that's why I'm here.\par \par \uc2 CLIP OF NEWS CAST BY ANCHOR KARI LAKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FOX 10, ONE-ON-ONE WITH THE GOVERNOR, DUCEY DISCUSSES RANGE OF TOPICS\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (REP) (ARIZONA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2  welcoming tech\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For decades, Lake worked at the Fox-affiliate station in Phoenix before quitting journalism, a move some of her supporters find admirable.\par \par \uc2 JASON BAKER (ARIZONA VOTER)\par \par \uc2 You know, doing the news every night for a decade-more, you know what the problems are in your valley, in your area, and you can deal with that, now. And she's a humble candidate.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But other voters, like Matt Kenny, we're hoping to move on from the chaos that has engulfed the state since 2020.\par \par \uc2 MATT KENNY (ARIZONA VOTER)\par \par \uc2 I can't just keep being dragged, like, through this, over and over again. He keeps saying, "Hey, there was voter fraud." And then we say, "Okay, show it to us. And then they show us nothing."\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, you're looking forward. You don't wanna look back.\par \par \uc2 MATT KENNY (ARIZONA VOTER)\par \par \uc2 I'm looking forward. Yeah, that's absolutely right.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Those voters choosing Robson, who is back by former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 There is only on candidate in this race who will be ready on day one to lead Arizona even to greater heights, and that's Karrin Taylor Robson.\par \par \uc2 JOHN MENDIBLES (ARIZONA VOTER)\par \par \uc2 We want level heads. We don't want no more craziness. We've had enough of that. Right? That's behind us. We're only concerned about what's in front of us.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Many have tried to make this race about a battle versus the GOP establishment and the Trump MAGA loyalists. Do you see the race that way?\par \par \uc2 KARRIN TAYLOR ROBSON (REP) (ARIZONA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I see this race about the future of Arizona and what direction we're going. Arizona has a lot of challenges, like many states across this country. And Arizonans are looking for tough, principled, experienced leadership. They're tired of the drama.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's too early to know which side will prevail.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 As polarizing as Trump is, in many circles, and as many people, even the Republican Party are, are trying to rally to oppose him and his choices, he remains an extraordinarily valuable commodity in Republican politics.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Another test of Trump's hold over the party in Arizona, the race for state Senate between Trump-backed candidate David Farnsworth and the current State Speaker of the House \uc2 Rusty Bowers\uc2 . Trump attacked Bowers on the campaign trail, after he testified during the January 6th hearings.\par \par \uc2 CLIP OF JULY 22 SPEECH\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Rusty Bowers\uc2 , he's a RINO coward who participated against the Republican Party in the totally partisan, unselect committee of political thugs and hacks.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Bower's telling the committee that despite immense pressure from Trump and his associates, he refused to overturn Arizona's election results.\par \par \uc2 CLIP OF JUNE 21 STATEMENT\par \par \uc2 RUSTY BOWERS (REP) (ARIZONA HOUSE SPEAKER)\par \par \uc2 I said, "Look, you are asking me to do something that is counter to my oath when I swore to the Constitution to uphold it."\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (DEM) (CALIFORNIA)\par \par \uc2 And to quote the former president, "During the conversation he told me the election was rigged, and that I won Arizona," unquote. Did you have such a conversation with the President?\par \par \uc2 RUSTY BOWERS (REP) (ARIZONA HOUSE SPEAKER)\par \par \uc2 I did have a conversation with the President. That certainly isn't it. Anywhere, anyone, any time has said that that I said the election was rigged, that would not be true.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) He spoke to my colleague, \uc2 Jon Karl\uc2 , last week.\par \par \uc2 JON KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, let's go back to what Trump actually wanted you to do after the election in 2020, and \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 . I mean, they, they, they basically wanted you to overturn the results in Arizona. I mean, that's\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 RUSTY BOWERS (REP) (ARIZONA HOUSE SPEAKER)\par \par \uc2 That would be the result of what they asked, yes.\par \par \uc2 JON KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Did you ever consider going along with it? Did you ever\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 RUSTY BOWERS (REP) (ARIZONA HOUSE SPEAKER)\par \par \uc2 The idea of throwing out the election of the president is like, okay, so what part of stupider do I get to land on and colonize?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Bowers, a longtime Republican state lawmaker, voted for Trump twice, but not again.\par \par \uc2 RUSTY BOWERS (REP) (ARIZONA HOUSE SPEAKER)\par \par \uc2 I'll never vote for him. But I won't have to, because I think America's tired. And there's some absolutely forceful, qualified, morally defensible and upright people. And that's what I want. That's what I want in my party, and that's what I want to see.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Primaries were held in five states, tonight, but in Kansas, voters weighed in on another hot-button issue.\par \par \uc2 KANSAS ADVOCATE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 We want to not only make abortion illegal, but unthinkable.\par \par \uc2 KANSAS ADVOCATE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I believe strongly that women need to have this choice for themselves.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For the first time since the overturning of Roe v. Wade, abortion on the ballot. Kansans voting to keep constitutional abortion protections that allow most abortions up to 22 weeks, a rare instance where citizens, not just legislators, have a say this their state's abortion laws. My colleague Rachel Scott.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tonight, a major victory for abortion rights advocates here in Kansas. It is a strong signal that this state, these voters, do value abortion rights. The battle still not over, yet. There are 14 states where abortion has all but stopped. Others are considering bans, as well. But for this group, those gathered here, today, they were shattered by the news of Roe versus Wade being overturned, weeks ago. Today, they are celebrating this victory and hope that it sends a message ahead of the critical midterm elections, less than 100 days away.\par \par \uc2 KYLE O'BRIEN (KANSAS VOTER)\par \par \uc2 Kansas has long prided itself as, for being a free state, and prizing freedom.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Couples like Briana and Kyle O'Brien are relieved abortion protections will remain in their state. The devout Catholics terminated their pregnancy in June after learning their child was diagnosed with two rare genetic conditions that would cause significant disability and a shortened life span.\par \par \uc2 BREANNA O'BRIEN (KANSAS VOTER)\par \par \uc2 Every family has, has, should have that right to decide what's best for their babies. It's not just about Kansas women. This is gonna be an example that's set throughout the whole US.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The fiercely competitive races across the ballot giving us a glimp (sic) of what's to come in November, and ultimately which party could control Congress and beyond.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) If we learned anything from this primary season, is that uncertainties around electoral politics continue. There continue to be upsets. There continue to be surprises. There continue to be issues that the change is almost by the day, almost by the week. And it's all going to culminate in 2022 and, ultimately, 2024. There's still a lot of dividing and a lot of anger for this country to work itself through.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our thanks to Whit.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LUKE BRYAN\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Up next, the country superstar Luke Bryan getting ready to take country music's biggest stage.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020220803ei8200002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 VOTERS IN KANSAS DECIDING CRITICAL ABORTION ISSUE STATES STOPPING NEARLY ALL ABORTION SERVICES\b0\par\par\uc2 345 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ZAWAHIRI, KILLED, DRONE, AL-QAEDA, BIDEN, KIRBY\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, TAIWAN, ONE-CHINA, GANG, BEIJING, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 CALIFORNIA, WILDFIRE, DEATHS, EVACUATION\par \par \uc2 KENTUCKY, FLOOD, VICTIMS, BESHEAR, POWER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KENTUCKY\par \par \uc2 WATCHDOG, TRUMP, TEXTS, DELETED, USSS, DOD, FRA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JANUARY 6TH PROBE\par \par \uc2 RACE, LAKE, ROBSON, FOSTER, TRUMP, PENCE, KENNEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH-STAKES CONTESTS\par \par \uc2 KANSAS, ABORTION, VOTE, ROE V WADE, DOJ, IDAHO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRST POST-ROE VOTE\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We go now to Kansas, where abortion is on the ballot, tonight. Voters are deciding whether to change the state's Constitution, which considers abortion a fundamental right. ABC's Rachel Scott is there. And, and Rachel, this is the first time that we're seeing abortion on the ballot since Roe versus Wade was overturned.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEVELOPING NOW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION ON THE BALLOT\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's exactly right, Linsey. Kansas now becoming the first state in the nation to put the question of abortion rights directly to voters after the fall of Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTERS IN KANSAS DECIDING CRITICAL ABORTION ISSUE\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) If this amendment passes, advocates say that it would clear the way for the state of Kansas to severely restrict access to abortion or ban it, outright. Already across the country, at least 14 states have stopped nearly all abortion services. And today's vote comes as the Justice Department launched its first lawsuit against the state of Idaho, set to enact a near total ban on abortion in just a matter of weeks.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATES STOPPING NEARLY ALL ABORTION SERVICES\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The Attorney General saying while the state's law does make an exception to save the life of the mother, it does not make exceptions for medical emergencies, a clear violation of federal law. And the Department of Justice now sending a very strong message to states with similar restrictions, Linsey.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's so interesting to see how voters decide. Rachel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220803ei8200008\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 8:30 HALF HOUR\b0\par\par\uc2 422 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 July 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MIDDLE EAST MEETINGS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, BACKLASH, SAUDIS, SALMON, KHASHOGGI, AL-JUBEIR\par \par \uc2 KIRBY, BIDEN, CROWN PRINCE, KHASHOGGI, OIL, SAUDI ARABIA\par \par \uc2 CRASH, PILEUP, HIGHWAY, DUST STORM, DROUGHT\par \par \uc2 COVID, CASES, HOSPITALS, TESTING, MANDATES\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, ATTACKS, RUSSIA, RESCUED, ALEXNDROV, GATELEY\par \par \uc2 WALKER, AUTOPSY, KOHLER, WOUNDS, MYLETT, ABBARNO\par \par \uc2 TOPS, REOPENING, SHOOTING, MEMORIAL, PATTERSON, BROWN\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRES, FIREFIGHTERS, EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 MEGA MILLIONS, JACKPOT, JULY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 DEREK JETER ("THE CAPTAIN")\par \par \uc2 One thing with me, I'm very loyal. Loyalty one way is stupidity.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 There he is, the captain. Welcome back to "GMA" on this Saturday morning. That right there is a clip from the new \uc2 ESPN\uc2  film's seven-part documentary, "The Captain," with Derek Jeter. It debuts this coming Monday. Coming up, we'll have a live report from Los Angeles where this weekend's MLB All-Star Game and all the festivities that come with it are taking place.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 We have a lot of \uc2 Yankees\uc2  fans.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Yes, we do. We're excited about that.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE ABORTION BILLS\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 We certainly do. Well, let's take a look at some of the other big stories we're following this morning. Happening right now, the House has passed two abortion bills aimed at protecting access to abortion in the wake of the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe V. Wade.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 One protects women who travel across state lines to get an abortion and the providers who treat them. The other would enshrine Roe V. Wade into law. But the odds of these bills making it through the Senate are incredibly slim since Senate Republicans have already rejected them.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Also right now, a fugitive drug kingpin convicted of killing an American DEA agent has been captured in Mexico. Rafael Caro-Quintero was convicted in Mexico of a 1985 murder, but he was released early by a judge in 2013 and was on the run ever since. The US says it will seek extradition. He faces several counts of drug trafficking here.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And the New York medical examiner saying former President Trump's first wife, Ivana, died of blunt impact injuries to her torso sustained as the result of a fall. The 73-year-old was found dead at the bottom of the stairs in her Manhattan apartment on Thursday. Officials say her death was an accident and is not suspicious.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220802ei7g0005w\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ARREST IN CHILD RAPE CASE YOUNG GIRL DREW GLOBAL ATTENTION ABORTION BATTLE\b0\par\par\uc2 526 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 July 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 VA, MD, STATE OF EMERG., FLOODING, FIRES, SEARCH\par \par \uc2 PRICES, CPI, ZANDI, RODRIGUEZ, SANDERSON, BIDEN, BOA\par \par \uc2 UVALDE, POLICE, RESPONSE, VIDEO, REYES, CROSS\par \par \uc2 CHILD, RAPE, ABORTION, SUSPECT, ARREST, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Next tonight here, that major development in a disturbing case that's become a national flashpoint in America's battle over abortion rights, after Roe was overturned. The 10-year-old Ohio girl who had been raped, who went to Indiana to get an abortion, because there are no exceptions for rape in Ohio. Some had claimed the family story was not true. Tonight, a suspect has now been charged in the case, and what Ohio authorities are now saying. Here's Alex Perez.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW DEVELOPMENTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ARREST IN CHILD RAPE CASE\par \par \uc2 ALEX PEREZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, an Ohio man is under arrest, charged in the rape of a 10-year-old girl whose case made national headlines. Authorities say that girl went to another state, to Indiana, to get an abortion. Ohio, instituting a ban on nearly all abortions after six weeks with no exceptions for rape, following the overturning of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 JUDGE CYNTHIA EBNER (FRANKLIN COUNTY MUNICIPAL COURT)\par \par \uc2 The charge is rape, felony, the first degree, under 13 years old.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXT OF STATEMENT FROM DAVE YOST\par \par \uc2 ALEX PEREZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The 27-year-old suspect, who authorities say is undocumented, arraigned in a Columbus courtroom, today. Prosecutors saying he admitted to having sexual contact with the girl. The Ohio attorney general issuing a statement, saying, "My heart aches for the pain suffered by this young child. Justice must be served, and the Bureau of Criminal Investigations stands ready to support law enforcement across Ohio putting these criminals behind bars." Last month, the girl's mother reporting to Child Services that her daughter was pregnant.\par \par \uc2 DETECTIVE JEFFREY HUHN (COLUMBUS POLICE DEPARTMENT)\par \par \uc2 The victim went to, went out of state to have a medical, medically terminated - abortion.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PEREZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The story making headlines worldwide after the young girl traveled from Ohio to Indianapolis, Indiana for an abortion. Abortion rights advocates outraged. They're saying this is the new reality. Among those horrified by the case, President Biden, who just days ago signed an executive order to protect access to abortion, and pointing to the case of this young girl.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Ten years old. Ten years old, raped, six weeks pregnant, already traumatized, was forced to travel to another state. Imagine being that little girl. Just - I'm serious. Just imagine being that little girl.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PEREZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And David, this case, just one example of how polarizing this case can be across the country. David, there were many people who did not believe this young girl's story, but of course, prosecutors today confirming her account with today's arrest. That suspect is being held on $2 million bail. He has not yet entered a plea. If convicted, he could face life in prison. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Alex Perez, tonight. Alex, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220715ei7d00005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS PROTESTERS MARCH TO WHITE HOUSE MARCHES CALL ON BIDEN TO DO MORE TO PROTECT ACCESS\b0\par\par\uc2 793 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 July 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS MORNING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SAVING THE SEQUOIAS\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRE, SEQUOIAS, EVACUATIONS, YOSEMITE\par \par \uc2 ABE, INVESTIGATION, SECURITY, LAPSE\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, GRINER, TRIAL, RICHARDSON, PLEA, ROOD, REZAIAN\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, MIDDLE EAST, OIL, GAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH-STAKES TRIP TO THE MIDDLE EAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT BIDEN TO MEET WITH CROWN PRINCE AMID HIGH U.S. GAS PRICES\par \par \uc2 CAPITOL, RIOT, HEARINGS\par \par \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS, HEARING, CAPITOL RIOT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS PROTESTERS MARCH TO THE WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARCHES CALL ON BIDEN TO DO MORE TO PROTECT ACCESS TO SERVICES\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And thousands of abortion rights protesters marched to the White House on Saturday, calling on President Biden to do more to protect access.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The President signed an executive order on Friday seeking to protect access to abortion care and other reproductive health services. But the protest organizers and some Democrats say it doesn't go far enough to safeguard women's rights.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Joining us now from Washington is ABC political reporter, Britney Shepherd. Thanks so much for being with us, this morning.\par \par \uc2 BRITNEY SHEPHERD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Of course, happy to be here.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEBATE OVER REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PUSH FROM BOTH SIDES FOR LEGISLATORS TO ACT AS CONGRESS RETURNS\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's start with the push from both sides to get legislators to act on abortion. Congress is back in session this week. What are the odds on any movement on abortion rights?\par \par \uc2 BRITNEY SHEPHERD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, Eva, the odds are slim. But that's not going to stop Democrats from trying to make strides. They're going to operate under two channels, through Congress and through the White House. Speaker Pelosi saying that she is going to bring legislation to codify Roe back on the floor of the House. About a year ago exactly, last year, the House passed legislation, tried to codify Roe, and it failed, unsurprisingly, in the Senate. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is also going to bring a second piece of legislation that would banned states that have already banned abortions from penalizing those leaving states seeking abortions, from either fining them, jailing them or any other punitive action against folks who want abortion who would leave and travel.\par \par \uc2 BRITNEY SHEPHERD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think the situation in the Senate is going to be a lot less clear. We already, they already don't have Republican votes on their side and they already know that the White House is limited in their scope to push Republicans from the bully pulpit. As we just heard, Biden signed an executive order last week to help those who were seeking pill abortions have more access through \uc2 HHS\uc2 , but there are really few details on how they're going to put rubber to the road to help states, to help them get money, and protect them from punitive action from Republican legislatures. You hear progressives and Democrats saying, while they are accepting any help they can get from DC, it just might be just a little too late.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And let's go back to what he heard from Kenneth's report, those January 6th hearings are resuming, this week. Has the committee made a strong case? And do you think the American public is even paying attention?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FINAL TWO JAN 6 HEARINGS TO BEGIN THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HAS THE COMMITTEE MADE A STRING CASE\par \par \uc2 BRITNEY SHEPHERD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, they surely hope so. I think the question is, who's watching? We're going to hear this week, the crystallized final message that they hope that they can get to the American public that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  not only was cognizant, was instrumental in overthrowing Congress in trying to jimmy up the election results. I think that Republicans know, Republicans legislators know that there are really important primaries coming up in August, especially for \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  and that the January 6th hearings this week will help those voters push from one way to the other.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Britney, thanks so much for your analysis. And just a reminder, tune into "This Week" later this morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS MORNING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GINA RAIMONDO, SECRETARY OF COMMERCE\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) George Stephanopoulos goes one-on-one with Commerce Secretary \uc2 Gina Raimondo\uc2  about the state of the economy and the Biden administration's latest plan to fight inflation.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REP. \uc2 ADAM KINZINGER\uc2  (REP-IL), MEMBER JAN. 6 COMMITTEE\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Plus, George has an exclusive with Republican Representative \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  ahead of the January 6th committee's final public hearings. Janai?\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220729ei7a0008j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 DO YOU BUY THAT? NATE SILVER FROM FIVETHIRTYEIGHT\b0\par\par\uc2 520 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 July 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 JOBS, INFLATION, BIDEN, BOSTIC, POLL, FINANCE, GAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RED-HOT ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO, RECESSION, GAS, BIDEN, POLL, CHIPS, OIL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE-ON-ONE WITH COMMERCE SECRETARY GINA RAIMONDO\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON, RESIGNS, ZELENSKYY, WEAPONS, ABE, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL, ABE, ASSASSINATED, JAPAN, GUNS, LEGACY\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER, JAN 6, CIPOLLONE, TRUMP, BANNON, AUDITS\par \par \uc2 SILVER, 538, SCOTUS, ABORTION, MIDTERMS, VOTERS\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We need two additional pro-choice senators and a pro-choice House to codify Roe as federal law. Your vote can make that a reality. If you want to change the circumstances for women and even little girls in this country, please go out and vote.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Biden announcing new executive actions aimed at protecting abortion rights after two more state abortion bans went into effect this week. Democrats are counting on the issue to energize their voters for the midterms, but will it be enough to stave off big losses? Here's Nate Silver of FiveThirtyEight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DO YOU BUY THAT?\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (FIVETHIRTYEIGHT)\par \par \uc2 Traditionally, abortion is an issue that motivated Republican voters, but this year might be different. Let's start, of course, with the data. Six polling firms, since the court's decision, asked voters who they prefer for Congress. On average, those polled showed the Democrats ahead by 1.5 percentage points. But here's the thing, if you look at what the same poll showed just before the abortion ruling, they had Republicans up by 1.3 points, on average. All six polls have shown some type of movement toward Democrats. It could be a statistical quirk, but it's certainly worth keeping an eye on. But let's back up a second. Why does the President's party, in this case, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats, almost always lose ground in the midterms?\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (FIVETHIRTYEIGHT)\par \par \uc2 One explanation from political science is that voters want to balance the scales. Usually, if a party controls both the presidency and the Congress, they'll implement changes that some voters think go too far, like in 2010, when Obamacare was unpopular and contributed to a 63-seat Republican gain in the House. But this year, Conservative judges have reversed laws for federal government policies of everything from abortion to requiring masks on airplanes. So, the traditional roles are somewhat reversed.\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (FIVETHIRTYEIGHT)\par \par \uc2 Where the Supreme Court won't help Democrats is with the economy. This week's Monmouth poll found that 36 percent of voters mentioned inflation as their most important concern, and 15 percent said gas prices, compared to just 5 percent who said abortion. But it may give Democrats an enthusiasm boost. A recent Marist College poll found that 78 percent of Democratic voters are more likely to vote in the midterms in response to the abortion decision. On the whole, the odds still favor Republicans. But I buy that Roe being overturned evens the equation a bit toward Democrats.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Our thanks to Nate Silver. The Roundtable's next. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220713ei7a00007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PRESIDENT SIGNS REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS EXECUTIVE ORDER BIDEN AIMS TO STRENGTHEN EXISTING LAWS\b0\par\par\uc2 667 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 July 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRE DANGER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER\par \par \uc2 YOSEMITE, WILDFIRE, SEQUOIAS, HUMIDITY\par \par \uc2 ABE, ASSASSINATION, MOURNING, TRIBUTES, YAMAGAMI\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, EXECUTIVE ORDER, ABORTION, CARMONA, NEWTON\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Bob Woodruff for us there on the ground in Tokyo. Back here at home now to President Biden getting pushback from activists on both sides of the abortion issue after signing an executive order aimed at protecting abortion access nationwide. ABC's Alex Presha is at the White House with why some say the President is going too far while others say he isn't going far enough. Alex, good morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT SIGNS REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS EXECUTIVE ORDER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN AIMS TO STRENGTHEN EXISTING LAWS ON ACCESS TO CARE & SERVICES\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Janai. President Biden has been clear that his power on this is limited, urging Americans to make this a ballot issue in November. But this new executive order reaffirming his administration's commitment to enforcing existing laws.\par \par \uc2 DEMONSTRATORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Bring abortion on demand.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The pressure from allies for President Biden to protect abortion rights started with the leaked Supreme Court draft, and only intensified when the high court overturned Roe V. Wade. Friday he signed an executive order while blasting the court's decision.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 The truth is today's Supreme Court majority that is playing fast and loose with the facts.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Biden's executive order expands access to medication abortion and emergency contraceptive, it safeguards patients' privacy, protects mobile clinics used to offer care for out-of-state patients, and bolsters legal services to protect the rights to travel out of state for abortion. Health and Human Services Secretary \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2  now has 30 days to develop a game plan. Some abortion rights advocates say, this should have come sooner.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL O'LEARY CARMONA (EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR WOMEN'S MARCH)\par \par \uc2 The fact it took President Biden two weeks to issue the executive order was disappointing. We want to see the whole of government response that he promised last year enacted.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN FACES BACKLASH OVER EXECUTIVE ORDER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT CRITICIZED BY SOME ON THE BOTH SIDES OF REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS FIGHT\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The White House telling our Rachel Scott this.\par \par \uc2 WHITE HOUSE AIDE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 This is the not going to be solved by one day's actions. The report from the Secretary is just one step in the process.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Anti-abortion rights advocates casting doubt.\par \par \uc2 ANTI-ABORTION RIGHTS ADVOCATE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I think it'll be a big nothing burger to be honest with you.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At least 14 states have stopped nearly all abortion services. Louisiana is the latest. There a temporary restraining order blocking the state's trigger laws has expired, effectively banning almost all abortions for now.\par \par \uc2 LILLIAN NEWTON (PATIENT ADVOCATE NEW HOPE MEDICAL)\par \par \uc2 We still have to call and cancel appointments.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LOUISIANA BAN ON REPRODUCTIVE SERVICES TAKES EFFECT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATE SUPREME COURT MOVES LEGAL CHALLENGE TO DIFFERENT JURISDICTION\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Lillian Newton is a patient advocate at New Hope Medical in Shreveport. Abortion rights advocates there are now hoping for a successful challenge to the state's ban. The case delayed, transferred to a different court's jurisdiction.\par \par \uc2 LILLIAN NEWTON (PATIENT ADVOCATE NEW HOPE MEDICAL)\par \par \uc2 We are still fighting this. We will be here as long as we possibly can be to answer questions.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PENNSYLVANIA SENATE PROPOSAL ADVANCED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAN CHANGES STATE CONSTITUTION ALLOWING VOTERS TO DECIDE ON POSSIBLE BAN\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Abortion rights could also soon be on the ballot in Pennsylvania. A Republican proposal there passed this week that could add a provision to the state's constitution and jeopardize abortion rights in the state. It could be on the ballot as soon as next spring. Whit.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220802ei790002w\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PRESIDENT SIGNS REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS EXECUTIVE ORDER BIDEN AIMS TO STRENGTHEN EXISTING LAWS\b0\par\par\uc2 575 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 July 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRE DANGER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER\par \par \uc2 YOSEMITE, WILDFIRE, SEQUOIAS, HUMIDITY\par \par \uc2 ABE, ASSASSINATION, MOURNING, TRIBUTES, YAMAGAMI\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, EXECUTIVE ORDER, ABORTION, CARMONA, NEWTON\par \par \uc2 MUSK, TWITTER, WITHDRAWAL, SUE, BREAKUP FEE\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, FUNERALS, VICTIMS, LOIZZI, INJURED\par \par \uc2 UVALDE, POLICE, RESPONSE, MASSACRE\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, HIMARS, AID, RUSSIA, DRUEKE, ANTONOV\par \par \uc2 GAS, PRICES, DECREASE, OIL, DEMAND\par \par \uc2 WIMBLEDON, JABEAUR, RYBAKINA, DJOKOVIC, KYRGIOS, BRENNAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "SOPRANOS"\par \par \uc2 GRINER, RUSSIA, GUILTY, SHARPTON, GILBERT, SUPPORT\par \par \uc2 CAR, CHILDREN, PARENTS, TIPS, DALE, ROLLINS\par \par \uc2 JOBS, NEGOTIATIONS, COMPENSATION, CHAPMAN, BUSHMAN\par \par \uc2 AKERS, CAR, 33 MILES, WORK, ARSENAULT, GIFT\par \par \uc2 NAYA, EXERCISE, YOGA\par \par \uc2 ABE, INVESTIGATION, JAPAN, YAMAGAMI\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ABORTION RIGHTS, REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to President Biden looking to protect abortion rights with an executive order.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT SIGNS REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS EXECUTIVE ORDER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN AIMS TO STRENGTHEN EXISTING LAWS ON ACCESS TO CARE & SERVICES\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ABC's Alex Presha joins us now from the White House with details. Alex, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Whit. President Biden has faced pressure to protect abortion rights since that leaked Supreme Court draft hinted that the High Court was poised to overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN'S EXECUTIVE ORDER\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) He signed this executive order, which expands access to medication abortion and emergency contraception. It safeguards patient privacy. It protects mobile clinics used to offer care to out-of-state patients and bolsters legal efforts to protect the rights to travel out of state for abortions. Health and Human Services Secretary \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2  now has 30 days to develop a game plan. The White House says this is just a step in their process, but some abortion rights advocate say it should have come sooner and doesn't do enough. Anti-abortion rights advocates has cast a doubt it won't change much at all.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN FACES BACKLASH OVER EXECUTIVE ORDER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT BIDEN CRITICIZED BY SOME ON BOTH SIDES OF REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS FIGHT\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But to date, at least 14 states have nearly stopped all abortion services and while he's announced his executive order President Biden has made it clear that his powers are limited saying, the fastest way to protect abortion rights is to pass a federal law codifying Roe. He's urging Americans to make it a voting issue in November.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And speaking of voting issues, abortion rights could soon be on the ballot in Pennsylvania. There a Republican had just passed this week which could add a provision to the state constitution and jeopardize abortion rights there. It could be on the ballot as soon as this next spring. Guys?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Alex Presha there in Washington.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IN 3 MINS, GOLDMAN FAMILY SPEAKS OUT\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Still coming up here in our "GMA Morning Menu," the family of Ron Goldman speaking out about the trial of the century nearly 30 years later and surviving a media circus.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IN 8 MINS, SUMMER FASHION DEALS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Also, ahead here, summer is heating up and we've got the "Deals & Steals" to keep you cool.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IN 13 MINS, LARA IN LONDON!\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And Lara is in London serving up "Pop News" grand slam from Wimbledon. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220802ei790003d\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE INDEX\b0\par\par\uc2 246 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 July 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Saturday\par \uc2 WNSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THOUSANDS RALLY OVER ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, RALLY, BIDEN,, CARMONA, HAWKINS, GODDARD\par \par \uc2 ABE, ASSASSINATED, YAMAGAMI, TASK FORCE, BLINKEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABE ASSASSINATION INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRE, YOSEMITE, HEAT, STORMS, TORNADOES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILDFIRE EXPLODES IN YOSEMITE\par \par \uc2 MUSK, TWITTER, BATTLE, MELKONIAN, TESLA, STOCK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MUSK TWITTER BATTLE\par \par \uc2 JAN 6, CIPOLLONE, TRUMP, LOFGREN, HUTCHINSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KEY JANUARY 6TH TESTIMONY\par \par \uc2 SHARK, PATROL, BITES, ISAF, WALTERS, MULLINS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHARK PATROLS ON HIGH ALERT\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, WAR, US, AID, ROCKETS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: US SENDING MORE AID TO UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 SRI LANKA, PROTESTS, PALACE, HIGHLAND PARK, UVALDO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SRI LANKA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROTESTERS SEIZE PRESIDENTIAL PALACE\par \par \uc2 GRINER, RICHARDSON, RUSSIA, GIRL, SHOOTING, OHIO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORKING TO FREE GRINER\par \par \uc2 WALNUTS, "SOPRANOS," DEATH, RYBAKOVA, GRAND SLAM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE INDEX\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) To "The Index," now, and paying tribute to a former star of "The Sopranos."\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Actor Tony Sirico has died, best known for playing Pauly Walnuts on "The Sopranos." Former co-star Michael Imperioli calling him "tough, loyal, and big hearted." Tony Sirico was 79.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the new Wimbledon champion at center court, Elena Rybakova, winning her first Grand Slam tournament and the first for Kazakhstan.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Born in Moscow, she became a citizen there in 2018. Going into Wimbledon, the odds of her winning were 100 to one.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When we come back, the pint-size firefighter suiting up and reporting for duty.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNSA000020220712ei790000b\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEWS HEADLINES WORKING TO FREE GRINER/4-YEAR-OLD GIRL KILLED\b0\par\par\uc2 314 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 July 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Saturday\par \uc2 WNSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THOUSANDS RALLY OVER ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, RALLY, BIDEN,, CARMONA, HAWKINS, GODDARD\par \par \uc2 ABE, ASSASSINATED, YAMAGAMI, TASK FORCE, BLINKEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABE ASSASSINATION INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRE, YOSEMITE, HEAT, STORMS, TORNADOES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILDFIRE EXPLODES IN YOSEMITE\par \par \uc2 MUSK, TWITTER, BATTLE, MELKONIAN, TESLA, STOCK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MUSK TWITTER BATTLE\par \par \uc2 JAN 6, CIPOLLONE, TRUMP, LOFGREN, HUTCHINSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KEY JANUARY 6TH TESTIMONY\par \par \uc2 SHARK, PATROL, BITES, ISAF, WALTERS, MULLINS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHARK PATROLS ON HIGH ALERT\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, WAR, US, AID, ROCKETS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: US SENDING MORE AID TO UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 SRI LANKA, PROTESTS, PALACE, HIGHLAND PARK, UVALDO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SRI LANKA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROTESTERS SEIZE PRESIDENTIAL PALACE\par \par \uc2 GRINER, RICHARDSON, RUSSIA, GIRL, SHOOTING, OHIO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORKING TO FREE GRINER\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Next, tonight, \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star Brittney Griner is getting help in her fight for freedom from Russia.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Following her guilty plea on drug charges, a source telling ABC News former Governor Bill Richardson is now planning to travel to Russia, hoping to strike a deal to win her release. Richardson, who played a role in the prisoner exchange for former US Marine Trevor Reed and other Americans, is expected there in the next couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 4-YEAR-OLD GIRL KILLED\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And city officials calling for calm after a deadly shooting in Akron, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Authorities say two people, including a four-year-old girl, were shot and killed when someone fired into a crowd during a family celebration. While the shooting was not related to protests over the deadly police shooting of Jalen Walker, the mayor, police chief, and pastors are asking protesters to stand down for 48 hours to help ease tensions in the city.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When we come back, paying tribute to a former star of "The Sopranos."\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNSA000020220712ei790000a\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THOUSANDS RALLY OVER ABORTION RIGHTS AT LEAST 14 STATES CEASED NEARLY ALL ABORTION SVCS\b0\par\par\uc2 827 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 July 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Saturday\par \uc2 WNSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THOUSANDS RALLY OVER ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, RALLY, BIDEN,, CARMONA, HAWKINS, GODDARD\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. It's great to have you with us on this Saturday. I'm Whit Johnson. We begin tonight with the battle over abortion. Thousands taking to the streets. Abortion rights supporters protesting today in our nation's Capitol.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The call to action coming just 24 hours after President Biden's executive order, aimed at protecting access to reproductive health-care services. Many of the demonstrators saying the President's actions do not go far enough. They want him to do more. Protesters also holding a sit-in outside the White House, some traveling hundreds of miles. Strong opinions on both sides of the issue with anti-abortion rights groups blasting the President's move. All of this as 14 states have now stopped nearly all abortions. Louisiana, the latest state to join those already with bans or heavy restrictions.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THOUSANDS RALLY OVER ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And tonight, Pennsylvania could soon become the first state to put abortion on the ballot since the Supreme Court's ruling. ABC's Alex Presha leading us off, from Washington.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AT LEAST 14 STATES CEASED NEARLY ALL ABORTION SERVICES\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, thousands in the nation's Capitol...\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Two, four, six, eight, separate the church and state.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 ...demanding more action on abortion rights in front of the White House.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 It was very important to come down here, especially with my daughter.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today's protest coming 24 hours after President Biden signed an executive order, which some supporters say doesn't go far enough.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL O'LEARY CARMONA (EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR WOMEN'S MARCH)\par \par \uc2 It was a great first step. We are really, you know, interested in him continuing to find the limits of his power on this.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The executive order aims to expand access to medication abortion and emergency contraception, protects mobile clinics used to offer care for out-of-state patients, and bolsters legal efforts to protect the rights of those traveling out of state for abortions. One anti-abortion rights group saying, "It's tragic that this administration is using its power to push for more and more painful abortions." Others doubt it will do much at all.\par \par \uc2 KRISTIN HAWKINS (PRESIDENT STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 Any time a politician says they're gonna form a task force, I wouldn't be too excited about that language. This was simply a statement.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEARLY ALL ABORTION SERVICES BANNED\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, at least 14 states have ceased nearly all abortion services. Louisiana is the latest, after a temporary restraining order blocking the state's trigger laws was lifted.\par \par \uc2 JEFF LANDRY\uc2  (LOUISIANA ATTORNEY GENERAL)\par \par \uc2 We certainly intend to continue to defend the laws of the state and to enforce those laws.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Abortion rights advocates there now hoping for a successful legal challenge to the state's ban. The case delayed, transferred to a different court's jurisdiction. Abortion rights in Pennsylvania could soon be on the ballot. A Republican proposal could add a provision to the state's constitution, jeopardizing abortion rights. It could be set for a vote as early as next spring. At least two other states have similar ballot initiatives. And in Mississippi, the clinic at the center of the Supreme Court's abortion decision closing up shop after the state's abortion trigger law went into effect. Relief for anti-abortion rights advocates there.\par \par \uc2 PRO-LIFE ADVOCATE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I've been working for the last 40 years to close all the clinics.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Back in DC, we met Shelly Goddard, who traveled here with her two daughters from Mississippi with a much different outlook.\par \par \uc2 SHELLY GODDARD (ABORTION RIGHTS ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 This affects so much more than abortion. It affects women's health care, in general, and I don't think a lot of people understand that.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Alex Presha joining us now from the White House. And, Alex, President Biden has acknowledged that his power is limited, pleading with supporters of abortion rights to make their voices heard at the ballot box for the midterms.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRESHA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right, Whit. President Biden has urged Americans to vote for Democrats this November, saying that that is the fastest way to codify Roe. And abortion rights advocates that we spoke to today say this absolutely is a motivating issue for the midterm. They say the Republicans will be put on notice, but so will Democrats that refuse to act. Whit?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Alex, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSA000020220712ei7900002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WORLD NEWS TONIGHT SATURDAY\b0\par\par\uc2 433 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 July 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Saturday\par \uc2 WNSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THOUSANDS RALLY OVER ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Tonight, protesters by the thousands descend on Washington, DC, demanding the protection of abortion rights. Two week after the Supreme Court's bombshell decision overturning Roe v. Wade, protesters swarming outside the White House. Some of President Biden's supporters believe his new executive order does not go far enough. While one anti-abortion rights group calls the President's action tragic, Mississippi closing the clinic at the center of the Supreme Court ruling, and Pennsylvania could soon be putting abortion rights on the ballot. At least 14 states now ceasing nearly all abortions.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABE ASSASSINATION INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Overseas, tonight, Japan now a nation consumed by grief. The body of former Prime Minster Shinzo Abe, assassinated while giving a campaign speech, returned to Tokyo. His wife traveling with him. A 90-member task force now investigating how the suspect, allegedly armed with a homemade gun, was able to get so close. Bob Woodruff, in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILDFIRE EXPLODES IN YOSEMITE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Back here at home, dangerous heat alerts across much of the US, a large wildfire exploding at Yosemite National Park. Red-flag warnings in the West, and tracking severe storms and possible tornadoes into tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MUSK TWITTER BATTLE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk's \uc2 Twitter\uc2  battle. Twenty-four hours after saying he's terminating the $44 billion deal, will the court have the final decision?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KEY JANUARY 6TH TESTIMONY\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 News, tonight, about that much-anticipated testimony from former president Trump's White House counsel, Pat Cipolline. What we're learning about what he told the January 6th Committee behind closed doors.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHARK PATROLS ON HIGH ALERT\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Shark patrols on high alert along America's beaches. Two recent shark attacks involving lifeguards off New York. The warning to everyone going into the water.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SRI LANKA TURMOIL\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Chaos on the streets of Sri Lanka. The President and Prime Minister both vowing to resign amid violent protests. Tens of thousands storming the presidential residence.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRITTNEY GRINER RELEASE EFFORT\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 More help may be on the way for Brittney Griner, the big name now trying to win her release from Russia.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ANSWERING THE CALL\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And "America Strong," the three-year-old firefighter suiting up to answer the call.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORLD NEWS TONIGHT\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 From ABC News world headquarters in New York, this is "World News Tonight."\par \par \uc2 Document WNSA000020220712ei7900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BIDEN TO SIGN EXECUTIVE ORDER ON ABORTION WHAT WILL BE THE IMPACT?\b0\par\par\uc2 277 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 July 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ASSASSINATED\par \par \uc2 ABE, ASSASSINATION, SHOT, BLINKEN, YAMAGAMI\par \par \uc2 GRINER, PLEA, \uc2 WNBA\uc2 , BLAGOVOLINA, FARKAS, REED\par \par \uc2 JOBS REPORT, ECONOMY, RECESSION, LAYOFFS\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, FUNERALS, VICTIMS, LOIZZI, SURVIVORS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ABORTION, EXECUTIVE ORDER, CONGRESS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Alex. We're gonna go to Washington now where President Biden will sign an executive order today aimed at protecting access to reproductive health care services. It comes exactly two weeks after the Supreme Court's earth quaking decision to overturn Roe v. Wade. Rachel Scott has the latest. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN TO SIGN EXECUTIVE ORDER ON ABORTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT WILL BE THE IMPACT?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) George, good morning. And for weeks the White House has been under pressure. Democrats have been calling for a more urgent response to protect access to abortion. So in just a few hours the President will be signing this executive order. It would take steps to protect access to medication abortion and push back on efforts to keep women from traveling across state lines.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It would also move to protect patients' sensitive medical information, and the administration will provide leave for federal workers traveling for medical care. Much of this has already been outlined by administration officials over the last few weeks. It does not go as far as Democrats or advocates are calling for. But the President has been very clear on this, without congressional action, his options are very limited. And this morning that list is growing, at least 13 states have stopped nearly all abortion services, T.J.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220809ei7800006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PRESIDENT BIDEN TO SIGN EXECUTIVE ORDER PROTECTION FOR ABORTION ACCESS\b0\par\par\uc2 4,216 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 July 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Special Report\par \uc2 ABSR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS SPECIAL REPORT\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ABORTION, EXECUTIVE ORDER, BECERRA, MONACO\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm Whit Johnson in New York. We're coming on the air because President Biden is about to sign an executive order aimed at protecting access to reproductive healthcare services. Today's action coming two weeks after the Supreme Court's bombshell decision to overturn Roe v. Wade. Nearly all abortions have now ceased in the 13 states and more states are expected to follow. The White House now is under growing pressure to protect abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AT LEAST 13 STATES CEASED NEARLY ALL ABORTION SERVICES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT BIDEN'S EXECUTIVE ORDER\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The President's executive order will direct agencies to protect access to medication abortion and emergency contraception, take steps to protect patient privacy online, and provide leave for federal workers traveling for medical care. Abortion rights advocates are also pushing for the administration to protect Americans crossing the border into Canada to obtain abortion pills and then bring them back home. You're looking at a live picture of the White House right now. We are expecting President Biden to enter at any moment. The anti-abortion group March for Life is calling the President's decision today tragic.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Biden is limited, of course, in what actions he can actually take on his own. Most other options require congressional authorization. Once again, as we await the President to enter the room, let's bring in ABC's Rachel Scott who is at the White House. Rachel, you've been covering this for months. Another thing that the President has talked about, we know advocates of abortion rights have talked about, is trying to add some protection and assistance for women who may need to travel across state lines.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, and that's the reality for women in states where abortion is nearly banned. It does not stop abortion entirely. But what we have found through our reporting is that women will go through whatever hurdle, whatever length necessary in order to have a choice, including traveling hundreds of miles out of state to another state where abortion may be legal. So we know the President today will announce some steps to help protect those women...\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel, forgive me. President Biden has walked into room. Let's go ahead and listen.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, everyone. Before I speak to the Supreme Court extreme decision overturning Roe, I want to comment on one piece of good news, economic good news today. Today, the Labor Department reported that we added 372,000 jobs last month. 372,000. Here's why it's important. Our private sector has now recovered all of the jobs lost during the pandemic and added jobs on top of that. We have more Americans working today in the private sector than any day under my predecessor. More today than any time in American history, today. In the second quarter of this year, we created more jobs than any quarter under of my predecessors in nearly 40 years, before the pandemic. Think about that.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 At a time when our critics said the economy was too weak or having already added more jobs in, we had already added more jobs in my first year as president of any president in history, we still added more jobs in the past three months than any administration in nearly 40 years. Now, look, I know times are tough. Prices are too high. Families are facing the cost of living crunch. But today's economic news confirms the fact that my economic plan is moving this country in a better direction. The unemployment rate is near a historic low of 3.6%. Private sector jobs are at a record high. Gas prices still way too high, have fallen now 25 days in a row, and this week, we saw the second largest single day decrease in gas prices in a decade.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We still have a lot of work to do. I'm not suggesting, there's a lot more work to do, but I am suggesting we're making significant progress. The program is working. Now with the Vice President, Secretary Becerra, and Deputy Attorney General Monaco, I want to talk about an executive order I'm signing to protect reproductive rights of women, in the aftermath of the Supreme Court's terrible, extreme, and I think so totally wrongheaded decision to overturn Roe v. Wade, in both formalized actions I announced right after the - decision, as well as adding new measures today. Let's be clear about something from the very start. This was not a decision driven by the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Let me say it again, this was not a decision driven by the Constitution, and despite what those justices in the majority said, this was not a decision driven by history. You've all probably had a chance to read the decision and the dissent. The majority rattles off laws from the 19th century to support the idea that Roe was a historic anomaly, because states outlawed abortion in the 1880s, toward the end, but that's just wrong. The truth is, today's Supreme Court majority that is playing fast and loose with the facts. Even 150 years ago, the common law and many state laws did not criminalize abortion early in pregnancy, which is very similar to the viability line drawn by Roe.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 But the Dobbs majority ignores that fact, and the Dobbs majority ignores that many laws were enacted to protect women at the time when they were dying from unsafe abortions. This is the horrific reality that Roe sought to end. The practice of medicine should not, - emphasize, should not be frozen in the 19th century. So, what happened? The dissenting opinion says as clear as you can possibly say it, and here's the quote, "neither law nor facts nor attitudes have provided any new reason to reach a different result than Roe and Casey did, and that has changed," excuse me, "and all that's changed is this court," end of quote. All that's changed is this court.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 That wasn't about the Constitution or the law. It was about a deep, long seated antipathy toward Roe and the broader right to privacy. As the justices wrote in their dissent, and I quote, "the majority has overruled Roe and Casey for one and only reason, because it has always despised them, and now it has the votes to discard them," end of quote. So what we're witnessing wasn't a constitutional judgment. It was an exercise in raw political power. On the day the Dobbs decision came down, I immediately announced what I would do. But I also made it clear based on the reasoning of the court, there is no constitutional right to choose - the only way to fulfill and restore that right for women in this country is by voting, by exercising the power at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Let me explain. We need two additional prochoice senators and a prochoice House to codify Roe as federal law. Your vote can make that a reality. I know it's frustrating, and it made a lot of people very angry, but the truth is this, and it's not just me saying it. It's what the court said. When you read the decision, the court has made clear, it will not protect the rights of women, period. Period. After having made the decision based on a reading of a document that was frozen in time in the 1860s, when women didn't even have the right to vote, the court now, now, practically dares the women of America to go to the ballot box and restore the very rights they've just taken away.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 One of the most extraordinary parts of the decision in my view is the majority writes, and I quote, - it's a quote now, from the majority, "women are not without electoral or political power. It is noteworthy that the percentage of women who registered to vote and cast a ballot is consistently higher than the percentage of the men who do so," end of quote. Repeat the line. Women are not without electoral and/or - let me be precise. Not and/or. Or political power. That's another way of saying that you, the women of America, can determine the outcome of this issue. I don't think the court, or for that matter, the Republicans who for decades have pushed the extreme agenda, have a clue about the power of American women, but they're about to find out, in my view.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 It's my hope and strong belief that women will in fact turn out in record numbers to reclaim the rights that have taken from them by the court. And let me be clear, while I wish it had not come to this, this is the fastest route available. I'm just stating a basic fundamental notion. The fastest way to restore - Roe is to pass a national law codifying Roe, which I will sign immediately upon its passage on my desk. And we can't wait. Extreme Republican governors, extreme Republican state legislators, and Republican extremists in the Congress overall, all of them have not only fought to take away the right, our rights, but they're now determined to go as far as they can.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Now, the most extreme Republican governors and state legislators have taken the court's decision as a green light to impose some of the harshest and most restrictive laws seen in this country in a long time. These are the laws that not only put women's lives at risks, these are the laws that will cost lives. What we're witnessing is a giant step backwards in much of our country. Already the bans are in effect in 13 states. 12 additional states are likely to ban choice in the next - coming weeks. And in a number of these states, the laws are so extreme, they've raised the threat of criminal penalties for doctors and healthcare providers. They're so extreme that many don't allow for exceptions, even for rape or incest. Let me say that again.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Some of the states don't allow exceptions for rape or incest. This isn't some imagined horror. This is already happening. Just last week it was reported that a 10 year old girl was a rape victim. 10 years old. And she was forced to have to travel out of the state to Indiana to seek to terminate the pregnancy and maybe save her life. That last part is my judgment. 10 years old. 10 years old. Raped, six weeks pregnant. Already traumatized. Was forced to travel to another state. Imagine being that little girl. I'm serious, just imagine being that little girl, 10 years old. Does anyone believe that it's the highest majority view that that should not be able to be dealt with? Or in any other state in the nation? A 10 year old girl should be forced to give birth to a rapist's child?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I can tell you what, I don't. I can't think of anything that's much more extreme. The court's decision has already been received by Republicans in Congress as a green light to go further and pass a national ban, a national ban. Remember what they're saying, they're saying there's no right to privacy, so therefore it's not protected by the Constitution, so left up to the states and the Congress, what they want to do. And now my Republican friends are talking about getting the Congress to pass a national ban. The extreme positions that they're taking in some of these states. That will mean the right to choose will be illegal nationwide if in fact they succeed.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Let me tell you something, as long as I'm president it won't happen, because I'll veto it. So the choice is clear. If you want to change the circumstance for women and even little girls in this country, please go out and vote. When tens of millions of women vote this year, they won't be alone. Millions and millions of men will be taking up the fight alongside them to restore the right to choose, and the broader right to privacy in this nation, which they denied existed. And the challenge from the court to the American women and men, this as a nation, the challenge is, go out and vote. Well, for God's sake, there's an election in November. Vote, vote, vote, vote. Consider the challenge accepted, court. But in the meantime I'm signing this important executive order.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I'm asking the Justice Department that much like they did in the civil rights era, to do something, do everything in their power to protect these women seeking to invoke their rights. In states where clinics are still open, to protect them from intimidation, to protect the right of women to travel from state that prohibits seeking the medical attention that she needs, to a state to provide that care. To protect a woman's right to \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved Federal Drug Administration approved medication that's been available for over 20 years. The executive order provides safeguards to access care. A patient comes into an emergency room in any state in the union, she's - experiencing a life-threatening miscarriage. But the doctor is going to be so concerned about being criminalized for treating her, they delay treatment to call the hospital lawyer who is concerned the hospital will be penalized if a doctor provides the lifesaving care.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 It's outrageous. I don't care what your position is, it's outrageous and it's dangerous. That's why this executive order directs the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 , \uc2 HHS\uc2 , to ensure all patients, including pregnant women and girls, experiencing pregnancy loss, get emergency care they need under federal law, and that doctors have the clear guidance on their own responsibilities and protections. No matter what - state they're in. The executive order protects access to contraceptions, that I'm about to sign. Justice Thomas himself said under the reasoning of this decision, this is what Justice Thomas said in his concurring opinion, that the court should reconsider the constitutional right to contraception, to use contraception, even among married couples.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Now, what century are they in? Used to be a case called Connecticut versus Griswold, which was declared unconstitutional in the late '60s. It said a married couple, in the privacy of their bedroom, could not decide to use contraception. Right now in all 50 states and the District of Columbia, the Affordable Care Act guarantees insurance coverage for women's health services, including, including free birth control. The executive order directs \uc2 HHS\uc2  to identify ways to expand access to reproductive health services like IUDs, birth control pills, emergency contraception. And equally important, this executive order protects patient privacy and access to information, which looking at the press assembled before me, probably know more about it than I do.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I'm not a tech guy. I'm learning. But right now when you use a search engine, or the app on your phone, companies collect your data, they sell it to other companies or they even share it with law enforcement. There's an increasing concern that extremist governors and others will try to get that data off of your phone, which is out there in the ether, to find what you're seeking, where you're going, and what you're doing with regard to your health care. Talk about no privacy. No privacy in the Constitution, there's no privacy, period. This executive order asks the FTC to crack down on data brokers that sell private information to extreme groups. Or in my view, sell private information to anybody.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 It provides private health information, it protects private health information in states with extreme laws. And the executive order strengthens coordination at a federal level. It establishes a task force led by the White House Department and the Department of Human Services, focused specifically on using every federal tool available to protect access to reproductive health care. Let me close with this, the court and its allies are committed to moving America backwards with fewer rights, less autonomy and politicians invading their most personal decisions. Remember the reason of the decision has an impact much beyond Roe, and the right to privacy generally. Marriage equality, contraception, and so much more is at risk.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 This decision affects everyone, unrelated to choice, beyond choice. We cannot allow an out of control Supreme Court working in conjunction with the extremist elements of the Republican party to take away freedoms, and our personal autonomy. The choice we face as a nation is between the mainstream and the extreme. Between moving forward and moving backwards, between allowing politicians to enter the most personal parts of our lives, and protecting the right of privacy. Yes. Yes. Embedded in our Constitution. This is a choice. This is a moment, the moment, the moment to restore the rights that have been taken away from us, and the moment to protect our nation from an extremist agenda that is antithetical to everything we believe as Americans. Now I'm going to sign this executive order.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Biden speaking from the Roosevelt Room at the White House, getting ready to sign this executive order to protect reproductive rights for women and help protect abortion access. You can see there he's flanked Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , and Health and Human Services Secretary Javier Becerra. Also Deputy Attorney General Lisa Monaco on hand as well.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Mr. President, what does Prime Minster Abe's death last night say about the security situation in Japan, and can you give us an update on your thinking about lifting China tariffs, please?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Let me, I tried to put a call in to the present prime minister, and he was, it was very late there at night. I'll be talking there in the morning. I'm going to be stopping to sign the condolence book at the Japanese embassy on the way to the CIA. This hasn't happened in Japan in decades and decades. I'm told all the way back to the late '30s or mid-'30s. And it's a homemade weapon. I've only seen a photograph of it. The Justice Department is going to be going in and giving me more detail later as they find out the detail. But the fact is that, one thing did strike my, get my attention, that this is the first use of a weapon to murder someone in Japan, and I think we have thus far had 3,000, don't hold me to the number, 3,668. I mean, between 3,000 and 4,000 cases.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 They have one. One. One. And so, what we're going to learn more about as time goes on about motive, about the whole, but Japan, Fumio, the present prime minster, is a very solid guy. Japan is a very, very stable ally. And we, I do not believe it's likely to have, but I don't know yet, likely to have any profound destabilizing impact on Japanese security or Japanese solidarity. Thank you all so very much.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Mr. President, can I ask you about privacy.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible) tariffs as well, sir, could you just give us your thoughts...\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Biden there as he leaves the Roosevelt Room. He did make some comments, answering one question from a reporter there about the assassination of former Japanese Prime Minster \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 , saying he does not believe that that assassination will have any destabilizing effect on the country or its relations with other countries like the United States, which is a strong ally. Going back to the news of the day though, and why we were here and on the air, President Biden signing that executive order to protect reproductive rights for women. And the President getting emotional, and strong words against the Supreme Court, as he criticized the court for overturning Roe v. Wade, saying that the majority got it wrong, and that it wasn't a constitutional judgment, it was an exercise in raw political power, saying what we're witnessing is a giant step backwards in our country, calling the Supreme Court out of control.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He also expressed serious frustration, almost grinding his teeth as he told the story of a 10 year old girl who reportedly was raped and had to cross state lines to receive abortion services. I do want to bring in Rachel Scott, who's our congressional correspondent, who's at the White House right now. Rachel, you have been covering this story for months now, crisscrossing the country, going to different states. To the executive order, break it down for us what these actions actually mean for women across this country now.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, Whit, the President was under immense pressure from Democrats in his own party to do something on this issue, to move to protect access to abortion for women in this country. So today we did see the President lay out a list of executive actions to protect access to medication abortion, to also help women who may have to travel out of state if they live in states where abortion is now banned, in order to get access to that care. He's also directing the Attorney General, the Department of Justice to provide legal assistance to help protect those women, and trying to safeguard privacy concerns for patients. But again, this does not go as far as some Democrats and some advocacy groups wanted, and it still may be a little bit late for some of those advocacy groups that called on the President to act and release all of these executive actions much sooner.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, President Biden has made it clear that his power is limited from the executive office, really pleading with people to go and cast their votes at the ballot box to really make a difference. We know that the President even talked about ending the filibuster on this issue, so that legislation could be passed to codify abortion rights, but he doesn't even have to votes to be able to do that right now. So Rachel, I want to ask you about what's actually happening as the battle goes back to the states. We talked about the 13 states where nearly all abortions have been banned, and we know a number of other states are expected to do the same in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the President said that just now. He said 12 more states are expected to move to severely restrict access to abortion. 13 have near total bans on abortions right now, where it stands. And I thought it was particularly notable that clear frustration you heard from President Biden today. In fact, he spent so much at the top of his speech talking about what he cannot do, and that is restore the constitutional right to an abortion for women in this country. He says that is going to require action from Congress. There is not enough bipartisan support for that, not even enough support in his own party to change the Senate rules for Democrats to push that across the finish line. So you heard the message from the President today, just about four months exactly to the midterm elections. He says this is a choice, he says this is a moment, and he's encouraging Americans to vote.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel Scott at the White House. Thank you so much. Our coverage continues on ABC News Live and abcnews.com. You can also get breaking alerts on the ABC News app. I'll be back here with the entire "World News Tonight" team for a full wrap-up. For now I'm Whit Johnson in New York. Have a great day.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS SPECIAL REPORT\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 This has been a special report from ABC News.\par \par \uc2 Document ABSR000020220712ei7800002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 EXECUTIVE ORDER ON ABORTION ACCESS PRES. BIDEN STRENGTHENS EXISTING LAWS ON REPRODUCTIVE CARE\b0\par\par\uc2 522 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 July 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHINZO ABE ASSASSINATED\par \par \uc2 ABE, ASSASSINATION, SHOT, JAPAN, YAMAGAMI, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABE ASSASSINATION SHOCK WAVES\par \par \uc2 MUSK, TWITTER, TERMINATE, COURT, BREAKUP FEE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MUSK BAILS ON TWITTER DEAL\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, EXECUTIVE ORDER, ABORTION, VOTE, CONGRESS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EXECUTIVE ORDER ON ABORTION ACCESS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And expensive breakup. That's for sure. Rebecca, thank you. Next, here, tonight, President Biden responding to mounting pressure to protect abortion rights following the Supreme Court's sweeping decision to overturn Roe v Wade. Nearly all abortions now ceased in 14 states, Louisiana the latest joining the list. The President, today, signing an executive order aimed at protecting access to reproductive health services, but even he admits his power on this issue is limited. Here's ABC's Rachel Scott, at the White House.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EXECUTIVE ORDER ON ABORTION ACCESS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRES. BIDEN STRENGTHENS EXISTING LAWS ON REPRODUCTIVE CARE\par \par \uc2 DEMONSTRATORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 My body.\par \par \uc2 DEMONSTRATORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 My choice.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Under mounting pressure from his own allies, President Biden today signing a new executive order aiming to protect abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We cannot allow an out-of-control Supreme Court working in conjunction with extremist elements in the Republican Party to take away freedoms and our personal autonomy.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It comes two weeks after Roe versus Wade was overturned and after at least 14 states have ended nearly all abortion services. In Ohio, a 10-year-old girl was reportedly forced to travel to Indiana to terminate her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Ten years old. Raped, six weeks pregnant, already traumatized, was forced to travel to another state. Imagine being that little girl. I'm serious, just imagine being that little girl. Ten years old. Does anyone believe it's the highest majority view, that that should not be able to be dealt with?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Still, the President acknowledges there's little he can do on his own. Today he urged women to elect more Democrats in the midterms so Congress can make the right to abortion federal law.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 For God's sake, there's an election in November. Vote, vote, vote, vote. But in the meantime, I'm signing this important executive order.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The executive order aims to expand access to medication abortion and emergency contraception, safeguard patient privacy, and bolster legal efforts to protect the right to travel out of state for abortions. But it's notably light on details, leaving it to Health Secretary \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2  to come up with a plan to get it all most of it done.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I asked the White House repeatedly, today, what concrete steps they are taking to expand access to medication abortion. No firm details, but they insisted that Secretary Becerra will be laying out that plan in the coming weeks. And tonight, anti-abortion rights groups say that the President's executive action today are a tragic overreach. Whit.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220711ei7800004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\b0\par\par\uc2 3,946 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 July 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 CHENEY, JAN 6, TRUMP, HUTCHINSON, 2024, GOP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHENEY ONE-ON-ONE\par \par \uc2 JAN 6, HEARING, TRUMP, DOJ, TESTIMONY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ATTACK AT THE CAPITOL: THE INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILL TRUMP FACE CHARGES FOLLOWING JAN 6 HEARINGS?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS, 'REMAIN IN MX,' SCOTUS, MIGRANTS, CBP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FRIDAY\par \par \uc2 WAR, PUTIN, UK, CIVILIANS, BLACK SEA, HROMOV, \uc2 NATO\par \par \uc2 SILVER, 538, MIDTERM, FORECAST, DEMS, GOP\par \par \uc2 2022, TRUMP, 2024, SCOTUS, BIDEN, POLL, ROE, UVALDE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's bring in the Roundtable, now. Senior White House correspondent Mary Bruce, ABC News political reporter Brittany Shephard, "Time" Magazine national political correspondent Molly Ball, and "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " national political correspondent Alex Burns. Welcome to all of you. And Alex, I want to start with you, and I want to start with the midterms, kind of. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reporting that some Republicans are worried that there will be a huge distraction from the midterms because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is eyeing an early 2024 announcement, writing that, "Aides are scrambling to build out basic campaign infrastructure in time for an announcement as early as this month?" This month?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NYT REPORT: TRUMP EYEING EARLY ANNOUNCEMENT FOR 2024 BID\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS ("NYT" NAT'L POLITICAL ./CO-AUTHOR 'THIS WILL NOT PASS')\par \par \uc2 Right. This is not exactly what the doctor ordered for Republicans in the midterm elections. And Martha, I think it's not coincidence that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is talking about doing this, looking at doing this quite seriously at a moment when his legal jeopardy appears to be increasing as a result of the January 6th Committee hearings, that he, there's been this pattern over the last few years that, when he appears more vulnerable to investigations of one kind or another, he works harder to put himself out there as a potential candidate.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW WOULD TRUMP COMEBACK BID IMPACT 2022 MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS ("NYT" NAT'L POLITICAL ./CO-AUTHOR 'THIS WILL NOT PASS')\par \par \uc2 And for Republican candidates running in House races, Senate races, gubernatorial races, that means \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  putting himself out there is a potential distraction. The GOP wants this midterm election to be an up or down vote on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's handling of the economy, not a choice between how \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has performed as president and what voters think about the last guy.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Mary, there are many reasons that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would want to announce early, 80 percent approval rating with Republicans, but there really are some hurdles if he does that.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) There's serious hurdles. Look, I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  may be very eager for a rematch. He is eager to relitigate the last election, to reassert himself, especially in light of these hearings, but it is not a given and, and it certainly isn't going to be smooth sailing. I think there are, first of all, just practical fundraising implications to declaring this early. It can limit how much he can raise. But, more than that, I think there are Republicans, Republican officials and Republican lawmakers and likely some Republican voters who think that, while they may support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , that he is too divisive to run again.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, of course, there are other Republicans who are interested, we know, in, in jumping in, and I think that only is strengthened by what you are hearing in these hearings. But look, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  seems intent on getting out there early and saying to Republican officials, to the Republican Party and Republican voters, you're either with me or against me. And to Alex's point, I mean, there are real challenges to doing this before the midterms. It certainly is not something that Republicans want. And I can tell you, based on conversations I've had in the last 24 hours, Democrats in the White House, would certainly welcome \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  getting in early.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and Brittany, he really could just suck up all the energy around the midterms. And, and as Alex said, the economy, that's what the Republicans want to talk about.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY SHEPHERD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly. And I was talking to a House GOP leadership aide just a couple days ago, with the state of play, and they're wringing their hands. They're stressed because the question is, Republicans are probably going to take back a couple of seats coming in the fall, and so it's not that will Republicans still win if Trump's in the game, but what kind of Republican's going to win their primary. Because of redistricting, there's fewer and fewer competitive races.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY SHEPHERD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It's a little wonky, but that means that folks who are going to be winning the primaries are going to be a lot more partisan. And it eliminates the kind of who are able to skirt through the primaries just this week. Think five of the thirty-five Republicans who voted for the bipartisan commission on January 6th that ultimately failed the Senate of course, four out of the five were able to survive their primaries just this Tuesday, with one falling. I think those kind of Republicans are going to survive if Trump is the narrative? It becomes a question, like Mary says, of loyalty.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY SHEPHERD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And a question, well, can fringe Republicans kind of usurp fundraising? Can they usurp campaigning? And it's worrisome, worrisome to folks on the Hill who would think, we're trying to reclass what Republicanism means. Is it MAGA, or is it something more? And I think they're really stressed about that.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMS SPEND MILLIONS TO AID FAR-RIGHT CANDIDATES IN PRIMARIES\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and Molly, let's talk about the Democrats. The Democrats have a rather jaw-dropping strategy in the primaries. They have spent millions to help these very extreme right-wing candidates, giving them millions and millions of dollars, thinking in the general election, that will help them, obviously. Let me read you an opinion piece in by \uc2 David Brooks\uc2  in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ," part of it. "What the Democrats are doing is sleazy in the best of circumstances. If you love your country more than your party, you should want the best candidates to advance in either party. And in these circumstances, what they are doing is insane." What do you think of the strategy?\par \par \uc2 MOLLY BALL ("TIME" MAGAZINE NATIONAL POLITICAL ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 Well, this is not the first time it's been tried, but it's certainly the most aggressive that, that any party has been about trying to boost the challengers that they would prefer not to face. And, of course, they're sort of haunted by the biggest example of this, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2016, when the Clinton campaign famously wanted to face him, and that didn't work out so well. But it's also been done, you know, at the Senate, at the gubernatorial level. Interestingly, in Colorado this past week, it didn't work, and they ended up elevating the moderate candidate.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY BALL ("TIME" MAGAZINE NATIONAL POLITICAL ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 It'll be very ironic if what Democrats get for their effort to try to elevate extreme Republicans is actually a kinder, gentler Republican Party, where the Moderates make it through and then increase their credibility with independent voters because they have seen this barrage of attacks on the more conservative, or, or boosting for the more conservative candidate. But in many places, it is working, you know, as a political strategy. We'll see how it goes in November. But this is the Democrats' sort of best hope, is that the Republican candidates are, are just too extreme for the electorate in a year when every other political condition favors the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY BALL ("TIME" MAGAZINE NATIONAL POLITICAL ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 And most Democrats are still pretty fatalistic about the midterms, in general. But you do see with, with the January 6th Commission, changing the conversation a little bit, with Trump threatening to be more a part of the narrative, and with all the Supreme Court decisions. You see the Democratic base getting more fired up. So, there are glimmers of optimism in the Democratic establishment that we haven't seen for a while.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SCOTUS DELIVERS BLOW TO BIDEN'S AGENDA WITH EPA RULING\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and let's talk about the Supreme Court decisions, Alex. On the climate ruling, a blow to the Biden - administration in trying to combat climate change. Tell us what this ruling means and what the effect will be.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS ("NYT" NAT'L POLITICAL ./CO-AUTHOR 'THIS WILL NOT PASS')\par \par \uc2 Well, broadly, Martha, what it means is that the specific set of powerplant regulations that the Obama administration drafted and released seven years ago never going to go into effect and that the \uc2 EPA\uc2  has not been, you know, not entirely defanged but constrained in terms of whether it can impose sort of system-wide grid-scale requirements to transition away from dirty power and towards a cleaner power.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Like, they can't say you have to put in solar panels.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS ("NYT" NAT'L POLITICAL ./CO-AUTHOR 'THIS WILL NOT PASS')\par \par \uc2 You can't say, you can't say you got to get rid of your coal plants in five years, right? They can still do a whole lot. And the burden and the challenge for the Biden administration now is to show that they saw this coming, which they certainly should have, given the composition of the Supreme Court, and show that they can get Congress to do something to advance their climate agenda. You know, so much, excuse me, so much of what we've heard from the Supreme Court in this term is pushing the ball back to Congress to say, no, the administrative agencies can't do policy on that scale. No, the courts are not going to sort of defend basic rights by judicial fiat. You've got to do this through legislation.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS ("NYT" NAT'L POLITICAL ./CO-AUTHOR 'THIS WILL NOT PASS')\par \par \uc2 And the Biden administration and, by the way, a whole lot of other administrations have had a devil of a hard time showing they can get Congress to do any of that. So, if the burden was already high on Biden to get something more out of this Congress, perhaps on climate, perhaps on prescription drug regulation, abortion now obviously a big part of the conversation.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS ("NYT" NAT'L POLITICAL ./CO-AUTHOR 'THIS WILL NOT PASS')\par \par \uc2 The burden is that much higher, and the midterm clock and the pressure from the midterms, it makes it all the more intense, that if they can't get something significant on climate policy, on clean energy policy, and on, I think, the, the betting odds with the prescription drug regulation would be the one other thing that you get in a reconciliation bill, then, gosh, that's a hell of a message to send to the Democratic base and to independent voters who elected you to get stuff done.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN LEFT WITH FEW OPTIONS AS STATES MOVE TO BAN ABORTIONS\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and as you mentioned, abortion, Mary, it has been a little over a week since Roe was overturned. Since then, 12 states have ceased nearly all abortion services. I know President Biden met with governors this week, but what is he really doing?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It's a really good question, and, I mean, look, you have one week since this decision came down. You have women across the country in limbo, not knowing their status. You have it playing out chaotically across the states, and a lot of people looking to Washington and the administration. The President promised that he would be ready when this ruling came down, and that he would take action. And you have a lot of advocates and a lot, and even Democrats saying, what's the holdup? Where is the big response from, from the President? And, you know, his response, so far, has been largely rhetorical.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He has taken some steps. You know, he's promising to defend women who travel to different states to get an abortion, promising to make sure the abortion pill is still readily available. The White House feels they aren't getting enough credit for that. But those are largely steps that simply restate, you know, existing policy. And I think, you know, the President now is saying, what, what we all know to be true, which is that this really does come down to Congress. And, right now, he doesn't have the support to codify Roe into law. They don't even have the support to change the Senate rules to try and do that.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, it does really all come down to the midterms, and that's the message from the President. But still, where is the sweeping executive action that he promised he was looking into? And I'm told we can expect some announcements this week, but whether that's going to be enough to satisfy people that are really looking to this White House to, to lead more on this, whether or not it can really have an impact remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CONFIDENCE IN THE SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 25%, GREAT DEAL/QUITE A LOT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 43%, SOME\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 30%, VERY LITTLE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and Brittany, Roe and climate were of course just two of the consequential decisions this week. There was religious liberty, guns, all decided by a supermajority conservative court. But, according to a Gallup poll, only one in four Americans say they have a great deal of confidence in the court. How significant is that, when you look to the Supreme Court? Some of those decisions were before this week, I said this week.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GALLUP POLL SHOWS CONFIDENCE IN COURT AT HISTORIC LOW\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY SHEPHERD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, I think there's to levels of significance. I think there's a national big picture one. There's recent polling and recent studies that came out that says that the current makeup of the Supreme Court is about 75 percent more conservative than the average American, which means that these Americans are going to be feeling disenfranchised and apathetic about the state of the Supreme Court. And the Supreme Court doesn't have legislative powers that Congress has, doesn't have a budget, doesn't have an army like the President, so they over-index on public confidence so that states can follow out their jurisprudence.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY SHEPHERD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, they need public support, really, in order to bolster the things that they have and to get it done. But on a micro level, I think this really impacts voters. You know, when I was out in the cycle last for 2020, and now for 2024, I think the real swing voter, and I'm curious what you guys think about this, too, it's not the Democrat who flips to Republican, even though there's some anecdotal evidence that says that such, but it's a voter, it's from the nonvoter to the voter. And with apathy in the Supreme Court, we already see Biden's, you know, tectonic ratings, you know.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY SHEPHERD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And with a possible \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  presidency, I think it's vexing a lot of people who are on that fringe of, well, maybe I just won't come out in November. What's it going to do for me? And that stresses out the parties, too, because the Republicans are hinging on, you know, I hate to say it all comes out to turnout in the midterms what is really true. Republicans are hoping that they're going to get a little bit more turnout to get this red wave, to get their candidates not just in the Congress, but in the governors' mansions, secretaries of state offices, where abortion is really going to be a huge issue. And Democrats are hoping that that voter turnout has a little bit of a blue firewall, for lack of a better term, against that red wave.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY SHEPHERD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And with less turnout, what's going to happen next?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What do you think, Molly?\par \par \uc2 MOLLY BALL ("TIME" MAGAZINE NATIONAL POLITICAL ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 Well, I think, I, I think that's all right, and I think that it's even bigger than that, because for a lot of people this contributes to the, the crisis of trust in all of our institutions, right, and we see voters losing confidence in the courts. For a long time, people have not had a lot of confidence in Congress or the media. And then, you know, coming out of COVID, coming out of the 2020 election, the sense of chaos, the sense that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was elected to restore a sort of normalcy that didn't happen, inflation, you know, skyrocketing, people just feel very unsettled, and like there - there aren't any grownups in charge.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY BALL ("TIME" MAGAZINE NATIONAL POLITICAL ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 And so, I think on, on a very big picture level, Americans are feeling like there's nothing that they can trust, and there's a sense almost that there's a societal breakdown in the works. People don't trust elections and so on. And so, you know, we could be heading for, for a sort of larger reckoning in terms of just how this country functions, I think.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And...\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS ("NYT" NAT'L POLITICAL ./CO-AUTHOR 'THIS WILL NOT PASS')\par \par \uc2 Martha, if I , if I could.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Please, please.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS ("NYT" NAT'L POLITICAL ./CO-AUTHOR 'THIS WILL NOT PASS')\par \par \uc2 You know, I do think you talk to Democratic officials and, and strategists and there is this sense that the Supreme Court, for the reasons that you just described, is a phenomenal political villain for the party today and in this campaign and going forward, and most people don't know who the Supreme Court justices are. If they know any of them, it's usually the most controversial. They're a set of sort of largely obscure characters guarded by this, sort of, priesthood of elite lawyers, right? And so, for your average voter, they are not a sympathetic force in American life, and they're declining in terms of the institutional trust they have.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS ("NYT" NAT'L POLITICAL ./CO-AUTHOR 'THIS WILL NOT PASS')\par \par \uc2 So, when you talk to Democrats who are younger, you talk to Democrats who are more progressive, there is a sense that the party should get out there and run against these sort of priest in black robes. And there is a lot of questions about whether Biden is going to be up for doing that. You know, Molly mentioned declining trust in institutions, generally. There's one word that you hear over and over again to describe \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , it is institutionalist. Is he going to be comfortable going to these swing states in the fall and campaigning against the Supreme Court, institutionally, as a malignant force in the country?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Molly, just quickly, the next term, with the Supreme Court, too. We've got interesting cases coming up.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SCOTUS TO HEAR CASE THAT COULD UPEND ELECTION LAWS\par \par \uc2 MOLLY BALL ("TIME" MAGAZINE NATIONAL POLITICAL ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 A lot of interesting cases potentially coming up, and the Court laying down a marker at the very end this term, saying that they will hear this case on, on the independent state legislative doctrine, how that relates to elections, and whether, whether state legislators will have the greater power over federal elections than they ever have. And that is another potentially destabilizing decision, depending on which they go, and another decision that I think Democrats are looking at as something that potentially puts the Court further out of sync with public opinion and potentially looking more like an institutional outlier that, that, that people don't trust.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Brittany, just quickly, if you will, politically, does anything change because of January 6th?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILL HEARINGS CHANGE AMERICANS' MINDS ABOUT JAN 6?\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY SHEPHERD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, for Republicans, probably not. You know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is still the strongest endorsement sought out for in the midterms. We haven't seen a brand-new swath of Republicans come out. The folks who have been being critical of Trump before are critical of him again. We don't have a lot of narrative changes, there. I think, potentially, it could shift for folks in the suburbs. I was just down in North Carolina for Nancy Mace's race in Mount Pleasant, that's a suburb of Charleston, and there are those Republicans I call the country club Republicans, folks who came down from Long Island and Connecticut who believe that January 6th is their final straw, but at the end of day, they're still Republicans.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY SHEPHERD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They still really care about inflation. They really care about gas prices. So, they're going to be playing in the primaries hoping that they can kick off the Trump-endorsed folks. But when it comes to November, if they see an "R," they're probably going to check the box next to it.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FAMILIES OF UVALDE SHOOTING STILL DEMANDING ANSWERS\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Mary, we really just have about 20 seconds, but I want to go back to Uvalde. What is President Biden doing about that? Those families want answers. We had the resignation of Pete Arredondo, who was in charge that day, from the city council.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, he, he, he did resign, though. We saw that yesterday, you know, and, and he faced so much criticism and so much heat for the response to that. He says he wasn't, you know, the commander on that day, or wasn't, you know, in charge of the response, but yet, over an hour for them to get in there. And I think the big picture is that these families are just being failed at every level, over and other again, and the lack of a response only makes that pain even more painful. How much can the federal government do to step in and get them some answers? It's pretty unclear. But this White House continues to point to that gun legislation they're passing. Would that have solved this? We just don't know.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Thanks very much to all of you. Happy Fourth. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that is all for us, today. But before we go, a big welcome to the newest member of our "This Week" family. Ella Rose Tejera, daughter of our executive producer Dax Tejera and his wife, Veronica. She joins big sister Sofia. We are wishing them all the best. Thanks for sharing part of your Sunday with us. Have a happy and safe Independence Day.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK: DOWNLOAD THE APP\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220706ei7300007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BIDEN MEETS WITH GOVERNORS ON REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS URGING VOTERS TO CHANGE MAKEUP OF CONGRESS\b0\par\par\uc2 470 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 July 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRAVEL TURBULENCE\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, HOLIDAY, AIRPORTS, BUTTIGIEG, DELAYS\par \par \uc2 HOLIDAY, EXODUS, AAA, GAS, DRIVERS\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAIN, WINDS, COLIN\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GOVERNORS, ROE, PROTESTS, CONGRESS, NORTHROP\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Busy weather weekend, Sam, thank you for that. Well now to the battle over abortion rights. President Biden meeting with governors following the Supreme Court overturning Roe V. Wade. ABC's Faith Abubey has the latest from the White House.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN MEETS WITH DEMOCRATIC GOVERNORS ON REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT URGING VOTERS TO CHANGE MAKEUP OF CONGRESS\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, amid growing pressure to do more to safeguard access to abortion.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 This is what democracy looks like.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) President Biden urging voters to take their anger to the polls this November.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 You either elect federal senators and representatives who will codify Roe, Republicans who are elected to the House and Senate will try to ban abortions nationwide.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The President acknowledging it's the most viable way for Democrats to overcome the 60-vote threshold needed in the Senate to codify reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Right now, we don't have the votes in the Senate to change the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) President Biden issuing the warning in a virtual brainstorming session to safeguard women's reproductive rights with nine Democratic governors Friday. The President vowing to protect women crossing state lines for abortions, and ensure access to FDA-approved abortion pills. Following the landmark Supreme Court decision reversing Roe V. Wade, and nearly five decades of precedent, abortion services have nearly stopped in 12 states.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And over the next few weeks and months, 26 states in total are likely to severely restrict or ban abortion rights. Legal challenges mounting on both sides of the debate. From Texas State Supreme Court, which is blocking a lower court order that would resume abortion services, to Kentucky where a judge has blocked the state's near total and six-week abortion bans. Across the country a lack of clarity on women's reproductive rights as challenges to statewide bans head to court creating uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 NANCY NORTHROP (PRESIDENT & CEO THE CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS)\par \par \uc2 It is very confusing for patients, for women, for pregnant people on the ground who are trying to figure out if the clinics in their area are open.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the White House says President Biden has no immediate plans to take executive action on abortion access. However, they are pointing to things the administration has done, including the FDA moving to increase access to abortion pills. Whit.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220803ei7200005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WORLD NEWS SATURDAY\b0\par\par\uc2 451 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 July 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Saturday\par \uc2 WNSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Tonight, chaos at the airports and on the roads, with millions of Americans on the move this 4th of July weekend.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Long lines at some of the nation's busiest airports, the \uc2 TSA\uc2  screening passengers in record numbers.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOLIDAY TRAVEL CHAOS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 But frustration growing with thousands of delays and hundreds of cancellations. And now, news about \uc2 American Airlines\uc2 . How a computer glitch could impact thousands of flights this month. What the airline is saying, tonight. And for the 42 million Americans deciding to hit the road, traffic and sky-high gas prices as they start their holiday getaway.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER THREAT\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 All of this made even worse by powerful storms targeting the I-95 corridor in the Northeast. Fifty million under a severe weather threat. And the new named tropical storm putting a damper on fireworks in the Carolinas. Rob Marciano timing it all out.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY AIR SHOW DISASTER\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Breaking news. At least one person is dead after a large explosion at a Michigan air show, a crowd of people watching in horror. Late details just coming in. What investigators are saying, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATES TAKE ON ABORTION BATTLE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The battle over abortion moves to the states more than a week after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. At least 12 states have stopped nearly all abortion services. The fierce legal fight in Texas. The state's Supreme Court blocking a lower court ruling that had allowed some abortions to go forward.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHARK ATTACK SURVIVOR SPEAKS OUT\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The Florida shark attack survivor speaking out from her hospital bed. What her brother did to save her life. New information on her condition, and news, now, of another attack in the state.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FAMILY DEMANDS ANSWERS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 A family and community demanding answers. Protests in Akron, Ohio, after the police shooting of a 25-year-old black man. What we're learning about the investigation and the preliminary records from the medical examiner.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EMBATTLED UVALDE OFFICIAL RESIGNS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 A shakeup in Uvalde, Texas. Late today, the school's police chief at the center of the mass shooting investigation stepping down from his post on the city council.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICA STRONG\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And "America Strong," tonight. The soccer player behind this inspiring viral moment, making history, and what it means to that little boy on the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORLD NEWS TONIGHT\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 From ABC News world headquarters in New York, this is "World News Tonight."\par \par \uc2 Document WNSA000020220705ei7200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 STATES TAKE ON ABORTION BATTLE TEXAS SUPREME COURT BLOCKS RULING ALLOWING SOME ABORTIONS\b0\par\par\uc2 516 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 July 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Saturday\par \uc2 WNSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, BUTTIGIEG, CANCELLATIONS, GAS, PRICES\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORM, TRACK, TROPICAL STORM COLIN, HEAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER THREAT\par \par \uc2 EXPLOSION, AIR SHOW, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, PROTESTS, BIDEN, HOCHUL, JONES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATES TAKE ON ABORTION BATTLE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tonight, one week after the Supreme Court's landmark ruling on abortion, the battle has moved to the states, sparking a flurry of legal challenges and confusion. Legislatures now rushing to enact laws, and both sides are taking the issue to the lower courts. Here's ABC's Faith Abubey.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS SUPREME COURT BLOCKS RULING ALLOWING SOME ABORTIONS\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We won't go back.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, legal whiplash in Texas, with nearly all abortions banned, once again, in the state. The state's Supreme Court blocking lower court ruling just days ago that had allowed some abortions to go forward.\par \par \uc2 ANNE JONES (TEXAS RESIDENT)\par \par \uc2 Honestly, I think it makes women second-class citizens.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The decision reviving a nearly 100-year-old Texas law. Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , who appealed the case to the state's highest court, tweeting, quote, "I'll keep winning for Texas's unborn babies." The legal battle over abortion now playing out in the states after the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  struck down Roe v. Wade. Overnight, the Ohio Supreme Court upholding the state's six-week abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Abortion is a human right.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Since the federal right to an abortion was overturned just over a week ago, at least 12 states have stopped nearly all abortion services. And while some applaud the moves...\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We are the pro-life generation.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...on Friday, President Biden warning that the state bans are only just the beginning.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We either elect federal senators and representatives who will codify Roe, or Republicans who will elect the House and Senate, who will try to ban abortions nationwide.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The President urging voters to make their voices heard at the polls this November. A majority of Americans support some reproductive rights. A recent Gallup Poll finding only 13% of Americans back a total abortion ban. Overnight, New York's legislature taking the first steps to enshrine the right to an abortion into the State Constitution.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL (DEM) (NEW YORK)\par \par \uc2 With respect to the Dobbs decision, it wash biggest setback for women's rights in modern history.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Faith Abubey with us, now. And, Faith, President Biden is doubling down on his calls to suspend the filibuster in the Senate in order to codify abortion rights, but some moderate Democrats remain hesitant.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Whit, they've consistently warned that this could backfire. Meaning, if Republicans regain control of the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 , they could potentially use the same tactic to push through other controversial measures. Whit?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Faith, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSA000020220705ei7200005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BATTLE OVER ABORTION RIGHTS PRESIDENT BIDEN CALLS ON CONGRESS TO ACT\b0\par\par\uc2 619 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOLIDAY TRAVEL EXODUS\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, AAA, HOLIDAY, DRIVING, SKIES\par \par \uc2 HOLIDAY, TRAVEL, TRAFFIC, AAA\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, AIRLINES, TSA, CANCELATIONS, DELTA\par \par \uc2 HOLIDAY, STORMS, RAIN, FLOODING, HAIL\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, MISSILE, VICTIMS, ZELENSKYY, BLACK SEA, DRUEKE\par \par \uc2 WALL STREET, STOCKS, 1970, INFLATION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, BATTLE, BIDEN, CONGRESS, PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Eva, now to the Supreme Court and the battle over abortion. President Biden blasting the court for what he called its outrageous behavior in overturning Roe V. Wade. He called for Congress to lift the filibuster and pass abortion rights. Our senior White House correspondent Mary Bruce joins us with more. Mary, good morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BATTLE OVER ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT BIDEN CALLS ON CONGRESS TO ACT\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Whit. Yeah, the President trying to up the pressure a bit on Congress. As today Biden plans to meet here at the White House with governors who are moving to protect abortion rights. All this as now one week after the court overturned Roe, this battle is now chaotically playing out state by state, leaving many women in limbo.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) One week after the Supreme Court ruling.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Save Roe.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 This court.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Illegitimate.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And with the President facing growing pressure, Biden is ramping up his rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 The first and foremost thing we should do is make it clear how outrageous this decision was.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The President pushing Congress to enshrine abortion rights into law. And for the first time, Biden now says he supports making an exception to change the Senate rules so that Democrats could do this with just a simple majority, rather than 60 votes.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I believe we have to codify Roe V. Wade in the law, and the way to do that is to make sure that Congress votes to do that.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Top Senate Republican \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  calling Biden's comments an "Inappropriate attack on the court that undermines equal justice and the rule of law." But even with Biden's backing, a rule change is not going to happen. Two moderate Democrats are opposed. The President's critics, even members of his own party, are calling for him to do more, demanding sweeping executive action to counter the court.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I'm the only president they've got, and I feel extremely strongly that I'm going to do everything in my power.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BATTLE OVER REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROTESTS CONTINUE AS SOME WOMEN CROSS STATE LINES FOR SERVICES\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In the meantime, protests across the country rage on, while the reality sinks in. Some women now forced to travel across state lines to seek abortions. This Louisiana woman didn't want to be identified, driving to New Mexico to receive care on a trip that cost her $2,000.\par \par \uc2 LOUISIANA RESIDENT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 It's my body. I have that, and I have the right, and I don't think anybody should be able to decide that for me.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In at least 12 states nearly all abortions have now stopped. That's down from 13, after a judge temporarily halted Kentucky's near total ban on abortion. And in Florida, a judge there temporarily blocking the state's 15-week ban on abortion, but the Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  there does plan to appeal. Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And these legal battles still playing out. All right, Mary, thank you. Gio.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220802ei7100008\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BIDEN MEETS WITH GOVERNORS PRES. HOSTS PANEL TO DISCUSS ABORTION RIGHTS PROTECTIONS\b0\par\par\uc2 572 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, HOLIDAY, GROSS, GAS, PRICE, STAFF SHORTAGE\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOLIDAY STORM THREATS\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, HUNT, INVESTIGATION, STORZ, BARTLEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OFFICERS KILLED IN SHOOTOUT\par \par \uc2 SHARK, ATTACK, BETHEA, BLAKEMAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEEN ATTACKED BY SHARK\par \par \uc2 GRINER, RUSSIA, INVASION, UKRAINE, ROOD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY MISSILE STRIKE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ABORTION, HOCHUL, DESANTIS, MURPHY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN MEETS WITH GOVERNORS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back here at home, President Biden meeting with 10 Democratic governors trying to protect abortion rights after the Supreme Court's overturning of Roe v. Wade. The President telling them the fight is not over and warning of a possible nationwide ban on abortion if Republicans gain control of Congress. Here's ABC's senior White House correspondent, Mary Bruce.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRES. HOSTS PANEL TO DISCUSS ABORTION RIGHTS PROTECTIONS\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) One week after Roe was overturned, and with the fight over abortion chaotically playing out state by state, President Biden today convened a panel of Democratic governors to figure out what they can do.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL (DEM) (NEW YORK)\par \par \uc2 Mr. President, I have a brand-new granddaughter. I didn't think we'd have to fight this battle for her generation, as well, but apparently we do.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Well, I didn't think we would, either.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Biden is under increasing pressure, some Democrats calling him slow to respond to the decision that has rocked the country.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEARLY ALL ABORTION SERVICES HAVE CEASED\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Right now, at least 12 states have stopped nearly all abortion services. In Florida, a judge is expected to temporarily block the state's 15-week abortion ban, but Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is vowing to appeal.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS (REP) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 These are unborn babies that have heartbeat. They can feel pain.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In New York, today, the State Senate taking the first step to enshrine the right to an abortion into the State Constitution. In New Jersey, Democratic Governor \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  signing a law protecting women who come to the state seeking an abortion and the health care providers who perform the procedures.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR PHIL MURPHY (DEM) (NEW JERSEY)\par \par \uc2 These laws will make New Jersey a beacon of freedom for every American woman.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Back in Washington, Biden is looking ahead to the midterms, warning if Republicans take control of Congress, they could pass a nationwide abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 So, the choice is clear. We either elect federal senators and representatives who will codify Roe, or Republicans who will elect the House and Senate will try to ban abortions nationwide. Nationwide.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mary Bruce, back with us, now. And, Mary, we heard President Biden say recently he wants the Senate to do away with the filibuster to pass legislation to protect abortion rights. But today, even he admitted that's not likely to happen.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, Whit, the President today admitting the political reality, here, that even with Biden's backing, he still doesn't have enough support to change the Senate rules to get this done. Two moderate Democrats are still opposed. And that is why you are hearing the President repeatedly stressing the importance of the upcoming midterm elections. Whit?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mary, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220705ei7100007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FALLOUT FROM ABORTION BAN ONE MISSOURI HEALTH CARE SYSTEM BRIEFLY STOPS PLAN B\b0\par\par\uc2 614 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, HOLIDAY WEEKEND, GAS PRICES\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON, HEARING, RIOT, CIPOLLONE, SUBPOENA, TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FALLOUT FROM BOMBSHELL JAN 6 TESTIMONY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FORMER WHITE HOUSE COUNSEL PAT CIPOLLONE SUBPOENAED\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, MANHUNT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MOTHER KILLED IN NEW YORK CITY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 20-YEAR-OLD WOMAN SHOT WHILE PUSHING STROLLER WITH 3-MONTH-OLD BABY\par \par \uc2 HOOD, FLORES, ZAMARANO, HUMAN SMUGGLING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FOUR NOW CHARGED IN HUMAN SMUGGLING HORROR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUSPECTED DRIVER DUE IN COURT WITH DEATH TOLL AT LEAST 53 PEOPLE\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, INVASION, BLACK, DRUEKE, HUYNH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW VIDEO OF AMERICAN CAPTIVES IN UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FAMILIES OF THE VETERAN FIGHTERS SPEAK OUT\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, \uc2 NATO\uc2 , SUMMIT, UKRAINE, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT ON WORLD STAGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN TO GIVE REMARKS AS NATO WELCOMES TWO NEW MEMBERS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, RULINGS, JACKSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRACING FOR SUPREME COURT DECISIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUSTICES SET TO END TERM WITH MAJOR RULINGS ON IMMIGRATION & CLIMATE CHANGE\par \par \uc2 LINDERGREN, PAZ, THOMAS, ABORTION, PLAN B, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FALLOUT FROM ABORTION BAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE MISSOURI HEALTH CARE SYSTEM BRIEFLY STOPS PROVIDING PLAN B\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Gio, we're also following the latest in the battle over abortion rights. Confusion at one Kansas City health care system that briefly stopped providing Plan B emergency contraceptives following Missouri's abortion ban there. Congressional correspondent Rachel Scott has more.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A leading hospital system in Missouri sparking panic and confusion.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE LINDERGREN (ASSISTANT PROFESSOR UMKC SCHOOL OF LAW)\par \par \uc2 Now, we have doctors who are turning to legal departments and ethicists to get guidance rather than relying on their own medical judgment.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) St. Louis Health System says the state's abortion ban was so unclear, it stopped providing the popular Plan B pill. The so-called morning after pill is used to prevent pregnancy after unprotected sex.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAN B BRIEFLY HALTED\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Later, the provider reversing course after learning the state's law does not affect emergency contraception, warning the ambiguity in the law continues to cause grave concern. As of now, emergency contraception is legal nationwide and can be purchased over the counter. But across the country, many women are stocking up, fearing it could be targeted next.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE PAZ (ARIZONA RESIDENT)\par \par \uc2 I was talking to my sister and we're like, we just need to start stocking up. It's not even for us, for the women in our family. For our girlfriends, our friends.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Some major pharmacies like \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 , \uc2 Rite Aid\uc2 , even Amazon imposing limits on how much customers can buy, citing the increase in demand.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STOPPED NEARLY ALL ABORTION SERVICES\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Meanwhile, at least 13 states have now stopped nearly all abortion services. Ten bans now facing legal challenges.\par \par \uc2 PEDESTRIAN (MALE)\par \par \uc2 The battle still rages. Abortion is still legal in our land. It needs to be abolished.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Here in Mississippi, health care is a scarce resource, especially for poorer, underserved communities. This mobile health clinic is on a mission to fill the gaps, providing reproductive care, family planning and emergency contraceptives.\par \par \uc2 JULIET THOMAS (MOBILE CLINIC)\par \par \uc2 And we're going to be more prevalent and more important in these communities now more than ever.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is Mississippi's last abortion clinic. It was at the center of the Supreme Court's case. The clinic is still trying to fight the state's abortion ban now set to go into effect in just a matter of days, Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, those battles far from over. Rachel, thank you. Robin?\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220801ei6u00031\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BIDEN BLASTS COURT CALLS ON CONGRESS TO LIFT FILIBUSTER TO PROTECT ABORTION RIGHTS\b0\par\par\uc2 530 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, \uc2 EPA\uc2 , MORRISEY, IMMIGRATION, JACKSON\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, SUPREME COURT, ROE V. WADE, \uc2 EPA\uc2 , G7 SUMMIT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN BLASTS COURT\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And after a week of high-stakes meetings with allies overseas, President Biden also turning his focus to the Supreme Court, blasting the decision overturning Roe v. Wade, accusing the justices of destabilizing America.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEARLY ALL ABORTION SERVICES HAVE CEASED\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, at least 12 states have ceased nearly all abortion services. But just today, courts in Florida and Kentucky temporarily halting significant new limits in those states.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) ABC's chief White House correspondent, Cecilia Vega, reporting from Madrid, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CALLS ON CONGRESS TO LIFT FILIBUSTER TO PROTECT ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) On the world stage for the last day of this high-stakes summit...\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Good afternoon, everyone.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...President Biden turning his attention back home, blasting the Supreme Court and what he called its outrageous behavior. The historic ruling overturning Roe v. Wade now prompting a major shift for the President. He wants Congress to pass abortion rights protections at the federal level, and says he now supports carving out an exception to Senate rules that would allow Democrats to do this with just a simple majority, rather than 60 votes.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I believe we have to codify Roe v. Wade in the law. And the way to do that is to make sure the Congress votes to do that.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But the President well aware that key members of his own party have said they're not on board with eliminating the filibuster, even when it comes to abortion rights. And just as the President was about to board Air Force One for the flight home, another blow to his domestic agenda with that Supreme Court ruling limiting the \uc2 EPA\uc2 's ability to regulate greenhouse gasses. The decision effectively making it impossible for his administration to reach its goal of cutting greenhouse gas emissions in half by 2030 and eliminating carbon by 2050. The White House calling the decision devastating, saying, "It aims to take our country backwards."\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A whirlwind day for the President on this final leg of his trip. Cecilia Vega traveling with him, joining us now from Madrid. And, Cecilia, at a press conference, President Biden was also pressed on those record-high gas prices and was asked how much longer Americans should expect to be paying so much.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, Whit, well, the President didn't give a date, but he basically told Americans to brace yourself. This is not going to end anytime soon. We have heard this from him before. He is blaming Russia and this war in Ukraine for the sky-high prices. And right now, he said that Americans should expect to feel the effects of this, quote, Whit, "for as long as it takes."\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Cecilia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220701ei6u00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ABORTION BATTLE LEGAL CHALLENGES TO STATE RESTRICTIONS RAMP UP\b0\par\par\uc2 645 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BOMBSHELL HEARING\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON, HEARING, CAPITOL, TRUMP, MEADOWS\par \par \uc2 SECRET SERVICE, HEARING, TESTIMONY, MOTORCADE\par \par \uc2 TESTIMONY, HUTCHINSON, TRUMP, CHENEY\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, DRUEKE, RUSSIA, SHAW\par \par \uc2 MIGRANTS, SEMI-TRUCK, HOOD, MCMANUS, EVERITT\par \par \uc2 ROE, ABORTION, NORTHROP, GALLOWAY, WHITAKER\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Michael, now to the lawsuits ramping up across the country in the wake of that landmark Supreme Court decision. And the Biden administration now coming under pressure to take action and increase access to abortion services. Our congressional correspondent Rachel Scott is tracking it all from Flossmoor, Illinois. Rachel, good morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LEGAL CHALLENGES TO STATE RESTRICTIONS RAMP UP ACROSS THE COUNTRY\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Whit, good morning to you. The Biden administration says that every option is on the table when it comes to protecting access to abortion, including expanding access to medication, but offering few specifics as the landscape quickly changes state by state.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, confusion and uncertainty as the fight over abortion plays out state by state.\par \par \uc2 NANCY NORTHROP (PRESIDENT & CEO CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS)\par \par \uc2 Right now we're anticipating that services will be able to be continued subject to the constant back and forth of litigation.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Abortion bans are being challenged in court. Judges in Louisiana, Texas and Utah, allowing some abortions to resume for now.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW FALLOUT FROM SUPREME COURT RULING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PATCHWORK OF ABORTION ACCESS CAUSING WHIPLASH FOR ADVOCATES ON BOTH SIDES\par \par \uc2 KARRIE GALLOWAY (PRESIDENT & CEO PLANNED PARENTHOOD ASSOCIATION UTAH)\par \par \uc2 We will be calling back the women who we had to turn away on Saturday so that they can come back and get their procedure if they haven't made other arrangements.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The patchwork of abortion access causing whiplash for advocates on both sides.\par \par \uc2 MARY TAYLOR (PRESIDENT PRO LIFE UTAH)\par \par \uc2 Nobody believed that the fight was over, that overturning Roe was an end all.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In total at least 13 states have now stopped nearly all abortion services, and over a dozen others are likely to take action in the coming weeks and months. We met Dr. Amy Whitaker at a clinic in Flossmoor, Illinois. This state has quickly become a safe haven for legal abortion, as surrounding states move to block abortion access.\par \par \uc2 DR. AMY WHITAKER (CHIEF MEDICAL OFFICER PLANNED PARENTHOOD ILLINOIS)\par \par \uc2 It's been a difficult couple of days. I'm not going to sugar coat it. It is so heartbreaking to see patients who've had travel so far to get basic health care.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Studies show the states with the most restrictive abortion laws have invested the least in policies and programs for women and children.\par \par \uc2 DR. AMY WHITAKER (CHIEF MEDICAL OFFICER PLANNED PARENTHOOD ILLINOIS)\par \par \uc2 The idea that you would make a complete abortion ban without having that investment, without having universal access to free comprehensive quality prenatal care, without having access to funded child care, to me is unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In the wake of Roe versus Wade being overturned there has been an increase in demand for over-the-counter emergency contraceptives like Plan B, and this morning we are learning that CVS and Rite Aid are limiting its supply. Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Rachel Scott, thank you. Robin.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEATH TOLL RISES IN AMTRAK TRAIN DERAILMENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 NTSB\uc2 : CROSSING HAD NO CROSS ARMS, BELLS OR LIGHTS\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And coming up, we'll have the latest on that deadly \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  derailment. There have been concerns about this particular crossing for years.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Plus, the new warning about a popular baby pool product with the holiday weekend coming up. But first, let's go to Ginger. Good morning, Ginger.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220801ei6t00007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 REALITY AFTER ROE REVERSAL OF ROE V. WADE\b0\par\par\uc2 1,839 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 KELLY, SENTENCING, PEACE, MARTINEZ\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, ABORTION, GARIEPY, FRANCIS\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And now to our other major story, tonight, this one with historic consequences, the reversal of Roe v. Wade. Women now turning to other medical measures. And for some health care providers, the best answer may be preventing unwanted pregnancies before they begin. Here's ABC's congressional correspondent, Rachel Scott.\par \par \uc2 EMMA (PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 Every day that you're carrying an unwanted pregnancy is torturous. You're going through all of these physical challenges, and it's not even a goal that you want to achieve. I'd hoped to see a future where abortion care is freely accessible.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For Emma, that future may never be a reality. She lives in Texas, where abortion is banned after six weeks of pregnancy. She asked to be filmed in shadow, because she had an illegal abortion earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 EMMA (PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 I found myself pregnant, and I didn't want to be. I immediately knew I didn't want to proceed with this pregnancy. I didn't have any sort of dilemma internally about it, at all.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She felt the best way to end the pregnancy would be by medical abortion pills, an option that's quickly becoming harder to access.\par \par \uc2 EMMA (PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 My immediate concern was to not leave a paper trail of the pregnancy, and so I didn't work with any sort of physician.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATES STOPPING NEARLY ALL ABORTION SERVICES\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Stories like Emma's are becoming more common across the country. With a near-total ban on abortion now in 13 states, many women are finding themselves in confusing and difficult situations.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Hands off our bodies.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And now, they're racing to find solutions for unwanted pregnancies, exposing long-standing failures of our health care system. In Emma's case, it was too late to get a legal abortion in Texas. Her local Planned Parenthood clinic said they couldn't help her end her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 EMMA (PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 They instead offered me a phone number to a New Mexico clinic, so that I could continue the conversation there.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But she says traveling to New Mexico, where abortions are still legal, was out of the question due to the debilitating nausea and fatigue of early pregnancy. And ordering medical abortion pills on online would take too long. With the clock ticking, she needed them immediately.\par \par \uc2 DR AILEEN GARIEPY (DIR COMPLEX FAMILY PLANNING WEIL CORNELL MEDICINE)\par \par \uc2 We know that medication abortion is safe and effective to end a pregnancy up to 11 weeks. It's \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved, and has been since the year 2000.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Emma didn't know exactly how far along she was. Texas criminalizes those who help women get abortions, so she didn't want to go to a doctor for an ultrasound or sonogram. But it turned out, a friend had some pills that she had gotten from Mexico.\par \par \uc2 EMMA (PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 I was able to begin the process of self-managing an abortion without seeking any medical counseling at all.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Mexican pharmacists and doctors are bracing for a surge of American women crossing the border, looking for reproductive health care.\par \par \uc2 PHARMACIST (MALE)\par \par \uc2 (Speaking foreign language).\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS IS CYRUX, MISOPROSTOL. IT COSTS AROUND 23 TO 25 USD.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In the town of Nuevo Progresso, just a five-minute walk from the US border, pharmacist Miguel Mejia (PH) says he only sells medication abortion pills to people who have a prescription. The majority of the people buying them are young Americans.\par \par \uc2 PHARMACIST (MALE)\par \par \uc2 (Speaking foreign language).\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NO ONE ASKS FOR IT FOR THE PURPOSE OF ABORTION, BUT WE KNOW WHAT THEY ARE USING IT FOR. WE SELL IT TO TREAT GASTRITIS OR ULCERS.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Medication abortions account for more than half of all abortions in the United States, according to Guttmacher, an organization that supports abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 DR AILEEN GARIEPY (DIR COMPLEX FAMILY PLANNING WEIL CORNELL MEDICINE)\par \par \uc2 Medication abortion was accomplished using two sets of pills. The first is mifepristone. And what does that is, it causes weakening of the attachment of the pregnancy to the uterine wall. And then the second medication, misoprostol, is a prostaglandin, which causes the uterus to contract and expel the pregnancy. And those medications work together to be synergistic to end the pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Emma's friend only had misoprostol pills. After taking them, Emma said she experienced cramping, and then bled heavily.\par \par \uc2 EMMA (PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 I thought it had been successful. I was very happy to, in my mind, no longer be pregnant. But about a week and a half passed, and all the symptoms started coming back slowly.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Another at-home test showed she was still pregnant.\par \par \uc2 DR AILEEN GARIEPY (DIR COMPLEX FAMILY PLANNING WEIL CORNELL MEDICINE)\par \par \uc2 Misoprostol alone can be effective in ending a pregnancy, but it is not as effective as the combination of the mifepristone and misoprostol. The risk is that it might not work.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) After getting both mifepristone and the misoprostol combinations from a nonprofit organization, Emma was able to have a self-managed abortion at home, her dog sitting by her.\par \par \uc2 EMMA (PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 It was an excruciating pain. I uttered the words like, "I just want to die." It was that level of pain that I was experiencing. And that went on for over four hours.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) This is not like going in for a haircut or going into a nail salon. This is basically the same process, at the end of the day, as a miscarriage. In some cases, with a medication abortion or a miscarriage, emergency surgery is required in the setting of a potentially life-threatening hemorrhage. That's uncommon, but it absolutely happens.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But the pain worth it to Emma.\par \par \uc2 EMMA (PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 It was the greatest sigh of relief of, okay, I'm not pregnant anymore. Abortion medication is very safe, but we're utilizing it in a criminally risky environment. It's the fact that I couldn't have an honest conversation with a medical professional to understand what the correct way to terminate this pregnancy was.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Medication abortion is generally considered safe and effective. But Dr. Christina Francis said that, in rare instances, using medical abortion pills without consulting a health care provider can be life-threatening in cases of an undiagnosed ectopic pregnancy, in which an embryo implants outside the uterus and ultimately ruptures. They are rare. Less than 2% of all pregnancies in the US are ectopic, but the abortion pills don't work on them.\par \par \uc2 DR CHRISTINA FRANCIS (OB-GYN)\par \par \uc2 The symptoms of a ruptured ectopic pregnancy are very similar, if not identical to the symptoms a woman experiences when she's having a medication abortion. The concern is she'll stay at home. She'll be bleeding into her abdomen and not know it, and she's losing, then, precious minutes that might be life-threatening for her.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As a woman of color, Emma knew she was already at a disadvantage for accessing health care.\par \par \uc2 EMMA (PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, abortion restrictions are going to impact communities that are already overpoliced, overly criminalized. And that's a community I am part of. I have my doomsday prepper kit for my reproductive care. I have Plan B at the ready. I have home pregnancy tests, ready to go. I have a spare set of mifepristone and misoprostol, just in case.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Here in Hollandale, Mississippi, Plan A, a mobile health clinic, is helping provide reproductive care to patients in the Mississippi Delta. Julia Thomas and Antoinette Robin often drive hundreds of miles to vulnerable communities in the state, addressing the lack of access for women of color to health care.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Do you feel that people in this state, particularly people of color in this state, have equal and fair access to the health care that they need?\par \par \uc2 JULIET THOMAS COMMUNITY HEALTH WORKER PLAN A HEALTH\par \par \uc2 Not really. Being from the Mississippi Delta, you know, a lot of clinics have closed due to lack of funding and other things. So, having access to quality health care people of color don't have and even being educated on what questions to ask when they go to their provider, you know, that's kind of not done in the African-American communities.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Mississippi is known for its strict restrictions on abortion. It's the scene at the heart of last week's Supreme Court decision. Plan A doesn't provide abortion services, but takes a holistic approach on helping patients prevent unwanted pregnancies before they happen, including offering plan "B" or emergency contraception.\par \par \uc2 JULIET THOMAS COMMUNITY HEALTH WORKER PLAN A HEALTH\par \par \uc2 In a lot of communities, the clinics are not trained to do family planning or anything like that. So, therefore, they don't have those services. Yes, they may come in for a Plan B, but we counsel them on how to use that and we counsel them on contraception, period. Whether it's birth control or whatever it is, we want you to be educated on sexual and reproductive health and your options.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Dr. Francis said that increased access to health care is a good thing, and that abortion is only a symptom of the racial and economic disparities in our system, not a fix for them. She's a rarity, an OB-GYN against abortion. Most medical organizations support a woman's right to abortion.\par \par \uc2 DR CHRISTINA FRANCIS (OB-GYN)\par \par \uc2 There's a horrendous disparity in maternal mortality rates between African-American women and Caucasian women in this country, and that absolutely needs to be addressed. It needs to be fixed. But instead of just throwing abortion at it, what we need to do is fix the root causes.\par \par \uc2 EMMA (PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 I'm saddened that people will not have access to health care in the way that's most comfortable for them. They would rather be able to speak with a doctor. They would rather be given the options and be able to take their time considering their options. And that's not going to be available for many. That's going to be a thing of the past.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our thanks to Rachel Scott.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARRIED TO BRITNEY\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Up next, they met on the set of "Slumber Party." Now, he's the man married to Britney spears. An exclusive interview with actor Sam Asghari.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020220630ei6t00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FALLOUT FROM SUPREME COURT ABORTION DECISION STATEWIDE ABORTION BANS FACE LEGAL CHALLENGES\b0\par\par\uc2 697 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 HUMAN SMUGGLING, NIRENBERG, IMMIGRATION\par \par \uc2 AMTRAK\uc2 , DERAILMENT, INVESTIGATION, KRUID, SKRYPCZAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY TRAIN DERAILMENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AT LEAST THREE KILLED, DOZENS INJURED WHEN AMTRAK TRAIN HITS TRUCK\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, COLEMAN, PROTESTS, PITTMAN, MCNICHOLAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW FALLOUT FROM SUPREME COURT ABORTION DECISION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATEWIDE ABORTION BANS FACE NEW WAVE OF LEGAL CHALLENGES\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Robin, turning to the latest now, the reaction from the Supreme Court's decision overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Legal battles are ramping up over abortion rights as the focus moves to state courts and legislatures. Our senior congressional correspondent Rachel Scott is tracking it all from Waukegan, Illinois. Rachel, good morning.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Whit, good morning. And, yes, this is only the beginning. The Supreme Court's decision has ignited a messy and fierce legal battle as states decide whether women should have access to abortion.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 My body, my choice.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, the chaotic next chapter in the fight over abortion rights as statewide abortions face a wave of legal challenges. Judges in Utah and Louisiana temporarily blocking trigger laws, but providers worry it won't hold.\par \par \uc2 KATHALEEN PITTMAN (HOPE MEDICAL GROUP FOR WOMEN)\par \par \uc2 For now, we're back up and running. How long that will last, I do not know. I fear that we will be closed permanently.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION BANS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Beyond Utah and Louisiana, at least six states are now enforcing near total abortion bans. In four more, clinics have stopped providing abortions and more than a dozen others set to take action in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SCAN THE QR CODE OR GO TO GOODMORNINGAMERICA.COM FOR A STATE-BY-STATE BREAKDOWN OF ABORTION LAWS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Laws in Idaho, Kentucky and Texas all facing lawsuits as well as the state at the center of the Supreme Court case, Mississippi, where the state's last abortion clinic is fighting to stay open.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 They want you to kill your child.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Democratic states like Illinois have become a safe haven for legal abortion. We went inside this clinic in Fairview Heights.\par \par \uc2 EMPLOYEE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible) and when is your appointment?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The staff fielding calls from out-of-state patients.\par \par \uc2 DR COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS (PLANNED PARENTHOOD ST LOUIS)\par \par \uc2 Our first patient was from Arkansas, our second from Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Dr. Colleen McNicholas helps run the clinic.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Are you bracing to see a surge in patients?\par \par \uc2 DR COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS (PLANNED PARENTHOOD ST LOUIS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, we said this is ground zero and we're bracing for impact for sure. We see patients six days a week, eight hours. But soon, that will be 10 hours or 12 hours a day.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But just 15 miles away in Missouri, nearly all abortions are illegal.\par \par \uc2 STATE REPRESENTATIVE MARY ELIZABETH COLEMAN (REP) (MISSOURI)\par \par \uc2 When I was a little girl, I said, I'm part of the pro-life generation. And we would say, we are the generation that's going to end Roe.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Republican State Representative Mary Elizabeth Coleman tells me she wants to prevent Missouri health care workers from helping patients seek abortions in other states.\par \par \uc2 STATE REPRESENTATIVE MARY ELIZABETH COLEMAN (REP) (MISSOURI)\par \par \uc2 What I want to do is make sure that anyone who is operating within the state of Missouri is following the laws of the state of Missouri and not creating like a shadow network.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) These laws really do vary state to state. Here in Illinois, abortion is legal. But this clinic behind me is less than ten miles from Wisconsin, where abortions have come to a complete stop. Planned Parenthood expects up to 30,000 additional patients to travel here to the state of Illinois over the course of the next year, Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Rachel Scott for us, thank you. Michael?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, thank you, Whit.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220729ei6s0005o\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FALLOUT FROM SUPREME COURT ABORTION DECISION STATEWIDE ABORTION BANS FACE NEW LEGAL CHALLENGES\b0\par\par\uc2 874 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 HUMAN SMUGGLING, NIRENBERG, IMMIGRATION\par \par \uc2 AMTRAK\uc2 , DERAILMENT, INVESTIGATION, KRUID, SKRYPCZAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY TRAIN DERAILMENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AT LEAST THREE KILLED, DOZENS INJURED WHEN AMTRAK TRAIN HITS TRUCK\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, COLEMAN, PROTESTS, PITTMAN, MCNICHOLAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW FALLOUT FROM SUPREME COURT ABORTION DECISION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATEWIDE ABORTION BANS FACE NEW WAVE OF LEGAL CHALLENGES\par \par \uc2 RELIGION, SUPREME COURT, PRAYER, KENNEDY, PETERSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LANDMARK SCHOOL PRAYER DECISION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT SIDES WITH HIGH SCHOOL FOOTBALL COACH\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, INVASION, UKRAINE, ZELENSKYY, OCHENASH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEATH TOLL RISING IN SHOPPING MALL ATTACK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ZELENSKYY & G7 LEADERS CONDEMN RUSSIAN STRIKE IN CIVILIAN TARGET\par \par \uc2 HEARING, CAPITOL, RIOT, HUTCHINSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JANUARY 6TH COMMITTEE CALLS SURPRISE HEARING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PANEL SET TO MEET OVER "RECENTLY OBTAINED EVIDENCE"\par \par \uc2 QUEEN ELIZABETH, PRINCE CHARLES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: QUEEN ELIZABETH STEPS OUT IN SCOTLAND\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MONARCH MAKES PUBLIC APPEARANCES AFTER MISSING SEVERAL JUBILEE EVENTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MOVIE, "HOCUS POCUS"\par \par \uc2 GRINER, RUSSIA, DETENTION, BOIKOV, NYGAARD, TRIAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRITTNEY GRINER APPEARS IN RUSSIAN COURT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WNBA STAR'S DETENTION EXTENDED; TRIAL SET TO BEGIN FRIDAY\par \par \uc2 GRINER, RUSSIA, TRIAL, DETENTION\par \par \uc2 SPEARS, ALEXANDER, ROSENGART, TRIAL, STALKING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRITNEY SPEARS'S EX-HUSBAND ORDERED TO STAND TRIAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JASON ALEXANDER FACING FELONY STALKING CHARGE OVER ATTEMPTED WEDDING CRASH\par \par \uc2 STUDENT LOAN, REPAYMENT, SCAM, JENNINGS, CORDRAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STUDENT LOAN REPAYMENT ALERT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARNING ABOUT COMPANIES OFFERING LOAN PAYOFF PLANS\par \par \uc2 GRADUATES, FIREFIGHTERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 STORY2\par \par \uc2 MIGRANTS, SMUGGLING, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AT LEAST 46 FOUND DEAD INSIDE TRACTOR-TRAILER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FEDERAL INVESTIGATION UNDERWAY\par \par \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2 , ROE, ROBBS, STATES, LAW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW FALLOUT FROM SUPREME COURT ABORTION DECISION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATEWIDE ABORTION BANS FACE NEW LEGAL CHALLENGES\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Robin, we're going to turn to the latest reaction from the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe versus Wade. Let's go back to our congressional correspondent, Rachel Scott, with more on the legal battles ramping up. Good morning, again, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Michael, good morning to you. This fight really does turn to the states, abortion providers and patients now caught in the middle of a messy and fierce legal battle.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, the chaotic next chapter in the fight over abortion rights as statewide abortions face a wave of legal challenges. Judges in Utah and Louisiana temporarily blocking trigger laws.\par \par \uc2 KATHALEEN PITTMAN (HOPE MEDICAL GROUP FOR WOMEN)\par \par \uc2 For now, we're back up and running. How long that will last I do not know.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Beyond Utah and Louisiana, at least six states are now enforcing near total abortion bans. In four more, clinics have stop providing abortions and more than a dozen others set to take action in the coming weeks. Laws in Idaho, Kentucky and Texas all facing lawsuits, as well as the state at the center of the Supreme Court case, Mississippi, where the state's last abortion clinic is fighting to stay open.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 They want you to kill your child.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Democratic states like Illinois have become a safe haven for legal abortion. We went inside this clinic in Fairview Heights.\par \par \uc2 DR COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS (PLANNED PARENTHOOD ST LOUIS)\par \par \uc2 Our first was from Arkansas. Our second patient was from Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Dr. Colleen McNicholas helps run the clinic.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Are you bracing to see a surge in patients?\par \par \uc2 DR COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS (PLANNED PARENTHOOD ST LOUIS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, we said this is ground zero, and we're bracing for impact for sure.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Just 15 miles away in Missouri, nearly all abortions are illegal. Republican State Representative Mary Elizabeth Coleman tells me she wants to prevent Missouri healthcare workers from helping patients seek abortions in other states.\par \par \uc2 STATE REPRESENTATIVE MARY ELIZABETH COLEMAN (REP) (MISSOURI)\par \par \uc2 What I want to do is make sure that a anyone who is operating within the state of Missouri is following the laws of the state of Missouri and not creating like a shadow network.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) These laws vary state by state. Here in Illinois, abortion is legal, but this clinic behind me is less than 10 miles from Wisconsin, where abortions have completely stopped. Planned Parenthood tells me they are expecting to see 30,000 patients travel here over the course of the next year, guys.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Rachel Scott for us, thank you. Coming up here in our "GMA Morning Menu," a new study reveals the staggering number of parents who experience postpartum depression more than a year after their child was born.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Also this morning, our new series, finding the positive in the negative, looks at regret an how to turn that painful emotion into a force for hope and change.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Plus, first on "GMA," "Consumer Reports" sharing their list of top vegan ice creams. Becky Worley putting her taste buds on the line for this tough assignment. And "WandaVision's" Elizabeth Olsen and musician Robbie Arnett, there they are. Well, they're here to tell us about their new children's book. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220729ei6s00063\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 STATE ABORTION BATTLES JUDGES IN SEVERAL STATES TEMPORARILY BLOCK ABORTION BANS\b0\par\par\uc2 648 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 HEARING, CAPITOL RIOT, TRUMP, HUTCHINSON, CHENEY\par \par \uc2 HEARING, CAPITOL RIOT, HUTCHINSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SECRET SERVICE ACCOUNT\par \par \uc2 HUMAN SMUGGLING, MCMANUS, HOOD, EVERITT, WOLFF\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MIGRANT TRUCK HORROR\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, PROTEST, ABORTION, BECERRA, WHITAKER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATE ABORTION BATTLES\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In the meantime, the abortion battle now on the state level after the supreme court decision overturning Roe versus Wade. We have reported here on the 13 states with trigger laws looking to ban abortion immediately. Well, tonight, in some of shoes states, those trigger laws halted by the courts for now. ABC's Rachel Scott traveling the country, again, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUDGES IN SEVERAL STATES TEMPORARILY BLOCK ABORTION BANS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, facing growing pressure, President Biden's health secretary insisted the administration is looking at every option to protect abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 XAVIER BECERRA\uc2  (SECRETARY OF HEALTH & HUMAN SERVICES)\par \par \uc2 There is no magic bullet. But if there is something we can do, we will find it, and we will do it.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Secretary \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2  outlining steps like increasing access to medication abortion, but offering few specifics. Our Mary Bruce pressing for details.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When can we expect more concrete steps to be announced? What's the holdup, here?\par \par \uc2 XAVIER BECERRA\uc2  (SECRETARY OF HEALTH & HUMAN SERVICES)\par \par \uc2 It was a long decision, and it did upend 50 years of precedent. And so, you want to make sure that what you do is within, as I said, the confines of the law. We're not interested in going rogue.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The battle over abortion rights now turns to the states.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CEASED NEARLY ALL ABORTIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIKELY TO BAN OR SEVERELY RESTRICT ABORTIONS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At least 13 have stopped nearly all abortions. About a dozen others set to take action in the coming weeks and months.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 My body. My choice.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Abortion rights demonstrators marching from Illinois to Iowa.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 We have to protect our siblings who this does affect in other states.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Illinois now a safe haven, surrounded by states banning or severely restricting access to abortion, bracing for up to 30,000 more women to travel here over the year.\par \par \uc2 DR AMY WHITAKER (CHIEF MEDICAL OFFICER PLANNED PARENTHOOD ILLINOIS)\par \par \uc2 Patients who are struggling to make ends meet, who are deciding, do I pay my rent or do I feed my kids? And now, they have this, not only added expense and time of needing an abortion, but also the time they have to take off work and the childcare and the lodging. It's really heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The legal challenges continue, tonight. Abortion bans in Louisiana and Utah have been put on hold for now, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel Scott, in Illinois, for us. Rachel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RUSSIAN STRIKE ON MALL\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Overseas, tonight, and to the war in Ukraine. At least 20 people are dead, 40 more missing after that Russian missile strike on a shopping mall that's far from the frontlines.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) President Zelenskyy, tonight, sharing this video the moment the missile hit the mall, a car factory behind it also destroyed. And new images of the debris falling on a nearby park, the impact knocking people down, some jumping into the water. Families scrambling to get to safety when those missiles hit.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When we come back, Ghislaine Maxwell sentenced for child sex trafficking connected to the Jeffrey Epstein case. What the judge decided, today. And news on the pandemic tonight involving boosters this fall, what FDA advisers voted in favor of. That was late today, in a moment.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220629ei6s00005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SUPREME COURT OVERTURNS ROE V. WADE ABORTION BECOMES ILLEGAL OR SEVERELY RESTRICTED\b0\par\par\uc2 624 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASS DEMONSTRATIONS NATIONWIDE\par \par \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2 , ROE, PROTEST, BAN, BIDEN, STATES, WARREN, CROSS\par \par \uc2 ROE, SCOTUS, BAN, RUTLEDGE, MCNICHOLAS, HAYWOOD\par \par \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2 , ROE, PILLS, CRIMINAL, THOMAS, GAY RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, G-7, SCOTUS, TRUDEAU, UKRAINE, WEAPONS, TARIFFS\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, UKRAINE, ATTACKS, ZELENSKYY, BIDENKO\par \par \uc2 STANLEY CUP, AVALANCHE, LIGHTNING, LANDESKOG\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WESTERN HEAT WAVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "TOP GUN: MAVERICK"\par \par \uc2 GRINER, HOUSE, COURT, MOSCOW, DETAINED, \uc2 WNBA\par \par \uc2 PRINCE CHARLES, QATAR, CASH, JASSIM, CHARITY, QUEEN\par \par \uc2 LIGHTING, STRIKE, BOATERS, RESCUE, GUY, RUMER\par \par \uc2 BET AWARDS, HENSON, PERFORMANCES, ABORTION, WEST, DIDDY\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY, LENNON, DUET, JACKSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTEST, ASHCROFT, NOEM\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But first, we have a lot, there they are, we have a lot of news this morning starting with the Supreme Court overturning Roe v. Wade, ending abortion as a constitutional right after nearly a half century. The impact felt across the country as soon as it came down.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT OVERTURNS ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION BECOMES ILLEGAL OR SEVERELY RESTRICTED IN 11 STATES\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's go back to Rachel Scott who's tracking the rapidly developing situation state by state. Good morning again, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Robin, good morning to you again. The Supreme Court's decision sending shockwaves across the nation, the impact was immediate. This was the only clinic in the entire state of Missouri still providing abortions. Within minutes of the decision, it stopped completely.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Overnight, taking to the streets to denounce the decision to undo Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 My body, my choice.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) While others celebrated a reversal achieved after decades of activism. The protests mostly peaceful, but tensions boiling over at some. A truck running through protesters in Cedar, Rapids, Iowa. Police deploying tear gas at protesters at the Arizona State House and tasing and detaining abortion rights protesters in Greenville, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OUTLAWED ABORTION\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In the last 72 hours, at least eight states have enforced abortion bans, including right here in Missouri where there are no exceptions for rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 JAY ASHCROFT\uc2  (REP) (SECRETARY OF STATE MISSOURI)\par \par \uc2 Forty-nine years ago they said no, they told us it was over, they told to shut up and live with the killing of children. You all didn't give up, did you? You didn't give up.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Women here will now have to travel across state lines.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS (CHIEF MED OFFICER PLANNED PARENTHOOD ST LOUIS)\par \par \uc2 What it looks like practically for them, hundreds of miles, bus trips and airplane tickets. It's navigating now multiple days off of work, figuring out how to pay not only for the care but for the logistics.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For some, that won't be an option. Studies show 49% of abortion patients have an income below the poverty line. Most abortions are performed by medication. Republican South Dakota Governor \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  signaled she wants to crack down on pills prescribed online.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM (REP) (SOUTH DAKOTA)\par \par \uc2 I don't believe that telemedicine abortions are safe for individuals for women to conduct at home.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Nearby clinics in Democratic-run states like Illinois are bracing for a surge in out-of-state patients this morning. Because that's the new reality for women in states like Missouri, if they want an abortion, they'll have to travel to a state where it is legal in order to get it. Robin?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is not over. All right, Rachel, thank you. Michael?\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220729ei6r00038\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CHALLENGES AHEAD OVER ABORTION PILLS COULD WOMEN FACE CRIMINAL CHARGES?\b0\par\par\uc2 509 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASS DEMONSTRATIONS NATIONWIDE\par \par \uc2 SCOTUS, ROE, PROTEST, BAN, BIDEN, STATES, WARREN, CROSS\par \par \uc2 ROE, SCOTUS, BAN, RUTLEDGE, MCNICHOLAS, HAYWOOD\par \par \uc2 SCOTUS, ROE, PILLS, CRIMINAL, THOMAS, GAY RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, thank you, Amy. Let's bring in ABC News contributor Kate Shaw, Professor of Constitutional Law at Cardoza Law School. Kate, thank you so much for joining us this morning. And, we see it, in states where abortion has now become illegal, could a woman turning to the abortion pill be criminally prosecuted?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FOLLOWING SCOUTS RULING CHALLENGES AHEAD OVER ABORTION PILLS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COULD WOMEN FACE CRIMINAL CHARGES AFTER RECEIVING MEDICATION IN THE MAIL?\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So opponents of abortion have long maintained that although they want to end abortion, they actually don't want to see women criminally prosecuted for obtaining abortions. In the wake of the Dobbs opinion, some of them have renewed that pledge that there won't be prosecutions. But, you know the laws that are now going into effect, they're pretty broadly worded some of them, and they target anyone who administers an abortion.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And if a woman is self-administering, using the abortion pill, I think there's every possibility that a local prosecutor could bring charges. So I think we are in unchartered terrain. But there is absolutely the possibility that women could face criminal charges.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT OVERTURNS ROE V WADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CLARENCE THOMAS TAKES AIM AT RIGHTS TO CONTRACEPTION AND GAY RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's pretty broad. Even if she wants to try to travel across state lines as well. And as we just heard in his concurring opinion, Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  said the court should reconsider landmark cases on contraception, same-sex relations, gay marriage. Could those rights now be at risk?\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, Sam Alito's majority opinion says, no, this case is just about abortion. But the method the opinion used is to decide if abortion is protected by the Constitution, is basically to look to history. Say, does history recognize this, right?\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And when the court has recognized other rights, right to access contraception, to sexual intimacy, to same-sex marriage, it hasn't just looked to history, it's looked to other values and principles of liberty and equality. And if the new method requires looking to history, I think those rights are absolutely in jeopardy. And Justice Thomas, in his concurrence, not only suggests this, but calls for the court to overrule those other cases. So I do think this goes well beyond abortion.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So the fear that this could expand is justified?\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think so.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Kate, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Really appreciate your insight. Robin.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220729ei6r0002w\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FAMILIES DIVIDED OVER SUPREME COURT RULING HISTORIC DECISION PUTS MOTHERS AND DAUGHTERS AT ODDS\b0\par\par\uc2 783 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASS DEMONSTRATIONS NATIONWIDE\par \par \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2 , ROE, PROTEST, BAN, BIDEN, STATES, WARREN, CROSS\par \par \uc2 ROE, SCOTUS, BAN, RUTLEDGE, MCNICHOLAS, HAYWOOD\par \par \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2 , ROE, PILLS, CRIMINAL, THOMAS, GAY RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, G-7, SCOTUS, TRUDEAU, UKRAINE, WEAPONS, TARIFFS\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, UKRAINE, ATTACKS, ZELENSKYY, BIDENKO\par \par \uc2 STANLEY CUP, AVALANCHE, LIGHTNING, LANDESKOG\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WESTERN HEAT WAVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "TOP GUN: MAVERICK"\par \par \uc2 GRINER, HOUSE, COURT, MOSCOW, DETAINED, \uc2 WNBA\par \par \uc2 PRINCE CHARLES, QATAR, CASH, JASSIM, CHARITY, QUEEN\par \par \uc2 LIGHTING, STRIKE, BOATERS, RESCUE, GUY, RUMER\par \par \uc2 BET AWARDS, HENSON, PERFORMANCES, ABORTION, WEST, DIDDY\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY, LENNON, DUET, JACKSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTEST, ASHCROFT, NOEM\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, LEGAL, CORPORATIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CORPORATIONS RESPOND TO SCOTUS OVERTURNING ROE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOME COMPANIES PLAN TO OFFER EMPLOYEES HELP WITH ANY LEGAL TROUBLE\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, LUCAS\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to our "GMA" cover story, more on how the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade is impacting families across this country.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FAMILIES DIVIDED OVER SUPREME COURT RULING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HISTORIC DECISION PUTS MOTHERS AND DAUGHTERS AT ODDS\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Some mothers and daughters are at odds over the issue. And our Deborah Roberts sat down with a mom and a daughter. Good morning to you, Deborah.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Amy. Yeah, this mom and daughter agree on so much, but they are fiercely at odds over the abortion question and they have been for years. They are living proof this passionate debate isn't limited to fiery protests and political arguments. In many cases, there are homes divided.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Days after that Supreme Court decision, emotions still running high.\par \par \uc2 LEIGH LUCAS (PRO-CHOICE PROTESTER)\par \par \uc2 I feel like my country doesn't care about me.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But for some, the passionate difference is playing out at home, Nicole and daughter Kathleen a tight-knit pair from the Midwest can't agree. Nicole was a 21-year-old college student back in 1988 when she met Kathleen's father.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How tough was it for you being so young and have a baby?\par \par \uc2 MOTHER OF KATHLEEN AGAINST ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 Very tough. We didn't have money. We had cardboard boxes for tables. We didn't have a TV.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Pregnant and scared, Nicole says she considered an abortion, still recalling that first doctor visit more than 30 years ago.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (MOTHER OF KATHLEEN / AGAINST ABORTION RIGHTS)\par \par \uc2 She showed just a picture of where the fetus was at that point. And to me, that look like a lot like a baby.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, abortion was off the table for you?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (MOTHER OF KATHLEEN / AGAINST ABORTION RIGHTS)\par \par \uc2 It was off the table immediately.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) So, Nicole is now against abortion while her daughter firmly believes in a woman's right to choose.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN (DAUGHTER OF NICOLE / FOR ABORTION RIGHTS)\par \par \uc2 I have a right to control my own body, my own life, my own future, my own destiny.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And for you, Kathleen, to know that you formed her opinion against abortion.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN (DAUGHTER OF NICOLE / FOR ABORTION RIGHTS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. I hate it.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (MOTHER OF KATHLEEN / AGAINST ABORTION RIGHTS)\par \par \uc2 I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She doesn't want to be a pro-life mascot.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (MOTHER OF KATHLEEN / AGAINST ABORTION RIGHTS)\par \par \uc2 You're not. You're just a wonderful person that is in this world and you're my daughter.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Their differences are intense, they say, but so is their tight bond.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN (DAUGHTER OF NICOLE / FOR ABORTION RIGHTS)\par \par \uc2 It wouldn't be our relationship if this wasn't something that I could talk to you about and disagree with you about and try to convince you on. That's part of what makes us so close I feel that we talk.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, she is a little uncomfortable about women losing their rights. Her daughter says she is not going to stop arguing with her mom over this issue, determined to convince her that she and other women should have that same right to choose which her mother had way back when she made a tough decision. Amy? Robin?\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, Deb. And I bet that's with a lot of families that this discussion is going on.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. But now, we have our "GMA," thank you, Deb.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220729ei6r0003a\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 08:00 HALF-HOUR\b0\par\par\uc2 539 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASS DEMONSTRATIONS NATIONWIDE\par \par \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2 , ROE, PROTEST, BAN, BIDEN, STATES, WARREN, CROSS\par \par \uc2 ROE, SCOTUS, BAN, RUTLEDGE, MCNICHOLAS, HAYWOOD\par \par \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2 , ROE, PILLS, CRIMINAL, THOMAS, GAY RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, G-7, SCOTUS, TRUDEAU, UKRAINE, WEAPONS, TARIFFS\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, UKRAINE, ATTACKS, ZELENSKYY, BIDENKO\par \par \uc2 STANLEY CUP, AVALANCHE, LIGHTNING, LANDESKOG\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WESTERN HEAT WAVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "TOP GUN: MAVERICK"\par \par \uc2 GRINER, HOUSE, COURT, MOSCOW, DETAINED, \uc2 WNBA\par \par \uc2 PRINCE CHARLES, QATAR, CASH, JASSIM, CHARITY, QUEEN\par \par \uc2 LIGHTING, STRIKE, BOATERS, RESCUE, GUY, RUMER\par \par \uc2 BET AWARDS, HENSON, PERFORMANCES, ABORTION, WEST, DIDDY\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY, LENNON, DUET, JACKSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America. It's 8:00 AM.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ROE V. WADE OVERTURNED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT'S MAJOR SHIFT\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Mass demonstrations nationwide. After the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade, ending nearly 50 years of a constitutional right to an abortion. Now, which states are racing to ban the procedure or severely limit access? While others look to protect abortion rights and how the decision is dividing families. Like a mother who considered abortion and her now-grown daughter, this as some major corporations announce how they'll help employees seeking abortions.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA EXCLUSIVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DANNY BONADUCE OPENS UP\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 "GMA" exclusive. "The Partridge Family" actor Danny Bonaduce speaking about his battle with a mystery illness for the first time and his message this morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIG REVEAL!\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUMMER CONCERT SERIES\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And we're ready to shake it off and head back to Central Park. We've partied with Taylor and danced with BTS. Now, we're just moments away from finding out who will be rocking out with us this summer.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIVE ON GMA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TESSA THOMPSON\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Tessa Thompson is bringing the thunder to Times Square this morning,\par \par \uc2 TESSA THOMPSON ("THOR: LOVE AND THUNDER")\par \par \uc2 Am I sensing feelings?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 She's here live talking "Thor: Love and Thunder."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIVE IN TIMES SQUARE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARTIN LAWRENCE\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And Martin Lawrence is here live celebrating 30 years of his ground-breaking show, "Martin." Its impact and getting the gang back together. And he's saying...\par \par \uc2 MARTIN LAWRENCE (COMEDIAN / ACTOR)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Live in Times Square, this is "GMA."\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 There he is, there's my guy Martin Lawrence. Good morning, Martin. We're excited to have him here and we're excited because we have some exciting news coming up in our next hour. We're going to reveal our Summer Concert Series lineup, a lot of great acts.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 We got our interns outside in Times Square helping us reveal, we're already giving you guys the first artist. So, everyone, go ahead and turn over those signs. Yeah, the Black Eyed Peas, just one of the amazing acts performing in Central Park. We're going to reveal the entire incredible lineup coming up.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And we have the best interns, can we just say that? The interns that we have here, they're all in.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220729ei6r00037\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CORPORATIONS RESPOND TO SCOTUS OVERTURNING ROE COMPANIES OFFER EMPLOYEES HELP WITH ANY LEGAL TROUBLE\b0\par\par\uc2 702 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASS DEMONSTRATIONS NATIONWIDE\par \par \uc2 SCOTUS, ROE, PROTEST, BAN, BIDEN, STATES, WARREN, CROSS\par \par \uc2 ROE, SCOTUS, BAN, RUTLEDGE, MCNICHOLAS, HAYWOOD\par \par \uc2 SCOTUS, ROE, PILLS, CRIMINAL, THOMAS, GAY RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, G-7, SCOTUS, TRUDEAU, UKRAINE, WEAPONS, TARIFFS\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, UKRAINE, ATTACKS, ZELENSKYY, BIDENKO\par \par \uc2 STANLEY CUP, AVALANCHE, LIGHTNING, LANDESKOG\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WESTERN HEAT WAVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "TOP GUN: MAVERICK"\par \par \uc2 GRINER, HOUSE, COURT, MOSCOW, DETAINED, \uc2 WNBA\par \par \uc2 PRINCE CHARLES, QATAR, CASH, JASSIM, CHARITY, QUEEN\par \par \uc2 LIGHTING, STRIKE, BOATERS, RESCUE, GUY, RUMER\par \par \uc2 BET AWARDS, HENSON, PERFORMANCES, ABORTION, WEST, DIDDY\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY, LENNON, DUET, JACKSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTEST, ASHCROFT, NOEM\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, LEGAL, CORPORATIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CORPORATIONS RESPOND TO SCOTUS OVERTURNING ROE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOME COMPANIES PLAN TO OFFER EMPLOYEES HELP WITH ANY LEGAL TROUBLE\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Robin, corporations are among those responding to the overturning of Roe. Many offering to help employees seek abortions as well as aiding anyone who faces legal trouble for helping a woman get the procedure. Chief business correspondent Rebecca Jarvis is here now with more. Good morning, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning to you, Michael. And in the wake of the decisions in the weeks leading up, dozens of major American companies, including \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 , \uc2 Apple\uc2 , Amazon, \uc2 Dick's Sporting Goods\uc2  and \uc2 Disney\uc2 , the parent company of ABC News, have announced expanded benefits and outlined how their healthcare policies can cover travel costs for employees in states that restrict abortion access.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) \uc2 Lyft\uc2  and Uber have also vowed to cover legal fees for rideshare drivers if they face lawsuits for transporting women to clinics. And Patagonia, in a post on LinkedIn, Friday announced it will also pay bail for full and part-time employees who peacefully protest for reproductive justice.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Most large health insurance plans do cover the cost of abortion. But what's not clear here, according to legal experts, is how these private policies may be impacted by state laws and what happens to both the corporations and employees who take advantage of the benefit and travel elsewhere because they live in states where abortion is banned, Michael.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The big statements by those companies. Rebecca, thank you. Amy?\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, Michael, coming up, in our "GMA Morning Menu," the Roe v. Wade decision dividing some families.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA MORNING MENU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:11, FAMILIES DIVIDED OVER SCOTUS RULING\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We're going to hear from a woman who considered abortion, and her daughter.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:13, DANNY BONADUCE SPEAKS OUT\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Also, this morning, our exclusive with "Partridge Family" star Danny Bonaduce talking about a mystery illness that made speaking and walking difficult.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:43, SUMMER CONCERT SERIES!\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Plus, we're revealing our Summer Concert Series lineup. It's just moments away.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:38, MARTIN LAWRENCE HERE LIVE!\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And Lara's with a very special guest. Hey, Lara.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Michael. I sure am. Martin Lawrence in the house. And we are talking all things Martin. We're going to have a great time coming up on "Good Morning America." Don't go anywhere. And more of our Summer Concert Series. Who's plating? We'll tell you, coming up on "GMA."\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Uh-oh, watch out now, we got some dancing people on this set at "GMA."\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) George is here?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Because that is Megan, Megan Thee Stallion. That's her performing at the AMA Awards. And you know where else she's going to be performing?\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, baby.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Live in Central Park for our "GMA" Summer Concert Series. And she's just one of the amazing lineup. We're going to reveal the entire list coming up.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So good.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, very exciting. More dancing to come.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220729ei6r00039\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SUPREME COURT OVERTURNS ROE V WADE 26 STATES PLAN TO BAN OR LIMIT ABORTION ACCESS\b0\par\par\uc2 624 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASS DEMONSTRATIONS NATIONWIDE\par \par \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2 , ROE, PROTEST, BAN, BIDEN, STATES, WARREN, CROSS\par \par \uc2 ROE, SCOTUS, BAN, RUTLEDGE, MCNICHOLAS, HAYWOOD\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Robin, let's go now to Rachel Scott, who's been tracking the rapidly developing situation state by state, where abortion bans and restrictions are already coming into effect. Rachel joins us from St. Louis, Missouri, this morning. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT OVERTURNS ROE V WADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 26 STATES PLAN TO BAN OR LIMIT ABORTION ACCESS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Amy, good morning. And that impact was immediate. This was the only clinic in the entire state of Missouri providing abortions, within minutes of the Supreme Court's decision it stopped completely, leaving women in this state scrambling for options.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, states across the country are racing to restrict access to abortion.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE RUTLEDGE (ATTORNEY GENERAL ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  has in fact overruled Roe versus Wade, thereby restoring the state of Arkansas the authority to prohibit abortions.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In the last 72 hours, at least eight states have enforced abortion bans, including right here in Missouri, where there are no exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 JAY ASHCROFT\uc2  (REP) (SECRETARY OF STATE MISSOURI)\par \par \uc2 49 years ago they said no. They told us it was over, they told us to shut up and live with the killing of children. You all didn't give up, did you? You didn't give up.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Women here will now have to travel across state lines.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS (CHIEF MED OFFICER PLANNED PARENTHOOD ST LOUIS)\par \par \uc2 What it looks like practically for them is hundreds of miles, bus trips and airplane tickets. It's navigating now multiple days off of work, figuring out how to pay not only for the care but for the logistics.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For some that won't be an option. Studies show 49% of abortion patients have an income below the poverty line. In Louisiana, where the maternal mortality rate is one of the worst of the nation, especially among black women, advocates say they are receiving a flood of calls.\par \par \uc2 DEON HAYWOOD (EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR "WOMEN WITH A VISION")\par \par \uc2 If you have a job where you're being paid hourly and you're being paid minimum wage, how easy would it be for you to be able to leave your job or, or existing kids to have an abortion and then come back? It wouldn't work for them. It just wouldn't work. It's not practical for their lives, the lives that they are living.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Three other states, Arizona, West Virginia and Wisconsin, waiting on state officials to give further guidance, but for now stopping abortions all together. Most abortions are performed by medication. Republican South Dakota Governor \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 , signaled she wants to crack down on pills prescribed online.\par \par \uc2 SEN KRISTI NOEM (REP) (SOUTH DAKOTA)\par \par \uc2 I don't believe that the telemedicine abortions are safe for individuals, for women to conduct at home.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So this morning, Democratic governors are banding together to expand access to abortions, because that is the new reality for women in states like Missouri. If they want an abortion they will have to travel to a state where it is legal in order to get one. And I can tell you that nearby clinics in states like Illinois, they are expecting a flood of patients this morning, Amy.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Rachel Scott, we appreciate it, thank you. Michael.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220729ei6r0002v\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MASS DEMONSTRATIONS ACROSS THE COUNTRY NATION REACTS TO SUPREME COURT'S HISTORIC DECISION\b0\par\par\uc2 719 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASS DEMONSTRATIONS NATIONWIDE\par \par \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2 , ROE, PROTEST, BAN, BIDEN, STATES, WARREN, CROSS\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, of course, that's where we begin with that big shift, the Supreme Court overturning Roe versus Wade, ending abortion as a constitutional right after nearly a half century. The impact a as you know felt across the country as soon as it came down. At least 26 states are expected to ban or severely restrict abortion rights. Our senior national correspondent Terry Moran starts us off from the Supreme Court. Good morning, Terry.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASS DEMONSTRATIONS ACROSS THE COUNTRY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATION REACTS TO SUPREME COURT'S HISTORIC DECISION\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Robin. When the Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade, it changed the status of American women as citizens under the Constitution. And this morning, that judicial decision is swiftly becoming hard law in states across the country, triggering anguished protests and joyful celebrations.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Abort this court. Abort this court.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Overnight, the tectonic shift by the Supreme Court causing two waves across the country.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 My body.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 My choice.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Americans in dozens of cities and states taking to the streets to denounce the decision to undo Roe versus Wade. While others celebrated reversal achieved after decades of activism. And while most protests were peaceful, at times the two waves crashed. A truck running through protesters in Cedar Rapids, Iowa. Police deploying tear gas against protesters at the Arizona statehouse. And tasing and detaining abortion rights protesters in Greenville, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT OVERTURNS ROE V WADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 31 MILLION AMERICANS IN 8 STATES NOW AFFECTED BY BANNED PROCEDURE\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Supreme Court decision Friday overturned nearly 50 years of a woman's right to choose abortion under the constitution. The conservative majority on the bench clearing the way for individual states to restrict or outright ban abortion services. This morning, eight states are enforcing bans with few exceptions, and additional 18 are likely to ban or severely restrict abortion services in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COUTH OVERTURNS ROE V WADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT BIDEN URGED TO TAKE BOLD ACTION TO INCREASE ABORTION ACCESS\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Pressure is growing on President Biden from within his own party, 34 Democratic senators urging him to, quote, "Take every step available to help women access abortions."\par \par \uc2 SEN ELIZABETH WARREN (DEM) (MASSACHUSETTS)\par \par \uc2 It also means asking the President of the United States to make abortion as available as possible with the tools he has including medication abortion, including using federal lands as a place where abortions can occur.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The White House says it will challenge states that try to stop women from traveling across state lines to receive abortion services. But Biden says the larger issue is one for the voters in November. A fight anti-abortion rights advocates are ready for.\par \par \uc2 KAREN CROSS\uc2  (POLITICAL DIRECTOR NATIONAL RIGHT TO LIFE)\par \par \uc2 I cried. I don't usually cry. But, and it's not the end. It's the beginning of a lot to do, you know, to protect babies.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The big question in those midterms now that Roe versus Wade has been overturned, will the issue drive Democratic turnout the same way that it drove Republican turnout for decades? Robin.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But, Terry, beyond the elections there is also the possibility now that the Supreme Court decision could open the door to other cases being overturned?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There is that possibility, Robin. Justice \uc2 Sam Alito\uc2  in his opinion said that it should only apply to abortion. The ruling only applies to abortion. But Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , who's the most senior and most influential justice on the court, said that the reasoning that the court used to overturn Roe should be used to reconsider cases that guarantee the right to same-sex marriage, to purchase contraceptives, even to private sexual conduct, that as the dissent said, one thing tends to follow another. Robin.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Terry, thank you. Amy.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220729ei6r0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 A HOUSE DIVIDED\b0\par\par\uc2 1,225 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 18-WHEELER, DEAD, MCMANUS, MIGRANTS, HOOD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLAS, WALES, COLEMAN, HAYWOOD, PITTMAN, DEMPSEY\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, NICOLE, WALSH, ROE, PODCAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The abortion battle has divided the nation, causing a split between families, neighbors and friends. Tonight, our Deborah Roberts takes you inside the rift between a mother and daughter.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (MOTHER OF KATHLEEN WALSH)\par \par \uc2 You put that baby inside of you, you are responsible.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN WALSH (FREELANCE WRITER)\par \par \uc2 No, (censored by network) that. What...\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (MOTHER OF KATHLEEN WALSH)\par \par \uc2 Whatever your choice, you are responsible.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN WALSH (FREELANCE WRITER)\par \par \uc2 No, (censored by network) that. No.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (MOTHER OF KATHLEEN WALSH)\par \par \uc2 Okay. Well, we're just going to never agree on this, baby doll.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) That's Kathleen Walsh and her mom Nicole, sparring about abortion rights on a podcast for "The Cut" and Vox media. The two are very close, but strongly disagree on the top of abortion. Kathleen, a writer, is vehemently pro-abortion rights. Her mother is not. It's a familiar family feud to many, a house painfully divided.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (MOTHER OF KATHLEEN WALSH)\par \par \uc2 Until you are pregnant, you don't know how you will feel about that fetus inside you. You don't know that.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN WALSH (FREELANCE WRITER)\par \par \uc2 There's a whole world of other experiences. Whatever maternal bond or instinct or like magical spiritual moment that you've experienced, that's not universal.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Nine months after Kathleen recorded that podcast and penned her article for "The Cut," we sat down with mom and daughter to discuss their decade-long disagreement.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN WALSH (FREELANCE WRITER)\par \par \uc2 Hi, mom.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It was just days before the Supreme Court would make its landmark new ruling overturning Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When do you think abortion should be legal and not legal?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (MOTHER OF KATHLEEN WALSH)\par \par \uc2 Before 16 weeks. After 16 weeks, I don't think that it should be.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And what is it about 16 weeks for you?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (MOTHER OF KATHLEEN WALSH)\par \par \uc2 The feeling. You feel the baby inside you, at that point.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Every pregnancy's unique, but on average, women are able to feel movement from their fetus for the first time between 16 and 20 weeks.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Why is it so important for you to try to change her mind?\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN WALSH (FREELANCE WRITER)\par \par \uc2 I have the right to control my own body, my own life, my own future, my own destiny.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) When Kathleen was conceived, her mom Nicole was a 21-year-old college student. She met Kathleen's dad back in 1988, on a summer break. Kathleen's parents eventually married. More than 30 years later, Nicole still recalls that first visit to a doctor.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (MOTHER OF KATHLEEN WALSH)\par \par \uc2 She showed me just the picture of where the fetus was, at that point, and to me, that looked an awful lot like a baby.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, abortion was off the table for you?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (MOTHER OF KATHLEEN WALSH)\par \par \uc2 Was off the table, immediately.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Life was a little tough. Did you regret it?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (MOTHER OF KATHLEEN WALSH)\par \par \uc2 Never. Not for a moment. I just looked at her and said, "Did the world exist before she came along? Because I don't think it could have."\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) That sentiment at the heart of this heated argument between mom and daughter, an anti-abortion origin story Kathleen finds problematic.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN WALSH (FREELANCE WRITER)\par \par \uc2 I feel like so much of her position about abortion, the way it was framed to me growing up was I'm against abortion, because I loved you so much. I don't want to be that. I don't feel that way. I don't, I don't want to be the reason anybody is against abortion, because now I feel like that's limiting my own freedom and my own power to choose my own future.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You two had an enlightening discussion on your podcast. Let's see if I can play this.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN WALSH (FREELANCE WRITER)\par \par \uc2 It just feels like I'm the reason she's so pro-life, and I don't want to be that. Like I don't want to be a pro-life mascot.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She doesn't want to be a pro-life mascot.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (MOTHER OF KATHLEEN WALSH)\par \par \uc2 You're not. You're not. You're just a wonderful person that is in this world, and you're my daughter.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) For you, Kathleen, to know that you formed her opinion against abortion.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN WALSH (FREELANCE WRITER)\par \par \uc2 I hate it.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (MOTHER OF KATHLEEN WALSH)\par \par \uc2 I'm sorry. We don't have to hate each other because we don't agree on something.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN WALSH (FREELANCE WRITER)\par \par \uc2 I will always love you, and I, it wouldn't be our relationship if this wasn't something that I could talk to you about and disagree with you about and try to convince you on.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (MOTHER OF KATHLEEN WALSH)\par \par \uc2 We do have quite a few things in common, reading the same books.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For these two, a possible new turn in a debate both say holds very little middle ground.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The idea that, now, that there are so many states that are starting to enact laws that are prohibiting abortion, some of them, Oklahoma, very stringent laws, Texas, what was your reaction to those moves?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (MOTHER OF KATHLEEN WALSH)\par \par \uc2 I have to say a little bit of fear.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Fear?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (MOTHER OF KATHLEEN WALSH)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. Because as much as I don't really agree with it for me, it's a huge change. And anytime you lose a freedom, even one that is controversial, that's frightening to me.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Kathleen is smiling, over there.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN WALSH (FREELANCE WRITER)\par \par \uc2 I'm so happy.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Is that progress, in your mind?\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN WALSH (FREELANCE WRITER)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. I feel like that's what I would say when you, when a freedom is taken away, that's terrifying. And it just, oh, mom.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade, the decision 6-3 in favor of Mississippi, with five conservative judges voting to overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN WALSH (FREELANCE WRITER)\par \par \uc2 Roe being overturned is something that I've been preparing for, but the final decision feels gutting. Even if she and I disagree about this, she loves and supports me. So, she's changing her votes. She's signing petitions. She's sending me supportive text messages. I do think that's a special quality of hers.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And that's "Nightline." You can watch all of our full episodes on \uc2 Hulu\uc2 . We'll see you right back here, same time tomorrow. Thanks for staying up with us. Good night, America.\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020220628ei6r0005o\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AFTER ROE\b0\par\par\uc2 2,110 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 18-WHEELER, DEAD, MCMANUS, MIGRANTS, HOOD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLAS, WALES, COLEMAN, HAYWOOD, PITTMAN, DEMPSEY\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We turn now to the clash over abortion rights. ABC News congressional correspondent Rachel Scott has traveled the country covering this issue in the months leading up to the Supreme Court's historic decision. Tonight, she's reporting from Illinois and Missouri, one state where abortion is legal, and the other illegal.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, the divided states of America, as a nationwide firestorm continues following Friday's Supreme Court decision that overturned Roe versus Wade. Over the weekend, many Americans taking to the streets. From New York...\par \par \uc2 PROTESTORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We will rise up.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...to Mississippi...\par \par \uc2 PROTESTOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Your baby is alive, right now.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...to California, in protest.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Rise up for abortion. Rise up for abortion.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Others celebrating...\par \par \uc2 PROTESTORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Roe v. Wade is going to go.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...this monumental moment.\par \par \uc2 PRO-LIFE PROTESTOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I honestly cried tears of joy.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The conservative majority of the highest court in the land clearing the way for states to outright ban abortion services for the first time in nearly 50 years.\par \par \uc2 REP ELISE STEFANIK (REP) (NEW YORK)\par \par \uc2 Today's historic Supreme Court decision is a victory for the sanctity of life.\par \par \uc2 GOV KRISTI NOEM (REP) (SOUTH DAKOTA)\par \par \uc2 What I would say is this was wonderful news in the defense of life. Every life is precious.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Health care providers just miles apart, now divided by varying laws.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Are you bracing to see a surge in patients?\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS (CHIEF MED OFCR PLANNED PARENTHOOD ST. LOUIS REGION)\par \par \uc2 We said this is ground zero, and we're bracing for impact, for sure.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Right now, at least six states are enforcing near-total abortion bans.\par \par \uc2 KATHY ZENTNER (VOLUNTEER CLINIC PATIENT ESCORT)\par \par \uc2 I said to her, do you realize you're the last woman to get a legal abortion in Alabama? And she said, yeah, and I'm so grateful.\par \par \uc2 EMILY WALES (PRESIDENT & CEO PLANNED PARENTHOOD GREAT PLAINS)\par \par \uc2 There is no doubt that we're going to end up with haves and have-nots. People who have resources will get out of this part of the country to get care. People without those resources will not have that option.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In the coming weeks and months a total of 26 states are likely to ban or severely restrict access to abortion.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) There's a range of laws that sort of all are known as trigger bans. So, they're not all total bans. Some of them prohibit abortion after six or eight or 10 weeks of pregnancy. That's still a significant reduction in access to abortion. And the exceptions in these laws look different from state to state.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, some do have exceptions for abortion in the case of rape or incest. Some do not. There's a range of approaches to how exceptions for the life of the woman are described. Some say life. Some say life or health.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Missouri, one of the 13 states with such trigger laws and where almost all abortions are now banned, Republican lawmaker Mary Elizabeth Coleman says this victory is decades in the making.\par \par \uc2 MARY ELIZABETH COLEMAN (REP) (STATE REP MISSOURI)\par \par \uc2 When I was a little girl, I said, "I'm part of the pro-life generation," and we would say, "We're the generation that's going to end Roe." I didn't expect that we would see it maybe even in my lifetime, and certainly not while I was still serving. So...\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You didn't expect that trigger law to actually go into effect?\par \par \uc2 MARY ELIZABETH COLEMAN (REP) (STATE REP MISSOURI)\par \par \uc2 No. And, actually, we drafted it in a way that had three different ways for it to be enacted, because we weren't sure that there would be a Republican majority, even when it would ultimately happen, because I thought we were writing a bill for 40 or 50 years from now.\par \par \uc2 MARY ELIZABETH COLEMAN (REP) (STATE REP MISSOURI)\par \par \uc2 I think that Roe was a disastrous opinion that was not based in anything in the Constitution. To be able to right a bad decision is, I think, a real victory for our country.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Coleman is pushing legislation aimed at preventing health care workers in Missouri from helping their patients get abortions in other states.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You would like to crack down on abortion providers from helping women in this state get appointments and access to abortion in other states?\par \par \uc2 MARY ELIZABETH COLEMAN (REP) (STATE REP MISSOURI)\par \par \uc2 What I would like to see happen is people's activity in the state of Missouri follow the law in the state of Missouri. And if people are going to choose not to do that and to violate the laws of our state, then they will be liable under the penalties in our code for that activity.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Do you want to stop women in Missouri from traveling out of state to get an abortion?\par \par \uc2 MARY ELIZABETH COLEMAN (REP) (STATE REP MISSOURI)\par \par \uc2 So I don't think there's anything that the state can do or should do, and I'm certainly not interested in limiting a woman's movement. But what I am interested is protecting the life of all children, not just in the state of Missouri, but across the country. Our laws and our Constitution mean that we have to focus on providing and pushing for legislation either in Congress and having national federal legislation that's going to guide these things. And then also, in Missouri, making sure that people aren't aiding or abetting violating the laws in the state of Missouri.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Just 15 miles away, across state lines in Illinois, abortion is still legal.\par \par \uc2 HEALTHCARE WORKER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 How can I help you? Your first and last name.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Health care providers at the Fairview Heights Clinic have been fielding calls all day.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS (CHIEF MED OFCR PLANNED PARENTHOOD ST. LOUIS REGION)\par \par \uc2 Right now, we with see patients six days a week, eight hours a day. But soon, that will be 10 hours or 12 hours a day. And eventually, we'll probably add Sunday to the mix.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Have you seen patients, today, traveling from out of state? Are they frantic? Can you give us a window into those?\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS (CHIEF MED OFCR PLANNED PARENTHOOD ST. LOUIS REGION)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. Our first couple of patients were from out of state, today. We had our first patient was from Arkansas. Our second patient was from Oklahoma. And so, that will continue to be the case. But outside of our normal service area, Tennessee, Texas, Oklahoma, Arkansas, Mississippi, we are seeing those folks already coming to us.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But for some, that won't be an option.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What is the impact of low-income women, women of color? Does this put women at risk?\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS (CHIEF MED OFCR PLANNED PARENTHOOD ST. LOUIS REGION)\par \par \uc2 Absolutely. We know that all of these bans and restrictions impact them the most. No matter how robust of a system we can set up to help folks travel to our clinic here in Illinois, there will always be people that don't make it.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Studies show 49% of abortion patients have an income below the poverty line. In Louisiana, where the maternal mortality rate is one of the worst in the nation, especially among black women, advocates say they are receiving a flood of calls.\par \par \uc2 DEON HAYWOOD (EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR" WOMEN WITH A VISION")\par \par \uc2 If you have a job where you're being paid hourly and you're being paid minimum wage, how easy would it be for you to be able to leave your job or, or existing kids, to leave to drive somewhere, to be driven somewhere or drive somewhere to have an abortion and then come back? It wouldn't work for them.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Dionne Haywood is Executive Director of Women With A Vision in New Orleans. Since Roe was overturned, she's been receiving calls from desperate women.\par \par \uc2 DEON HAYWOOD (EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR" WOMEN WITH A VISION")\par \par \uc2 One person wanted to know, how much bleach should I mix with my cold drink or juice to take to end my pregnancy? And so, we're saying, absolutely don't take bleach.\par \par \uc2 KATHALEEN PITTMAN (ADMINISTRATOR HOPE MEDICAL GROUP FOR WOMEN)\par \par \uc2 The majority of patients we see here are persons of color. The majority already have one or more children. We are looking at women who are already struggling, financially. They're trying to take care of the families they already have, and yet now they're told that their choice is gone.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Kathaleen Pittman is the administrator at the Hope Medical Group for Women in Shreveport, Louisiana, where abortions resumed today after a judge temporarily blocked their statewide ban from going into effect.\par \par \uc2 KATHALEEN PITTMAN (ADMINISTRATOR HOPE MEDICAL GROUP FOR WOMEN)\par \par \uc2 As of this morning, we were granted a temporary restraining order in New Orleans, and so, for now, we are up and running again. We will resume procedures tomorrow. How long that will last, I do not know.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The order remains in effect until the end of next week, but Pittman says she fears the worst is still yet to come.\par \par \uc2 KATHALEEN PITTMAN (ADMINISTRATOR HOPE MEDICAL GROUP FOR WOMEN)\par \par \uc2 I fear that we will be closed permanently. I fear that women will be forced to continue pregnancies they can't care for. I fear women will show up in emergency rooms, in very bad situations from complications. I fear more women will die, literally.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) While some states have near-total bans on abortion, others declaring themselves safe havens. Sixteen states, plus Washington, DC, have laws that explicitly protect access to abortion care.\par \par \uc2 GOV KATE BROWN (DEM) (OREGON)\par \par \uc2 We will continue to protect patients from any state who come to our states for abortion care.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Governors of California, Washington and Oregon, even forming a self-proclaimed West Coast offense, welcoming women seeking abortions with open arms. The state of Oregon also donating $15 million to community-based organizations like the Lilith Clinic in Portland.\par \par \uc2 GRAYSON DEMPSEY (SPOKESPERSON LILITH CLINIC)\par \par \uc2 We feel incredibly fortunate to be on the West Coast. We are fortunate to have leaders who not only promote pro-choice policies, but I think go the distance and are proactive in figuring out how we can protect access to abortion care.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But Grayson Dempsey fears that investment won't be enough to sustain a continued influx of patients.\par \par \uc2 GRAYSON DEMPSEY (SPOKESPERSON LILITH CLINIC)\par \par \uc2 I don't think that that 15 million is going to be the answer to all of our problems. I think that that needs to be an initial investment in the infrastructure that we build up in the state, making sure that we have the workface to meet the demand, making sure that we are training the next generation of providers, making sure that clinics have the infrastructure in place to be able to serve this huge surge in patients.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For women and health care providers across the country, no matter where they live, a post-Roe world is the new normal.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I have to say that I don't think any place in the country is going to be untouched by this decision. So, even in the states that will continue to provide robust access to abortion, there will be a significant change, because there will be an influx of individuals from other states, and that's going to impact the availability of people in that state to access abortion care. Everyone is going to feel the impact.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: A HOUSE DIVIDED\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Our thanks to Rachel. Up next, a house divided, the rift between the mother and daughter over the abortion debate.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020220628ei6r0005n\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE INDEX\b0\par\par\uc2 252 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 AMTRAK\uc2 , COLLISION, RESCUE, NIGHTINGALE, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ABORTION, LUCAS, MCNICHOLAS, COLEMAN, EVERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE STATES BANNING ABORTIONS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, G7 SUMMIT, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN UNDER PRESSURE\par \par \uc2 RELIGION, SCHOOLS, KENNEDY, PETERSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT RULES IN PRAYER CASE\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, INVASION, RUSSIA, RESCUE, OCHENASH, SEARCH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MISSILE STRIKE ON SHOPPING MALL\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, CANCELLATIONS, LIGHTNING, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HUNDREDS OF FLIGHT CANCELLATIONS\par \par \uc2 GAS, PRICES, QUEEN ELIZABETH II\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE INDEX\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) To "The Index" of other news, tonight, gas prices now falling at least slightly for a second week in a row.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GAS PRICE NATIONAL AVERAGE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) GasBuddy tonight reporting the national average has dropped to $4.88 per gallon. It's down from $4.97 a week ago. We'll take anything. Analysts expect prices to keep falling heading into the July 4th weekend, possibly down 20 cents from the record peak.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Queen Elizabeth, tonight, making her first public appearance since her Platinum Jubilee.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Queen traveling to Scotland to take part in the Ceremony of the Keys, a tradition dating back centuries. The Queen, now 96, cutting back on her public schedule because of mobility issues, but she is expected to attend a military ceremony, tomorrow. We love seeing the Queen smile.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When we come back here, tonight, the retired kindergarten teacher. You have to see her smile. A surprise, years later. Did they really remember her?\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220628ei6r00008\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEWS HEADLINES HUNDREDS OF FLIGHT CANCELLATIONS/BOAT HIT BY LIGHTNING\b0\par\par\uc2 322 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 AMTRAK\uc2 , COLLISION, RESCUE, NIGHTINGALE, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ABORTION, LUCAS, MCNICHOLAS, COLEMAN, EVERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE STATES BANNING ABORTIONS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, G7 SUMMIT, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN UNDER PRESSURE\par \par \uc2 RELIGION, SCHOOLS, KENNEDY, PETERSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT RULES IN PRAYER CASE\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, INVASION, RUSSIA, RESCUE, OCHENASH, SEARCH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MISSILE STRIKE ON SHOPPING MALL\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, CANCELLATIONS, LIGHTNING, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HUNDREDS OF FLIGHT CANCELLATIONS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Next, tonight, here, hundreds of flights canceled for a third day in a row here in the US. More than 800 flight cancellations today alone, after 1,500 flights were canceled this weekend.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Adding to all this, at the airport in Charlotte, today, passengers were evacuated off an \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  flight bound for Florida when an odor was reported in the cabin. Nationwide cancellations heading into the 4th of July holiday being blamed on weather, but also staffing shortages for pilots, crew and air traffic controllers. Many say that the airlines simply are not ready for passenger volume reaching pre-pandemic levels again.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BOAT HIT BY LIGHTNING\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're also learning more, tonight, after that lightning strike hitting a boat about 100 miles off the coast of Tampa Bay.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Seven people onboard when their boat was hit by a bolt of lightning, right there. Incredible pictures. One passenger feeling it move right through him, briefly knocking him unconscious. His pregnant girlfriend and the others not injured. And then the images, the Coast Guard helicopter air lifting them to safety. The Coast Guard praising the boaters from having the necessary safety equipment to speed up that search and rescue. We're glad they're all okay.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And when we come back here, tonight, the news on gas prices just in, tonight, as we head into the July 4th holiday.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220628ei6r00007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AMERICA STRONG RETIRED KINDERGARTEN TEACHER FROM INDIANA\b0\par\par\uc2 339 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 AMTRAK\uc2 , COLLISION, RESCUE, NIGHTINGALE, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ABORTION, LUCAS, MCNICHOLAS, COLEMAN, EVERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE STATES BANNING ABORTIONS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, G7 SUMMIT, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN UNDER PRESSURE\par \par \uc2 RELIGION, SCHOOLS, KENNEDY, PETERSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT RULES IN PRAYER CASE\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, INVASION, RUSSIA, RESCUE, OCHENASH, SEARCH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MISSILE STRIKE ON SHOPPING MALL\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, CANCELLATIONS, LIGHTNING, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HUNDREDS OF FLIGHT CANCELLATIONS\par \par \uc2 GAS, PRICES, QUEEN ELIZABETH II\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE INDEX\par \par \uc2 PEARSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: #AMERICASTRONG\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Finally, tonight, the retired kindergarten teacher from Indiana, Mrs. Pearson, who noticed the high school graduates walking by, so she applauded them. What happened next is "America Strong."\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Take a look at retired kindergarten Jane Pearson, congratulating high school seniors walking by in their caps and gowns.\par \par \uc2 JANE PEARSON (RETIRED KINDERGARTEN TEACHER)\par \par \uc2 Hey. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And this is the moment Mrs. Pearson realizes those graduates aren't just walking by, they're coming for her.\par \par \uc2 JANE PEARSON (RETIRED KINDERGARTEN TEACHER)\par \par \uc2 What's happening?\par \par \uc2 STUDENT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Hi.\par \par \uc2 RELATIVE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Liz. Hi.\par \par \uc2 JANE PEARSON (RETIRED KINDERGARTEN TEACHER)\par \par \uc2 Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 RELATIVE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 These are your kindergarteners.\par \par \uc2 JANE PEARSON (RETIRED KINDERGARTEN TEACHER)\par \par \uc2 Did you know this was happening? Oh, my gosh. I love you all. Look at you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Mrs. Pearson taught in Chesterton, Indiana for 38 years. She retired 12 years ago, and this was the last kindergarten class. They never forgot her.\par \par \uc2 JANE PEARSON (RETIRED KINDERGARTEN TEACHER)\par \par \uc2 Oh, my God. You're all just gorgeous.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Me, too?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In fact, tonight, take a look at this image. Mrs. Pearson, back when they were all so little. Now, the graduates coming to thank the kindergarten teacher who got them started.\par \par \uc2 JANE PEARSON (RETIRED KINDERGARTEN TEACHER)\par \par \uc2 Look at you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We celebrate Mrs. Pearson, too, and my kindergarten teacher, Mrs. Young. See, we don't forget. I'm David Muir. I'll see you right back here, tomorrow. Good night.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220628ei6r00009\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MORE STATES BANNING ABORTIONS NATIONWIDE PROTESTS CONTINUE: SIX STATES ENFORCING BANS\b0\par\par\uc2 842 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 AMTRAK\uc2 , COLLISION, RESCUE, NIGHTINGALE, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ABORTION, LUCAS, MCNICHOLAS, COLEMAN, EVERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE STATES BANNING ABORTIONS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We turn, now, to the battle over abortion in this country, now on the state level after the Supreme Court overturned nearly 50 years of Roe versus Wade. Tonight, Mississippi, one of several states with these trigger laws, looking to ban abortions immediately. That state certifying their trigger law today, soon making it a felony to perform an abortion. And tonight, anti-abortion rights groups are now gearing up for another fight. Could they soon take aim at women seeking abortion pills from out of state? And could women who order those pills and their health care providers be tracked with their internet IP addresses? Rachel Scott, tonight, traveling the country.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONWIDE PROTESTS CONTINUE: SIX STATES ENFORCING BANS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, the battle over abortion now being fought state by state, with a growing of number pushing near total bans.\par \par \uc2 LEIGH LUCAS (PROTESTERS-CHOICE PROTESTER)\par \par \uc2 I feel like my country doesn't care about me. I feel like they are willing to, I know that they are willing to let me die instead of make my own decisions.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CLINICS HAVE STOPPED PROVIDING ABORTIONS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At least six states now enforcing abortion bans. In four more, clinics have stopped priding abortions. These images from San Antonio, Texas, showing the moment patients waiting for an abortion were told it would not happen. Today, abortions resumed in Louisiana after judge temporarily blocked their statewide ban from going into effect, but clinics know they are on borrowed time. In Fairview, Illinois, where abortion is legal, the phones keep ringing. The staff are helping out-of-state patients secure transportation and hotels.\par \par \uc2 DR COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS (PLANNED PARENTHOOD ST LOUIS REGION)\par \par \uc2 Outside of our normal service area, Tennessee, Texas, Oklahoma, Arkansas, Mississippi. We are seeing those folks already coming to us.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Are you bracing to see a surge in patients?\par \par \uc2 DR COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS (PLANNED PARENTHOOD ST LOUIS REGION)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. We said this is ground zero, and we're bracing for impact, for sure.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Just 15 miles away in Missouri, where abortion is banned, I sat down with Republican State Representative Mary Elizabeth Coleman, a mother of six. She'd been waiting for this day all her life.\par \par \uc2 MARY ELIZABETH COLEMAN (REP) (STATE REPRESENTATIVE MISSOURI)\par \par \uc2 When I was a little girl, I said, I'm part of the pro-life generation, and we would say we are the generation that's going to end Roe. I didn't expect that we would see it, maybe even in my lifetime, and certainly not while I was still serving.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Roe was overturned, yesterday. This is illegal.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She's now working on legislation to prevent health care workers in Missouri from helping their patients get abortions in other states.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You would like to crack down on abortion providers from helping women in this state get appointments and access to abortion in other states.\par \par \uc2 MARY ELIZABETH COLEMAN (REP) (STATE REPRESENTATIVE MISSOURI)\par \par \uc2 What I would like to see happen is people's activity in the state of Missouri follow the law in the state of Missouri. And if people are going to choose not to do that and to violate the laws of our state, then they will be liable under the penalties in our code for that activity.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In other states, Republican lawmakers are looking at ways to prevent women from receiving abortion pills from out of state, questions about tracking online activity. But in Wisconsin, where a law written in 1849 has now gone into effect, Democratic Governor \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  is vowing to fight.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR TONY EVERS (DEM) (WISCONSIN)\par \par \uc2 I don't think that a law that was written before the Civil War or before women secured the right to vote should be used to dictate these intimate decisions on reproductive health.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Rachel Scott with us live, tonight, from Fairview Heights, Illinois. And, Rachel, as you reported, there, of course, this battle now being fought state by state. And, obviously, that means abortion rights will be a major issue come the midterms, in particular, as we just saw there, in many of these governors' races, because, in some cases, it would be the governor who determines whether abortion rights stay or go in a particular state.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly, David. The midterm elections are now just months away. There are critical races for governor in Michigan, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin. You heard from Wisconsin's governor, there. Well, today, he pledged clemency for any doctors who could be charged for performing abortions. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel Scott with us, tonight, traveling the country on this important issue on all sides. Rachel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220628ei6r00003\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl